window.DEEP_STORIES["internet"] = {"slug":"internet","title":"The Internet","emoji":"🌐","tagline":"How billions of computers learned to talk to each other","intro":"Right now, tiny bundles of information are racing through glass cables under the ocean, bouncing off satellites and zipping through the air around you. Together they make the Internet: the biggest machine humans have ever built, and nobody owns all of it. But it did not appear overnight. In these 20 parts you will travel from telegraph wires and Cold War worries to a computer that crashed after typing just two letters. You will meet the people who invented packet switching, email, TCP/IP and the World Wide Web, and find out how a message really gets from your screen to a friend on the other side of the planet. Then we will explore search engines, Wi-Fi, online shopping, social media and the cloud, look honestly at hackers, scams and misinformation, learn how to stay safe and kind online, and imagine an Internet that might one day stretch all the way to Mars.","outline":[{"n":1,"title":"Messages Faster Than a Horse","when":"1790s – 1950s"},{"n":2,"title":"Dreamers, Rockets and a Galactic Idea","when":"1945 – 1963"},{"n":3,"title":"Chop It Into Packets!","when":"1960 – 1967"},{"n":4,"title":"LO! The ARPANET Is Born","when":"1966 – 1970"},{"n":5,"title":"You've Got Mail (and Other Networks Too)","when":"1965 – 1982"},{"n":6,"title":"A Network of Networks: TCP/IP","when":"1973 – 1983"},{"n":7,"title":"The Journey of a Packet","when":"How it works (part 1)"},{"n":8,"title":"Addresses and Names: IP and DNS","when":"How it works (part 2), 1970s – today"},{"n":9,"title":"From Labs to Living Rooms","when":"1979 – 1995"},{"n":10,"title":"Weaving the World Wide Web","when":"1945 – 1993"},{"n":11,"title":"Browsers and the Web Explosion","when":"1993 – 2000s"},{"n":12,"title":"Searching the Haystack","when":"1990 – today"},{"n":13,"title":"Wires, Glass and Undersea Cables","when":"1960s – today"},{"n":14,"title":"Cutting the Cord: Wi-Fi and Mobile Internet","when":"1940s – today"},{"n":15,"title":"Boom, Bust and a Brand-New Economy","when":"1994 – 2010s"},{"n":16,"title":"Everyone's a Publisher: Social Media","when":"1997 – today"},{"n":17,"title":"Hackers, Viruses and Secret Codes","when":"1970s – today"},{"n":18,"title":"Being Safe, Smart and Kind Online","when":"Today"},{"n":19,"title":"Living in the Cloud","when":"1999 – today"},{"n":20,"title":"The Next Hop: The Future of the Internet","when":"Today – the future"}],"people":[["Claude Chappe (1763–1805)","French inventor of an optical semaphore telegraph network in the 1790s"],["Samuel Morse (1791–1872)","American painter and inventor who helped develop the electric telegraph and Morse code"],["Alexander Graham Bell (1847–1922)","Scottish-born inventor credited with the telephone (patent 1876)"],["Vannevar Bush (1890–1974)","American engineer who imagined the 'Memex' in 'As We May Think' (1945)"],["J.C.R. Licklider (1915–1990)","psychologist and computer scientist whose vision of an 'Intergalactic Computer Network' inspired ARPANET"],["Paul Baran (1926–2011)","RAND engineer who designed distributed networks with message blocks in the early 1960s"],["Donald Davies (1924–2000)","British computer scientist at the National Physical Laboratory who independently invented packet switching and named the 'packet'"],["Leonard Kleinrock (born 1934)","UCLA professor whose network mathematics and lab hosted the first ARPANET node"],["Bob Taylor (1932–2017)","ARPA official who launched the ARPANET project and later led Xerox PARC's computer lab"],["Larry Roberts (1937–2018)","ARPA program manager who designed and led the building of the ARPANET"],["Wesley Clark (1927–2016)","computer pioneer who suggested using separate small computers (IMPs) to run the network"],["Frank Heart (1929–2018)","led the BBN team that built the Interface Message Processors"],["Steve Crocker (born 1944)","wrote RFC 1 in 1969 and helped create the first ARPANET protocols"],["Charley Kline (born about 1948)","UCLA student programmer who sent the first ARPANET message in 1969"],["Bill Duvall","SRI programmer who received the first ARPANET message in 1969"],["Ray Tomlinson (1941–2016)","BBN engineer who sent the first network email in 1971 and chose the @ sign"],["Bob Kahn (born 1938)","co-designer of TCP/IP and organiser of the 1972 ARPANET public demonstration"],["Vint Cerf (born 1943)","co-designer of TCP/IP, often called one of the 'fathers of the Internet'; later worked on interplanetary networking"],["Louis Pouzin (born 1931)","French engineer who led the CYCLADES network and pioneered datagrams"],["Norman Abramson (1932–2020)","professor who built ALOHAnet, an early wireless packet network in Hawaii"],["Bob Metcalfe (born 1946)","co-inventor of Ethernet at Xerox PARC in 1973"],["Elizabeth 'Jake' Feinler (born 1931)","ran the ARPANET Network Information Center at SRI and helped shape domain naming"],["Jon Postel (1943–1998)","editor of the RFCs and caretaker of Internet addresses and names (IANA)"],["Paul Mockapetris (born 1948)","inventor of the Domain Name System (1983)"],["Radia Perlman (born 1951)","network engineer who invented the spanning-tree protocol used in large networks"],["Robert Tappan Morris (born 1965)","graduate student who released the 1988 Morris worm; later became a computer science professor"],["Gary Thuerk (born 1940s)","DEC marketer who sent the first mass unsolicited email in 1978"],["Scott Fahlman (born 1948)","Carnegie Mellon computer scientist who proposed the :-) smiley in 1982"],["Ted Nelson (born 1937)","coined the word 'hypertext' in the 1960s and dreamed of Project Xanadu"],["Douglas Engelbart (1925–2013)","showed the mouse, hypertext links and video calls in the 1968 'Mother of All Demos'"],["Tim Berners-Lee (born 1955)","British computer scientist who invented the World Wide Web at CERN in 1989–1991"],["Robert Cailliau (born 1947)","Belgian engineer at CERN who partnered with Berners-Lee to develop and promote the Web"],["Mike Sendall (1939–1999)","Berners-Lee's boss at CERN who wrote 'Vague but exciting' on the proposal and let the work go ahead"],["Marc Andreessen (born 1971)","co-author of the Mosaic browser and co-founder of Netscape"],["Eric Bina (born 1964)","co-author of the Mosaic browser at NCSA"],["Al Gore (born 1948)","US senator and vice president who sponsored laws funding high-speed networks, including the 1991 High Performance Computing Act"],["Brendan Eich (born 1961)","created JavaScript at Netscape in 1995"],["Alan Emtage (born 1964)","creator of Archie, the first Internet search tool (1990)"],["Jerry Yang (born 1968) and David Filo (born 1966)","Stanford students who founded Yahoo! in 1994"],["Larry Page (born 1973) and Sergey Brin (born 1973)","Stanford students who created PageRank and founded Google in 1998"],["Charles Kao (1933–2018)","engineer who showed glass fibre could carry data over long distances; Nobel Prize 2009"],["Hedy Lamarr (1914–2000)","film star and inventor who co-patented a frequency-hopping idea with composer George Antheil in 1942"],["George Antheil (1900–1959)","American composer and co-inventor of the 1942 frequency-hopping patent"],["Vic Hayes (born 1941)","Dutch engineer who chaired the IEEE 802.11 committee that created Wi-Fi standards"],["John O'Sullivan (born 1959)","Australian engineer who led the CSIRO team behind key wireless networking patents"],["Jeff Bezos (born 1964)","founder of Amazon (1994)"],["Pierre Omidyar (born 1967)","founder of eBay (1995)"],["Jimmy Wales (born 1966) and Larry Sanger (born 1968)","co-founders of Wikipedia (2001)"],["Linus Torvalds (born 1969)","Finnish programmer who created the Linux kernel in 1991"],["Mark Zuckerberg (born 1984)","co-founder of Facebook (2004)"],["Chad Hurley (born 1977), Steve Chen (born 1978) and Jawed Karim (born 1979)","co-founders of YouTube (2005)"],["Whitfield Diffie (born 1944) and Martin Hellman (born 1945)","published public-key cryptography in 1976"],["Ron Rivest (born 1947), Adi Shamir (born 1952) and Leonard Adleman (born 1945)","inventors of RSA encryption (1977)"],["Clifford Cocks (born 1950)","British GCHQ mathematician who secretly devised a public-key method in 1973"],["Kevin Ashton (born 1968)","British technologist who coined the phrase 'Internet of Things' in 1999"]],"glossary":[["Internet","the worldwide network of networks that all use the TCP/IP rules to pass data"],["World Wide Web","a service on the Internet made of pages linked together, viewed in a browser"],["packet","a small chunk of data with an address label, sent separately and reassembled at the end"],["packet switching","sending data as many independent packets that share lines, instead of holding one line open"],["protocol","an agreed set of rules computers follow to talk to each other"],["TCP/IP","the two main Internet protocols: IP addresses and delivers packets; TCP makes sure they all arrive in order"],["router","a device that reads a packet's address and sends it on to the next step of its journey"],["IP address","a number that identifies a device on the Internet, like a postal address"],["DNS (Domain Name System)","the Internet's phone book that turns names like example.com into IP addresses"],["bit / byte","a bit is a single 1 or 0; a byte is 8 bits"],["bandwidth","how much data a connection can carry each second, measured in bits per second (e.g. Mbps)"],["HTML / HTTP / URL","the language of web pages / the rules for sending them / the address of a page"],["browser","a program that fetches web pages and shows them on your screen"],["fibre optic cable","a cable of thin glass strands that carry data as flashes of light"],["Wi-Fi","a family of wireless networking standards (IEEE 802.11) that carry data by radio waves"],["encryption","scrambling data so only someone with the right key can read it"],["malware","harmful software, such as viruses, worms and ransomware"],["phishing","a trick message pretending to be trustworthy in order to steal passwords or money"],["the cloud","services and storage running on computers in data centres that you reach over the Internet"],["RFC (Request for Comments)","an open document describing Internet rules, started in 1969"]],"parts":[{"n":1,"title":"Messages Faster Than a Horse","when":"1790s – 1950s","summary":"Long ago, a letter could take weeks or even months to reach someone far away. Then people built towers with waving arms, wires that carried sparks, cables under the ocean and telephones that carried voices. But the first computers were giant machines that could not talk to anyone at all!","sections":[{"heading":"A World Waiting for News","paragraphs":["Imagine you live in the year 1800 and your cousin has just moved across the ocean. You write a letter, fold it, seal it with a blob of wax and hand it to a messenger. Then you wait. The letter rides in a bag on a horse to a port, sits in a ship's hold for weeks while the sails catch whatever wind there is, rides another horse at the other end, and finally lands in your cousin's hands. If your cousin writes back straight away, you might hear the answer two or three months after you asked the question.","For almost all of human history, news could travel only as fast as the fastest thing that could carry it. That meant a running person, a galloping horse, a sailing ship or, in a few places, a trained pigeon. Kings and generals built clever systems to speed things up. Riders would hand a message to a fresh rider waiting at the next post, so the message never had to rest even when the horses did. This idea of passing a message along a chain of stations, called relaying, is worth remembering. It turns up again and again in this story, all the way to the Internet you use today.","Even the best relay systems were slow by our standards. In America, the famous Pony Express ran from April 1860 to October 1861. Young riders galloped about 3,000 kilometres (about 1,900 miles) between St. Joseph, Missouri, and Sacramento, California, swapping horses at stations along the way. A letter that went the whole distance usually took about ten days. People thought that was astonishingly fast. Today a message makes the same trip in a small fraction of a second.","Slow news could change history. In the War of 1812 between Britain and the United States, peace was agreed when a treaty was signed in the city of Ghent, in what is now Belgium, on 24 December 1814. But nobody fighting in America knew. On 8 January 1815, more than two weeks later, the two armies fought the Battle of New Orleans, and many soldiers were killed or wounded in a battle that happened after the peace had already been signed. The news of the treaty was still crossing the Atlantic Ocean by ship."]},{"heading":"Towers That Talked: Chappe's Semaphore","paragraphs":["In the early 1790s France was in the middle of its Revolution and at war with several of its neighbours. The new government badly needed a way to send orders to its armies and hear back quickly. A young Frenchman named Claude Chappe, born in 1763 in the small town of Brûlon, had an idea. Working with his brothers, he designed a machine that could make signals big enough to be seen many kilometres away.","Chappe's machine sat on top of a tower. It had a long wooden crossbar, about 4.6 metres long, with a smaller arm about 2 metres long at each end. Operators below pulled handles connected by ropes and pulleys to swing the crossbar and the two arms into different positions. Each arm could take seven positions and the crossbar could tilt at four angles, so the whole thing could make 196 different shapes.","Here is the clever part. The towers were built in a line, each one within sight of the next, usually about 10 to 15 kilometres apart. An operator in one tower peered through a telescope at the neighbouring tower, saw what shape its arms were making, and copied that shape on his own tower. The operator in the next tower copied him, and so on down the line. The message hopped from hilltop to hilltop like a game of copycat across a whole country.","In 1792 the French government asked Chappe to build a line from Paris to the city of Lille near the northern border, about 230 kilometres away. By 1794 it was working. That year it carried news that French troops had captured the town of Condé-sur-l'Escaut, and the report reached Paris less than an hour after the event. A rider on horseback would have needed a day or more. One early test showed that the first symbol of a message could pass through 15 stations in about nine minutes.","Chappe called his invention a 'tachygraph', meaning 'fast writer', but officials preferred the word 'telegraph', from Greek words meaning 'far writing'. That word stuck, and it was later used for the electric telegraph too. To save time, operators did not spell out every word letter by letter. They used a codebook in which pairs of symbols stood for thousands of different words and phrases, a bit like using emoji shortcuts instead of typing whole sentences.","The Chappe system grew into a real network. At its biggest it had about 556 stations stretching about 4,800 kilometres across France, with branches reaching out from Paris like the spokes of a wheel. Other countries, such as Sweden and Britain, built their own versions. In a way, it was a kind of network: messages were passed along by relay stations, each of which only needed to know which way to send the message next. Routers on today's Internet do something similar, as you will see in later parts.","But the semaphore had big weaknesses. It did not work at night, and fog, rain or snow could stop it completely. Each tower needed people on duty from dawn to dusk. One signalman in 1819 said he earned 25 sous a day and worked from daylight to dark. And the system was so slow that a full message of a few dozen symbols could take around half an hour just to travel the Paris–Lille line. It was mostly used by the government, not ordinary people. Chappe himself died in 1805, but his towers kept working for decades, until a new invention using electricity made them old-fashioned. In 1846 France decided to replace them with electric telegraph lines."]},{"heading":"Sparks on a Wire: Samuel Morse","paragraphs":["Samuel Morse did not start out as an inventor. Born in Massachusetts in 1791, he was a talented portrait painter who painted famous Americans. In 1825, while he was away from home working on a painting in Washington, his wife Lucretia became ill and died. According to the usual telling, the news reached him by slow letter, and by the time he got home she had already been buried. Many historians think this painful experience helped push him to find a faster way to send news.","In 1832, sailing home from Europe, Morse got talking with a fellow passenger named Charles Thomas Jackson about electromagnets. An electromagnet is a piece of iron wrapped in coiled wire. When electric current flows through the wire, the iron becomes a magnet. When the current stops, it is no longer a magnet. Morse realised that you could switch the current on and off at one end of a long wire, and a magnet at the far end would click on and off in exactly the same pattern, almost instantly.","He was not the only person with this idea. In Britain, William Cooke and Charles Wheatstone built a working electric telegraph in 1837 that used needles pointing at letters, and it was used on British railways. The American scientist Joseph Henry had already done important experiments showing how to make strong electromagnets work over long wires. Morse's partner Alfred Vail, a skilled mechanic, helped build the equipment and helped create the code. Like the Internet itself, the telegraph was the work of many people.","The code is what made Morse's system so simple. Instead of needing many wires or a machine to point at letters, it used just one wire and two kinds of signal: a short pulse called a dot and a longer pulse called a dash. Each letter got its own pattern. E, the most common letter in English, got the shortest code of all, a single dot. T got a single dash. Rarer letters like Q got longer patterns. Choosing short codes for common letters made messages quicker to send, an idea that computer scientists still use today when they squeeze files to make them smaller.","Building a long telegraph line was expensive, so Morse asked the United States Congress for money. In 1843 Congress gave him 30,000 dollars to build an experimental line about 60 kilometres (38 miles) long between Washington, D.C., and Baltimore, Maryland. Workers strung the wire on wooden poles beside a railway line.","On 24 May 1844, Morse sat in the Supreme Court chamber inside the US Capitol building in Washington and tapped out a message. In Baltimore, at the Mount Clare railway station, Alfred Vail received it and sent it straight back. The words were 'What hath God wrought', a line from the Bible (Numbers 23:23). They had been chosen by Annie Ellsworth, the young daughter of a friend of Morse who had helped him get the money from Congress. The phrase means something like 'Look what God has done', and it captured how amazing it felt to send words across a whole state in the blink of an eye.","After that, telegraph lines spread with incredible speed. Companies strung wires along railways and roads, and by 1861 a telegraph line stretched right across the United States. The Pony Express, which had seemed so fast, closed down just two days after the coast-to-coast telegraph opened in October 1861. Newspapers began printing news from distant cities on the same day it happened. Businesses checked prices in other markets. Families sent short, expensive telegrams for weddings, births and emergencies, paying by the word, so people learned to write very short messages, a bit like text messages today.","Each telegraph office had trained operators who could listen to the clicks of the receiver and write down the letters as fast as they came in. Good operators could recognise each other by the rhythm of their tapping, the way you might recognise a friend's handwriting. Some writers have called the telegraph network 'the Victorian Internet', because it was the first time a huge web of wires let people swap messages almost instantly across great distances."]},{"heading":"A Wire Across the Ocean","paragraphs":["Wires on poles were fine on land, but the Atlantic Ocean is more than 3,000 kilometres wide and in places several kilometres deep. Could anyone lay a wire along the sea floor all the way from Europe to North America? An American businessman named Cyrus Field thought so. In the 1850s he helped set up the Atlantic Telegraph Company, raised money in Britain and America, and set out to do something that many people thought was impossible.","An undersea cable is much more than a bare wire. The copper core that carries the electricity has to be wrapped in a material that keeps seawater out, because salty water conducts electricity and would leak the signal away. The early cables used gutta-percha, a rubbery gum from trees in Southeast Asia, as insulation, then wrapped that in tarred hemp and twisted iron wires to make it strong. The cable was so heavy that no single ship of the time could easily carry all of it.","The first attempt in 1857 failed when the cable snapped and sank. In 1858 two warships, the British HMS Agamemnon and the American USS Niagara, met in the middle of the ocean, joined their two halves of the cable together, and steamed in opposite directions, paying out cable behind them. One end came ashore at Valentia Island off the southwest coast of Ireland and the other at Newfoundland. In August 1858 the cable worked. Celebrations broke out on both sides of the ocean, with fireworks and parades in New York.","Queen Victoria sent a message of congratulations to US President James Buchanan. It was only 98 words long, but it took about 16 hours to get through, because the signals arriving at the far end were so weak and blurry that operators struggled to read them. The cable could manage only about one word every ten minutes or so.","Then things went wrong. The company's chief electrician, Wildman Whitehouse, believed the way to push signals through faster was to use very high voltages, reportedly up to about 2,000 volts. That damaged the cable's insulation, and the signals got worse and worse. By early September 1858, after working for only about three weeks, the cable went silent for good. Whitehouse was dismissed. Some people even began to say the whole thing had been a hoax.","A brilliant Scottish-Irish scientist named William Thomson, who later became Lord Kelvin, had argued for a gentler approach. He had worked out the mathematics of how signals spread out and smear as they travel along a long cable, and he invented a very sensitive detector called the mirror galvanometer. It used a tiny mirror on a magnet to reflect a spot of light that wiggled when even a faint current arrived, so operators could read signals that were far too weak to click a normal receiver.","The next tries used a new star: the Great Eastern, a gigantic iron steamship designed by the engineer Isambard Kingdom Brunel. It was about 211 metres long, by far the largest ship in the world at the time, and it was big enough to carry an entire Atlantic cable in its enormous tanks. In 1865 the cable broke in mid-ocean and was lost. But in 1866 the Great Eastern tried again and succeeded, landing the cable at Heart's Content in Newfoundland. Service began on 27 July 1866. Then, in an amazing piece of seamanship, the crew went back, fished the broken 1865 cable up from the deep sea floor with grappling hooks, joined it to new cable and finished it too. Now there were two working lines.","The new cables were far better made and used Thomson's gentle methods and detectors. They could carry about 8 words per minute, around 80 times faster than the 1858 cable. Sending a message was extremely expensive at first, so it was used mostly by governments, newspapers and businesses. Still, news that once took ten days or more to cross the ocean by ship could now cross in minutes. Over the following decades, telegraph cables were laid to India, Australia, South America and Asia, wrapping the world in a web of copper. These undersea telegraph cables are the great-great-grandparents of the glass fibre cables that carry most of the Internet's traffic between continents today."]},{"heading":"Hello! The Telephone and the Switchboard","paragraphs":["The telegraph was amazing, but you had to learn a code, and you needed a trained operator. What if a wire could carry the sound of a voice itself? Several inventors were racing to find out. One of them was Alexander Graham Bell, who was born in Edinburgh, Scotland, in 1847 and moved to North America with his family. Bell was a teacher of deaf students, and he spent his life studying how sound and speech work, which turned out to be perfect training for inventing the telephone.","The basic idea is beautifully simple. When you speak, your voice makes the air vibrate. In a telephone, those vibrations shake a thin sheet called a diaphragm. The moving diaphragm changes an electric current flowing through a wire, so that the current wobbles up and down in the same pattern as your voice. At the other end, the wobbling current moves another diaphragm, which shakes the air and recreates the sound. Unlike the telegraph's simple on and off clicks, the telephone sent a smoothly changing signal that copied the shape of the sound wave.","Bell's patent application reached the United States Patent Office on 14 February 1876, the very same day that another inventor, Elisha Gray, filed a document describing a similar idea. Historians still argue about who did what first, and an Italian-American inventor named Antonio Meucci had also worked on voice-carrying devices years earlier. But Bell was granted US Patent 174,465 on 7 March 1876, and on 10 March 1876 he spoke the famous first words through his device to his assistant Thomas Watson in the next room: 'Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you.'","At first, telephones were sold in pairs, with a wire running directly between them, maybe from a shop to the owner's house. But if everyone who owned a phone needed a separate wire to every other phone, the number of wires would quickly become ridiculous. With 100 telephones you would need almost 5,000 separate wires to connect every pair! The solution was the telephone exchange, a central office where all the lines in a town came together.","The first commercial telephone exchange opened in New Haven, Connecticut, in January 1878. Each customer had one line running to the exchange. To make a call, you lifted the receiver and spoke to an operator, who sat in front of a big board called a switchboard covered in holes called jacks. You told the operator who you wanted, and she plugged a cord into your jack and another cord into your friend's jack, connecting your two wires together so that you could talk.","The very first operators were often teenage boys, but many customers complained that they were rude and played pranks. In September 1878 a woman named Emma Nutt was hired in Boston and became one of the first female telephone operators. Telephone companies soon hired thousands of young women, who were known for their calm, polite voices. For decades, every call in a town passed through the hands of these operators, who had to be fast and accurate as they plugged and unplugged cords all day long.","Later, machines took over. In 1891 an American undertaker named Almon Strowger patented an automatic switch. A popular story says he was annoyed because a local operator kept sending his customers' calls to a rival undertaker, so he invented a system that let callers connect themselves by dialling numbers. Automatic exchanges using his ideas spread slowly over the following decades. By the middle of the 1900s, telephone lines connected homes and offices across whole continents, and in 1915 Bell made the first coast-to-coast telephone call across the United States, from New York to San Francisco, speaking once again to Thomas Watson."]},{"heading":"The Big Idea: A Line Just for You","paragraphs":["Here is an idea that is going to matter for the rest of this whole story, so let's slow down and look at it carefully. When an operator plugged your line into your friend's line, she made a complete electrical path, called a circuit, running from your phone, through the exchange, to your friend's phone. As long as your call lasted, that circuit belonged only to you. Nobody else could use those wires.","This way of connecting calls is called circuit switching. It is a bit like booking an entire train just for yourself. Even if you only want to carry one small backpack, the whole train is yours from the moment you leave until you arrive, and nobody else can ride on it. If you and your friend stop talking for a minute to think, the line stays reserved, carrying silence.","For a phone call between two people, circuit switching works quite well. People talk in a steady flow, and a good, clear, private line is exactly what you want. But it is wasteful in an important way. For long-distance calls, the circuit had to pass through many exchanges and along long trunk lines between cities. Each of those expensive lines could carry only a limited number of calls at the same time. If they were all in use, the next caller simply heard a busy signal and had to try again later. That is one reason long-distance calls were so expensive for so many years.","Keep this picture of the reserved train in your mind. A few decades later, when people wanted computers to send messages to each other, they realised that computers do not talk like people. A computer might send a quick burst of information, then go quiet for a long time, then send another burst. Booking a whole circuit for that would be like booking a whole train to deliver one postcard every few minutes. Solving that puzzle would lead, in part 3 of this story, to one of the most important ideas behind the Internet."]},{"heading":"Radio Waves and Giant Brains","paragraphs":["Not every message needed a wire. In the 1880s the German scientist Heinrich Hertz proved that invisible electromagnetic waves, which we now call radio waves, could travel through the air. An Italian inventor named Guglielmo Marconi, along with many other experimenters, turned this discovery into wireless telegraphy, which sent Morse code through the air. On 12 December 1901, Marconi reported receiving a faint signal, the three dots of the letter S, sent across the Atlantic from Cornwall in England to Newfoundland. By the 1920s radio stations were broadcasting music, news and sport into millions of homes.","Meanwhile, a completely different kind of machine was being born: the electronic computer. During the Second World War, armies needed huge numbers of difficult calculations, for example to work out how far an artillery shell would fly depending on the angle of the gun, the wind and the air. Rooms full of people, many of them women whose job title was literally 'computer', worked out these tables by hand with mechanical calculators. It was slow, exhausting work.","At the University of Pennsylvania in Philadelphia, two engineers, John Mauchly and J. Presper Eckert, designed a machine to do these calculations electronically. Construction began in 1943, paid for by the US Army, and the machine was finished in 1945. It was called ENIAC, short for Electronic Numerical Integrator and Computer, and it was shown to the public in February 1946.","ENIAC was a monster. It filled a room about 9 by 15 metres (30 by 50 feet), with 40 tall panels arranged in a U shape. It weighed about 27 tonnes, as much as four or five elephants. It contained about 18,000 vacuum tubes, glass bulbs that acted as electronic switches, and hundreds of thousands of hand-soldered joints (about 500,000, according to the University of Pennsylvania). It used about 150 kilowatts of electricity, enough to power many homes, and gave off lots of heat. A popular story says the lights of Philadelphia dimmed when it was turned on, but that is a legend, not a recorded fact.","What it could do was astonishing for its day. ENIAC could perform about 5,000 additions per second. A shell trajectory that took a skilled person about 12 hours to calculate by hand took ENIAC about 30 seconds. But programming it was hard work. There was no keyboard and no screen. To give ENIAC a new task, people had to plug cables into panels and set rows of switches by hand, a job that could take days. That work was done mainly by six brilliant women: Kathleen McNulty, Jean Jennings, Betty Snyder, Marlyn Wescoff, Frances Bilas and Ruth Lichterman. For many years they received very little credit, but today they are celebrated as pioneers of programming.","In the late 1940s and 1950s, more computers appeared, such as the Manchester computers in England and the UNIVAC I in the United States in 1951. Each was a rare and costly machine, often owned by a government, a university or a large company, and kept in its own special room. Data went in on punched cards or paper tape and came out on printed paper. If you wanted to move information from one computer to another, someone usually had to carry the cards or tapes across by hand. Each computer was like an island, brilliant at its own work but cut off from all the others.","So by the 1950s the world had two great inventions that had grown up separately. On one side was a vast web of telegraph wires, telephone lines, undersea cables and radio links that could carry human messages across oceans in seconds. On the other side were a handful of giant electronic brains that could calculate faster than any human, but sat alone in their rooms. People could now talk across oceans. But what would happen when the new thinking machines wanted to talk to each other?"]}],"deepDive":"Why did the 1858 Atlantic cable struggle so badly, even before it was damaged? The answer is physics, and it shaped telecommunications for a century. A long undersea cable behaves like a very long, leaky capacitor: the copper core and the seawater around the insulation act as two conductors separated by a thin layer of gutta-percha. Each pulse sent into the cable has to charge up that capacitance before the far end sees much change, so a crisp on-off pulse arrives as a slow, smeared hump. In the 1850s William Thomson worked out that this delay grows roughly with the square of the cable's length, a result often called the 'law of squares'. Double the length and the signal becomes about four times slower, which is why a cable thousands of kilometres long could manage only a fraction of a word per minute with crude equipment. Raising the voltage, as Wildman Whitehouse did, does not fix the smearing; it only stresses the insulation. Thomson's answer was the opposite: use small currents, a thicker copper core, better insulation and a supersensitive detector (his mirror galvanometer, and later his siphon recorder) so operators could read faint, blurred signals. The 1866 cable, about 8 words per minute, showed he was right. Later, Oliver Heaviside's mathematics explained how adding inductance to a line could reduce distortion, an idea that improved long telephone lines. The second big idea of this part is circuit switching. Telephone engineers measured traffic carefully, and the Danish mathematician Agner Krarup Erlang developed formulas in the early 1900s to work out how many circuits an exchange needed so that only a small share of callers heard a busy signal. Those formulas assumed calls arriving at random and lasting minutes, with each call holding its circuit for the whole time. That model fits human conversation well, but it fits computer traffic badly, because computers send short bursts separated by long silences. Historians of the Internet often point to this mismatch as the problem that packet switching later solved. It is also worth remembering that the 'Victorian Internet' comparison, popularised by writer Tom Standage, is an analogy: the telegraph network moved messages by human operators and was not a network of computers, but it raised many of the same issues we still face, from coded shorthand and privacy to fraud and information overload.","funFact":"The ship that finally laid a lasting Atlantic telegraph cable in 1866, the Great Eastern, was about 211 metres long, and no ship longer than it was built for roughly 40 years.","check":{"q":"What were the words of Samuel Morse's famous telegraph message sent from Washington to Baltimore in 1844?","a":"What hath God wrought","wrong":["Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you","Hello, Baltimore, can you hear me?","LO"]},"sources":[{"title":"Semaphore line","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semaphore_line"},{"title":"Claude Chappe","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_Chappe"},{"title":"Samuel Morse","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Morse"},{"title":"Transatlantic telegraph cable","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transatlantic_telegraph_cable"},{"title":"Invention of the telephone","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Invention_of_the_telephone"},{"title":"Telephone exchange","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telephone_exchange"},{"title":"ENIAC","publisher":"Penn Engineering, University of Pennsylvania","url":"https://www.engineering.upenn.edu/about/history-heritage/eniac/"},{"title":"ENIAC","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ENIAC"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/01-1.jpg","alt":"A restored Chappe optical telegraph tower near Saverne, France, with its signal arm on the roof.","file":"Tour du telegraphe Chappe Saverne 02.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_du_telegraphe_Chappe_Saverne_02.JPG","author":"Hans-Peter Scholz Ulenspiegel","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0 de"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/01-2.jpg","alt":"Cable-laying machinery on the deck of the Great Eastern, painted by Robert Dudley in 1866.","file":"Deck of the Great Eastern, the Cable Trough, etc., 1866 MET DP820957.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deck_of_the_Great_Eastern,_the_Cable_Trough,_etc.,_1866_MET_DP820957.jpg","author":"Robert Charles Dudley","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/01-3.jpg","alt":"Two ENIAC programmers set up the giant computer for its public demonstration in February 1946.","file":"Two women operating ENIAC (full resolution).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Two_women_operating_ENIAC_(full_resolution).jpg","author":"Unidentified U.S. Army photographer","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4392},{"n":2,"title":"Dreamers, Rockets and a Galactic Idea","when":"1945 – 1963","summary":"After a big war, a scientist dreamed of a magic desk that could link every book and note together. Then a tiny Soviet satellite went beep-beep-beep around the Earth, and America rushed to invent new things. A clever scientist nicknamed Lick imagined computers all over the world joined in one giant 'Intergalactic' network!","sections":[{"heading":"A Desk That Could Remember Everything","paragraphs":["In the summer of 1945, the Second World War was ending. One of the most powerful scientists in the United States was an engineer named Vannevar Bush. During the war he had led the government office that organised American scientific research for the war effort, overseeing thousands of scientists working on radar, medicines, weapons and much more. Now he asked himself a big question: once the fighting stops, what should all these brilliant scientists do next?","His answer appeared in July 1945 in an American magazine called The Atlantic Monthly, in an essay titled 'As We May Think'. Bush pointed out a problem that you might recognise. Scientists were producing so many reports, books and articles that nobody could keep up with them all. Important discoveries were getting lost, simply because no one could find them in time. The world was drowning in information.","Bush noticed something about the way people think. Our minds do not file ideas in neat alphabetical order like a library card catalogue. Instead, one thought reminds us of another, which reminds us of another, in a chain of connections. He called this association. What if a machine could store information the same way, by linking related things together?","So Bush imagined a machine he called the Memex. It would look like an ordinary office desk, but inside it would hold a huge library stored on microfilm, which is photographic film that shrinks pages down to tiny pictures. On top of the desk would be slanting translucent screens for reading, plus a keyboard and buttons and levers. You could call up any book or note in seconds, and you could even photograph your own handwritten notes or pictures onto the film by pressing a lever.","The most exciting part was what Bush called trails. While reading, you could tie two items together, so that whenever you looked at one, you could instantly jump to the other. Step by step, you could build a whole trail of linked documents about a subject you were exploring, add your own comments, and even give a copy of your trail to a friend. In September 1945, Life magazine printed illustrations showing what a Memex desk might look like.","The Memex was never built. It was based on microfilm and mechanical parts, not on computers or networks, and it was a dream rather than a design. But its central idea, linking pieces of information together so you can jump from one to another, inspired a whole generation. Douglas Engelbart read the essay and later invented many tools of modern computing. Ted Nelson, who coined the word 'hypertext' in the 1960s, quoted Bush at length. Decades later, when you click a link on a web page, you are following a kind of trail that Bush imagined. You will meet these hypertext pioneers again in part 10."]},{"heading":"One Giant Computer, Many Users","paragraphs":["In the 1950s computers were rare, enormous and extremely expensive. A large computer could cost millions of dollars, and only governments, big companies and a few universities could afford one. Because computer time was so precious, people were not allowed to sit at the machine and play around with it. Instead, they used a system called batch processing.","Here is how batch processing worked. A programmer wrote a program on paper, then turned it into a stack of punched cards, stiff cards with rows of little holes where each pattern of holes stood for a letter or number. The programmer handed the stack, called a deck, to an operator, who fed it into the computer along with many other people's decks, one batch after another. Hours later, or even the next day, the programmer collected a printout. If there was a single typing mistake, the program failed, and the whole process started again. Fixing a small program could take days.","Some researchers found this maddening. They wanted to have a conversation with the computer: type something, get an answer straight away, try again. In 1959 the British computer scientist Christopher Strachey described an idea called time-sharing, and in the same year the American scientist John McCarthy proposed a time-sharing system at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT). The trick was speed. A computer works so fast that it can switch between many users in a fraction of a second, giving each one a tiny slice of time in turn. To each person, it feels as if they have the whole computer to themselves.","Imagine a chess master playing 30 people at once, walking quickly from board to board and making one move at each. Each player gets the master's attention only for a moment, but the master is so quick that every player feels the game is moving along nicely. Time-sharing let one computer play that role for dozens of people sitting at terminals.","A terminal was usually a machine like an electric typewriter, called a teletype, connected to the computer by a cable or by a telephone line. You typed a command, and the computer's reply was typed back onto a roll of paper in front of you. There were no colourful screens in most places yet, but for the first time ordinary programmers could talk to a computer almost as if it were a person.","At MIT, a team led by Fernando Corbató built the Compatible Time-Sharing System, known as CTSS, which had a working version by November 1961. Others followed. At Dartmouth College in New Hampshire, the mathematicians John Kemeny and Thomas Kurtz built a time-sharing system that began working in 1964 so that students, not just experts, could use a computer, and they invented the simple programming language BASIC for them. Users on time-sharing systems soon discovered that they could leave notes and files for one another on the shared machine. Without quite meaning to, they had created small online communities, years before the Internet.","Time-sharing also showed that a computer could be used from far away. If a terminal could connect to a computer over a telephone line from across a building, why not from across a city or a country? In fact, the US military had already built a giant air-defence system called SAGE in the 1950s, with computer centres linked by telephone lines to radar stations, using early devices called modems to turn computer data into sounds the phone lines could carry. Computers were starting to reach beyond their rooms. But they still could not easily talk to other computers of different makes."]},{"heading":"Beep... Beep... Beep: The Sputnik Shock","paragraphs":["On 4 October 1957, a rocket roared into the night sky from a launch site in the desert near Tyuratam, in what is now Kazakhstan, then part of the Soviet Union. On top of the rocket was a shiny metal ball about 58 centimetres (22 inches) wide, weighing about 83 kilograms (183 pounds), with four long spring-loaded antennas trailing behind it like whiskers. It was called Sputnik 1, and it became the first human-made object to orbit the Earth.","Sputnik circled the planet about once every 96 minutes. Inside, a small radio transmitter sent out a simple beep, beep, beep. Radio operators and amateur radio fans around the world tuned in and heard it as it passed overhead. On clear nights, people could even spot the rocket's bright upper stage, which was also in orbit, crossing the sky. Sputnik itself kept orbiting for about three months, until it fell back into the atmosphere and burned up on 4 January 1958.","Sputnik also taught scientists something useful. At the Johns Hopkins Applied Physics Laboratory in Maryland, two young physicists, William Guier and George Weiffenbach, listened to its beeps and noticed that the pitch rose as the satellite came towards them and fell as it moved away, the same effect you hear when an ambulance siren races past. By measuring that change carefully, they could work out the satellite's whole orbit. Their colleagues then turned the idea around: if you know exactly where a satellite is, you can use its signal to work out where you are on Earth. That led to Transit, an early satellite navigation system for ships and submarines, and an ancestor of the satellite navigation in phones today.","To understand why a beeping ball caused such a stir, you need to know about the Cold War. After the Second World War, the United States and the Soviet Union became rivals. They did not fight each other directly, but they competed fiercely in weapons, science, sport and space. Many Americans had assumed that their country was ahead in technology. Sputnik suggested that the Soviet Union might be ahead instead. And if a Soviet rocket could put a satellite in orbit, people worried, it might also be able to carry a weapon across the world.","Historians often describe the reaction in the United States as a shock, and some at the time compared it to the surprise attack on Pearl Harbor in 1941. Things got worse when the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 2 on 3 November 1957, carrying a dog named Laika. Then, on 6 December 1957, an American Vanguard rocket, watched on live television, rose only about a metre off the launch pad before it sank back and exploded in a ball of fire. Newspapers mocked it with names like 'Flopnik'.","The United States finally reached orbit on 31 January 1958 with a satellite called Explorer 1, launched by the US Army. Its instrument, designed by the scientist James Van Allen, discovered belts of radiation around the Earth, now called the Van Allen belts. But the government decided that catching up would take more than one satellite. It needed new organisations and much more money for science and education.","In 1958 Congress passed laws that changed American science for decades. On 29 July 1958 President Dwight D. Eisenhower signed the act that created NASA, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, which opened on 1 October 1958. Later that year a law called the National Defense Education Act gave money to schools and universities to train more scientists, engineers, mathematicians and language experts. And even before NASA, the government had created a smaller, unusual agency with a very big mission."]},{"heading":"ARPA: An Agency for Big Ideas","paragraphs":["On 7 February 1958, only four months after Sputnik, the US Department of Defense created the Advanced Research Projects Agency, known as ARPA. Its job was to make sure the United States would never again be caught by surprise by another country's technology. It started with a budget of about 520 million dollars, and its first director was Roy Johnson, a business executive.","At first ARPA was in charge of America's space projects. But when NASA opened later in 1958, most of the space work and much of ARPA's money moved over to the new space agency. That left ARPA with a smaller role and a question: what should it do now? It became the place that funded bold, risky research that might take years to pay off, such as new materials, ways of detecting nuclear tests, and better computers.","ARPA worked in an unusual way. It did not have its own big laboratories full of scientists. Instead, it had a small number of program managers who chose promising researchers at universities and companies and gave them money to chase ambitious ideas. The managers had a lot of freedom to make decisions quickly and to take chances on ideas that other funders might think too wild. Many projects failed, and that was accepted as part of the deal, because a few successes could change everything.","Some of ARPA's early projects show the kinds of problems it tackled. Project Vela looked for ways to detect secret nuclear weapons tests, whether underground, in the atmosphere or in space, using sensitive earthquake detectors and special satellites. Project Defender studied ways to defend against long-range missiles. These were huge, difficult challenges that needed new science, and they made ARPA a regular customer for the most powerful computers available. Its leaders soon realised that the way people used computers was itself a problem worth studying. Computers were becoming essential for gathering information and helping commanders make decisions quickly, yet they were awkward and slow to use.","Its name changed several times over the years. In March 1972 it became DARPA, the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, which is what it is mostly called today. DARPA still describes its mission as preventing technological surprise and creating surprises of its own. Over the decades, research it funded contributed to things like stealth aircraft, self-driving car competitions and much more. But its most famous contribution of all began with a quiet psychologist who loved computers."]},{"heading":"Meet Lick: A Psychologist Who Loved Computers","paragraphs":["Joseph Carl Robnett Licklider was born in St. Louis, Missouri, in 1915. That is a long name, so almost everyone called him J.C.R., or simply Lick. Friends remembered him as warm, funny and generous, a man who would rather share an idea than take credit for it. He is not as famous as some inventors in this story, yet many of the people who built the Internet said he was the person who first showed them where they were going.","Lick was not trained as a computer scientist, because in the 1930s and 1940s there was no such subject. He studied psychology, and he became an expert in psychoacoustics, the science of how people hear sounds. He developed a well-known theory of how we hear the pitch of a sound. During and after the Second World War he studied how people could communicate clearly over noisy radios and telephones.","In the 1950s, at MIT, Lick worked on the human side of the SAGE air-defence system: how operators sitting at screens could work together with computers to track aircraft quickly. In 1957 he joined a Boston company called Bolt Beranek and Newman, known as BBN, where he got to use a computer interactively, typing and getting answers right away, and he helped develop a time-sharing system. He was hooked. He realised that computers could be much more than giant calculators. They could be partners in thinking.","In March 1960 Lick published a paper called 'Man-Computer Symbiosis' in a scientific journal for electronics engineers. Symbiosis is a word from biology. It describes two different living things that live closely together and help each other. Lick gave the example of the fig tree and a tiny wasp: the tree depends on the insect to pollinate its flowers, and the insect's young depend on the tree for food. Neither does as well alone. Lick suggested that humans and computers could form a partnership like that.","In this partnership, he wrote, people would do what people are best at: setting goals, asking questions, making guesses and judging results. Computers would do the routine work, such as searching through data, calculating and drawing graphs. To show why this mattered, Lick had timed his own working day. He estimated that about 85 percent of his 'thinking' time was actually spent getting into a position to think, gathering information, plotting graphs and converting data, rather than actually thinking. A computer partner could take over much of that drudgery.","His paper opened with the line: 'Man-computer symbiosis is an expected development in cooperative interaction between men and electronic computers.' He listed what would be needed to make it happen: time-sharing, better memory, easier programming languages, better displays, and even computers that could understand speech. He also imagined a network of 'thinking centers', computers linked together by communication lines and shared by many users. Sixty years later, many of his wishes have come true."]},{"heading":"Members of the Intergalactic Computer Network","paragraphs":["In October 1962 ARPA asked Lick to set up a new office to fund research on computers for command and control, meaning how leaders gather information and make decisions. He became the first head of what came to be called the Information Processing Techniques Office, or IPTO. Now Lick had money to spend and the chance to turn his dreams into real projects.","He chose to spread it around the country's best computer research groups. He funded MIT's Project MAC, which began in 1963 and pushed time-sharing much further, as well as work at places such as the University of California at Berkeley, Stanford University, Carnegie Institute of Technology in Pittsburgh, UCLA, and the System Development Corporation in Santa Monica, California. Many of the researchers he backed went on to become leaders of computing for decades. He also encouraged them to work together and share their ideas, rather than compete in secret.","In August 1962, even before he joined ARPA, Lick wrote memos describing what he called a 'Galactic Network'. He pictured a set of computers all around the world, connected together, so that anyone at any site could quickly reach data and programs stored anywhere else. The Internet Society, which helps look after the Internet today, says that in spirit this idea was very much like the Internet we have now.","In April 1963, Lick sent a long memo to the researchers he was funding. With his usual sense of humour, he addressed it to the 'Members and Affiliates of the Intergalactic Computer Network'. Of course, nobody was really planning to connect computers on other planets. The grand name was a joke, but it was also a hint of how big and connected he imagined things could become.","The memo was serious underneath the fun. Lick pointed out that each research group had its own computer, its own programming languages and its own way of doing things. If these computers were ever going to work together, they would need to agree on a shared language for talking to each other. He compared the problem to science-fiction stories about meeting beings from another world: how do you start communicating with someone who does not share your language at all? Today we call those shared agreements protocols, and they are the true foundation of the Internet.","Lick left ARPA in 1964, and others continued his work. He did not build the network himself, and the technology to do it did not yet exist. But his vision became the guiding star. In 1968 he and Robert Taylor, who later led the office himself, wrote an article called 'The Computer as a Communication Device'. It began with a bold prediction: 'In a few years, men will be able to communicate more effectively through a machine than face to face.' Considering that you may chat with friends through a screen every day, that sounds remarkably accurate."]},{"heading":"Why Connect Computers at All?","paragraphs":["Why did researchers want to connect their computers? It was not mainly to send each other jokes or play games, although they would soon do both. The first reason was money. Computers cost a fortune, and every university Lick funded wanted the latest machines. If a powerful computer at one university could be used by researchers at another, through a network, the government would not have to buy the same expensive machine over and over again.","The second reason was programs. Each research group wrote special software for its own machine, such as programs for graphics, for studying language or for complex mathematics. Usually, that software only ran on that one computer. A researcher in California who wanted to use a brilliant program in Massachusetts might have to fly across the country, or rewrite the whole program from scratch. With a network, they could simply log in from home.","The third reason was data and people. Researchers wanted to share results, collections of data and documents, and they wanted to work together across long distances. The Internet Society notes that sharing computer resources was a major reason for building both the ARPANET and, later, the Internet. And Lick saw something more: connected computers could bring people with the same interests together, wherever they lived, forming what he called online communities.","Here it is worth clearing up a famous myth. People sometimes say that the network that grew into the Internet was built mainly so the military could survive a nuclear war. That is not quite right. The researchers of the 1960s chiefly wanted to share computers, programs and data. Another engineer, working separately, really was thinking about how communications could survive an attack, and you will meet him in part 3. But that was not the main reason ARPA built its network.","But there was still a giant puzzle. The only long-distance lines available were telephone lines, and you know from part 1 how telephone networks work: each call ties up a whole circuit from start to finish. Computers do not talk like people. They send a quick burst of data, then pause for a long time, then send another burst. Holding a whole line open for that would be slow, wasteful and expensive. So the dreamers had a vision and a funder, but they faced a problem nobody had solved yet: how do you send computer messages over a network without tying up whole lines, the way a phone call does?"]}],"deepDive":"Historians of the Internet often argue about how much credit visionaries like Bush and Licklider deserve compared with the engineers who actually built networks. Bush's Memex was a mechanical, microfilm-based fantasy; it had no network and no computer, and some critics point out that his 'trails' were closer to stored bookmarks than to true hypertext. Yet both Douglas Engelbart and Ted Nelson said the essay shaped their thinking, so its influence on later hypertext and the Web is well documented. Licklider is a more direct ancestor. His 1960 paper set out an agenda: time-sharing, interactive graphics, better memory organisation, easier programming languages and speech input. As first director of ARPA's computer research office from October 1962, he steered money to a handful of university groups instead of building a central government laboratory, which helped create a research community that later built the ARPANET. His successors, including Ivan Sutherland and Robert Taylor, inherited both that community and his conviction that computers should be linked. Another debate concerns the role of the Cold War. Without Sputnik there would have been no ARPA in 1958, and ARPA was a defence agency; the money came because of military competition with the Soviet Union. But the agency's computer office gave researchers wide freedom, and the stated purpose of the network that followed was resource sharing among researchers, not surviving a nuclear attack. Historians therefore describe the Internet's origins as military funding combined with an academic culture of openness. Time-sharing also matters more than it might seem. By letting many users share one machine through terminals, systems such as MIT's CTSS (working by November 1961) and Dartmouth's system (1964) created the first online communities, complete with shared files and messages, and they made the idea of reaching a computer from far away feel normal. They also exposed the networking problem clearly: a connection from a terminal to a computer carries bursty traffic, mostly silence punctuated by short bursts of data, which is a poor match for telephone circuits priced and engineered for steady conversation. That mismatch is the technical gap the next generation of researchers would fill.","funFact":"The Sputnik 1 satellite that shocked the world in 1957 was only about 58 centimetres wide, a little bigger than a beach ball.","check":{"q":"How did J.C.R. Licklider jokingly address his April 1963 memo to the researchers he funded?","a":"To the 'Members and Affiliates of the Intergalactic Computer Network'","wrong":["To the 'Friends of the World Wide Web'","To the 'Crew of Sputnik 1'","To the 'Memex Users Club'"]},"sources":[{"title":"Brief History of the Internet","publisher":"Internet Society","url":"https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/"},{"title":"Internet History of 1960s","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/internethistory/1960s/"},{"title":"Networking & The Web timeline","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/timeline/networking-the-web/"},{"title":"Sputnik and the Origins of the Space Age","publisher":"NASA History","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/history/sputnik/sputorig.html"},{"title":"Man-Computer Symbiosis (J.C.R. Licklider, 1960)","publisher":"MIT CSAIL","url":"https://groups.csail.mit.edu/medg/people/psz/Licklider.html"},{"title":"About DARPA","publisher":"DARPA","url":"https://www.darpa.mil/about"},{"title":"Memex","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memex"},{"title":"Time-sharing","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time-sharing"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/02-1.jpg","alt":"A replica of Sputnik 1, the shiny metal ball with four antennas, at the National Air and Space Museum.","file":"Sputnik asm.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sputnik_asm.jpg","author":"NSSDC, NASA","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/02-2.jpg","alt":"Engineer Vannevar Bush at his desk. In 1945 he wrote \"As We May Think\".","file":"Vannevar Bush portrait.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vannevar_Bush_portrait.jpg","author":"This portrait is credited to \"OEM Defense\", the Office for Emergency…","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/02-3.jpg","alt":"A 1968 Teletype Model 33 ASR terminal: a keyboard, printer and paper-tape punch on a stand.","file":"Teletype Model 33 ASR (1968) (14689737122).png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teletype_Model_33_ASR_(1968)_(14689737122).png","author":"Dennis van Zuijlekom from Ermelo, The Netherlands","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3820},{"n":3,"title":"Chop It Into Packets!","when":"1960 – 1967","summary":"Three clever thinkers in America and Britain had the same surprising idea: cut every message into tiny labelled pieces and let them find their own way. One of them gave the pieces a name we still use today: packets!","sections":[{"heading":"A Problem Hiding in the Phone Line","paragraphs":["At the end of the last part, we left the computer scientists of the early 1960s with a puzzle. They wanted their machines to talk to each other, but the only big network that reached everywhere was the telephone system. And the telephone system had been designed for a completely different kind of conversation.","Remember the big idea from Part 1: a telephone call is a circuit. When you ring your grandmother, the exchange connects a path of wires from your phone to hers, and that path belongs to the two of you until somebody hangs up. Even when you both go quiet to think, or you put the phone down to fetch a biscuit, the line stays reserved. Nobody else can use it.","For people chatting, that is mostly fine, because when we talk, we talk fairly steadily. Computers are different. A person sitting at a terminal connected to a distant computer might type a command, then stare at the screen for half a minute reading the answer, then type a few more letters, then go and get a coffee. The data comes in short, sudden bursts with long silences in between. Engineers call this kind of traffic 'bursty'.","Now imagine paying for a long-distance telephone line between Boston and Los Angeles, holding it open all day, and using it for perhaps one or two per cent of the time. It would be like hiring a whole train to carry one envelope, then keeping the train waiting at the station in case you wanted to send another envelope later. In the 1960s long-distance lines were expensive, and computers were even more expensive. Wasting either one hurt.","There was a second, darker worry too. The United States and the Soviet Union were in the middle of the Cold War, both building nuclear weapons. Military planners asked an uncomfortable question: if an attack destroyed a few big telephone exchanges, would the country's leaders still be able to send messages at all? The phone network of the time was built around a fairly small number of important switching centres. Knock out the centres, and huge parts of the system would go silent.","Two very different worries, one about waste and one about survival, led to the same answer. And remarkably, that answer was discovered more than once, by people working thousands of kilometres apart who had never met."]},{"heading":"Paul Baran and the Fishnet Nobody Built","paragraphs":["Paul Baran was born in 1926 in Grodno, a city that was then in Poland, and moved to the United States with his family when he was about two years old. He grew up in Philadelphia, studied electrical engineering, worked on some of the early computers, and in 1959 joined the RAND Corporation in Santa Monica, California. RAND was a 'think tank': a place where clever people were paid to think hard about difficult problems, many of them for the US Air Force.","Baran's problem was the survival question. How could you build a communications network that would keep working even if large parts of it were destroyed? He started by looking at the shapes networks could have.","A centralised network looks like a wheel: every line runs to one hub in the middle. It is cheap and simple, but if the hub is destroyed, everything stops. A decentralised network is a set of smaller wheels whose hubs are joined together. It is tougher, but each hub is still a weak spot, and losing a few hubs cuts the network into islands. Baran proposed a third shape, which he called distributed. In a distributed network there is no centre at all. Every station, or node, is linked to several neighbours, and the whole thing looks like a fishnet. To cut someone off, you would have to destroy all of the paths around them, not just one hub.","Baran tested the idea with computer simulations, which was itself quite new at the time. He found something surprising and encouraging. You did not need a hugely expensive web with every node joined to every other node. If each node had just three or four links to its neighbours, the network could lose a large share of its nodes, in some of his tests about half of them, and the surviving nodes could usually still reach each other. Redundancy, meaning having spare paths, was the secret, and a little of it went a long way.","But a fishnet raised a new question. If there are many possible routes between two points, how does a message know which one to take, especially if parts of the net are broken? Baran's answer was the clever part. Instead of setting up one fixed path in advance, like a phone call, he suggested chopping each message into small, standard-sized pieces that he called 'message blocks'. Each block would carry a label saying where it was going. Every node would act like a tiny post office: it would receive a block, store it for a moment, check the label and pass it on to whichever neighbour looked like the best next step. This is called 'store and forward'.","Baran even imagined the nodes learning as they worked. Each node would keep notes on how long blocks took to arrive from different directions, and use those notes to guess the fastest way to send blocks onwards. If a route broke, the notes would soon change and blocks would flow around the damage. Because each node passed blocks along quickly rather than holding on to them, the idea became known as 'hot-potato routing': nobody wants to hold a hot potato for long.","Baran first presented the message block idea around 1961 and wrote about it in RAND papers over the next few years. In 1964 RAND published his work as a series of eleven reports called 'On Distributed Communications'. They described almost everything: the shapes of networks, the blocks, the routing, the switching machines and even how to keep messages secret.","You might expect that such a clever plan would be built at once. It was not.","Baran's network was meant to carry digital voice and data, and to do that across the country it would have needed the cooperation of AT&T, the giant company that ran almost all American telephone lines. AT&T's engineers were experts in circuits. They had spent decades perfecting a system where a call gets its own reserved path, and the idea of chopping conversations into little blocks that wandered across a net of cheap switches seemed strange and unworkable to them. According to Baran's own later accounts, they dismissed it. A company that earned its money from renting out whole lines also had little reason to welcome a design that shared lines between many users.","There were other problems. The military organisation that would have been put in charge did not have experience with digital computers, and Baran worried that it would build the system badly, which could make the whole idea look like a failure for years. In the end, his network was not built during the 1960s.","So the reports went onto library shelves at RAND. They were not secret, and some people read them, but for a few years the idea simply waited. Baran went on to an inventive career: he helped found the Institute for the Future in 1968 and later started companies that made modems and wireless networking equipment. Much later he received many honours, including the US National Medal of Technology and Innovation in 2007. He died in 2011, having lived to see the fishnet he imagined cover the whole planet."]},{"heading":"Across the Ocean: Donald Davies Names the Packet","paragraphs":["Now travel about 8,800 kilometres east, to Teddington, a leafy town on the River Thames just outside London. Here stands the National Physical Laboratory, or NPL, Britain's national centre for measurement and science, where people look after the exact definitions of things like the metre and the second.","Donald Davies worked at NPL. He was born in 1924 in Treorchy, a mining town in the Rhondda valley in Wales. His father died when he was a baby, and his mother moved the family to Portsmouth, where he grew up. He studied physics and then mathematics at Imperial College London, and in 1947 he joined NPL, where one of his colleagues was the famous mathematician Alan Turing. Turing had designed a computer called the ACE, the Automatic Computing Engine. The full machine was hard to build, and after Turing left NPL, Davies became one of the leaders of the team that built a smaller version, the Pilot ACE. It first ran in May 1950 and was among the fastest computers of its day.","So by the mid-1960s, Davies was a very experienced computer person. In 1965 he travelled to the United States, attended a big computing congress in New York and visited the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT), where people were excitedly using time-sharing, the trick from Part 2 where many users share one big computer through terminals. He talked with experts, including J.C.R. Licklider, about connecting distant users to these machines.","Back in England, Davies thought hard about the bursty traffic problem. He realised that a data line could be shared in the same way a time-sharing computer was shared. Instead of giving one user the line for a long time, cut everyone's data into short pieces of a fixed format and send them one after another, mixing pieces from many users on the same wire. Each piece would be so short that nobody would have to wait long. Late in 1965 he wrote down his plan, and in 1966 he gave a talk describing a fast national data network for Britain built on this idea.","Davies needed a name for the little pieces. He wanted a short, ordinary word that would not confuse people and could be translated easily, because he hoped his idea would be used all over the world. He reportedly checked with a language expert, who agreed that his choice would work well in many languages. The word was 'packet', as in a small parcel. The method became 'packet switching'. Nearly sixty years later, engineers everywhere still use both words every day.","Here is the remarkable twist. Davies had worked all of this out without knowing anything about Paul Baran. After his 1966 talk, someone from Britain's Ministry of Defence came up to him and told him that an American at RAND had already written about something very similar. Davies read Baran's reports and found that, although their reasons were different (Baran wanted survival, Davies wanted efficient and fast sharing), their designs were strikingly alike. Davies, by all accounts, was generous about it and acknowledged Baran's earlier work.","Unlike Baran, Davies managed to build something. His team at NPL, including Roger Scantlebury, Keith Bartlett and Peter Wilkinson, designed a local network to link computers and terminals around the laboratory site. Parts of it began working in early 1969, and it was fully running in January 1970. The NPL network was one of the very first packet-switched networks in the world. It was later upgraded, and it kept running until 1986."]},{"heading":"Leonard Kleinrock and the Maths of Waiting in Line","paragraphs":["Our third thinker worked with pencil, paper and equations rather than switches and wires. Leonard Kleinrock was born in New York City in 1934. As a boy he was fascinated by electronics, went to the Bronx High School of Science, then studied electrical engineering at the City College of New York. In 1957 he went to MIT as a graduate student.","Kleinrock chose to study how messages flow through a network. The networks that existed then were mostly 'message switching' systems used for telegrams, where a whole message was received at a switching centre, stored, and then sent on to the next centre. One system he studied was Western Union's telegram network. In 1961 he wrote a thesis proposal called 'Information Flow in Large Communication Nets', and he finished his doctoral thesis at the end of 1962. In 1964 it was published as a book called 'Communication Nets'.","The mathematics Kleinrock used is called queueing theory. A queue is simply a line of things waiting their turn: shoppers at a till, cars at a traffic light, or messages waiting at a switching centre for a free line. Queueing theory uses probability to answer questions such as: how long will the average message wait? How full can the lines get before the waiting becomes terrible? Does it help to make messages shorter, or to add more links?","These might sound like dull questions, but they are exactly what you need to know before you spend millions building a network. Picture a supermarket. If shoppers arrive at random, a single till that is busy 50 per cent of the time has fairly short queues. Push it to 90 per cent busy, and queues suddenly grow very long, because whenever a few shoppers arrive close together there is no spare capacity to catch up. Kleinrock's work explained effects like this for networks of many connected 'tills', and showed, among other things, that sharing lines among many users could make a network much more efficient.","In 1963 Kleinrock joined the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA) as a young professor. There he kept working on the mathematics of networks. His lab would soon play a starring role in our story, as you will see in the next part. Davies, too, used Kleinrock-style queueing calculations in his 1966 paper to show that a packet network could respond fast enough for people typing at terminals."]},{"heading":"The Postcard Trick: How Packet Switching Works","paragraphs":["Let's leave the inventors for a moment and look closely at their idea, because it is the beating heart of the whole Internet.","Imagine you want to send your best friend a very long letter, a whole story of 40 pages, but the only thing the post office will carry is postcards. So you cut the story into 40 pieces and glue each piece to a postcard. On every postcard you write three things: your friend's address, your own address, and a number, such as '17 of 40'. Then you drop all 40 postcards into the postbox.","The postcards do not have to travel together. Some might go through one sorting office, others through a different one that happens to be less busy today. Card 23 might even arrive before card 5. But that does not matter, because when the postcards reach your friend, they can lay them out in number order and read the whole story. If card 31 is missing, they can see the gap and send you a message asking for it again.","That is packet switching. A message, whether a sentence, a photo or a video, is chopped into packets. Each packet has a small label at the front, called a header, which holds the addresses and a sequence number, and behind the label comes a slice of the actual data. The switching machines along the way, which today we call routers, read each header and pass that packet one step closer to its destination. At the far end, the packets are put back in order.","Now here is the magic for efficiency. In a phone-style circuit, a line belongs to one conversation, even while it is silent. In packet switching, packets from many different conversations are mixed together on the same line, one after another, like cars from many different families sharing the same motorway. When you pause to read your screen, your packets stop for a while, and the line is instantly used by other people's packets instead. No space is wasted waiting for you.","This means a single line can serve many users at once. If a hundred people each use their connection only now and then, a packet network can carry all of them on a line that would be far too small to give every one of them a private circuit. That is why the planners could promise that lines would be used far more efficiently than in a phone call.","Packet switching also explains the survival idea. If one router breaks, the next packets can simply be sent another way, just as your postcards could be sorted by a different office if one burned down. The network does not need to rebuild a whole circuit. It just keeps finding paths, one packet at a time.","There are trade-offs, of course. Every packet carries a header, which takes up a little room, a bit like the space your address uses on each postcard. Packets may wait in queues at busy routers, which adds delay, and that is exactly the kind of problem Kleinrock's maths could measure. And somebody has to be responsible for putting the pieces back in order and noticing missing ones. Working out who does that job became a big part of the story later, when TCP/IP was invented, as you will see in Part 6."]},{"heading":"Wheels, Stars and Fishnets","paragraphs":["One of the most famous pictures in the history of the Internet appears in Baran's reports. It shows three networks side by side, drawn as dots joined by lines, and you can easily draw your own version on a piece of paper.","For the first, centralised network, draw one dot in the middle of the page and about twenty dots around it, each joined only to the centre. It looks like a wheel with spokes, or a star. Now cover the middle dot with your thumb. Every outer dot is cut off from all the others. One hit and the whole network is gone.","For the second, decentralised network, draw four or five medium-sized hub dots, join the hubs to each other, and then give each hub its own little cluster of outer dots. This is a bit like how airlines work, with big hub airports and smaller airports around them. Cover one hub and its whole cluster drops out, though the rest survives. Better, but still fragile.","For the third, distributed network, scatter about twenty dots across the page and join each one to its three or four nearest neighbours, until it looks like a fishnet or a honeycomb. Now try covering a dot with your thumb. Messages simply flow around it. Cover several, and there are usually still paths. You would need to cover a whole line of dots to cut the net in two.","Today's Internet is not a perfect fishnet. It has some very important hubs, such as large exchange points and undersea cable landing stations, which you will visit in Part 13. But it is much closer to Baran's distributed picture than to a single wheel, and that is one reason it so often keeps working when individual parts fail."]},{"heading":"The Nuclear War Story: What Is True and What Is Not","paragraphs":["You may have heard someone say that 'the Internet was built to survive a nuclear war'. It is one of the most repeated stories about the Internet, and it is only partly true, so let's untangle it carefully.","What is true: Paul Baran's work at RAND really was about survivable communications. He wanted a network that could still carry orders after an attack, and that is why he cared so much about distributed shapes and flexible routing. His reports were paid for by the US military with exactly that worry in mind.","What is not true: the ARPANET, the network you will meet in the next part, was not built mainly to survive a nuclear attack. It was funded by ARPA's computer research office for a much more everyday reason. Scientists at universities and research centres wanted to share expensive computers, programs and data with each other. Charles Herzfeld, who was director of ARPA when the project was approved, later said plainly that the ARPANET was not created to be a bomb-proof command system. It came from frustration that there were only a few powerful research computers in the country and researchers who needed them were scattered far apart.","Why does the myth survive? Partly because Baran's ideas did feed into the ARPANET's design, so his reasons got mixed up with ARPA's. Partly because the finished network really was quite tough, since its routers kept recalculating routes and could send traffic around a broken link. And partly because 'built to survive the end of the world' simply makes a more exciting story than 'built so professors could share computers'.","The honest version is still impressive. A design that was partly inspired by fears of war ended up being used for something peaceful: helping people share knowledge."]},{"heading":"Who Gets the Credit? Ideas Meet in the Mountains","paragraphs":["So who invented packet switching? This is a question historians still discuss, and the fair answer is that more than one person deserves credit, in different ways.","Paul Baran was first to work out a full design for a distributed network that cut messages into small blocks and routed them independently, from about 1960 to 1964. Donald Davies reached a very similar design on his own in 1965, gave it the name 'packet switching', aimed it at fast, efficient everyday computer use, and actually built an early working network at NPL. Most historians, and organisations such as the US National Inventors Hall of Fame, describe Baran and Davies as having independently invented packet switching.","Leonard Kleinrock's role is the one that people argue about most. His early 1960s work was about the mathematics of message flow in networks, and it gave engineers powerful tools to predict delays and plan capacity. From the 1990s onwards Kleinrock described this work as the theory behind packet switching. Davies and some other pioneers disagreed, pointing out that Kleinrock's thesis studied message switching, where whole messages are sent at once, rather than chopping messages into small packets. Kleinrock himself has more recently acknowledged that his early papers were about message switching. A fair summary is that Baran and Davies invented packet switching, and Kleinrock's queueing mathematics became an important tool for understanding and measuring packet networks, especially once his lab joined the ARPANET.","This kind of story happens often in science. When a problem is 'in the air', several people may solve it at around the same time, because they read the same papers and face the same needs. The telephone, calculus and the theory of evolution all have similar stories.","The three strands finally came together in October 1967, at a computer conference called the Symposium on Operating Systems Principles in Gatlinburg, a small town in the Smoky Mountains of Tennessee. A young American named Larry Roberts, who was now working for ARPA, presented a plan for connecting research computers. At the same meeting, Roger Scantlebury from Davies' team presented the NPL packet-switching design, and he told the Americans about Paul Baran's reports too.","For Roberts, it was a turning point. He took the NPL ideas home, read Baran's reports, and borrowed the word 'packet'. The British team also argued that faster lines would make the network respond better, and the plan's line speed was raised from 2.4 kilobits per second to 50 kilobits per second, about twenty times faster.","ARPA now had a design, a name for the pieces and the money to try it. The decision was made: the United States would build a real packet-switching network across the country, linking research computers at universities. It would need new machines that nobody had built before, rules that nobody had written yet, and long lines rented from the telephone company. And there was a deadline. The first computers were supposed to be connected and talking by 1969. Could a handful of engineers and students possibly do it in time?"]}],"deepDive":"Why is sharing lines with packets so much more efficient than reserving circuits? The key idea is called statistical multiplexing. Suppose 100 terminal users each need a line that can carry 10 kilobits per second while they are actually sending, but each one is busy only about 5 per cent of the time. With circuits you must reserve 100 lines' worth of capacity, 1,000 kilobits per second, even though on average only about 50 kilobits per second is ever in use. With packets you can provide something nearer the average plus a safety margin, because it is very unlikely that all 100 users will burst at the same instant. Queueing theory tells you how big that margin must be. As a link gets close to full, average waiting time grows sharply; in the simplest textbook model, delay is proportional to 1 divided by (1 minus the fraction of time the link is busy), so a link that is 90 per cent busy has queues about five times longer than one that is 50 per cent busy. That curve is why networks are designed with spare capacity. Historians also distinguish message switching from packet switching. In telegraph-style message switching, each centre must receive a whole message before forwarding it, so a very long message holds up everything behind it and needs lots of storage. Packet switching limits the size of each piece, so nodes need less memory, short interactive messages are not stuck behind long files, and different packets of one message can be in transit on different links at once, a trick called pipelining. Baran and Davies both chose small fixed limits for this reason. The credit debate became heated in the 1990s and 2000s. Kleinrock's supporters emphasised his early mathematical analysis of networks and his 1964 book; Davies, in a paper published after his death in 2000, argued that he could find no evidence Kleinrock had understood packet switching's principles in his early work; and Baran repeatedly said that he and Davies had reached their ideas independently. Many historians, such as Janet Abbate in 'Inventing the Internet', present the story as one of several people contributing different pieces: Baran's survivable distributed design, Davies' efficient packet network and its name, Roberts' decision to fund and build one at scale, and Kleinrock's tools for measuring how it behaved.","funFact":"Donald Davies, who named the packet, had earlier worked alongside Alan Turing and helped build the Pilot ACE, one of Britain's first computers, which first ran in May 1950.","check":{"q":"Who coined the word 'packet' for the small pieces of data sent across a network?","a":"Donald Davies of Britain's National Physical Laboratory","wrong":["Paul Baran of the RAND Corporation","Alexander Graham Bell","Tim Berners-Lee at CERN"]},"sources":[{"title":"Brief History of the Internet","publisher":"Internet Society","url":"https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/"},{"title":"Internet History of 1960s","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/internethistory/1960s/"},{"title":"Packet switching","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Packet_switching"},{"title":"Paul Baran","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Baran"},{"title":"Donald Davies","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Davies"},{"title":"Leonard Kleinrock","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonard_Kleinrock"},{"title":"ARPANET","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ARPANET"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/03-1.jpg","alt":"Centralised, decentralised and distributed networks, redrawn after Paul Baran's famous 1964 diagram.","file":"Centralised-decentralised-distributed.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centralised-decentralised-distributed.png","author":"1983 (talk) (Uploads)","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/03-2.jpg","alt":"Diagram of the NPL network model, the packet-switching design of Donald Davies's team in Britain.","file":"NPL Network Model - en.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NPL_Network_Model_-_en.png","author":"Michel Bakni","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/03-3.jpg","alt":"The Pilot ACE computer, built at the National Physical Laboratory, on display at the Science Museum, London.","file":"Pilot ACE in the Science Museum.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pilot_ACE_in_the_Science_Museum.JPG","author":"Philafrenzy","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4282},{"n":4,"title":"LO! The ARPANET Is Born","when":"1966 – 1970","summary":"In 1969 a team of engineers and students connected faraway computers for the very first time. Their first message was meant to say LOGIN, but the computer crashed after just two letters: L and O!","sections":[{"heading":"Three Keyboards in One Office","paragraphs":["Picture an office in the Pentagon, the enormous five-sided headquarters of the US Department of Defense near Washington, D.C. It is 1966. Inside sits Bob Taylor, the new head of ARPA's computer research office, the Information Processing Techniques Office that J.C.R. Licklider had led a few years earlier (you met him in Part 2).","Taylor was an unusual person to be in charge of computers. Born in Texas in 1932, he had studied psychology and mathematics, not engineering, and was fascinated by how people think and how machines might help them. He had worked at NASA, where he funded research into new ways of interacting with computers, before Ivan Sutherland, then head of the ARPA computer office, invited him to join in 1965. When Sutherland left in 1966, Taylor took over as the office's third director. He did not write programs himself, but he had a rare talent for spotting brilliant people and getting them to work together, a talent he would later use again when he led a famous computer lab at Xerox in California.","Taylor's office is cluttered with three computer terminals, each a kind of electric typewriter. Each one is connected, by its own long-distance telephone line, to a different computer in a different part of the country. One reaches a computer called the Q-32 at the System Development Corporation in Santa Monica, California. Another reaches Project Genie at the University of California, Berkeley. The third reaches a time-sharing system at MIT in Cambridge, Massachusetts.","All three were research projects that ARPA was paying for, and Taylor wanted to keep in touch with them. But every machine had its own way of logging in, its own commands and its own rules. To use a different computer, Taylor had to slide his chair over to a different terminal. If he learned something useful on one machine, he could not easily pass it to the others.","Taylor thought this was silly. It would be far better, he reasoned, to have one terminal that could connect to any computer, and to link the computers themselves so that researchers could share programs and data. There was a money reason too. ARPA was funding research computers at many universities, and each group kept asking for its own expensive machine, often duplicating what others already had. A network could let a scientist in Utah use a special program running in California, instead of ARPA buying another computer.","Taylor walked down the corridor to see the director of ARPA, a physicist named Charles Herzfeld, and explained his idea. Herzfeld liked it. According to the stories both men told later, the meeting was remarkably short, and Taylor left with about one million dollars for the project, money moved from another part of ARPA's budget. For a big government agency, that was lightning speed."]},{"heading":"Larry Roberts Draws the Map","paragraphs":["Now Taylor needed someone to design and run the project. He wanted Larry Roberts, a brilliant young computer scientist at MIT's Lincoln Laboratory. Roberts was born in 1937 in Westport, Connecticut, and had earned all three of his degrees at MIT. He had also already done a small networking experiment.","In 1965, Roberts and a colleague named Thomas Marill linked the TX-2 computer at Lincoln Laboratory in Massachusetts with the Q-32 computer in Santa Monica, on the other side of the continent, using a telephone line. The two machines could exchange messages and run programs on each other. It worked, but it was slow and clumsy, and it convinced Roberts that a dialled phone circuit was a poor way to connect computers.","At first Roberts did not want the ARPA job. He enjoyed doing research, and running a project from an office in Washington sounded like paperwork. Taylor kept persuading him, and in early 1967 Roberts finally agreed to join ARPA as the network's program manager.","Roberts got to work drawing up a plan. In April 1967 he gathered the leaders of ARPA-funded computer projects at a meeting in Ann Arbor, Michigan. Not everyone was keen. Some of the researchers worried that strangers from other universities would use up precious time on their computers, and they did not want to spend effort writing complicated networking programs for their own busy machines.","The answer came from Wesley Clark, a computer pioneer from Washington University in St. Louis who had helped build some of the earliest small interactive computers. His suggestion was simple and very wise. Do not make the big research computers handle the network at all, he said. Instead, put a separate small computer next to each big one. These small computers would be joined to each other by the long-distance lines, and they would do all the work of packets, routing and checking. Each big computer, called a 'host', would only need to talk to its own little neighbour.","Roberts loved it. The little computers were given the name Interface Message Processors, or IMPs. That split made the job much easier. Every IMP could be the same kind of machine running the same program, built by one team, while each university only had to connect its own host to its own IMP. If you look at a router in a school or office today, you are looking at a distant descendant of the IMP.","Then, in October 1967, came the conference in Gatlinburg, Tennessee, that you read about in Part 3. There Roberts presented his plan and learned about Donald Davies' packet switching and Paul Baran's reports. He adopted packets and raised the planned line speed to 50 kilobits per second. The ARPANET had its design. Now someone had to build it."]},{"heading":"The IMP Guys of Cambridge","paragraphs":["In the summer of 1968, ARPA sent out a 'request for quotation', a document describing what it wanted and inviting companies to offer to build it. It went to about 140 possible bidders. Many big computer companies did not even bother to reply; some thought the idea would never work. In the end only about a dozen sent proposals.","The winner, announced at the end of 1968, was a fairly small company in Cambridge, Massachusetts, called Bolt Beranek and Newman, or BBN. BBN had started as a firm of experts in acoustics, the science of sound, and had designed the sound for concert halls. Over the years it had hired many clever computer people. Licklider himself had worked there before joining ARPA.","The BBN team was led by Frank Heart, a careful, demanding engineer who had worked on real-time computer systems at Lincoln Laboratory. His group was small, only a handful of people at first, and they later became known as 'the IMP Guys'. Will Crowther, Dave Walden and Bernie Cosell wrote much of the software. Severo Ornstein and Ben Barker designed the hardware. Bob Kahn, a mathematician and engineer, worked on how the whole network should behave; you will meet him again as one of the inventors of TCP/IP in Part 6.","When news of the contract spread, Senator Edward Kennedy of Massachusetts sent BBN a congratulations telegram. He thanked them for winning the contract to build an 'Interfaith Message Processor'. It was a muddle, but the engineers loved it, and the joke has been retold ever since.","For the IMPs, BBN chose a minicomputer called the Honeywell DDP-516. 'Mini' is a funny word for it: it was about the size of a large refrigerator. BBN picked a 'ruggedised' version built for military use, with a heavy steel cabinet that could survive knocks and rough handling. That was handy, because these machines would sit in university computer rooms full of curious graduate students who might be tempted to fiddle with them.","Inside, each IMP had about 12,000 'words' of memory, roughly 24 kilobytes. A modern phone has hundreds of thousands of times more. Into that tiny space the team squeezed a program of about 6,000 words, written in assembly language, the low-level code that tells a processor exactly which steps to take. The IMP had to receive data from its host, cut it into packets, add headers, send packets along the lines to other IMPs, check every packet for errors, resend any that went missing, choose routes, and keep running for weeks without crashing.","The deadline was tight. BBN signed its contract around the start of 1969 and had to deliver the first IMP to UCLA by the beginning of September, about eight months later, with three more following quickly. The team worked long hours, built a test version, and even wrote programs to pretend to be a network so that they could test the IMP before any real lines existed."]},{"heading":"Inside the Little Box","paragraphs":["What exactly did an IMP do all day? Let's follow a message from UCLA to SRI and watch.","First, the host computer at UCLA hands its IMP a message, such as a line a user has just typed. The host does not need to know anything about telephone lines or routes. It simply says to its IMP: please deliver this to the host at SRI.","The IMP looks at the message. If it is long, the IMP cuts it into packets of up to about 1,000 bits each, roughly the size of a short paragraph of text. To each packet it adds a header saying where the packet is going, where it came from and which piece of the message it is.","Next the IMP checks its routing table, a small list in its memory that estimates how long it would take to reach each destination through each of its neighbours. It sends the packet down the line that looks fastest. Along with the packet goes a checksum, a special number calculated from all the bits in the packet. If even one bit is flipped by electrical noise on the line, the checksum will no longer match.","When the next IMP receives the packet, it recalculates the checksum. If everything matches, it sends back a short reply called an acknowledgement, which means 'got it, thanks'. Only then does the first IMP throw away its copy. If the packet arrived damaged, the receiving IMP simply ignores it and sends no acknowledgement. After a short wait the first IMP notices the silence and sends the packet again. This is store and forward in action: hold on to a packet until you are sure it is safely in the next hands.","Finally, the IMP at SRI collects all the packets of the message, puts them back together in the right order and passes the complete message to SRI's host computer. To the people at the keyboards, all this happens in a fraction of a second, and they never see the packets at all.","Every few moments, each IMP also chats with its neighbours, swapping its estimates of how long it takes to reach different places. That way, if a line breaks or gets busy, the tables change and packets start flowing another way. The IMPs even reported on their own health to a control centre at BBN, so engineers in Cambridge could often spot a problem at a faraway site before anyone there had noticed it."]},{"heading":"Graduate Students Write the Rules","paragraphs":["While BBN built the IMPs, someone had to decide how the host computers would actually talk to each other across the network. Nobody had done this before, and ARPA did not hand down any instructions. Instead, the job fell to a group of graduate students and young researchers at the first four sites.","They started meeting in the summer of 1968, travelling between the universities. They called themselves the Network Working Group. One of them was Steve Crocker, a graduate student in Kleinrock's group at UCLA, born in 1944. Crocker later remembered that they had no official instructions and half expected that some senior, grown-up experts would arrive at any moment and take the job away from them. Nobody did.","After one meeting in Utah in early 1969, the group decided they should write down their ideas and questions so everyone could see them. Crocker took on the task. He worried that if the notes sounded too bossy, they might annoy the official people in charge. He later described spending a sleepless night choosing humble words. He decided to call each note a 'Request for Comments'. The name said: this is not a final rule, it is an idea, please tell us what you think. As Crocker put it, the ground rules were that anyone could say anything and nothing was official.","The first Request for Comments, RFC 1, is dated 7 April 1969 and titled 'Host Software'. It describes how hosts and IMPs might exchange messages and lists many questions the group still had. It even warns that a response from a faraway host might take about half a second, slow enough that they had to think carefully about typing over it.","That friendly tradition turned out to be one of the most important inventions of the whole Internet story. RFCs are still written today. They are open, free documents that anyone can read, and they describe how almost every part of the Internet works, from email to web addresses. Thousands have been published. Instead of one company or government deciding the rules in secret, the Internet grew from people openly proposing ideas, testing them and improving them together. The group's first big creation was a set of rules for host-to-host connections called the Network Control Program, or NCP, which ran the ARPANET for more than a decade."]},{"heading":"A Refrigerator Arrives at UCLA","paragraphs":["On Saturday 30 August 1969, a few days earlier than the deadline, BBN's first IMP arrived by air at UCLA, in Los Angeles. It was wheeled into Leonard Kleinrock's lab in Boelter Hall, a concrete engineering building on the campus, and set up in room 3420.","Kleinrock's lab had been chosen as the first site because ARPA had asked his group to run the Network Measurement Center. Their job was to watch the new network closely, measure delays and traffic, and test whether it behaved the way the mathematics predicted. Who better to host the first IMP than the people whose job was to measure it?","The team at UCLA had been preparing frantically. They had to build their own connection between the IMP and their host computer, a Scientific Data Systems Sigma 7, and write software for it. With the IMP turning up a little early, the last days of preparation were a scramble. They need not have worried. Within a day or so, the IMP and the Sigma 7 were talking to each other.","Of course, one IMP on its own is not a network. A network needs at least two ends. The second IMP was delivered in early October 1969 to the Stanford Research Institute, known as SRI, in Menlo Park, near San Francisco, about 500 kilometres up the coast. SRI was home to Douglas Engelbart's team, who were inventing new ways for people to work with computers, including the computer mouse. Their host was an SDS 940 computer. AT&T provided a leased line running at 50 kilobits per second to link the two IMPs.","Everything was in place for the very first conversation between computers on the ARPANET."]},{"heading":"The Night of L and O","paragraphs":["It is the evening of Wednesday 29 October 1969. In room 3420 at UCLA, a student programmer called Charley Kline sits at a terminal connected to the Sigma 7. Professor Kleinrock is supervising. About 500 kilometres away at SRI, a programmer named Bill Duvall sits at the SDS 940. The two young men are also talking on an ordinary telephone, so that each can tell the other what is happening on his screen. Ironically, the old circuit-switched telephone is helping to test the new packet network.","The plan is for Kline to log in to the SRI computer from UCLA, as if he were sitting right there in Menlo Park. To do that he needs to type the command LOGIN. The SRI computer is set up to recognise commands and help complete them.","Kline types the letter L. Over the phone, he asks if it arrived. Duvall says yes, he got the L. Kline types O. Duvall confirms: he got the O. Kline types G, and the SRI system crashes.","So, at about 10:30 in the evening, the first message ever sent across the ARPANET from one host computer to another was two letters: L and O. Nobody chose 'LO' on purpose. They were trying to type LOGIN, and the system fell over halfway. Accounts from the people involved suggest the trouble was on the SRI side, where a buffer, a small holding area in memory, overflowed while the system was trying to handle the incoming characters.","Duvall found and fixed the problem. About an hour later, Kline tried again, and this time the whole login worked. A computer user in Los Angeles was now working on a computer in Menlo Park through a packet-switching network.","In UCLA's logbook for the IMP, which you can see in photographs today, there is a short handwritten entry for that night. It records the time, 22:30, notes that UCLA 'talked to SRI host to host', and is signed with Kline's initials, CSK. It is one of the most famous scraps of paper in computing history, and it is wonderfully ordinary: just a few words in a notebook.","Years later, Kleinrock liked to point out that 'lo' is an old English word, as in 'lo and behold', meaning 'look!' He joked that the network's first word could hardly have been better if they had planned it. They had not planned it, though. It was a happy accident, and in a way that makes it even better: the Internet began, like many great inventions, with something going slightly wrong."]},{"heading":"Four Nodes Make a Network","paragraphs":["Two connected computers were exciting, but a network needs more. Two more IMPs followed before the end of 1969.","The third went to the University of California, Santa Barbara (UCSB), on the coast north of Los Angeles. Its host was a large IBM 360/75, and researchers there, including Glen Culler and Burton Fried, were working on ways to display mathematics interactively on screens. The fourth went to the University of Utah in Salt Lake City, far inland among the mountains. Its host was a DEC PDP-10, and the Utah team, led by David Evans with Ivan Sutherland and others, was a world leader in computer graphics.","Look at those four hosts: a Sigma 7, an SDS 940, an IBM 360 and a PDP-10. They were made by three different companies, used different operating systems and stored data in different ways. Getting them to cooperate was exactly the kind of problem Wesley Clark's IMP idea and the Network Working Group's rules were designed to solve. Each host only had to talk to its own IMP, and the IMPs all spoke the same language.","The first permanent link between UCLA and SRI was set up on 21 November 1969. By 5 December 1969 all four sites were connected. If you drew it on a map, it looked like a small, lopsided diamond in the American West: three sites in California and one in Utah. UCLA was linked to SRI and to UCSB, SRI and UCSB were linked to each other, and Utah was joined to SRI. The three California sites formed a little triangle, so there was more than one path between them, just as Baran had recommended.","Meanwhile, Kleinrock's Network Measurement Center at UCLA did exactly what its name promised. The team sent streams of test traffic through the network, sometimes deliberately pushing it hard, to see where it would slow down or get stuck. They found real problems, including situations where IMPs filled up their small memories while waiting for each other, so that traffic froze. Each discovery went back to BBN, and the IMP software was improved. Testing a network by trying to break it on purpose is still an important part of engineering today.","Then the network began to grow. In March 1970 it reached the East Coast, when BBN itself in Cambridge, Massachusetts, joined. By the end of 1970 there were about 13 IMPs. By late 1972 there were about 30, including places such as MIT, Harvard, Carnegie Mellon, the RAND Corporation and NASA's Ames Research Center. Across the country, lines that once carried only telephone calls now also carried streams of packets.","Remember the myth from Part 3? Here is the reality. The ARPANET grew because researchers found it useful for sharing computers, programs and ideas, not because it was a military bunker system. Its distributed design did make it tough, and that was a pleasant bonus.","Yet the builders had a nagging question. They had made a marvellous machine for sharing computers. But what would people actually do with it, day after day? Would professors really log in to faraway computers to run programs? The answer turned out to be a surprise. The thing people loved most was not running programs at all. It was sending each other messages."]}],"deepDive":"The ARPANET's design split work between two layers of machines. The IMPs formed the 'subnet': they were connected by leased 50 kilobit-per-second telephone lines and handled packets, routing, error detection and retransmission between themselves. The hosts sat outside the subnet and exchanged larger 'messages' with their local IMP. In BBN's design, a host could hand its IMP a message of up to about 8,000 bits; the IMP broke it into packets of roughly 1,000 bits, each with a header, and sent them through the subnet, where the destination IMP reassembled the message before delivering it. Each IMP stored a packet until the next IMP acknowledged receiving it correctly. Errors were detected with a 24-bit checksum calculated in hardware; a damaged packet was simply discarded and not acknowledged, so the sending IMP would time out and transmit it again. This hop-by-hop reliability is different from the later Internet, where TCP at the end computers takes most of the responsibility, an important shift you will see in Part 6. Routing was adaptive and distributed, in the spirit of Baran's ideas. Each IMP kept a table estimating the delay to every destination via each of its neighbours, shared its estimates with neighbours frequently, and sent each packet along the line with the lowest estimated delay. This early 'distance-vector' method could be slow to react to some failures and sometimes produced loops, problems that led to new routing methods later in the 1970s and 1980s. Historians often stress how much was invented under deadline pressure. The BBN team had about eight months to deliver working IMPs; the host-to-host rules came from volunteers in the Network Working Group; and Kleinrock's Network Measurement Center ran experiments that deliberately loaded the network to find weak spots, uncovering problems such as deadlocks where IMPs ran out of buffer space while waiting for each other. Today the UCLA room where the first IMP was installed has been restored as the Kleinrock Internet Heritage Site, and SRI holds an IEEE Milestone plaque marking the birth of the ARPANET.","funFact":"When BBN won the contract to build the IMPs, Senator Edward Kennedy sent a congratulations telegram that mixed up the name and called the machine an 'Interfaith Message Processor'.","check":{"q":"What letters arrived at SRI before the system crashed during the first ARPANET message on 29 October 1969?","a":"L and O (from the word LOGIN)","wrong":["H and I","The whole word LOGIN","S and OS"]},"sources":[{"title":"The Day the Infant Internet Uttered its First Words","publisher":"Leonard Kleinrock, UCLA","url":"https://www.lk.cs.ucla.edu/internet_first_words.html"},{"title":"RFC 1: Host Software","publisher":"RFC Editor","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1"},{"title":"RFC 1000: The Request for Comments Reference Guide","publisher":"RFC Editor","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1000"},{"title":"ARPANET","publisher":"SRI International","url":"https://www.sri.com/hoi/arpanet/"},{"title":"Internet History of 1960s","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/internethistory/1960s/"},{"title":"Brief History of the Internet","publisher":"Internet Society","url":"https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/"},{"title":"ARPANET","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ARPANET"},{"title":"Interface Message Processor","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interface_Message_Processor"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/04-1.jpg","alt":"Front panel of a BBN Interface Message Processor (IMP), the ARPANET's first routers, at the Computer History Museum.","file":"Interface Message Processor - BBN Arpanet Router - Computer History Museum (2007-11-10 23.06.56 by Carlo Nardone).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Interface_Message_Processor_-_BBN_Arpanet_Router_-_Computer_History_Museum_(2007-11-10_23.06.56_by_Carlo_Nardone).jpg","author":"Carlo Nardone from Roma, Italy","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/04-2.jpg","alt":"UCLA's IMP log for 29 October 1969: at 22:30 \"Talked to SRI host to host\" - the first ARPANET message.","file":"The IMP Log The Very First Message Sent on the Internet (6293913865).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_IMP_Log_The_Very_First_Message_Sent_on_the_Internet_(6293913865).jpg","author":"Andrew \"FastLizard4\" Adams from United States","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/04-3.jpg","alt":"Hand-drawn map of the ARPA Network in December 1969: four nodes at UCLA, SRI, UCSB and Utah.","file":"A sketch of the ARPANET in December 1969.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_sketch_of_the_ARPANET_in_December_1969.png","author":"DARPA","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3813},{"n":5,"title":"You've Got Mail (and Other Networks Too)","when":"1965 – 1982","summary":"The ARPANET was built so scientists could share big computers, but people quickly found a better use: sending each other messages. Ray Tomlinson picked the @ sign, and soon email was everywhere. Meanwhile, other inventors built networks that used radio in Hawaii, cables in an office, and phone lines in France and Britain.","sections":[{"heading":"Mail Inside One Giant Computer","paragraphs":["Before email could travel between computers, it had to be invented inside one. To see how, we need to visit MIT in Cambridge, Massachusetts, in the early 1960s. There, a large IBM computer ran a system called CTSS, the Compatible Time-Sharing System. Time-sharing, as you met in Part 2, meant that dozens of people could sit at their own typewriter-like terminals and use the same big machine at once. The computer switched between them so quickly that each person felt as if it belonged to them alone.","Once lots of people share one computer, something interesting happens: they start to feel like neighbours in the same building. They leave files for each other. They want to say 'the printer is fixed' or 'please do not delete my program'. And the people running the machine need a way to tell users things, too.","In the summer of 1965, two young MIT programmers, Tom Van Vleck and Noel Morris, wrote a command for CTSS called MAIL. It was added to the system in August 1965. The idea started with a practical problem. Sometimes a user lost a file and asked the operators to restore it from a backup tape. When the job was done, how should the operators tell them? Van Vleck and Morris argued that a general messaging tool would be useful for far more than that, and they were right.","Their MAIL command was simple and clever. When you sent someone a message, the computer added your text to the end of a special file called MAIL BOX in that person's own storage area. The next time they logged in, the system checked whether that file existed. If it did, a notice popped up on their terminal saying YOU HAVE MAIL BOX. It was the great-great-grandparent of every 'You've got mail!' alert and every red badge on a phone app.","It was not perfect. You could not type a friendly name like 'Tom'. Instead you had to know the person's 'problem number' and 'programmer number', the codes CTSS used to identify each user. A message also had to be short enough to fit into a single record on the disk. But it worked, and people used it.","Historians have found other early message programs on other time-sharing computers around the same time, so it is fairer to say that 'computer mail' grew up in several places than to name one single inventor. What all of these systems shared was a limit: a message could only reach people who used the same computer. If your friend's account lived on a different machine across the country, your MAIL command could not help. Remember that limit, because the ARPANET was about to break it."]},{"heading":"Ray Tomlinson and the Little @ Sign","paragraphs":["Ray Tomlinson was born on 23 April 1941 in Amsterdam, a small town in New York State. He studied electrical engineering at Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute and then at MIT, and in 1967 he joined Bolt Beranek and Newman, the Cambridge company you met in Part 4 that built the ARPANET's Interface Message Processors.","At BBN, Tomlinson worked on an operating system called TENEX, which ran on DEC PDP-10 computers used at many ARPANET sites. He also helped write the software that let those computers use the network. So in 1971 he knew two programs very well.","The first was SNDMSG (say it 'send message'). Like the old CTSS MAIL command, it let a user leave a message for another user on the same computer. The second was CPYNET, an experimental program Tomlinson had written to copy files from one ARPANET computer to another.","Tomlinson had a neat thought: a message is really just a small file. If CPYNET could carry a file across the network, and SNDMSG could add a message to someone's mailbox, why not join the two? He added code from CPYNET into SNDMSG, so that instead of only adding text to a mailbox on the same machine, the program could send it over the ARPANET and add it to a mailbox on a different machine.","Picture the scene one day in 1971. In a room at BBN stood two computers, side by side. They were physically close, but they were not wired straight to each other. Each was connected to the ARPANET, so a message from one had to go out into the network and come back in to the other. Tomlinson typed a test message on one machine, walked over to the second, and checked. There it was: the first email sent between two computers over a network.","What did it say? Nobody knows for sure. Many books claim it was QWERTYUIOP, the top row of letters on a keyboard. Tomlinson himself said he might have typed something like that, but he could not remember. As he put it later, 'The test messages were entirely forgettable and I have, therefore, forgotten them.' So the honest answer is that history's first network email was probably a jumble of letters, and its exact words are lost.","Now for the famous little symbol. Tomlinson needed a way to write an address that said both who the message was for and which computer they used. He looked at his keyboard, a Model 33 Teletype, for a character that would not be confused with anything else. He chose @. It already meant 'at', as in '5 apples @ 10 cents', so an address like tomlinson@bbn-tenexa read naturally as 'Tomlinson at the BBN computer'. Just as important, the @ sign did not appear in anyone's name and was not used for anything else in the TENEX system, so programs would never get confused.","Tomlinson's improved SNDMSG spread to other TENEX computers on the ARPANET in early 1972. Users suddenly found they could write to colleagues hundreds or thousands of kilometres away and get an answer the same day, sometimes the same minute. Tomlinson later received many honours, including a place in the Internet Hall of Fame in 2012. When he died in March 2016, people around the world remembered him every time they typed an email address, because that @ sign sits in billions of them."]},{"heading":"The Surprise Hit","paragraphs":["Here is something that should make any inventor smile. The ARPANET was not built for email. As you saw in Parts 2 to 4, its planners wanted researchers to share expensive computers: to log in to a faraway machine, to run its programs, and to move files and data around. Messages between people were not part of the main plan at all.","Yet within a couple of years, email had become the most popular thing people did on the network. The Internet Society's history of the Internet says email became the largest network application for more than a decade. People who had never cared much about logging in to a distant computer suddenly cared a great deal about reading their messages.","The tools improved quickly. In July 1972, Larry Roberts, the ARPA manager who had led the building of the ARPANET, wrote the first program for managing email. It could list your messages, let you pick which ones to read, file them away, forward them and reply. Before that, reading your mail was a bit like reading one long scroll of paper from top to bottom. Roberts's program made it feel more like a tidy in-tray.","Soon people agreed on how an email should be laid out. In September 1973, a group including Ray Tomlinson wrote RFC 561, one of the Request for Comments documents you met in Part 4. It described the top part of a message, called the header, with labelled lines such as From, Subject and Date. Look at any email today and you will still find those same labels, half a century later. For years, email was carried using the ARPANET's file transfer rules, until a dedicated set of rules called SMTP, the Simple Mail Transfer Protocol, took over in 1982. SMTP, much updated, still moves email today.","Why was email such a hit? Think about the alternatives in the 1970s. A phone call needed both people to be free at the same moment, which was hard when one lived in California and the other in Massachusetts, three time zones away. A letter could take days. A telegram was expensive and short. Email was fast like a phone call but patient like a letter: you could write when you were ready, and your friend could read when they were ready.","It also made new kinds of groups possible. People began sending the same message to many readers at once using mailing lists. One of the most famous early lists, called SF-LOVERS, was not about work at all: it was for fans of science fiction to discuss books and films. Some officials grumbled that this was not what the network was paid for, but the lesson was clear. Give people a way to talk, and they will use it to talk about everything they care about. That lesson would be learned again and again, all the way to the social media of Part 16."]},{"heading":"Showtime in Washington, 1972","paragraphs":["By 1972 the ARPANET worked, but most people had never heard of it. Many experts in the telephone business still doubted that chopping messages into packets could ever be practical. Bob Kahn, an engineer who had helped design the IMPs at BBN, decided the best way to change minds was to let people try it for themselves.","With Larry Roberts, Kahn spent about a year preparing a giant public demonstration at the International Conference on Computer Communication, known as the ICCC, held in Washington, D.C., in October 1972. It was the first time the ARPANET was shown off to the public in a big way.","Imagine walking into a hotel exhibition hall packed with terminals, cables snaking across the floor and engineers looking slightly nervous. A special BBN machine called a TIP, a Terminal Interface Processor that let lots of terminals plug straight into the network, was installed at the conference. From those terminals, visitors could log in to computers in other cities and use them as if they were in the room.","There was plenty to try. Visitors played chess against a computer, answered geography quizzes and watched an air traffic control simulation. Most famous of all, two computer programs 'talked' to each other across the network. One was ELIZA, a program that pretended to be a therapist by turning your words back into questions. The other was PARRY, a program written to imitate a very suspicious, anxious person. Watching two programs on different machines have a strange, grumpy chat with each other was a crowd favourite.","The demonstration was a big success. According to the Computer History Museum, it convinced many people that packet switching really worked. A group of visitors from AT&T, the giant American telephone company, attended too, and reportedly left puzzled. To people who had spent their careers building circuits for phone calls, the idea of a network made of tiny independent packets still seemed odd.","The ICCC meeting also brought together network researchers from many countries. At the conference they formed the International Network Working Group, a club for sharing ideas about how different networks might one day be joined. A young researcher named Vint Cerf was invited to chair it. Keep his name in mind: in the next part, he and Bob Kahn will take centre stage."]},{"heading":"Networks by Radio, by Wire and in Other Countries","paragraphs":["The ARPANET was the most famous network of the early 1970s, but it was far from the only one. In several places, inventors were trying their own ideas, and each one added something important.","Start in Hawaii. At the University of Hawaii, professor Norman Abramson and his colleague Franklin Kuo had a problem. Their university had campuses and users on several islands, separated by ocean. Telephone lines between islands were costly and not very reliable. So in 1968 they started building a network that used radio instead. They called it ALOHAnet, after the Hawaiian greeting, and it began working in June 1971.","ALOHAnet connected terminals around the islands to a central computer on the main campus on Oahu. It used two radio channels: one for terminals to send to the central computer, and one for the computer to send back to everybody. Data travelled at 9,600 bits per second. That sounds slow today, but it was quick enough for people typing.","The really clever part was how ALOHAnet shared one radio channel among many users. There was no traffic controller deciding whose turn it was. Each terminal simply sent its packet whenever it had something to say. If two terminals happened to transmit at the same moment, their signals crashed into each other, which engineers call a collision, and both packets were garbled. How would anyone know? The central computer sent back an acknowledgement, a short 'got it' message, for every packet it received correctly. If a terminal did not hear 'got it', it waited a random amount of time and tried again. Because the waits were random, the two unlucky terminals were unlikely to crash into each other a second time.","This rule, often called 'pure ALOHA', is wonderfully simple. Its weakness is that when lots of users are busy, collisions waste much of the channel. Mathematicians worked out that at best only about 18 percent of the channel's time carried useful data. A smarter version, called slotted ALOHA, made terminals start sending only at the beginning of fixed time slots, which roughly doubled that. ALOHAnet was one of the first wireless packet networks, and its ideas about sharing a channel and retrying after a random wait were about to inspire something much bigger.","Now fly east to Britain. In Part 3 you met Donald Davies at the National Physical Laboratory, or NPL, in Teddington, near London, who invented packet switching independently and named the 'packet'. Davies did not just write about it. His team, including Roger Scantlebury and Keith Bartlett, built a real packet network across the NPL site. The first version began operating in early 1969 and was fully working by January 1970. It linked computers and terminals around the laboratory, and an improved Mark II version ran from 1973 until 1986. It was a local network rather than a national one, because Davies never got the money for the countrywide network he had dreamed of, but it proved his ideas in daily use.","Next, cross the Channel to France. There, the government research institute IRIA, today called Inria, asked an engineer named Louis Pouzin to build a research network. Pouzin had worked at MIT on CTSS, the same time-sharing system where the MAIL command was born, so he knew shared computers well. His network was called CYCLADES, after a group of Greek islands, perhaps because its computers were scattered like islands that needed linking. Planning began in 1971, the first demonstration came in November 1973, and it eventually grew to about 20 nodes, with links reaching NPL in London and a European space agency centre in Rome.","CYCLADES had a bold design. It made the network itself as simple as possible, and gave the job of checking that data had arrived correctly to the computers at the ends of each conversation. Its packets were called datagrams. You will see in the next part why this idea turned out to be so important. Sadly, CYCLADES did not survive. France's national telephone authority preferred its own network, called Transpac, and government money for CYCLADES dried up. It was shut down by around 1981. Its ideas, however, lived on inside the Internet we use today."]},{"heading":"Ethernet: A Network Inside the Building","paragraphs":["Our last stop on the network tour is a hillside in Palo Alto, California, at the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center, called PARC for short. In the early 1970s, the photocopier company Xerox gave PARC an exciting mission: invent the office of the future. The computer lab there was led by Bob Taylor, the same man who had started the ARPANET project at ARPA, as you saw in Part 4.","PARC's researchers built a small personal computer called the Alto, with a screen, a keyboard and a mouse, long before most people had ever seen any of those together. They also built one of the first laser printers. But a building full of personal computers and one amazing printer raises an obvious problem: how do you connect them all?","The job went to Bob Metcalfe, a young engineer who had worked on connecting MIT to the ARPANET and had studied ALOHAnet in detail. With David Boggs, a Stanford graduate student who knew a lot about radio, he designed a network that worked like ALOHAnet, but with a cable instead of the open air.","On 22 May 1973, Metcalfe wrote a memo describing what he called the Ether Network. The name was a joke with a history. In the 1800s, scientists believed that light travelled through an invisible substance called the luminiferous aether, or ether, that filled all of space. Experiments later showed the ether did not exist. Metcalfe borrowed the name for a cable that would carry messages to every computer attached to it, the way the imaginary ether was supposed to carry light everywhere. The name stuck, shortened to Ethernet. The first Ethernet ran in November 1973, at about 2.94 megabits per second, hundreds of times faster than ALOHAnet's radio.","Ethernet improved ALOHAnet's rules in two ways. First, a computer listened before it talked: if the cable was busy, it waited. Second, it kept listening while it sent. If it heard that another computer had started at the same moment, it stopped at once, waited a random short time and tried again. Think of polite people at a dinner table. You wait for a pause before speaking, and if you and someone else both start at once, you both say 'sorry, you go', and one of you tries again a moment later.","Ethernet was what engineers call a local area network, or LAN: a fast network inside one building or campus, rather than across a country. Metcalfe and Boggs published their design in July 1976. In 1979 Metcalfe left Xerox and founded a company called 3Com to sell Ethernet equipment, and he persuaded three big companies, DEC, Intel and Xerox, to agree on a shared 10-megabit Ethernet standard, published in 1980. In 1983 the IEEE, an international engineering organisation, published its own Ethernet standard, called 802.3.","Ethernet is one of the great survivors of computing history. The plugs and cables have changed, and speeds have climbed from about 3 megabits per second to 100 gigabits per second and beyond in big data centres, but the name and many of the ideas live on. If a computer, games console or router near you has a wide, clicky network socket, it is an Ethernet port. In 2023, Metcalfe was announced as the winner of the 2022 Turing Award, often called the Nobel Prize of computing, for inventing Ethernet."]},{"heading":"Spam and Smiles","paragraphs":["Whenever a new way to communicate appears, people soon find both annoying and delightful ways to use it. Email was no exception, and two famous firsts show both sides.","First, the annoying one. On 3 May 1978, Gary Thuerk, a marketing manager at Digital Equipment Corporation, the company that made the popular PDP and DECSYSTEM computers, wanted to tell people about two upcoming presentations of a new computer, the DECSYSTEM-20, which could connect to the ARPANET. Instead of sending messages one by one, he sent a single advertisement to a long list of ARPANET users. Accounts say it went to about 400 addresses.","The reaction was 'swift and highly negative', as the Computer History Museum puts it. The ARPANET was meant for government-funded research, not for selling things, and many users were furious to find an advert in their inbox. One recipient even claimed the message had shut down his system. The people responsible for running the network sent a stern reminder that this kind of use was not allowed. Even so, some accounts claim the advert helped DEC sell a number of computers.","At the time nobody called it spam. That name came later, in the 1980s and 1990s, borrowed from a 1970 comedy sketch by the British group Monty Python, in which a cafe's menu has the canned meat Spam in almost every dish and a group of Vikings drowns out every conversation by singing 'Spam, Spam, Spam'. Unwanted messages that repeat endlessly and drown out real conversation seemed like a perfect match. Today, spam filters block enormous numbers of junk messages every day, using some of the same pattern-spotting ideas you will meet when we talk about security in Part 17.","Now for the delightful one. In September 1982, people at Carnegie Mellon University in Pittsburgh used an online bulletin board, or bboard, where anyone in the computer science department could post messages for everyone else to read. One week, a thread began with a fun physics puzzle: if an elevator's cable snapped, what would happen to a helium balloon, a pigeon or a candle inside it? Someone then posted a joke 'warning' about a mercury spill in an elevator. Some readers took it seriously, and confusion followed.","Computer scientist Scott Fahlman realised the problem. In plain text, without a smile, a wink or a tone of voice, people could not tell when someone was joking. So at 11:44 in the morning on 19 September 1982, he posted a suggestion: 'I propose that the following character sequence for joke markers: :-)'. He told readers to 'Read it sideways.' He added that, given how things were going, it might be more useful to mark posts that were not jokes, using :-(","Tilt your head to the left and you will see it: a colon for eyes, a hyphen for a nose and a bracket for a smiling mouth. The idea spread from Carnegie Mellon to other universities and on to the wider world, and the sad face :-( soon came to mean unhappy or annoyed, rather than 'serious'. For years nobody had a copy of the original post. Then, in 2002, a team searched through old backup tapes and found it, which is why we can now give its exact date and time.","Fahlman has said he believes he came up with the sideways smiley on his own, while agreeing that someone, somewhere, may have typed something similar earlier. Either way, his post started a whole new way of showing feelings in text, which led to the little picture characters called emoji that appeared on Japanese mobile phones in the late 1990s and now fill messages all over the world."]},{"heading":"Too Many Networks, Not Enough Talking","paragraphs":["Step back and look at the world of networks by the late 1970s. In the United States, the ARPANET linked dozens of research centres. ARPA was also experimenting with packet radio networks, inspired by ALOHAnet, and with networks that sent packets via satellites. In Hawaii, ALOHAnet carried data by radio. In offices, Ethernet was linking computers inside buildings. In Britain, the NPL network was running, and in France, CYCLADES. Companies and telephone authorities were building their own networks too.","Each network was a small triumph. But each one had its own rules about how packets were shaped, how big they could be, how they were addressed, how fast they moved and what to do if something went wrong. A packet from ALOHAnet made no sense to the ARPANET. An Ethernet in an office could not simply hand its messages to a satellite. It was as if every town had built a wonderful railway, each with tracks of a different width, so no train could ever leave home.","There were now many different networks, and they could not talk to each other. Somebody needed to invent a way to join networks together into one great network of networks, without forcing every network to rebuild itself. As you will see in the next part, two engineers were already working on exactly that."]}],"deepDive":"Who invented email? It depends on what you count. If 'email' means sending a message to another user's mailbox on a shared computer, then the MAIL command on MIT's CTSS (August 1965) is one of the earliest well-documented examples, and similar tools appeared on other time-sharing systems in the mid-1960s. If it means sending messages between different computers across a network using user@host addresses, then Ray Tomlinson's work at BBN in 1971 is the usual starting point. Even that date is slightly fuzzy: Tomlinson's tests happened in 1971, often said to be late in the year, while the improved SNDMSG spread to other sites in early 1972, so some histories, such as the Internet Society's, date his email software to March 1972. Email then grew in layers rather than in one leap: Larry Roberts's mail-reading program (1972), the RFC 561 header format with From, Subject and Date (1973), and SMTP (1982). That is why historians and early network engineers reject claims that email was invented by one person in 1978; by then it was already the ARPANET's most popular application. The technical story of ALOHAnet shows how mathematics shaped networking. In pure ALOHA, a packet survives only if nobody else transmits during a window twice as long as the packet itself. Modelling arrivals as random gives a best possible throughput of 1/(2e), about 18.4 percent of the channel's capacity. Slotted ALOHA halves the vulnerable window and doubles the best throughput to 1/e, about 36.8 percent. Ethernet's carrier sensing (listen before talking) and collision detection (stop as soon as a clash is heard), known together as CSMA/CD, did much better on a short cable, because collisions were spotted within microseconds. A further trick, binary exponential backoff, made computers wait for a random time drawn from a range that doubles after each failed try, so a busy network calms itself down automatically. Modern switched Ethernet gives each device its own link to a switch, so collisions have largely disappeared, but Wi-Fi still uses close relatives of these listen-and-retry ideas. Finally, the CYCLADES story is a reminder that technical success does not guarantee survival. It was shut down for budget and political reasons, yet its idea of simple datagrams with reliability handled by the end computers became central to the Internet.","funFact":"Scott Fahlman's original :-) post was lost for about 20 years until a team recovered it from old Carnegie Mellon backup tapes in 2002, confirming it was posted at 11:44 am on 19 September 1982.","check":{"q":"Why did Ray Tomlinson choose the @ sign for email addresses?","a":"It meant 'at' and did not appear in people's names","wrong":["It was the only symbol on his keyboard","It stood for 'ARPANET'","It looked like a little envelope"]},"sources":[{"title":"The History of Electronic Mail","publisher":"Tom Van Vleck, Multicians.org","url":"https://multicians.org/thvv/mail-history.html"},{"title":"Brief History of the Internet","publisher":"Internet Society","url":"https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/"},{"title":"Internet History of 1970s","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/internethistory/1970s/"},{"title":"Raymond Tomlinson","publisher":"Internet Hall of Fame","url":"https://www.internethalloffame.org/inductee/raymond-tomlinson/"},{"title":"Smiley Lore :-)","publisher":"Scott Fahlman, Carnegie Mellon University","url":"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~sef/sefSmiley.htm"},{"title":"Original Bboard Thread in which :-) was proposed","publisher":"Carnegie Mellon University","url":"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~sef/Orig-Smiley.htm"},{"title":"Ethernet links personal computers","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/revolution/networking/19/381"},{"title":"What Happened on May 3rd","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/tdih/may/3/"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/05-1.jpg","alt":"Ray Tomlinson, the BBN engineer who sent the first network email and picked the @ sign.","file":"Ray Tomlinson.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ray_Tomlinson.jpg","author":"Andreu Veà, WiWiW.org","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/05-2.jpg","alt":"A 1973 Xerox Alto with its upright screen, keyboard, mouse and chord keyset, at the Computer History Museum.","file":"Xerox Alto I, 1973, Computer History Museum.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xerox_Alto_I,_1973,_Computer_History_Museum.jpg","author":"The wub","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/05-3.jpg","alt":"Bob Metcalfe's 22 May 1973 memo naming \"The Ether Network\", with his hand-drawn sketches of the cable.","file":"Metcalfe Memo Establishing Basic Principles of Ethernet (Cover + 1st page).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metcalfe_Memo_Establishing_Basic_Principles_of_Ethernet_(Cover_%2B_1st_page).jpg","author":"Robert Metcalfe","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4349},{"n":6,"title":"A Network of Networks: TCP/IP","when":"1973 – 1983","summary":"By the 1970s there were lots of networks, but they all spoke different languages. Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn invented TCP/IP, a shared set of rules that let any network join any other. On 1 January 1983 the ARPANET switched to these rules, and the Internet as we know it was born.","sections":[{"heading":"Islands That Could Not Talk","paragraphs":["At the end of Part 5 we left the world of networks in a muddle. The ARPANET linked research centres across the United States. ALOHAnet carried data by radio around Hawaii. Ethernet joined computers inside an office. Britain had the NPL network, France had CYCLADES, and telephone companies were building networks of their own. Each was clever. None of them could talk to the others.","In 1972, Bob Kahn left BBN, where he had helped design the IMPs, and joined ARPA, which was soon renamed DARPA, with a D for Defense. One of the first projects he took on was a packet radio network: a way for computers in moving vehicles, or carried by people, to send packets by radio, a bit like ALOHAnet but able to hop from one radio station to another over a wider area. DARPA was also starting a packet satellite network, called SATNET. It grew out of satellite links set up in 1973 that connected the ARPANET with research sites in Norway and Britain, bouncing signals off a communications satellite far above the Atlantic.","So Kahn had a very practical problem. A radio network, a satellite network and the ARPANET were completely different beasts. Radio signals could fade when a truck drove behind a hill, or be blocked by interference. Satellite signals took about a quarter of a second to go up to the satellite and back down. The ARPANET used telephone lines and was fairly reliable. Packets had different maximum sizes on each network, and each network had its own way of addressing computers.","Kahn did not want to force everyone to rebuild their networks to look like the ARPANET. That would have been slow, expensive and probably impossible. Instead he had a more daring idea, which he called open-architecture networking. Each network could be designed however its builders liked, for whatever job it was good at. Then a single, shared set of rules would sit on top, letting packets pass from one network to the next. The networks would stay different, but they would be joined.","The Internet Society's history describes four ground rules in Kahn's plan. First, each network would stand on its own, and no network should have to change inside in order to connect. Second, delivery would be 'best effort': the networks would try their best to deliver each packet, and if one got lost, the computer that sent it would send it again. Third, networks would be connected by special boxes called gateways, which would simply pass packets along without keeping track of each conversation. Fourth, there would be no single control centre running the whole thing. Those four rules still describe the Internet remarkably well today."]},{"heading":"Cerf and Kahn Team Up","paragraphs":["Kahn needed a partner who understood exactly how computers talked to the network. In the spring of 1973 he went to see Vint Cerf, a young assistant professor at Stanford University in California.","Cerf was born in 1943 in New Haven, Connecticut. He had been hard of hearing since childhood and has worn hearing aids for most of his life, and he has said that text messages like email suited him well. As a graduate student at UCLA in the late 1960s, he worked in Leonard Kleinrock's lab, the place where the first ARPANET IMP was installed and the famous 'LO' message was sent, as you saw in Part 4. There he worked with Steve Crocker and others on the ARPANET's first host-to-host rules, called NCP, the Network Control Program. Kahn and Cerf had first met around that time, when Kahn came to UCLA to test how the new network behaved under heavy traffic.","So the pair fitted together nicely. Kahn knew networks from the inside: the IMPs, the radio links and the satellites. Cerf knew how ordinary computers and their operating systems could be connected to a network. Cerf was also chairing the International Network Working Group, the club of network researchers formed at the 1972 ICCC conference, so he was in touch with experts from Britain, France and elsewhere.","Through the summer of 1973 they worked out the main ideas. In September 1973, at a meeting of the International Network Working Group at the University of Sussex in England, they handed round their first written version. After more polishing, their paper 'A Protocol for Packet Network Intercommunication' was published in May 1974 in IEEE Transactions on Communications, a respected engineering journal. It is one of the most important technical papers of the 20th century.","The paper described a program they called TCP, the Transmission Control Program. Its job was enormous. It had to take a stream of data from one computer, chop it into packets, put each packet into a standard 'envelope' with addresses that every network could understand, send it across as many different networks as needed, and on the far side check that everything had arrived, put the pieces back in the right order and ask for any missing ones to be sent again.","Cerf and Kahn were careful to give credit to others. Their paper thanked people whose ideas had shaped it, including Donald Davies and Roger Scantlebury of Britain's National Physical Laboratory, the team you met in Parts 3 and 5. Cerf has often said that the Internet was built by thousands of people, and that he and Kahn simply wrote down an early design. That generous view is also the historically accurate one."]},{"heading":"Gateways: the Border Crossings","paragraphs":["To join networks, you need something at the place where they meet. Cerf and Kahn called these meeting points gateways. Today we call them routers, and there are many millions of them, from huge machines in data centres to the little box with blinking lights that probably sits in your home.","A gateway is a computer that is connected to two or more networks at once. When a packet arrives from one network, the gateway reads the Internet address on its envelope, works out which neighbouring network will bring it closer to its destination, and sends it on. If the next network can only carry smaller packets, the gateway can cut the packet into smaller pieces, which are put back together later.","Think about a parcel that has to travel from a village in Scotland to a village in Japan. It might go by a local van, then a train, then a plane, then a Japanese delivery truck. Each carrier works in its own way, with its own vehicles and timetables. What makes the whole trip possible is that every carrier respects the same address label and hands the parcel on at the right place. Gateways are the sorting depots where the parcel moves from one carrier to the next.","A very important choice was that gateways should be simple and forgetful. In Kahn's plan, a gateway does not remember which conversation a packet belongs to. It just looks at the address on each packet, sends it the right way and forgets about it. This made gateways cheap, fast and easy to build. It also meant that if one gateway broke, packets could often be sent around it by another path, because no important information was stored inside it. The full story of how a packet finds its way from router to router is the subject of Part 7."]},{"heading":"Smart Edges, Simple Middle: the French Connection","paragraphs":["If the gateways in the middle are simple and forgetful, who makes sure your message arrives complete and in the right order? The answer is one of the deepest ideas in the Internet's design: the computers at the two ends of the conversation do it.","This is often called the end-to-end idea. The network in the middle only promises to try its best to deliver each packet. It might lose one now and then, deliver two in the wrong order or occasionally deliver a duplicate. The sending and receiving computers, at the edges of the network, number the packets, check them, put them in order and ask for any missing ones to be sent again.","Imagine sending a long story to a pen-friend as a pile of numbered postcards, the analogy you met in Part 3. The postal service does not promise that every card will arrive on time or in order. But you and your friend have a system. You number every card. Your friend lays them out in order, notices that card 17 is missing, and writes back: 'Please send 17 again.' The post office stays simple; the clever checking happens at the two ends.","This idea owes a great deal to Louis Pouzin and his CYCLADES network in France, which you met in Part 5. CYCLADES was the first network to make the host computers, rather than the network itself, responsible for reliable delivery. Its packets were called datagrams: each one was a self-contained little parcel with its full destination address, travelling on its own, with no fixed path set up in advance. The network just did its best with each datagram, and the computers at the ends took care of the rest.","The ARPANET worked differently. Its IMPs did a lot of careful work inside the network to make sure messages arrived in order, so the computers attached to it could assume the network was reliable. That was convenient, but it meant the ARPANET's host rules, NCP, could not cope with packets crossing other, less reliable networks such as packet radio. Cerf and Kahn needed something that would work over any network, even a crackly radio link, and the datagram approach fitted perfectly.","The connection between France and California was personal as well as technical. Gérard Le Lann, a researcher from the CYCLADES team, spent time working in Cerf's group at Stanford and helped bring CYCLADES ideas into the early design of TCP. Hubert Zimmermann, another member of the French team, later drew on the same experience in shaping a famous international model of network layers. Ideas crossed the Atlantic as easily as the packets would soon do.","Why is this approach so powerful? Because a simple network is easy to extend. Anyone can build a new kind of network, whether radio, cable, fibre or something not yet invented, and connect it, as long as it can carry datagrams. And because the clever parts live in the end computers, people can invent new uses for the Internet, such as the Web, video calls or online games, without asking the people who run the network for permission or waiting for them to rebuild it."]},{"heading":"One Program Becomes Two: TCP and IP","paragraphs":["In Cerf and Kahn's 1974 design, one program, TCP, did everything: addressing, forwarding, ordering, checking and resending. During the mid-1970s, teams at Stanford, at BBN and at University College London in Britain, all funded by DARPA, wrote working versions of TCP and tested them against each other. As they did, they found that one big program doing every job was not always the best design.","One surprise came from experiments with sending voice over packet networks. For a phone-style conversation, it is better to lose a tiny scrap of sound than to wait for it to be resent. If a missing piece arrives a whole second later, the conversation has already moved on. But TCP insisted on resending every missing packet and keeping everything perfectly in order, which made live voice stutter.","Jon Postel, a researcher at the University of Southern California's Information Sciences Institute and the editor of the RFC documents for many years, argued strongly that the design should be split into layers. In a 1977 note, he wrote, 'We are screwing up in our design of Internet protocols by violating the principle of layering.' His point was that each layer should do one job well and leave other jobs to other layers.","Around 1978, the single program was split into two partners. IP, the Internet Protocol, got the simple job: put an address on each packet and pass it from network to network, doing its best but promising nothing. TCP, now called the Transmission Control Protocol, got the careful job: run on the two end computers, number the packets, put them back in order, check for missing ones and ask for them again. A third, much simpler partner, UDP, was added for programs like live voice that wanted to use IP directly and handle losses in their own way.","This is exactly where the name TCP/IP comes from: two protocols working as a team. IP is like the postal system that moves envelopes; TCP is like the careful friend who numbers the postcards and checks that the whole story has arrived. Gateways in the middle only need to understand IP. Only the computers at the edges need to run TCP.","The new version of IP, called IP version 4 or IPv4, was written in 1978, and the finished descriptions of IPv4 and TCP were published as RFCs in 1981. IPv4 gave every computer a 32-bit address. Early on, the designers split that address into 8 bits for the network and 24 bits for the computer on that network, which meant there could be only 256 networks. At the time that seemed like plenty, since only a handful of networks existed. Once Ethernet put a little network in almost every university department, it clearly was not, and the addressing scheme had to be changed. You will find out how addresses work, and why they nearly ran out, in Part 8."]},{"heading":"The Van That Drove Into History","paragraphs":["Ideas on paper are one thing. To prove that TCP really could join different networks, the engineers needed tests, and some of the most memorable involved a large grey-silver step van owned by SRI, the research institute in Menlo Park, California, that had received the first ARPANET message in 1969.","SRI ran DARPA's packet radio experiments, and the van, fitted out by SRI engineer Don Cone, was a network node on wheels. Inside were radios, computer terminals and other equipment; on top was an oddly shaped antenna. As it drove around the San Francisco Bay Area, its radio connected it to packet radio stations on hills and buildings, and from there to a gateway at SRI that linked it to the ARPANET.","Tests across two networks came first, from 1975. One summer day, on 27 August 1976, the team parked the van beside Rossotti's, a popular old roadhouse in Portola Valley, near Stanford, today called the Alpine Inn. Sitting at a picnic table in the beer garden, they typed their regular weekly report on a terminal and sent it through the packet radio network and the ARPANET. It is a lovely thought: one of the early Internet transmissions was sent from a picnic bench outside a bar, by people who just wanted to file their homework.","The big test came on a rainy day, 22 November 1977. This time the goal was to join three completely different networks: packet radio, the ARPANET and SATNET, the satellite network. The van drove along the roads south of San Francisco, sending data by radio. From SRI's gateway the packets entered the ARPANET, which carried them right across the United States and then, over its own long-distance link, across the Atlantic to Europe and on to University College London. There, another gateway sent them into the satellite network, which bounced them back across the Atlantic to a ground station on the US East Coast. Back on the ARPANET, they crossed America once more to arrive at USC's Information Sciences Institute in Los Angeles.","Think about that route. The van and its destination were only a few hundred kilometres apart, both in California, but the packets went the long way round, across the continent, over the ocean, up to a satellite and back, on purpose. The point was to show that TCP could cope with very different networks along one path: a fading radio link, long-distance phone lines and a satellite hop with a long delay. The total journey is often described as about 150,000 kilometres, or about 94,000 miles, and the data came through intact.","More than 35 people from about eight organisations worked on the test, including engineers from SRI, BBN, Collins Radio, the satellite company Linkabit, University College London, where Peter Kirstein led the British side, and NDRE, the Norwegian Defence Research Establishment. The BBN team included Ray Tomlinson, the email pioneer from Part 5, who worked on TCP; Virginia Strazisar, who built the gateways; and Radia Perlman, whom you will meet again in Part 9. Many historians see this three-network test as the moment the Internet, as a working idea, came to life. The SRI van itself is now part of the collection of the Computer History Museum in Mountain View, California, which describes it as 'the van where the Internet was born'."]},{"heading":"Flag Day: 1 January 1983","paragraphs":["By the early 1980s, TCP/IP worked well in experiments. But the ARPANET itself, with its hundreds of computers, still ran on the old NCP rules from 1970. Changing a working network is a bit like changing the engines on a plane while it is flying, and many computer owners were in no hurry to do the work.","The US Department of Defense, which paid for the ARPANET, helped make up their minds. It adopted TCP/IP as a defence standard in 1980, and confirmed in March 1982 that TCP/IP would be its standard for military networking. In 1981, an RFC written by Jon Postel, RFC 801, set out the plan: on 1 January 1983, the ARPANET would stop supporting NCP. After that date, any computer that had not switched to TCP/IP would no longer be able to talk to the others.","Engineers call a change like this a 'flag day': a single date when everyone must switch over together, because the old and new systems cannot work side by side for ever. Through 1982, teams at universities, companies and military sites worked hard to rewrite and test their software. Some were ready early; others rushed to finish at the last minute.","When 1 January 1983 arrived, the switch happened. It had been planned for years, and the Internet Society's history says it went smoothly, although some sites needed extra time to get everything fully working. The people who had sweated through the change were proud enough to wear buttons that read 'I survived the TCP/IP transition.'","Because this was the day the ARPANET moved onto the rules that still run the Internet, 1 January 1983 is often called the birthday of the Internet. Like most birthdays in this story, it is a useful symbol rather than a single moment of invention. The ideas were from 1973 and 1974, the first three-network test was in 1977, and the network would keep growing and changing for decades afterwards.","Two more events in 1983 helped TCP/IP spread. First, the military part of the ARPANET was split off into its own network, called MILNET, for operational defence use, while the ARPANET continued as a research network. The two stayed connected using TCP/IP, which neatly showed the point of the whole design. Second, the University of California, Berkeley, with DARPA funding, included TCP/IP in its popular version of the Unix operating system, released in 1983. Universities everywhere used Berkeley Unix, so TCP/IP suddenly arrived, free, on a huge number of computers."]},{"heading":"Why We Call It the Internet","paragraphs":["Where does the word Internet come from? In the early 1970s, researchers talked about 'internetworking', meaning connecting networks together, and an 'internetwork' was a network made of networks. In December 1974, Vint Cerf, together with Yogen Dalal and Carl Sunshine, wrote RFC 675, the first detailed specification of TCP. In it they used the short form 'internet' for this network of networks. Over time, the one big, worldwide internetwork that used TCP/IP became known as the Internet, often written with a capital I to show that it is the name of one particular thing, like the Moon.","Here is a useful way to remember it. The prefix inter means between, as in international, between nations. The Internet is what you get between networks: all of them, joined by shared rules. Your home Wi-Fi is a network. Your school has a network. Your mobile phone company has a network. The Internet is the agreement that lets all of them pass packets to each other.","Cerf and Kahn are often called 'fathers of the Internet', and they have received many honours. They shared the Turing Award for 2004, often called the Nobel Prize of computing, and in 2005 President George W. Bush gave them the Presidential Medal of Freedom, the highest award for civilians in the United States. Kahn went on to found an organisation in Virginia that researches information networks, and Cerf later joined Google as its 'Chief Internet Evangelist' and worked on ideas for an Internet stretching into space, which you will read about in Part 20.","Yet both men are quick to point out that they did not do it alone. Baran, Davies and Kleinrock had shown how packet switching could work. The BBN team had built the IMPs. Pouzin and the CYCLADES team showed the power of datagrams and smart edges. Postel pushed for clean layers. Dozens of programmers at Stanford, BBN, University College London, SRI, USC, Norway's NDRE and many other places wrote and tested the code. The Internet was not invented by one person in one moment. It was assembled by a crowd of curious people who agreed to share open rules.","Those rules are called protocols, and TCP/IP is the most famous of them all. But rules on paper can feel abstract. What actually happens when you press Send? To find out, let's shrink ourselves down to the size of a single packet and ride along on its journey, from your screen, through the routers, to a computer on the other side of the world. That is where Part 7 begins."]}],"deepDive":"The design of TCP/IP was shaped by real engineering trade-offs, and historians still discuss how ideas moved between teams. One debate is about credit. Cerf and Kahn's May 1974 paper laid out the architecture, but the INWG meetings, the CYCLADES datagram network and the NPL work all fed into it, and the first full TCP specification (RFC 675, December 1974) had three authors. Historians generally credit Cerf and Kahn with the internetworking design while emphasising that it was refined by a large community. A second theme is the end-to-end argument. In a famous 1984 paper, Jerome Saltzer, David Reed and David Clark of MIT argued that functions such as reliable delivery can only be done completely by the end systems, so building them into the network core often adds cost without removing the need for end checks. This principle explains why IP offers only best-effort delivery, why routers can be relatively simple, and why new applications could be deployed without changing the network. Critics note that the modern Internet has drifted from the pure ideal: firewalls, address translation boxes and content filters now sit in the middle and inspect traffic. A third theme is the so-called Protocol Wars of the 1980s and early 1990s. Many governments and telephone companies backed the international OSI standards and X.25 virtual-circuit networks, which put more intelligence inside the network. TCP/IP won largely because it already existed in working, freely available code, especially in Berkeley Unix, while OSI was still being finalised. The early address design also shows the limits of foresight: 32-bit IPv4 addresses gave about 4.3 billion possible addresses, but the first split allowed only 256 networks, forcing later changes such as address classes (1981) and classless addressing (1993). Finally, the demonstration numbers deserve care. Accounts of the 22 November 1977 test describe a round trip of about 94,000 miles (about 150,000 km), largely because the satellite hop alone requires signals to climb to geostationary orbit about 36,000 km above the equator and come back down. Precise figures vary between retellings, so treat them as approximate.","funFact":"On 27 August 1976, one of the first transmissions linking the packet radio network and the ARPANET was sent from a picnic table in the beer garden of Rossotti's, a roadhouse near Stanford now called the Alpine Inn.","check":{"q":"On what date did the ARPANET switch from its old NCP rules to TCP/IP?","a":"1 January 1983","wrong":["29 October 1969","22 November 1977","6 August 1991"]},"sources":[{"title":"Brief History of the Internet","publisher":"Internet Society","url":"https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/"},{"title":"Internet History of 1970s","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/internethistory/1970s/"},{"title":"Internet History of 1980s","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/internethistory/1980s/"},{"title":"Born in a Van: Happy 40th Birthday to the Internet!","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://computerhistory.org/blog/born-in-a-van-happy-40th-birthday-to-the-internet/"},{"title":"SRI van model","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/revolution/networking/19/375/2167"},{"title":"Louis Pouzin","publisher":"Internet Hall of Fame","url":"https://www.internethalloffame.org/inductee/louis-pouzin/"},{"title":"Internet protocol suite","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_protocol_suite"},{"title":"CYCLADES","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CYCLADES"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/06-1.jpg","alt":"Vint Cerf (left) and Bob Kahn (middle), co-designers of TCP/IP, receive the Presidential Medal of Freedom in 2005.","file":"CerfKahnMedalOfFreedom.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CerfKahnMedalOfFreedom.jpg","author":"Paul Morse","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/06-2.jpg","alt":"SRI's packet radio van, the \"Internet van\" used in the first three-network Internet demonstration in 1977.","file":"SRI Packet Radio Van.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SRI_Packet_Radio_Van.jpg","author":"SRI International","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/06-3.jpg","alt":"The Alpine Inn (formerly Rossotti's) in Portola Valley, California, where the van sent messages in 1977.","file":"Casa de Tableta, Portola Valley, California.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Casa_de_Tableta,_Portola_Valley,_California.jpg","author":"W Nowicki","license":"CC BY 3.0"}],"words":3952},{"n":7,"title":"The Journey of a Packet","when":"How it works (part 1)","summary":"When you send a photo, it is chopped into thousands of tiny numbered pieces called packets. Each one races through cables and routers, maybe by a different road, and the computer at the other end clicks them back together like a jigsaw. If a piece goes missing, it simply asks for it again!","sections":[{"heading":"One Tap, Two Thousand Travellers","paragraphs":["Imagine you have just taken a funny photo of your dog wearing sunglasses, and you want to send it to your cousin who lives on the other side of an ocean. You tap Send. Less than a second later, your cousin's phone buzzes. It feels like magic, as if the picture simply jumped from one screen to the other.","It did not jump. In that blink of an eye, your photo was cut into roughly two thousand small pieces. Each piece was wrapped in a label, handed to your Wi-Fi router, pushed into a copper wire or a glass fibre, passed from one machine to the next across a country, shot under the sea as flashes of light, and handed along again until it reached your cousin's phone. There, every piece was checked, counted and snapped back into place in exactly the right order. If even one piece got lost on the way, your cousin's phone noticed and asked for it again.","In Part 6 we met Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn and their rules called TCP/IP, which let many different networks join into one Internet. Rules on paper are one thing, though. In this part we are going to follow those rules in action. We will shrink ourselves down to the size of a single packet and ride along on its journey, the way you might follow one letter through a giant postal system.","Along the way you will learn what data really is, what a packet looks like inside, what routers do all day, why packets sometimes arrive in a jumble, and how TCP, the careful bookkeeper of the Internet, makes sure nothing is missing. By the end, you will be able to read the kind of list network engineers use to watch a packet hop across the world, with times measured in thousandths of a second.","One important idea before we set off: nobody steers your packet from start to finish. There is no central control room watching it. Each machine along the way makes one small decision, passes the packet on, and forgets about it. The whole amazing journey is built out of thousands of these tiny, local decisions. That is exactly what the packet-switching pioneers of Part 3 had in mind."]},{"heading":"Everything Is Ones and Zeros","paragraphs":["Before we can understand packets, we need to understand what is inside them. Computers do not store pictures, songs or words the way we see and hear them. They store everything as numbers, and those numbers are written using only two digits: 1 and 0. Each single 1 or 0 is called a bit, short for 'binary digit'. Eight bits grouped together make a byte.","Why only two digits? Because two states are easy to make and hard to confuse. A wire can have a voltage or not. A tiny spot on a chip can hold an electric charge or not. A laser in a fibre-optic cable can flash or stay dark. A radio wave can shift one way or another. Telling 'on' from 'off' is much more reliable than telling apart ten different levels, especially when a signal has travelled thousands of kilometres and become faint and noisy.","So how does a letter become bits? Computers use agreed codes. In the common ASCII code, which dates back to the 1960s, the capital letter A is the number 65. Written in binary, 65 is 01000001, which fits neatly into one byte. Lower-case a is 97, and the digit 7 is 55. Modern text uses a bigger code called Unicode, which can also store Chinese characters, Arabic letters and even emoji, sometimes using two, three or four bytes for one symbol.","Pictures are made of pixels, tiny coloured squares. In a typical colour image, each pixel is stored as three numbers: how much red, how much green and how much blue it contains, each from 0 to 255. That is three bytes per pixel. A 12-megapixel phone photo has about 12 million pixels, which would be about 36 million bytes if stored plainly. Clever compression, like the JPEG format, squeezes this down, often to just a few million bytes, by throwing away details your eyes would hardly notice.","Sound works in a similar way. A microphone turns air vibrations into a wobbling electrical signal, and the computer measures that signal thousands of times every second. Music on a CD, for example, was measured 44,100 times per second for each of the two stereo channels. Every measurement becomes a number, and every number becomes bits.","This is the secret that makes the Internet possible. Because text, photos, voices, videos and game moves all become the same thing, long strings of 1s and 0s, one single network can carry all of them. The network does not need to know whether it is carrying a birthday message or a dinosaur documentary. It just carries bits."]},{"heading":"Inside a Packet: Label and Load","paragraphs":["Now imagine your dog photo as a long ribbon of about three million bytes. The Internet will not send that whole ribbon in one go. Instead, it chops it into packets. On Ethernet cables and most home networks, the largest packet allowed is about 1,500 bytes. Engineers call this limit the MTU, short for maximum transmission unit. So a 3-megabyte photo needs roughly two thousand packets.","Every packet has two main parts. The first part is the header, which is like the address label and stamp on an envelope. The second part is the payload, which is the actual slice of data being carried, a small strip of your photo. A good way to picture it is a long letter cut up into numbered postcards. Each postcard carries a few lines of the letter on one side and the address and its number on the other.","What is written in the header? For the classic version of the Internet Protocol, IPv4, described in a document called RFC 791 in September 1981, the header is usually 20 bytes long. It contains the address of the computer that sent the packet and the address of the computer it is going to. Each address is 32 bits long. It also says which version of IP is being used, how long the whole packet is, and which kind of message is inside, for example a TCP message.","One clever field is called time to live, or TTL. It is a small number, up to 255, and every router that handles the packet lowers it by one. If it ever reaches zero, the packet is thrown away. Why build in a self-destruct counter? Because if something goes wrong and routers accidentally pass a packet round in a circle, it would otherwise go round forever, clogging the network. With TTL, a lost packet eventually just fades away.","There is also a checksum, a number calculated from the header's contents. Each router recalculates it. If the numbers do not match, the header has been damaged on the way, perhaps by electrical noise, and the packet is discarded rather than being sent somewhere wrong.","Inside the IP packet sits a second header, the TCP header, which is usually another 20 bytes. It holds a sequence number, which tells the receiver where this slice belongs in the whole message, and an acknowledgement number, used to tell the other side what has arrived safely. So of the 1,500 bytes in a full packet, about 40 bytes are labels and about 1,460 are your photo. Only around 3 percent of the space goes on paperwork, which is a pretty good deal."]},{"heading":"Envelopes Inside Envelopes","paragraphs":["Engineers have a favourite trick for keeping a complicated system understandable: they split it into layers. Each layer has one job and only needs to talk to the layers just above and below it. The Internet is usually described with four or five layers, and the easiest way to imagine them is as envelopes tucked inside other envelopes.","At the top is the application layer. This is the program you are using, such as a messaging app, a web browser or a game. It decides what to say, for example 'here is a photo for my cousin'. Web browsers use a set of rules called HTTP at this layer, which you will meet properly in later parts of this story.","Next comes the transport layer, where TCP lives. TCP takes the app's data, cuts it into pieces of the right size, and puts each piece into its own envelope with a sequence number. It also adds a port number, a bit like a flat number in a big apartment building, so that the right program on the receiving computer gets the data. Your phone might be running a video, a chat app and a weather widget all at once, and port numbers keep their packets from getting mixed up.","Then comes the Internet layer, where IP lives. IP puts the TCP envelope inside a bigger envelope marked with the source and destination IP addresses. This is the envelope that routers read when deciding where to send things.","Below that is the link layer, which handles one single step of the journey: from your laptop to your Wi-Fi router, say, or from one router to the next along a fibre. Wi-Fi and Ethernet each wrap the IP packet in their own outer envelope, with local hardware addresses that only matter for that one hop. Finally, at the very bottom, is the physical layer: the actual radio waves, electrical pulses or flashes of light that carry the bits.","Here is the beautiful part. When a router receives a packet, it opens only the outer, link-layer envelope. It reads the IP envelope's destination, decides the next step, and then wraps the packet in a fresh outer envelope for the next link. It does not need to open the TCP envelope, and it certainly does not look at your photo. The packet might travel by Wi-Fi, then copper, then fibre under the sea, then radio again, changing its outer envelope at every step, while the inner envelopes ride along unchanged.","Layers also mean that inventors can improve one layer without breaking the others. When Wi-Fi got faster, nobody had to rewrite email. When new apps were invented, nobody had to rebuild the cables. Engineers sometimes draw this as an hourglass: lots of different apps at the top, lots of different cables and radios at the bottom, and one slim waist in the middle where everything passes through IP. That narrow, shared middle is a big reason the Internet has been able to grow and change for decades."]},{"heading":"Routers: The Sorting Offices of the Internet","paragraphs":["Picture a giant postal sorting office at night. Letters pour in on conveyor belts. A worker glances at each address, does not read the letter, and drops it into the bin for the right next van: this one to the north, this one to the airport, this one to the town down the road. The worker does not know the whole route the letter will take. They only know which van gets it one step closer.","A router does exactly this, but with packets, and unimaginably fast. A router is a specialised computer with several connections, called interfaces. When a packet arrives on one interface, the router reads the destination IP address in its header, looks it up in a list called a routing table, and sends the packet out of whichever interface leads one step closer. Each step from one router to the next is called a hop.","A routing table does not list every computer in the world. That would be impossible. Instead, it lists groups of addresses that share the same beginning, a bit like a sorting office knowing that every postcode starting with a certain letter goes to the same region. Most home routers have a very short table that basically says: 'If it is for a device in this house, send it here; for everything else, send it to the Internet provider.' That catch-all instruction is called the default route.","Big routers deep inside the Internet, owned by phone companies and network providers, have enormous tables with hundreds of thousands of entries. These machines can sit in tall metal racks in buildings full of humming equipment, and the largest of them can forward huge numbers of packets every second. To keep their tables up to date, routers constantly chat with their neighbours using routing protocols, sharing news such as 'I can reach these addresses' or 'that link has just broken'. If a cable is cut, routers learn about it and start sending packets around the damaged area, often within seconds or minutes.","How many hops does a packet usually take? It depends on where it is going, but a typical trip across the Internet often passes through somewhere around 10 to 20 routers. A message to a website in your own city might need fewer; one going to the other side of the planet might need more.","Routers do not have unlimited room. Each one keeps a small waiting area for packets, called a buffer or queue, like a line at a ticket window. When too many packets arrive at once, the queue grows, and packets wait a little longer. If the queue completely fills up, the router has no choice but to drop new packets on the floor. This sounds alarming, but the Internet was designed expecting it. IP itself makes no promises. It does its best to deliver each packet, but if one is lost, it is someone else's job to notice. That someone is TCP."]},{"heading":"Different Roads, Jumbled Arrivals","paragraphs":["Because every router makes its own decision for each packet, two packets from the same photo do not have to travel the same way. Most of the time they do follow the same path, because routers tend to keep sending traffic for one destination down the same route. But routes can change in the middle of a conversation. A link may get busy, a cable may be repaired and switched back on, or a router may spread traffic over two equally good paths to share the load.","This means packets can arrive out of order. Packet number 5 might arrive before packet number 4, just as a postcard sent on Tuesday sometimes beats one sent on Monday. Some might be delayed by a long queue. One might be dropped by a busy router. Very occasionally, one might even be delivered twice.","To a telephone engineer of the 1960s, this would have sounded like chaos. In a phone call, as we saw in Part 1, a whole circuit is reserved for you, and your voice flows along it in perfect order. But that reserved line sits idle whenever you pause for breath. Packet switching gives up that perfect, private road in exchange for something much more efficient: many conversations sharing the same lines at the same time, with each line kept busy almost all the time.","Louis Pouzin's idea of the datagram, which we met in Part 6, is at work here. Each IP packet is a datagram: a self-contained message that carries its full destination address and can find its own way, without the network remembering anything about the conversation it belongs to. The network in the middle stays simple and fast. All the hard work of making things neat and complete is pushed out to the two computers at the edges. That is the 'end-to-end' principle, and it is one of the Internet's deepest design ideas.","So the question becomes: how do the computers at the edges turn a messy shower of packets, some late, some missing, some swapped around, back into a perfect photo of a dog in sunglasses?"]},{"heading":"TCP: The Careful Bookkeeper","paragraphs":["TCP, the Transmission Control Protocol, is the part of the Internet that takes unreliable packet delivery and turns it into a reliable stream of data. Its rules were written down in RFC 793, published in September 1981, and they have been improved many times since. You can think of TCP as a very patient bookkeeper sitting at each end of the conversation.","First, TCP says hello. Before any data is sent, the two computers perform a three-way handshake. Your phone sends a packet marked SYN, short for synchronise, which means roughly 'I would like to talk, and I will start counting from this number.' The other computer replies with SYN-ACK: 'Got it, and here is my starting number too.' Your phone answers ACK: 'Great, got yours.' Now both sides know the other is listening and have agreed on how to count.","Second, TCP numbers everything. Its sequence numbers actually count bytes, not packets, so each packet's header says something like 'this piece starts at byte 14,601 of the message.' When pieces arrive out of order, the receiver puts them in their correct place, like slotting numbered jigsaw pieces into the right gaps. If a piece arrives twice, it simply ignores the copy.","Third, TCP says thank you. The receiver regularly sends back acknowledgements, often shortened to ACKs. An acknowledgement says, 'I have everything up to byte such-and-such, and I am now waiting for the next one.' The sender keeps a copy of every piece it has sent until it is acknowledged.","Fourth, TCP tries again. When the sender sends a packet, it starts a timer. If no acknowledgement arrives before the timer runs out, TCP assumes the packet was lost and sends it again. TCP also learns how long round trips usually take, so it does not wait too long or give up too quickly. Modern TCP has extra tricks too: if the receiver keeps acknowledging the same point again and again, the sender can guess that one piece is missing and resend it straight away, without waiting for the timer.","Fifth, TCP checks for damage. Every TCP piece carries a checksum covering its data. If a single bit has been flipped by noise, the numbers will not add up, and the receiver throws the piece away so it will be sent again.","Finally, TCP is polite. Each acknowledgement tells the sender how much more room the receiver has, so a fast computer does not drown a slow one. TCP also watches for signs of traffic jams. In October 1986, the early Internet suffered what engineers called a congestion collapse: on one link between Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory and the University of California, Berkeley, useful data slowed from about 32,000 bits per second to about 40, because computers kept resending lost packets into an already overloaded network. The researcher Van Jacobson and his colleague Michael Karels worked out rules, published in 1988, that make TCP slow down when packets are being lost and speed up gently when things are clear. Those ideas, refined over the years, still help keep the Internet from jamming today.","Not every app wants all this care. For a live video call or a fast online game, a packet that arrives late is useless anyway, because the moment has already passed. Those apps often use a simpler partner protocol called UDP, which sends packets with no handshake, no numbering checks and no resending. A tiny glitch in a video call is better than the whole call freezing while TCP waits for one missing piece."]},{"heading":"Watching a Packet Cross the Ocean","paragraphs":["Can we actually see a packet's journey? Almost. In 1987, the network researcher Van Jacobson wrote a small program called traceroute, building on a suggestion from Steve Deering. It is still used by network engineers today, and many computers have a version of it built in (on Windows it is called tracert).","Traceroute uses a sneaky trick with the time-to-live counter. It sends a packet with a TTL of just 1. The very first router lowers it to zero, throws the packet away, and politely sends back a short error message saying 'time exceeded', which reveals that router's address. Then traceroute sends a packet with a TTL of 2, which dies at the second router, and so on, one hop further each time, until a packet finally reaches the destination. By timing each reply, traceroute shows how long the round trip to each router takes, usually trying three times per hop. If a router does not answer, traceroute prints a star instead.","Let us imagine a traceroute from a home in London to a website server in New York. The exact names and numbers would be different for every real connection, so treat this as a typical example rather than a real record. Hop 1 is your home Wi-Fi router, reached in about 2 milliseconds, that is, two thousandths of a second. Hops 2 to 4 are routers belonging to your Internet provider in your town and in central London, at about 5 to 10 milliseconds. Hop 5 might be a big exchange building where many networks meet. Then comes the big jump: at hop 6 or 7, the time suddenly leaps from about 10 milliseconds to around 70 or 75. That leap is the Atlantic Ocean. The last few hops, inside New York, add only a millisecond or two each, and the server answers at hop 12 or so.","Why is crossing the ocean so slow, when light is the fastest thing there is? Light in empty space travels at about 300,000 kilometres per second. But inside a glass fibre it slows to about 200,000 kilometres per second, roughly two-thirds of its full speed, because glass bends and slows light. London and New York are about 5,600 kilometres apart in a straight line, and real cables follow longer paths across the seabed and over land. At 200,000 kilometres per second, every 200 kilometres of fibre adds about one millisecond. So a cable path of about 6,000 kilometres takes roughly 30 milliseconds one way, and about 60 there and back. Add a few milliseconds for routers, queues and detours, and you get the 70-odd milliseconds that traceroute shows. The delay is mostly just the speed of light.","Seventy milliseconds is less than the time it takes you to blink, which is usually about a tenth of a second or more. So all two thousand pieces of your dog photo can make the journey, be checked, reordered and acknowledged, and appear on your cousin's screen in well under a second. Packet by packet, hop by hop, the Internet quietly does its job.","But wait. Every router along the way had to read a destination address, a long string of numbers, to know where to send each packet. Where did those numbers come from? How does every computer on Earth get its own address? And when you type a name like a website's address instead of a number, how does your computer find the right one? That is the mystery of the next part."]}],"deepDive":"Network engineers measure a connection's quality with two different numbers that are easy to mix up: bandwidth and latency. Bandwidth is how many bits per second a link can carry, like the width of a motorway. Latency is how long a single bit takes to make the trip, like the driving time. A fibre link across the Atlantic can have enormous bandwidth, yet its latency can never fall below the time light needs to cross the ocean in glass. Light in silica fibre travels at about 200,000 km/s because the glass has a refractive index of roughly 1.47, so the absolute minimum one-way delay is about 5 microseconds per kilometre. This is why some financial firms have paid for microwave radio links between trading centres: radio through air travels closer to the full speed of light, shaving off milliseconds. Packet size involves a trade-off too. Bigger packets waste less space on headers, but they hold a link longer and cost more to resend after an error; on a slow 14.4 kbit/s modem, one 1,500-byte packet tied up the line for about a second. The 1,500-byte Ethernet limit dates from early Ethernet designs and became so widespread that much of the Internet still assumes it, although data-centre networks often use 'jumbo frames' of around 9,000 bytes. If a packet is too big for a link, IPv4 routers can split it into fragments, but losing any fragment loses the whole packet, so modern systems prefer 'path MTU discovery', in which the sender learns the smallest limit along the route and sizes its packets to fit. Historians and engineers also debate how to describe the layers. The official OSI model of the 1980s has seven layers, while the Internet's own design documents describe four; the five-layer version taught in many classrooms is a blend. Finally, TCP's congestion control remains an active research area. The 1986 collapse led to Jacobson's algorithms, and newer schemes such as CUBIC and Google's BBR, plus the QUIC protocol that runs over UDP and now carries a large share of web traffic, keep refining how the Internet shares its roads fairly.","funFact":"Every IPv4 packet carries a 'time to live' counter that each router lowers by one, so a packet that gets lost in a loop is thrown away instead of circling the Internet forever.","check":{"q":"What does TCP do if no acknowledgement comes back for a packet it sent?","a":"It sends the packet again","wrong":["It cancels the whole message","It switches the packet to a satellite","It asks the router to keep the packet forever"]},"sources":[{"title":"RFC 791: Internet Protocol","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc791"},{"title":"RFC 793: Transmission Control Protocol","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc793"},{"title":"A Brief History of the Internet","publisher":"Internet Society","url":"https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/"},{"title":"Network congestion","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_congestion"},{"title":"Traceroute","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traceroute"},{"title":"Maximum transmission unit","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximum_transmission_unit"},{"title":"Optical fiber","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optical_fiber"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/07-1.jpg","alt":"The IPv4 packet header, drawn as in RFC 791: fields like Time to Live, Source Address and Destination Address.","file":"Ip-header-format.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ip-header-format.png","author":"Gustinoob","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/07-2.jpg","alt":"Network switch racks full of yellow fibre cables at DE-CIX in Frankfurt, one of the world's biggest Internet exchanges.","file":"DE-CIX GERMANY - Switch Rack (6218137120).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DE-CIX_GERMANY_-_Switch_Rack_(6218137120).jpg","author":"Stefan Funke from Frankfurt, Germany","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/07-3.jpg","alt":"A bundle of thin glass optical fibres, each glowing at its tip with light that has travelled along it.","file":"Fibreoptic4.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fibreoptic4.jpg","author":"BigRiz","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4115},{"n":8,"title":"Addresses and Names: IP and DNS","when":"How it works (part 2), 1970s – today","summary":"Every computer on the Internet needs an address number, just like every house needs a street address. But numbers are hard to remember, so clever people built a giant shared phone book called DNS that turns names like example.com into numbers. And when the Internet started running out of numbers, engineers invented a new kind of address with more than enough for every grain of sand on Earth!","sections":[{"heading":"Every Door Needs a Number","paragraphs":["In Part 7 we rode along with a packet as routers passed it from hop to hop. Every one of those routers made its choice by reading a single thing: the destination address in the packet's header. Without addresses, routers would be like postal workers holding a pile of blank envelopes. So where do these addresses come from, and what do they look like?","The Internet's addresses are called IP addresses, because they belong to the Internet Protocol. The classic kind, used since the early 1980s, is IPv4, version 4 of the protocol. An IPv4 address is a number 32 bits long. Long strings of 1s and 0s are hard for people to read, so we usually split the 32 bits into four groups of 8 bits, turn each group into an ordinary number from 0 to 255, and put dots between them. An example looks like this: 192.0.2.1.","That particular address is special. Engineers set aside the block starting 192.0.2 just for examples in books and documents, so that nobody accidentally sends real traffic to a real person's computer by copying an example. You will never find a real website living there, which makes it perfect for a story like this one.","An IP address works a lot like a postal address, which runs from big to small: country, city, street, house. The first part of an IP address usually identifies a network, such as a university or an Internet provider, and the last part identifies one particular device on that network. Routers far away only need to look at the first part to send a packet in the right direction, just as a sorting office in another country only needs to read the country name. When the packet gets close, routers look at the rest.","Your home is a good example of how this works in practice. Your Internet provider usually gives your home router one public IP address that the rest of the world can see. Inside your house, the router hands out private addresses, often starting with 192.168, to your laptop, tablet, games console and smart speaker. When those devices go online, the router swaps their private address for the public one on the way out, and keeps careful notes so it can deliver the replies to the right device. This trick is called network address translation, or NAT. It means a whole family, or even a whole office, can share one public address, a bit like an apartment block with one street number and a front desk that sorts the mail for each flat.","How does a new phone or laptop get its private address in the first place? Nobody types it in. When a device joins a Wi-Fi network, it shouts a short message that means roughly, 'Hello, I am new here, can somebody give me an address?' The router answers with a free address, plus a few other useful details, such as which way leads out to the Internet and which server to ask about names. This automatic system is called DHCP, and it lends each address for a limited time, a bit like a library book, so that addresses from devices that have left can be reused. That is why your tablet's address at home might change from one week to the next, and why it gets a completely different one when you connect at a friend's house.","Numbers are perfect for routers. But imagine if, to visit a website or send an email, you had to remember a string of numbers for every place you wanted to go. People are much better with names. The story of how the Internet learned to use names begins with a single, ever-growing list, and a woman whose team kept it."]},{"heading":"One List for the Whole Network","paragraphs":["In the early days of the ARPANET, the network was small enough for everyone to know everyone else. Computers had short names, and somebody needed to keep track of which name belonged to which address. That job went to the Network Information Center, or NIC, at SRI, the same research institute in California that had received the very first ARPANET message in 1969.","The NIC was run by Elizabeth Feinler, known to her colleagues as Jake. She had trained as a chemist and had worked on huge reference books before joining SRI in 1960, where in 1972 she joined Douglas Engelbart's research centre. That background in organising information turned out to be exactly what the young network needed. From 1972 until 1989, Feinler's group managed the registry of host names for the network. They also published directories of the people and machines on the ARPANET, a kind of early 'white pages' and 'yellow pages' for the network, and they ran one of the first servers that could answer questions about who owned a name, a service called WHOIS.","The heart of the naming system was a single text file, usually called HOSTS.TXT. Each line paired a computer's name with its address. Whenever a new computer joined the network, or an old one changed its address, the people running it contacted the NIC, the NIC updated the master file, and every computer on the network was expected to download a fresh copy now and then. Today's computers still contain a small leftover of this idea, a file simply called 'hosts', which you can find hidden inside the system folders of Windows, macOS and Linux.","For a network of a few dozen, or even a few hundred, computers, one shared list worked well. But in the early 1980s, things were changing fast. Universities and companies were plugging in whole local networks full of workstations, and the number of machines was shooting up. Every new computer meant another change to the file, and every change meant thousands of copies around the network slowly going out of date. The list was getting too big, updated too often, and sent around too much. It was like trying to run the phone book for an entire country from one office, by posting a new printed copy to every home whenever anyone moved house.","In a technical document in November 1983, the problem was described plainly: the size of the table, and especially how often it had to be updated, were 'near the limit of manageability'. Something completely new was needed."]},{"heading":"Paul Mockapetris Builds a Phone Book That Shares the Work","paragraphs":["The person who designed that something new was Paul Mockapetris, a young computer scientist at the University of Southern California's Information Sciences Institute (ISI) in Marina del Rey, near Los Angeles. ISI was a busy hub of Internet work. Jon Postel, who edited the RFCs and kept track of the Internet's numbers, worked there too, and the two of them, together with others in the community, had been discussing how to replace the overloaded host table. In 1983 Mockapetris wrote up his design in two documents, RFC 882 and RFC 883. He called it the Domain Name System, or DNS.","His key idea was to stop keeping one giant list in one place. Instead, the job would be shared out among thousands of computers, each responsible for its own small piece. The names themselves would be arranged like a family tree, or like a set of folders inside folders.","At the very top of the tree is the root, which has no name at all. Just below the root sit the top-level domains, such as com, org and uk. Below each of those come the names that organisations choose, such as example under com. And below those, an organisation can create as many more names as it likes for its own computers, such as www or mail. When we write a full name, we read it from the most specific part on the left to the most general on the right, with dots in between: www.example.com means 'the computer called www, inside example, inside com, inside the root'.","The really clever part is called delegation. The people who run the root do not need to know about every name. They only need to know who is in charge of each top-level domain. The people running com only need to know who is in charge of each name like example. And the people running example decide everything underneath it themselves. If a school wants to add a new computer called library, it does not need to ask anyone else in the world. It just updates its own little part of the tree.","This made DNS like a phone book that writes itself. No single office has to handle every change, and no single list has to be copied to every computer. Instead, when a computer needs an address, it asks around, starting from the top of the tree and working down, until it finds the server that knows the answer.","To make this fast, DNS also uses caching, which means remembering answers for a while. Each answer comes with a 'time to live' saying how long it can be trusted, perhaps a few minutes, perhaps a day. Once your Internet provider's DNS server has looked up a popular name, it can answer the next thousand people who ask straight from memory. DNS was first described in 1983 and was being put into use from 1984, and over the following years it replaced HOSTS.TXT completely. It later became one of the Internet's original official standards, and it is still working away, billions of times a day, every time you open a website, send an email or start an online game."]},{"heading":"Dot-Com, Dot-Org, Dot-Everything","paragraphs":["Once there was a tree, someone had to decide what the first branches should be. In October 1984, Jon Postel and his colleague Joyce Reynolds published RFC 920, which listed the first general top-level domains. There was gov for government, edu for education, com for commercial businesses, mil for the military and org for other organisations. A temporary domain called arpa was kept for the old ARPANET names. Elizabeth Feinler's group at the NIC also played a big part in shaping this scheme, and the net domain, meant for network organisations, soon joined the list.","RFC 920 also planned for countries. Each country could have its own two-letter top-level domain, based on an international list of country codes. At the time the document was written, no country domains existed yet. The first ones arrived in 1985: us for the United States, uk for the United Kingdom and il for Israel. Many more followed. China's cn arrived in the early 1990s, and today there are well over 300 country-code domains, including versions written in non-Latin scripts such as Chinese, Arabic and Cyrillic.","Some small countries have found that their two letters are worth a lot. The Pacific island nation of Tuvalu has the code tv, which is popular with television and video companies, and the money from selling those names has become an important source of income for the country.","The very first name registered in com was symbolics.com, on 15 March 1985. It belonged to Symbolics, a company in Massachusetts that made powerful computers designed for artificial-intelligence research, called Lisp machines. Back then, a com name was free to register and hardly anyone wanted one. Only a handful of companies registered com names during all of 1985. Symbolics itself later faded away, but its domain lives on as a piece of Internet history, and in 2009 it was bought by a domain investment company that keeps it as a kind of online museum page.","Compare that slow start with today. There are now hundreds of millions of registered domain names, with com still by far the most popular. Since the 2010s, the list of top-level domains has also grown to include more than a thousand new endings, from app and blog to city names and brand names. Each one is just another branch at the top of Mockapetris's tree."]},{"heading":"Jon Postel, Keeper of the Numbers","paragraphs":["Names and numbers only work if they are unique. If two computers had the same address, routers would not know which one to deliver to, like two houses on the same street with the same number. So somebody has to hand out the numbers and keep the master records. For most of the Internet's first thirty years, that somebody was largely one man: Jon Postel.","Postel had been a graduate student in Leonard Kleinrock's lab at UCLA, where the first ARPANET node was installed, and he earned his doctorate there in 1974. When Steve Crocker started the Request for Comments series in 1969, Postel soon became its editor, a job he held for almost three decades. He read, polished and published the documents that described how the Internet worked, including key ones about IP, TCP, email and DNS. He was famous for a simple piece of advice for anyone writing network software, often called the robustness principle: be careful in what you send, but generous in what you accept from others.","Postel also volunteered to keep track of the numbers that the Internet's rules needed, such as which address blocks belonged to which networks and which port numbers belonged to which services. At first he did it by hand. Friends jokingly called him the 'numbers czar'. This job grew into the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority, or IANA. With his long beard and relaxed style, Postel did not look like a powerful official, but for years a surprising amount of the Internet's smooth running depended on his careful record-keeping at ISI.","As the Internet became a worldwide public network in the 1990s, many people felt that such an important job should not rest with one person and one small government-funded office. After long discussions involving the United States government, businesses, engineers and other countries, a new organisation was created in 1998: the Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers, or ICANN. It is a non-profit organisation based in California, and its policies are shaped by a wide mix of people, including businesses, governments, technical experts and ordinary Internet users. It took over the IANA functions, including looking after the root of the DNS tree and the master pool of IP addresses.","Sadly, Postel did not see ICANN grow up. He died on 16 October 1998, aged 55, just as the new organisation was being set up. The next day Vint Cerf wrote a memorial, published as RFC 2468, called 'I Remember IANA', describing him as the first of the Internet's giants to depart. The Internet Society later created the Postel Service Award in his honour.","Today, IANA does not hand out addresses to people directly. It passes big blocks of addresses to five regional Internet registries, each looking after one part of the world: ARIN for North America, RIPE NCC for Europe, the Middle East and parts of Central Asia, APNIC for Asia and the Pacific, LACNIC for Latin America and the Caribbean, and AFRINIC for Africa. They pass smaller blocks to Internet providers, who in turn give addresses to homes, schools and businesses. In 2016 the United States government formally ended its special oversight role, leaving the IANA functions in the hands of the global community through ICANN."]},{"heading":"Running Out of Numbers","paragraphs":["When the designers of IPv4 chose 32-bit addresses in the 1970s, it seemed like an enormous number. Thirty-two bits allows 2 multiplied by itself 32 times, which comes to 4,294,967,296 addresses, about 4.3 billion. At the time, the whole network had only a few hundred computers. Who could ever need four billion?","In fact, the original plan was even more limited. The first 8 bits of an address were used to name the network, and the other 24 bits named the computer inside it. That allowed only 256 networks, which seemed plenty when there were only a few big research networks. When local networks started appearing everywhere in the 1980s, engineers changed the rules, first by creating different 'classes' of address blocks of different sizes, and later, in 1993, by allowing blocks of almost any size. Even so, some early organisations had been given gigantic blocks of about 16.7 million addresses each, far more than they would ever use.","By the 1990s it was clear that the world was going to want far more than 4.3 billion addresses. Today there are more people on Earth than that, and many of them own a phone, a laptop and other connected gadgets. Tricks like NAT, which lets many devices share one public address, bought a lot of time. But the pool of free addresses kept shrinking.","The moment finally came in early 2011. On 3 February 2011, IANA handed out its last five large blocks of IPv4 addresses, one to each of the five regional registries. The central pool of free IPv4 addresses was empty. The regional registries ran out of their own normal supplies over the following years, and today IPv4 addresses are often bought and sold between organisations, sometimes for a noticeable price per address.","The long-term answer had been prepared years earlier: IPv6, version 6 of the Internet Protocol. (Version 5 had been used for an experimental project, so the number was skipped.) The main IPv6 specification first appeared in the 1990s and was finalised as a full Internet Standard in 2017. Its addresses are 128 bits long instead of 32. That gives about 340 undecillion possible addresses, or 3.4 times 10 to the power 38, a 34 followed by 37 zeros. It is a number so large that it is hard to picture; every person alive could have many trillions of trillions of addresses each.","IPv6 addresses look different too. They are written as eight groups of four hexadecimal digits, which use the numbers 0 to 9 and the letters a to f, separated by colons. Long runs of zeros can be shortened with a double colon, so an example address, from a block reserved for documentation, can be written as 2001:db8::1. Moving the whole Internet to IPv6 has been slow, because IPv4 and IPv6 cannot talk to each other directly, and every network, router, computer and app needs to support both during the long changeover. But the shift is well under way: a large and growing share of Internet users now reach big websites over IPv6, often without ever knowing it, and in some countries it is already the majority."]},{"heading":"What Happens When You Type a Name","paragraphs":["Let us put everything together. Imagine you type www.example.org into your browser and press Enter. What happens in the next fraction of a second?","Step one: your browser checks its own memory. If you visited the site a few minutes ago, it may already know the address. If not, it asks your computer's operating system, which keeps its own small memory of recent answers and also glances at that old-fashioned hosts file.","Step two: if your computer does not know, it sends a short question to a DNS server called a recursive resolver. Usually this is run by your Internet provider, though some people choose a public resolver run by another organisation. The resolver's job is to do the detective work for you. If it has answered the same question recently for someone else, it replies immediately from its cache.","Step three: if the resolver does not know either, it starts at the top of the tree. It asks one of the root servers, 'Where can I find www.example.org?' The root server does not know the full answer, but it knows who is in charge of org, and it replies with the addresses of the org servers. There are only 13 named root server identities, lettered A to M and run by twelve different organisations, but thanks to a technique that lets many machines share one address, copies of them are running at well over a thousand places around the world.","Step four: the resolver asks an org server. The org server does not know about www either, but it knows which name servers are in charge of example.org, so it passes on their addresses.","Step five: the resolver asks one of example.org's own name servers, which is called the authoritative server because it holds the official records for that name. This server finally gives the real answer: the IP address for www.example.org.","Step six: the resolver sends the answer back to your computer and remembers it for as long as its time to live allows. Your browser now has a number. It can open a TCP connection to that address with the three-way handshake you met in Part 7, ask for the web page, and receive it as a stream of packets. All of these DNS questions and answers usually take only a few tens of milliseconds, and often much less when the answer is already cached nearby.","So every time you visit a website, you are using two inventions from the early 1980s working together: IP addresses that let routers deliver packets, and DNS names that let humans find those addresses without memorising numbers. In the mid-1980s, though, the people using these systems were still mostly scientists, engineers, university researchers and the military. Ordinary families had never heard of TCP/IP or dot-com. That was about to change."]}],"deepDive":"DNS is one of the most successful distributed databases ever built, but it was designed in a gentler era and has had to be strengthened. Early DNS trusted any answer that looked right, so attackers could try to slip in fake answers, a trick called cache poisoning. In 2008 the researcher Dan Kaminsky showed that this was much easier than people had assumed, and software makers around the world rushed out coordinated fixes. A longer-term defence, DNSSEC, adds digital signatures so that resolvers can check that an answer really came from the right owner; the root zone was signed in 2010, although many domains still do not use it. Another concern is privacy: traditional DNS questions travel unencrypted, so anyone along the path can see which sites you look up. Newer methods such as DNS over HTTPS and DNS over TLS wrap the questions in encryption, which protects users but has also caused debate, because it can bypass network filters used by schools, parents or governments. The 13-letter limit on root server identities comes from an old rule that a DNS answer should fit in a 512-byte UDP packet; techniques called anycast let each letter be served from hundreds of locations, which also makes the root very hard to knock over with floods of traffic. Historians also discuss how much power ICANN really has. It controls what goes into the root zone, which means it can approve new top-level domains such as the more than 1,000 added after 2012, but it does not control the content of websites or the routing of packets. The IPv4 shortage has created a market where address blocks are traded, while IPv6 adoption varies widely from country to country. Some engineers argue that IPv4 and NAT will linger for decades, while others note that mobile networks, which need vast numbers of addresses, have pushed IPv6 forward fastest.","funFact":"The DNS root is served by only 13 named server identities, lettered A to M, yet copies of them run at well over a thousand locations around the world.","check":{"q":"Who designed the Domain Name System (DNS) in 1983?","a":"Paul Mockapetris","wrong":["Tim Berners-Lee","Ray Tomlinson","Bob Metcalfe"]},"sources":[{"title":"RFC 882: Domain Names - Concepts and Facilities","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc882"},{"title":"RFC 920: Domain Requirements","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc920"},{"title":"Paul Mockapetris","publisher":"Internet Hall of Fame","url":"https://www.internethalloffame.org/inductees/paul-mockapetris"},{"title":"Elizabeth Feinler","publisher":"Internet Hall of Fame","url":"https://www.internethalloffame.org/inductees/elizabeth-feinler"},{"title":"Jon Postel","publisher":"Internet Hall of Fame","url":"https://www.internethalloffame.org/inductees/jon-postel"},{"title":"RFC 2468: I Remember IANA","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2468"},{"title":"Free Pool of IPv4 Address Space Depleted","publisher":"Number Resource Organization","url":"https://www.nro.net/ipv4-free-pool-depleted/"},{"title":"RFC 8200: Internet Protocol, Version 6 (IPv6) Specification","publisher":"IETF / RFC Editor","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8200"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/08-1.jpg","alt":"Jon Postel, longtime RFC editor and caretaker of Internet addresses and names, in his office full of binders.","file":"Jon Postel sitting in office.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jon_Postel_sitting_in_office.jpg","author":"Carl Malamud","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/08-2.jpg","alt":"The Domain Name System as a tree: one root at the top, branching into zones, each looked after by a name server.","file":"Domain name space.svg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domain_name_space.svg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/08-3.jpg","alt":"A 1983 Symbolics 3620 Lisp workstation at the Computer History Museum. Symbolics owned symbolics.com, the first .com name.","file":"Symbolics LISP Workstation (Computer History Museum).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Symbolics_LISP_Workstation_(Computer_History_Museum).jpg","author":"Thomas Conté","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3809},{"n":9,"title":"From Labs to Living Rooms","when":"1979 – 1995","summary":"In the 1980s, people at home began plugging their computers into the phone line. Modems screeched, message boards buzzed and a speedy new science network called NSFNET grew into the Internet's main highway. By 1995 the Internet was open to everyone, not just scientists.","sections":[{"heading":"Squeals, Hisses and Bits Per Second","paragraphs":["Imagine it is a rainy evening in 1993. You switch on the family computer, click an icon, and from a little box beside it comes the strangest noise: a dial tone, a quick burst of beeps as a phone number is dialled, then a long, high whistle, followed by crunching, hissing and warbling like two robots arguing inside a seashell. After about twenty seconds the noise stops. You are online.","That noisy box was a modem. The word is short for modulator-demodulator. Telephone lines were built to carry human voices, which are wobbly sound waves, not the neat 1s and 0s that computers use. So a modem 'modulates' bits into sounds, sends the sounds down the phone line, and the modem at the other end 'demodulates' the sounds back into bits. In a way, two modems really were singing to each other.","The screeching had a purpose. It was the 'handshake', a short conversation in which the two modems tested the line. One tone told the telephone network to switch off its echo-cancelling equipment, which would otherwise mess up the data. Then the modems sent test signals back and forth to measure how noisy the line was, and agreed on the fastest speed it could safely handle. A crackly line meant a slower connection.","Modem speeds were measured in bits per second. One of the first popular modems for data, AT&T's Bell 103 from 1962, ran at 300 bits per second. Early home users sometimes used an 'acoustic coupler', a box with two rubber cups that gripped an ordinary telephone handset, so the computer literally whistled into the mouthpiece. In 1981 the Hayes Smartmodem, also at 300 bits per second, became a hit because a computer could control it with simple text commands, many of which started with the letters AT. Those 'AT commands' are still built into some modems today.","Over the next fifteen years speeds climbed: 1,200, then 2,400, then 9,600 bits per second, then 14,400 and 28,800 in the early and mid-1990s. The last big step was 56 kilobits per second, standardised in 1998. Even then, 56k modems rarely reached their top speed. Most homes got something more like 40 to 50 kilobits per second in the download direction, and uploads were slower still.","Let us do some maths to feel how slow that was. A single holiday photo from a modern phone might be about 3 megabytes, which is about 24 million bits. At 56,000 bits per second, even in perfect conditions, that one picture would take about seven minutes to arrive. At 2,400 bits per second it would take nearly three hours. Today a home fibre connection can fetch the same photo in a fraction of a second.","Dial-up had another catch. Because the modem used the phone line, nobody else in the house could make or receive a call while you were online. Remember the big idea from Part 1: a phone call ties up a whole circuit for as long as it lasts. Dial-up Internet was packet switching riding on top of an old circuit-switched phone call. In many countries, including Britain, phone calls were charged by the minute, so parents watched the clock, and some families saved their Internet time for evenings and weekends when calls were cheaper."]},{"heading":"Bulletin Boards and the Great Usenet Conversation","paragraphs":["Long before most families had heard of the Internet, hobbyists with modems were already chatting to strangers. In January and February 1978 a huge blizzard buried Chicago in snow. Stuck indoors, two computer club members, Ward Christensen and Randy Suess, built a program that let people phone up a computer and leave messages for each other, like pinning notes on a cork board. They called it CBBS, the Computerized Bulletin Board System, and it went online in February 1978.","The idea spread fast. A bulletin board system, or BBS, was usually one personal computer in someone's spare room or basement, attached to one phone line. The owner was called the 'sysop', short for system operator. Because there was only one line, only one person could be connected at a time, so popular boards were often busy and you had to keep redialling. Once connected, you could read and post messages, play simple text games and download small programs and files.","Most boards were local, because long-distance calls were expensive. So in 1984 a programmer in San Francisco, Tom Jennings, created FidoNet. Late at night, during a special 'mail hour' when calls were cheaper, FidoNet boards phoned each other and passed on bundles of messages, like a relay of postal vans moving mail from town to town. By the early 1990s there were tens of thousands of bulletin boards in the United States alone.","Meanwhile, at universities, a different conversation was beginning. In 1979 two graduate students at Duke University in North Carolina, Tom Truscott and Jim Ellis, wanted a way for computer users at different universities to share news and discussions. They worked with Steve Bellovin, a student at the nearby University of North Carolina, who wrote the first version of the software. In 1980 their system, called Usenet, linked Duke and UNC, and it soon spread to other campuses.","Usenet did not need the ARPANET. It ran on the Unix operating system, which AT&T had spread widely to universities, and it used a Unix program called UUCP to make computers phone each other, often at night, and swap new articles. Each computer kept a copy of the latest messages and passed them on to its neighbours, so a post written in North Carolina could gradually spread across the country and then the world. Some people called Usenet 'the poor man's ARPANET', because any university with a Unix computer and a modem could join.","Usenet messages were sorted into 'newsgroups', each about one topic. In 1987 the groups were reorganised into big families with names like comp (computers), sci (science), rec (hobbies and recreation), soc (society) and talk, and soon an 'alt' family for alternative topics appeared too. A group name read like an address: rec.arts.books, or sci.space. Thousands of groups grew, on everything from astronomy to cooking.","Usenet is where many online habits were born. Long-running groups wrote lists of frequently asked questions, or FAQs, so newcomers would not ask the same thing over and over. Usenet also became the place to announce big new ideas. In 1991 a Finnish student named Linus Torvalds announced his new operating system, later called Linux, on a Usenet group, and as you will discover in Part 10, the World Wide Web was announced on Usenet too."]},{"heading":"Walled Gardens: CompuServe, Prodigy, AOL and Minitel","paragraphs":["Most families who went online in the 1980s and early 1990s did not actually connect to the Internet at all. Instead, they joined an 'online service', a big company that ran its own private network. You paid a monthly fee, dialled the company's local phone number, and stepped into its own world of news, games, shopping, email and chat rooms. People later called these services 'walled gardens': pleasant and well kept, but with high walls around them.","CompuServe, based in Columbus, Ohio, started in 1969 as a business that rented time on its big computers. In 1979 it began offering a service for home computer owners, and in 1980 it launched the CB Simulator, one of the first public online chat systems, named after the citizens band radios that truck drivers used. CompuServe members had email addresses made of numbers with a comma in the middle. In 1987 a CompuServe engineer, Steve Wilhite, created the GIF image format, which is still used for short looping animations today.","Prodigy, launched across the United States in 1990 by IBM and the department store company Sears, tried to make going online colourful. Its screens had simple pictures and a strip of advertising along the bottom, and it charged a flat monthly price. Then came America Online, or AOL. It grew out of a small company called Quantum Computer Services, which in 1985 launched Q-Link, a service for owners of the Commodore 64 home computer. Renamed America Online in 1991, it became famous for mailing out free trial discs, first floppy disks and later CDs, by the millions. Opening one meant hearing a cheerful recorded voice announce: 'You've got mail!'","Across the Atlantic, France had built something even bigger. Starting with trials in 1980 and a national launch from 1982, the French telephone service rolled out Minitel. Instead of printing huge paper phone books, it gave families a small terminal for free: a beige box with a little screen and a fold-down keyboard that plugged into the phone line. At first people used it to look up phone numbers, but soon they were buying train tickets, checking bank accounts, reading the news, checking exam results and chatting.","Minitel had a clever way of paying for things. Services had short codes such as 3615 followed by a name, and the cost appeared on the family's normal phone bill, charged by the minute. The phone company shared the money with the companies that ran the services, which encouraged thousands of them to appear. Millions of French homes had a Minitel terminal, years before most of the world had seen a web page. It kept running until it was finally switched off on 30 June 2012.","Here is the important point: Minitel was not the Internet. It was a central, national system run by one organisation, built on its own rules, and it did not connect freely to other networks. CompuServe, Prodigy and AOL were similar islands. You could only talk to people inside the same garden. What these services proved, though, was that ordinary people, not only scientists, really wanted to be online. They wanted news, shopping, games and, above all, a way to talk to each other. When the walls later came down, those millions of users poured onto the open Internet."]},{"heading":"NSFNET: A Faster Highway for Science","paragraphs":["While families were dialling up bulletin boards, American scientists had a different problem. In the mid-1980s the US National Science Foundation, or NSF, paid for a handful of new supercomputer centres, packed with some of the fastest computers in the world. Supercomputers were extremely expensive, so only a few could be built. Researchers at hundreds of other universities needed a way to reach them from far away. Does that sound familiar? It was exactly the same reason ARPA had wanted a network back in the 1960s: sharing expensive machines.","In 1985 Dennis Jennings, an Irish computer scientist leading the NSF networking programme, made a decision that shaped the future: every network in the programme would have to use TCP/IP, the same rules as the ARPANET. That meant the new network would not be another island. It would plug straight into the growing Internet.","In 1986 the NSFNET backbone was switched on. A 'backbone' is the main set of high-speed links that carries traffic between big regions, like a motorway between cities. The first NSFNET backbone linked six sites, including supercomputer centres at Princeton, Cornell, the University of Illinois, Pittsburgh and San Diego, with links running at 56 kilobits per second. That is about the same as one late dial-up modem, which shows how much speeds would change in just a few years.","Around the backbone grew 'regional networks', such as NYSERNet in New York State and SURAnet in the south-eastern United States. Universities connected to their regional network, and regional networks connected to the backbone. The NSF also insisted that when a university connected, the link should be available to all qualified users on its campus, not just a few computer scientists. Suddenly students and professors in biology, history and music could use the Internet too.","So many people joined that the network quickly became overloaded. In October 1986 traffic jams became so bad that, on one well-known link between Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory and the University of California, Berkeley, only about 400 yards apart, the speed collapsed from about 32,000 bits per second to about 40. Packets were being lost, resent, lost again and resent again, clogging the lines. The computer scientist Van Jacobson studied the problem and in 1988 published changes to TCP that made computers slow down politely when the network was busy, then speed up again gradually. Versions of these 'congestion control' ideas still keep the Internet from jamming today.","The NSF also upgraded the backbone itself. In 1987 it chose Merit, a network group based at the University of Michigan, working with the companies IBM and MCI, to build a faster one. By 1988 the backbone ran at T1 speed, about 1.5 megabits per second, roughly 28 times faster than before, and that same year countries such as Canada, Denmark, Finland, France, Iceland, Norway and Sweden joined. By 1991 the backbone was upgraded again, to T3 links of about 45 megabits per second.","The numbers grew at an astonishing pace. The Computer History Museum records about 2,000 host computers on the Internet at the end of 1985, more than 10,000 in 1987, about 60,000 in 1988 and over 160,000 by late 1989. By 1992 more than a million computers were connected. Over its life, the NSFNET backbone grew from six sites joined by 56-kilobit links to a coast-to-coast network of 45-megabit links.","And what about the old ARPANET, where it all began? By the late 1980s it was slow and outdated compared with NSFNET, and its sites moved over to the newer networks one by one. In 1989 UCLA held a symposium to celebrate twenty years since the first 'LO', and Vint Cerf even wrote a poem for the occasion. The ARPANET was formally decommissioned in 1990. It had grown from four computers to a network that connected to hundreds of thousands, and then it quietly stepped aside. The Internet it had started simply kept going without it."]},{"heading":"Breaking Loops: Radia Perlman's Tree","paragraphs":["As networks grew, engineers faced a sneaky problem inside buildings and campuses. Remember Ethernet from Part 5, the way of linking computers in one building? To make an Ethernet network bigger, engineers used devices called bridges, which joined two or more network segments together and passed messages between them.","To be safe, network builders liked to add extra bridges and extra cables, so that if one link broke, another path would still work. But that created loops: paths that went round in a circle. Some messages on Ethernet are 'broadcasts', meant for every computer. When a broadcast reached a loop, each bridge copied it and passed it on, and the copies went round and round, multiplying each time, never stopping. Within seconds a network could be flooded so badly that nothing else could get through. Engineers called this a broadcast storm.","In 1984 Radia Perlman, an engineer at Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC), was asked to solve the problem, and her solution was published in 1985. She had studied mathematics and computer science at MIT, and the solution she designed is called the spanning-tree protocol. The idea is beautifully simple. First, all the bridges 'vote' to choose one bridge as the root, the one with the lowest identification number. Then every other bridge works out its shortest path to the root. Any extra links that would create a loop are switched into a waiting mode, blocked but ready. The result is a tree shape: every part of the network is connected, but there are no circles.","Here is the clever part. If a cable breaks or a bridge fails, the bridges notice and run the process again, waking up one of the waiting links to heal the network automatically. Nobody needs to rush around with a screwdriver. Her design was turned into an official standard, IEEE 802.1D, and spanning tree and its descendants have helped run Ethernet networks in offices, schools and data centres ever since.","Perlman even wrote a poem about her algorithm, called 'Algorhyme', which begins: 'I think that I shall never see / A graph more lovely than a tree.' It is a playful copy of a famous old poem called 'Trees'. In maths, a 'graph' means a set of points joined by lines, and a 'tree' is a graph with no loops.","Perlman went on to design other important systems, including a routing method called IS-IS that still helps route Internet traffic in big networks, and later TRILL, an improvement on spanning tree. She has been granted more than 100 patents and was inducted into the Internet Hall of Fame in 2014. Some newspapers have called her the 'Mother of the Internet', but she has pushed back on that nickname, pointing out that the Internet was built by many people. That is a very Internet-like thing to say."]},{"heading":"The Night the Worm Got Loose","paragraphs":["On the evening of 2 November 1988, a program was released onto the Internet from a computer at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology. It had been written by Robert Tappan Morris, a 22-year-old graduate student at Cornell University. His aim, it is usually said, was simply to find out how big the Internet was, not to cause damage. But his program contained a serious mistake, and within hours it was spreading out of control.","The program was a worm: a piece of software that copies itself from computer to computer across a network on its own, without anyone opening a file. To get in, it used weaknesses in common programs. One trick used a hidden testing feature in the email software called sendmail. Another overloaded a program called finger, which people used to look up information about other users, by sending it more data than it expected. A third tried to guess weak passwords, using a list of common words.","Morris had tried to stop the worm from copying itself onto computers it had already infected. But to stop people tricking it, he made it copy itself anyway about one time in seven. That was far too often. Computers became infected again and again, until they were running dozens of copies of the worm at once and slowed to a crawl or stopped working. The worm did not delete files or steal information, but it jammed thousands of machines at universities, research labs and military sites.","About 6,000 computers were hit, roughly one in ten of the 60,000 or so computers then on the Internet. That night, system managers at the University of California, Berkeley, MIT and other places stayed up for hours, pulling the worm apart to understand how it worked and writing fixes. Some sites unplugged from the network completely to protect themselves, which also meant they could not receive the warning messages that explained how to fight it. Within a few days the worm had been cleared out, but the clean-up cost a great deal of time and money. Estimates of the cost varied hugely, from about 100,000 dollars to as much as 10 million dollars.","Morris became the first person convicted under the US Computer Fraud and Abuse Act of 1986. In 1990 he was sentenced to three years of probation, 400 hours of community service and a fine of about 10,000 dollars. He later went on to become a respected professor of computer science at MIT, showing that one serious mistake does not have to define a whole life.","The Morris worm was the Internet's first big wake-up call about security. The early network had been built by a small community of researchers who mostly knew and trusted each other. Now it was huge, and one program could cause chaos across the whole country in a single night. Within weeks, DARPA paid for a new team at the Software Engineering Institute at Carnegie Mellon University in Pittsburgh: the Computer Emergency Response Team, or CERT. Its job was to act like a fire station for computers, collecting reports of attacks, warning people about weaknesses and helping them fix their systems. Many countries later set up similar teams. You will learn much more about hackers, worms and how to stay safe in Part 17."]},{"heading":"Open for Business: 1991 to 1995","paragraphs":["There was one big rule standing between the Internet and everyday life. NSFNET had an 'Acceptable Use Policy' that said its backbone was only for research and education. You could not use it to run a business or sell things. But the rule only covered the national backbone, not every local and regional network, and private companies began building their own long-distance networks to carry the traffic that NSFNET would not.","Companies such as UUNET, founded in 1987, and PSINet, founded in 1989, started selling Internet connections to businesses. In 1989 a company in Brookline, Massachusetts, called The World began selling dial-up Internet access to members of the public, and it is often said to be the first to do so. In 1991 several of these commercial providers set up the Commercial Internet Exchange, a place where their networks could meet and swap traffic directly, without sending it over NSFNET at all.","Between 1991 and 1992 the rules were loosened step by step, and in 1992 the US Congress passed a law that allowed NSFNET to carry more commercial traffic. Meanwhile, the walled gardens began opening their gates. In 1993 and 1994 big online services such as Delphi and AOL began giving their members access to Usenet. So many newcomers arrived that Usenet old-timers joked that September 1993 had become an 'Eternal September', because every September, new university students used to arrive and learn the rules, and now the newcomers never stopped coming.","The NSF had always planned for the backbone to stand on its own feet one day, without government money. Instead of running one main highway, it arranged for several 'network access points', places in different parts of the United States where commercial networks could connect to each other. Internet service providers, or ISPs, could now carry traffic from coast to coast on their own lines and hand it over at these meeting points.","On 30 April 1995 the NSFNET backbone was switched off. Hardly anyone outside the networking world noticed, and that was the point: the traffic simply flowed through the new commercial networks instead. The money the NSF saved was given to regional networks so they could buy connections from the private long-distance companies. In about eight and a half years, the backbone had helped the Internet grow to more than 50,000 networks. Now the Internet belonged to no single government or company. It was a giant web of independent networks, all agreeing to use the same open rules.","Think how far things had come since 1979. A few universities swapping messages by phone at night had become millions of people dialling in from their bedrooms and kitchens. But going online was still not easy. You had to know strange commands, remember long computer names and dig through menus of files. Information was scattered across thousands of computers, each organised in its own way.","In fact, one scientist had been grumbling about exactly that problem for years. At CERN, a giant physics laboratory on the border of France and Switzerland, thousands of researchers from all over the world came and went, bringing their own computers, programs and documents. Finding anything was a nightmare. A British physicist and software engineer there was frustrated that useful information kept getting lost in a maze of incompatible machines. His name was Tim Berners-Lee, and he had an idea."]}],"deepDive":"Historians often describe the years from about 1985 to 1995 as the moment the Internet changed from a government research project into public infrastructure, and the NSF's choices explain a lot of why it happened so smoothly. Three decisions stand out. First, in 1985 the NSF made TCP/IP compulsory for its networking programme. At the time there were serious rivals, including the international OSI protocols that many governments officially preferred, and private systems from companies like IBM and DEC. By building on TCP/IP, NSFNET joined the existing ARPANET community rather than starting another island, and the sheer growth of NSFNET helped TCP/IP win the so-called protocol wars. Second, the NSF built a hierarchy: a national backbone, regional networks below it, and campus networks below those, while encouraging regionals to find commercial customers. That meant private companies were already involved long before the backbone closed. Third, the Acceptable Use Policy, which banned commercial traffic on the backbone, had an unexpected effect: it pushed businesses such as UUNET and PSINet to build their own long-haul networks, so a commercial Internet existed ready to take over. Not everyone thinks privatisation went perfectly. In 1991 the NSF allowed ANS, a non-profit created by Merit, IBM and MCI to run the backbone, to set up a for-profit arm, and some critics, and a 1992 congressional hearing, questioned whether this gave one group an unfair head start. The fast growth also exposed technical weaknesses. The 1986 congestion collapses showed that TCP needed to react to busy networks, and Van Jacobson's 1988 algorithms, such as slow start, became one of the most important fixes in Internet history. The Morris worm revealed how trusting the old software was. It exploited a buffer overflow in fingerd, where a program writes more data into memory than the space set aside for it, and that kind of bug remained one of the most common security holes for decades. Meanwhile, Perlman's spanning tree shows a theme that runs through the whole story: rather than relying on a central controller, the network's parts follow simple shared rules and organise themselves, and can heal when something breaks.","funFact":"France's Minitel network kept running for more than 30 years, from its first trials in 1980 until it was switched off on 30 June 2012, long after most French homes were on the Internet.","check":{"q":"Which science network, switched on in 1986 to link supercomputer centres, became the Internet's main backbone until April 1995?","a":"NSFNET","wrong":["Minitel","CompuServe","FidoNet"]},"sources":[{"title":"Internet History of 1980s","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/internethistory/1980s/"},{"title":"Internet History of 1990s","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/internethistory/1990s/"},{"title":"A Brief History of the Internet","publisher":"Internet Society","url":"https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/"},{"title":"The Internet (NSF impacts)","publisher":"US National Science Foundation","url":"https://www.nsf.gov/impacts/internet"},{"title":"Radia Perlman","publisher":"Internet Hall of Fame","url":"https://www.internethalloffame.org/inductee/radia-perlman/"},{"title":"CERT Division","publisher":"Software Engineering Institute, Carnegie Mellon University","url":"https://www.sei.cmu.edu/divisions/cert/"},{"title":"Morris worm","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morris_worm"},{"title":"NSFNET","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NSFNET"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/09-1.jpg","alt":"A 1982 Hayes Smartmodem 300 with its row of red status lights, which let home computers talk over phone lines.","file":"Hayes Smartmodem 1982 (1).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hayes_Smartmodem_1982_(1).jpg","author":"Aeroid","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/09-2.jpg","alt":"A French Minitel 1 terminal made by La Radiotechnique, with its small screen and built-in keyboard, in a museum case.","file":"Minitel 1 La Radiotechnique.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minitel_1_La_Radiotechnique.jpg","author":"DMontagne en résidence","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/09-3.jpg","alt":"A 1991 picture of the NSFNET T1 backbone (white lines) floating above the USA, with traffic to regional networks below.","file":"NSFNET-traffic-visualization-1991.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NSFNET-traffic-visualization-1991.jpg","author":"Transferred from en.wikipedia to Commons by Fredlyfish4 using CommonsHelper.","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4218},{"n":10,"title":"Weaving the World Wide Web","when":"1945 – 1993","summary":"A scientist at a giant physics lab in Switzerland was tired of losing information. So he invented a way to link pages together with clickable words, and called it the World Wide Web. Then his lab gave it away to the whole world for free.","sections":[{"heading":"The Road and the Shops","paragraphs":["Before we meet the inventor of the Web, we need to clear up one of the most common mix-ups in all of technology. Many people use the words 'Internet' and 'Web' as if they mean the same thing. They do not.","Think of the Internet as a giant system of roads, motorways and delivery vans that stretches around the planet. Its job is to carry packets from one computer to another, using the TCP/IP rules you met in Parts 6 to 8. The roads do not care what is inside the vans. A van might be carrying an email, a video call, a game move, a file or a web page.","The World Wide Web is one of the things that travel along those roads. It is like a huge collection of shops, libraries and noticeboards along the roadside, all with signposts pointing to each other. The Web is made of pages that are linked together, and you look at them using a program called a browser. Email is not the Web. Online games are not usually the Web. Most of the apps on a phone use the Internet without being the Web at all.","The timing makes this clear. As the Computer History Museum points out, the Internet's roots lie in the United States in the late 1960s, while the Web was invented about 20 years later by an Englishman working in Switzerland. By the time the Web appeared, the Internet already connected hundreds of thousands of computers. People were already sending email, posting on Usenet and copying files. What they did not have was an easy way to find, link and read information spread across all those machines.","So in this part we are not watching the Internet being born. We are watching someone build a brilliant new service on top of it, one so popular that, for many people, it became the face of the whole Internet."]},{"heading":"Dreams of Linked Words","paragraphs":["The idea of jumping from one piece of information to another is much older than the Web. In Part 2 you met Vannevar Bush, who in 1945 imagined the Memex, a desk where a reader could build 'trails' connecting one document to another on microfilm. It was never built, but it inspired generations of computer scientists.","In the 1960s an American thinker named Ted Nelson took the idea further. He coined the word 'hypertext', and he presented it at a computing conference in 1965. Hyper means 'beyond', so hypertext is text that goes beyond the page: words that link to other words, documents that connect to other documents. Nelson dreamed of a worldwide system called Project Xanadu, a vast library in which every document could be linked to every other, quotes would always link back to their original source, and authors would be paid tiny amounts whenever their work was read. Xanadu was extremely ambitious, and although Nelson worked on it for decades, it was never finished in the form he imagined.","Meanwhile, at Brown University in 1967, Andries van Dam and his colleagues built one of the first working hypertext systems, the Hypertext Editing System, which ran on an IBM computer. But the most famous early demonstration came from Douglas Engelbart and his team at the Stanford Research Institute, the same SRI that later received the first ARPANET message.","Picture the scene. It is 9 December 1968, at a computer conference in the Civic Auditorium in San Francisco. Somewhere between 1,000 and 2,000 people pack the hall (accounts differ). Engelbart sits at a special console, wearing a headset, while behind him a huge screen shows what his computer is doing. The computer itself is not even in the room. It is back at SRI, about 30 miles away, connected by borrowed telephone lines and microwave links. For about 90 minutes, Engelbart shows things nobody in the audience has seen before: a pointing device he calls a 'mouse', text that can be edited on screen, words that are links you can click to jump to other information, and colleagues back at SRI appearing on screen and working on the same document with him. At the end the crowd gives him a standing ovation. People later called it 'the Mother of All Demos'.","Almost twenty years later, hypertext reached ordinary computer owners. In August 1987 Apple released HyperCard, created by the programmer Bill Atkinson, and gave it away with every new Macintosh computer. HyperCard let people build 'stacks' of cards with pictures, text and buttons. Clicking a button could take you to another card. Teachers made lessons with it, children made games with it, and the famous adventure game Myst was first built using it. But HyperCard stacks lived on one computer. You could not click a button in your stack and jump to a card on a computer in another country.","So by the late 1980s there were two great inventions sitting side by side. Hypertext could link information beautifully, but only inside one machine or one system. The Internet could connect millions of machines, but had no easy way to link their information together. Someone needed to marry the two."]},{"heading":"A Lab Full of Lost Information","paragraphs":["That someone was working at CERN, the European Organization for Nuclear Research, just outside Geneva, with parts of the laboratory in Switzerland and parts in France. CERN was founded in 1954 so that European countries could do big physics experiments together that none of them could afford alone. Its scientists study the tiniest particles of matter by smashing them together in giant machines called particle accelerators. In 1989 CERN finished a new accelerator called LEP, which ran around a circular tunnel 27 kilometres long, deep underground.","Thousands of physicists, engineers and students from dozens of countries worked on experiments at CERN, and many of them stayed only for a few years. They brought every kind of computer you can imagine, each with its own operating system, its own way of storing files and its own programs. If you wanted to know how a piece of equipment worked, or who had written a certain program, you might have to log in to several different computers, learn several different systems, or simply wander the corridors asking people.","Tim Berners-Lee knew this problem well. He was born in London in 1955. His parents, Mary Lee Woods and Conway Berners-Lee, were mathematicians who had both worked on one of the earliest commercial computers, the Ferranti Mark 1. He studied physics at the University of Oxford, graduating in 1976, and while he was a student he built his own computer using parts that included an old television set.","In 1980 Berners-Lee spent about six months at CERN as a consultant writing software. To help himself keep track of all the people, programs and projects, he wrote a little program called Enquire. Its full name, 'Enquire Within Upon Everything', came from an old Victorian book of household advice he remembered from his childhood. In Enquire, every piece of information was a 'node', and nodes could be linked to each other, with labels describing how they were connected. Enquire was a hypertext system, but it ran on only one computer, and when he left CERN it was lost.","Berners-Lee returned to CERN in 1984 on a fellowship, this time to stay for years. The problem had not gone away. If anything, it was getting worse, because CERN was preparing for its biggest experiments yet. He realised that what CERN needed was not one central database that everyone had to fit into. CERN was too messy and too free for that. It needed a system that could link information wherever it already lived, on any computer, using the Internet that was already starting to connect CERN to the world."]},{"heading":"Vague but Exciting","paragraphs":["In March 1989 Berners-Lee wrote down his idea and handed it around CERN. The document had a plain, rather boring title: 'Information Management: A Proposal'. Inside, though, was something revolutionary.","He began by describing the problem. CERN was, he wrote, like a constantly changing web of people and projects, and information was always being lost. Because many people only stayed for about two years, newcomers wasted time working out who knew what, and details of old projects sometimes had to be rediscovered by detective work. He explained why tree-shaped systems, like folders inside folders or the topics of Usenet newsgroups, could not show all the different ways that things were connected. Real information, he argued, looked less like a tree and more like a web.","His answer was a system of 'nodes' and 'links'. A node could be anything: a document, a person, a piece of equipment, a project. Links would connect them. Crucially, the information would not need to be moved into one big new database. The system would be split into two parts: 'servers' that store information, and 'clients', programs on people's own computers that fetch and display it. That way, any kind of computer could join in. Special 'gateways' could even show existing information, such as phone directories and Usenet news, as hypertext, so the system would be useful from day one. At this stage he did not even have a proper name for it. The proposal mentions that it was being called 'Mesh'.","Berners-Lee gave the proposal to his boss, Mike Sendall. Sendall read it and wrote three words at the top of the first page: 'Vague but exciting...' It was not an official 'yes', and CERN did not create a big project for it. CERN was a physics lab, after all, not a software company. But Sendall quietly gave Berners-Lee room to keep working on the idea.","In September 1990 Sendall approved buying a new computer: a NeXT cube, a sleek black machine made by NeXT, the company Steve Jobs had started after leaving Apple. The NeXT had powerful tools that made it quicker to build programs with windows, menus and buttons. Sendall suggested that Berners-Lee could try out the NeXT by building his hypertext system on it. It was a clever way of saying 'go ahead' without needing permission from anyone higher up.","Berners-Lee also needed a name. According to his own account, he considered names such as 'Mine of Information', and 'The Information Mine', but rejected the second because its initials spelled TIM, which seemed a bit too much about himself. In the end he chose 'World Wide Web'. Some colleagues worried it was a mouthful, especially as 'WWW' takes longer to say out loud than the words themselves. But he liked the picture it created: a web of information spreading across the whole world."]},{"heading":"Robert Cailliau and the Small Team","paragraphs":["The Web was not a one-man show. Robert Cailliau was a Belgian engineer, born in 1947, who had worked at CERN since 1974. He had been thinking about hypertext himself, and when he heard about Berners-Lee's plans, he became the project's most enthusiastic supporter.","In November 1990 the two of them wrote a new, sharper version of the proposal called 'WorldWideWeb: Proposal for a HyperText Project'. It set out a plan to build the system in a few months with a small team. Cailliau was skilled at something Berners-Lee needed: persuading people, finding money, recruiting helpers and explaining the idea to managers who were busy with physics. He also later worked on a browser for Apple Macintosh computers, and in May 1994 he organised the very first international World Wide Web conference, held at CERN. The press nicknamed it the 'Woodstock of the Web', after a famous music festival.","Another key helper was Nicola Pellow, a mathematics student from England who came to CERN on a work placement. Berners-Lee's own browser ran only on the expensive NeXT computer, which very few people owned. So Pellow wrote a 'line-mode browser', a simple program that showed web pages as plain text on almost any computer terminal. Instead of clicking on links, you typed a number shown beside each link. It was not pretty, but it meant that almost anyone with access to an Internet-connected computer could use the Web straight away. In many ways it was just as important as the fancy NeXT version.","Other CERN colleagues joined in too. Jean-François Groff helped write code, and the team turned parts of the system into a shared library of programs that others could use to build their own browsers and servers. That decision, to make the pieces easy to reuse, helped the Web spread far beyond CERN.","This is a good moment to remember one of the rules of this story: do not let anyone tell you that Tim Berners-Lee invented the Internet. He did not, and he has always said so. He invented the World Wide Web, building on the Internet that hundreds of other people had created over the previous twenty years, and he built it with help from people like Cailliau and Pellow."]},{"heading":"Three Simple Building Blocks","paragraphs":["What made the Web work was not one giant invention but three simple ideas that fit together like the pieces of a plug and socket. You still use all three every single time you open a web page.","The first is HTML, which stands for HyperText Markup Language. It is the language web pages are written in. HTML uses short labels called tags to mark up text: this part is a heading, this part is a paragraph, this word is a link. Berners-Lee based it on a system called SGML that CERN already used for documents, so it looked familiar to people there. The very first version of HTML had only a small number of tags. A browser reads the HTML and decides how to show it on the screen, which is why the same page can appear on a giant monitor or a tiny phone.","The second is HTTP, the HyperText Transfer Protocol. Remember that a protocol is an agreed set of rules. HTTP describes the conversation between a browser and a web server. In its simplest early form it was almost like a polite note: the browser connects to the server and says, in effect, 'GET me this page, please', and the server sends the page back and hangs up. Because HTTP is so simple, it was easy for anyone to write a server or a browser that could talk to everyone else's.","The third is the URL, the Uniform Resource Locator, which is the address of a page. Berners-Lee first called it a Universal Document Identifier, and the general idea is now called a URI. A URL packs together everything a browser needs to know. Take the address of the first web page: http://info.cern.ch/hypertext/WWW/TheProject.html. The 'http' part says which protocol to use. 'info.cern.ch' is the name of the computer, which the Domain Name System from Part 8 turns into an IP address. The rest, '/hypertext/WWW/TheProject.html', says which file on that computer you want.","The genius was in what the system did not need. There was no central list of all pages and no need to ask permission to add a link. Anyone could put up a server, write a page and link to any other page on any other server in the world. A link could even point to a page that did not exist any more. Earlier hypertext dreamers, such as Ted Nelson, had wanted links that could never break. Berners-Lee accepted broken links as the price of letting the Web grow freely, without anyone in charge. That trade-off is the reason you sometimes see a 'page not found' message today, and it is also a big reason the Web grew so fast."]},{"heading":"The First Website Goes Live","paragraphs":["By late 1990 the pieces were working. On his NeXT computer, Berners-Lee had written the first web browser, which he called WorldWideWeb (later renamed Nexus to avoid confusion with the Web itself). It was not just a browser but also an editor: you could write and change pages and create links right there on screen, a bit like a modern word processor. The same NeXT machine also ran the first web server, at first called nxoc01.cern.ch and later known as info.cern.ch. By Christmas 1990 both the NeXT browser-editor and the line-mode browser could be demonstrated.","Because that one NeXT cube was the entire Web's first server, switching it off would have made the Web disappear. So a handwritten label was stuck on the computer, warning that the machine was a server and must not be powered down. That NeXT computer, label and all, has been kept as a treasured piece of history.","The first web page lived at info.cern.ch. It explained what the World Wide Web project was, how to get a browser, how to set up a server and who was working on it. In other words, the first website was about the Web itself, a sort of instruction manual and welcome sign rolled into one. You can still visit a copy of it today at info.cern.ch, and even try a simulator that shows how it looked in the old line-mode browser.","In March 1991 the line-mode browser was released to a limited group of people, and over the following months the Web was made available more widely at CERN. Then came the moment the outside world found out. On 6 August 1991 Berners-Lee posted a short summary of the WorldWideWeb project on alt.hypertext, a Usenet newsgroup for people interested in hypertext, and told readers where they could download the software for free. He posted to several other newsgroups around the same time. It was not a grand press launch. It was a message on a discussion board, the same kind of place you read about in Part 9.","Slowly, people began to try it. In December 1991 Paul Kunz, a physicist at the Stanford Linear Accelerator Center (SLAC) in California, set up the first web server outside Europe. SLAC used it to make its huge database of physics research papers searchable through the Web, which gave physicists a real reason to use it. In 1992 and early 1993, new graphical browsers appeared for different kinds of computers, with names like Erwise, ViolaWWW and Midas. a CERN browser for the Macintosh, MacWWW, built by Cailliau with help from Pellow, appeared around the end of 1992, and in February 1993 a team at the National Center for Supercomputing Applications in Illinois released a test version of a browser called Mosaic, which you will meet properly in Part 11."]},{"heading":"A Gift to the Whole World","paragraphs":["By early 1993 the Web was catching on, but there was a worry hanging over it. Who owned it? CERN had paid for its development. Could CERN one day start charging people to use the software, or demand fees from anyone who built a browser? Some other information systems of the time were starting to talk about licence fees, and companies were nervous about building on something that might suddenly cost money.","Berners-Lee and Cailliau pushed CERN's management to settle the question for good. On 30 April 1993 CERN signed a short document putting the World Wide Web software into the public domain. It declared that anyone could use, copy and change the Web's technology freely, with no fees payable to CERN. Later, CERN also released the software under an open licence to make sure it stayed free.","That decision may be one of the most generous gifts in the history of technology. Because nobody had to pay or ask permission, students, companies, schools, governments and hobbyists all over the world could build web servers and browsers and write pages. The Web did not belong to one company; it belonged to everyone who used it. Berners-Lee chose not to patent his invention or get rich from it.","In 1994 Berners-Lee left CERN for the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in the United States, where he founded the World Wide Web Consortium, or W3C, a group that brings together companies and experts to agree on open Web standards, so that pages work in every browser. He was later knighted, becoming Sir Tim Berners-Lee, and he received the Turing Award for 2016, often called the Nobel Prize of computing, the same honour Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn had received for 2004. In 2013 he shared the first Queen Elizabeth Prize for Engineering with Cerf, Kahn, Louis Pouzin and Marc Andreessen, a fitting reminder that the Internet and the Web were built by many hands.","Yet in 1993 the Web was still tiny. Even by the end of that year there were only a few hundred websites in the whole world, many of them at universities and physics labs. Most pages were plain grey screens of text and blue underlined links, with few or no pictures. To see them, many people still had to type commands into a line-mode browser or own an unusual computer. The Web had been woven, and it had been given away. Now it was waiting for a browser that would make it easy, colourful and fun enough for everyone."]}],"deepDive":"Why did the Web succeed when earlier, more sophisticated hypertext systems did not? Historians and computer scientists usually point to a set of deliberate simplifications. Ted Nelson's Xanadu called for two-way links that could never break, version tracking of every document and built-in micropayments for authors. Those features were powerful but required tight central coordination. Berners-Lee chose one-way links stored inside the page that makes them, which meant nobody had to keep a master index or ask permission to link. The cost was 'link rot': links that lead nowhere when a page moves or vanishes, the familiar 404 error. Academic hypertext researchers at the 1991 Hypertext conference were reportedly unimpressed by how simple the system was, yet that simplicity let it scale. A second reason is that the Web reused what already existed. It ran on TCP/IP, used the Domain Name System for computer names, borrowed its markup style from SGML, and included gateways to older systems such as FTP and Usenet, so early users found useful material immediately. The client-server split meant browsers could be written for any machine, and Nicola Pellow's line-mode browser made the Web reachable from cheap terminals long before graphical browsers spread. A third reason is licensing. In 1993 the University of Minnesota signalled that it might charge some users of its Gopher server software, and around the same time CERN declared the Web technology free; many historians see this contrast as one factor in the Web's rapid rise, though the arrival of the graphical Mosaic browser mattered too. There are also debates about credit. Berners-Lee is rightly called the inventor of the Web, but his own accounts stress the roles of Cailliau, Pellow, Groff and others, and the long line of hypertext pioneers from Bush and Nelson to Engelbart and van Dam. Finally, the original proposal shows something often forgotten: the Web was designed as a read-write medium. The first browser was also an editor, and Berners-Lee has said he always hoped people would create and edit pages as easily as they read them, an idea that later returned with blogs and wikis.","funFact":"The very first web browser, called WorldWideWeb, was also a web page editor, so from the very beginning the Web was meant for writing pages as well as reading them.","check":{"q":"What did CERN do with the World Wide Web software on 30 April 1993?","a":"It put the Web software into the public domain so anyone could use it for free","wrong":["It sold the Web to Apple","It patented the Web and charged a fee for every page","It shut the Web down because it was too slow"]},"sources":[{"title":"Information Management: A Proposal (Tim Berners-Lee, 1989)","publisher":"W3C","url":"https://www.w3.org/History/1989/proposal.html"},{"title":"A Little History of the World Wide Web","publisher":"W3C","url":"https://www.w3.org/History.html"},{"title":"Tim Berners-Lee biography","publisher":"W3C","url":"https://www.w3.org/People/Berners-Lee/"},{"title":"http://info.cern.ch - home of the first website","publisher":"CERN","url":"https://info.cern.ch/"},{"title":"What's the Difference Between the Internet and the Web?","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/revolution/networking/19/314"},{"title":"The Demo","publisher":"Doug Engelbart Institute","url":"https://www.dougengelbart.org/content/view/209/"},{"title":"Internet History of 1990s","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/internethistory/1990s/"},{"title":"History of the World Wide Web","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_World_Wide_Web"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/10-1.jpg","alt":"The NeXTcube at CERN that became the first web server, with its \"Do not power it down!!\" sticker.","file":"First Web Server.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_Web_Server.jpg","author":"User:Coolcaesar at en.wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/10-2.jpg","alt":"Sir Tim Berners-Lee, inventor of the World Wide Web, at London's Guildhall in 2014.","file":"Sir Tim Berners-Lee.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Tim_Berners-Lee.jpg","author":"Paul Clarke","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/10-3.jpg","alt":"Douglas Engelbart's wooden 1960s mouse design, with one red button and a cable, on display at the Computer History Museum.","file":"Douglas Engelbart's prototype mouse, angled - Computer History Museum.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Douglas_Engelbart%27s_prototype_mouse,_angled_-_Computer_History_Museum.jpg","author":"Michael Hicks from Saint Paul, MN, USA","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":3811},{"n":11,"title":"Browsers and the Web Explosion","when":"1993 – 2000s","summary":"In 1993 two young programmers made a browser called Mosaic that showed pictures right next to words, and suddenly the Web looked fun. Big companies started fighting over who would make the browser everyone used. Within a few years, millions of people were clicking, scrolling and building their own web pages.","sections":[{"heading":"A Window With Pictures In It","paragraphs":["Picture a chilly December in Urbana, a small university town surrounded by the flat cornfields of Illinois. It is the end of 1992. Most students have gone home for the holidays, but in the offices of the National Center for Supercomputing Applications, usually called NCSA, a 21-year-old undergraduate named Marc Andreessen is tapping away at a keyboard. He works part-time at the centre for a few dollars an hour, and he has become fascinated by the World Wide Web that Tim Berners-Lee released from CERN.","The trouble was that the early Web was not much to look at. Berners-Lee's own browser ran only on NeXT computers, which were expensive and rare. CERN did not have the money to write versions for every kind of machine, so the Web team asked volunteers to help, and a handful of programmers around the world wrote browsers for Unix, Macintosh and PC computers. Two of them, called Viola and Midas, were the favourites. But most of these programs were fiddly to install, crashed easily, and showed pictures only in separate pop-up windows, if they showed them at all. A typical web page was a grey screen of plain text with some underlined links.","Over his Christmas break Andreessen roughed out a demonstration of a better browser. He then teamed up with Eric Bina, a full-time NCSA programmer who was an expert on Unix systems and a careful, quiet coder. Andreessen was the energetic one who talked to users, read their complaints and pushed out updates. Bina did much of the deep technical work that made the program solid. Together they built Mosaic, using the free code library that CERN had shared with the world.","The first versions of Mosaic appeared early in 1993, and version 1.0 for Unix computers was released in the spring. What made it special can be said in one sentence: Mosaic put pictures right inside the page, next to the words, the way a magazine does. To make that possible, Andreessen suggested a new piece of HTML, the img tag, which tells a browser 'put an image here'. Suddenly a web page could have a photo of a scientist beside her research, a map beside a travel guide, or a logo at the top of a company's page.","Mosaic was also easy. You did not need to be a computer expert to install it; you could download it, click, and start exploring. It had a Back button, a list of favourite pages and a 'What's New' page, kept up to date by Andreessen and Bina, that pointed visitors to the latest interesting sites. NCSA's founding director, Larry Smarr, and Joseph Hardin, head of its software group, put the centre's full support behind the project and assigned teams to make versions for Macintosh and Windows computers, plus web server software. By the end of 1993 people could run Mosaic on almost any common computer.","The result was like opening a window in a stuffy room. According to NCSA, by December 1993 more than 5,000 copies of Mosaic were being downloaded every month, and the centre was swamped with messages from enthusiastic users. That same month the New York Times put Mosaic on the front of its business section and suggested it might be the 'killer app' of networked computers, meaning the program so useful that it makes people want the whole technology. The Computer History Museum describes Mosaic as the first browser backed by a major institution, which helped move the Web from a research project toward mass success."]},{"heading":"Senator Gore and the Famous Joke","paragraphs":["Where did the money for a place like NCSA come from? Supercomputer centres are expensive. NCSA was one of several centres paid for by the US National Science Foundation, and in the early 1990s their funding received an important boost from a law called the High Performance Computing Act of 1991. Its main sponsor in the Senate was a senator from Tennessee named Al Gore, and it was signed by President George H. W. Bush in December 1991. People often nicknamed it the 'Gore Bill'.","Gore had been interested in computer networks since the 1980s. The Internet Society's history of the Internet notes that a 1988 report by the National Research Council, about the future of research networks, was influential on then-Senator Gore. He liked to talk about 'information superhighways': fast national networks that could link universities, libraries, schools and businesses, the way highways had linked cities. The 1991 law paid for research into faster computers and for a National Research and Education Network, and it helped support projects at centres like NCSA. Mosaic is often given as one of the best-known examples of work that this kind of government research funding made possible.","Now for the joke. In March 1999, when Gore was Vice President and getting ready to run for President, he gave a television interview on CNN. Talking about his record, he said that during his time in Congress he 'took the initiative in creating the Internet'. That sentence was clumsy, because of course no single politician created the Internet. Opponents and comedians quickly twisted it into a much sillier claim: that Gore had said 'I invented the Internet'. That exact phrase became one of the most repeated jokes of the 2000 election.","Here is the fair version. Gore never said he invented the Internet; those were not his words. He did not design packets, write TCP/IP or invent the Web, and he never claimed to. What he did do, over many years in Congress, was understand earlier than most politicians how important computer networks could be, and push for laws that paid for faster networks and research. Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn, the co-designers of TCP/IP whom you met in Part 6, later wrote that Gore deserved real credit for championing the Internet as a lawmaker. So the true lesson of the story is a good one to remember: the Internet was built by engineers, students, scientists and also by the people who decided to fund them. As always in this story, there is no single inventor."]},{"heading":"The First Browser Wars","paragraphs":["Mosaic belonged to the University of Illinois, and its student programmers were paid student wages. When Andreessen graduated at the end of 1993, he moved to California's Silicon Valley. There he met Jim Clark, a wealthy computer scientist who had founded Silicon Graphics, a company famous for powerful graphics computers used to make special effects in films. Clark had a plan: start a company to build a 'Mosaic killer', a browser even better than the original. In 1994 the two founded a company, and they hired Eric Bina and much of the rest of the Mosaic team.","At first the company was called Mosaic Communications. The University of Illinois objected, since Mosaic was its name, and as part of settling the dispute the company changed its name to Netscape Communications. Its browser, Netscape Navigator, was a complete rewrite rather than a copy of Mosaic, and it was faster and more reliable. Netscape announced it in October 1994, and version 1.0 was released in December 1994. Netscape's clever business plan was to let schools, universities and non-profit groups use Navigator free, while selling boxed copies and server software to companies. Within months, most people browsing the Web were using Navigator.","On 9 August 1995 Netscape sold shares on the stock market for the first time. The company had existed for less than two years and was not yet making a profit, but investors were so excited about the Web that the price of its shares roughly doubled on the first day, and the company was suddenly valued at a few billion dollars. Many historians treat that day as the starting gun of the 'dot-com boom', the rush of money into Internet companies that Part 15 will explore.","One company was watching very carefully. Microsoft made Windows, the software that ran most of the world's personal computers, and in 1995 it had been planning its own private online service, MSN. In May 1995 Bill Gates, Microsoft's co-founder, sent a famous memo to his managers called 'The Internet Tidal Wave', saying that he gave the Internet 'the highest level of importance'. Microsoft licensed a version of Mosaic from a company called Spyglass, which had bought the rights to the Mosaic name and technology from the University of Illinois, and turned it into Internet Explorer. Version 1.0 came out in August 1995, around the launch of Windows 95.","Then the battle began. Netscape and Microsoft released new versions at a furious pace, each adding new features, some of which only worked in their own browser. Website makers began to write little notes like 'Best viewed in Netscape Navigator' at the bottom of their pages. Netscape invented a blink tag that made text flash on and off, and Internet Explorer had a marquee tag that made text scroll across the screen like a sign at a train station. Both are now remembered fondly as symbols of the colourful, messy 1990s Web, though neither is used much today.","Microsoft's most powerful weapon was not a feature at all. Starting with later versions of Windows 95, Internet Explorer came free and already installed on new computers, so many people never bothered to download anything else. By around 1998 or 1999 Internet Explorer had overtaken Navigator, and in the early 2000s it was used by the great majority of web surfers. The US government and many states sued Microsoft in 1998, arguing that it had unfairly used its control of Windows to crush competitors; in 2000 a judge ruled against Microsoft, and after appeals the case ended in a settlement in 2001 and 2002. By then Netscape had been bought by the online service AOL, and it never regained its lead. This contest is now known as the first 'browser wars'."]},{"heading":"Ten Days in May: JavaScript and CSS","paragraphs":["Early web pages were a bit like posters: you could read them and click links, but they could not do anything. If you filled in a form and made a mistake, the page had to be sent all the way to a server and back before anyone could tell you. Netscape wanted pages that could react instantly, inside the browser itself.","In April 1995 Netscape hired a programmer named Brendan Eich. He had been promised that he could put a programming language inside the browser, and he hoped to base it on an elegant language called Scheme. But Netscape was also working with the company Sun Microsystems, which had just created a language called Java, and Netscape's managers insisted that the new browser language should 'look like Java'. Eich later explained that he mixed ideas together: Java-like spelling and punctuation on the outside, plus clever ideas borrowed from the languages Scheme and Self on the inside.","Eich built the first working version in May 1995 in an astonishingly short time, about ten days. It was first nicknamed Mocha, then renamed LiveScript, and finally, in December 1995, it was renamed JavaScript, partly to ride on the excitement around Java. This name confuses people to this day, because JavaScript and Java are two quite different languages; programmers like to say they are related about as much as 'car' and 'carpet'. JavaScript first shipped in Netscape Navigator 2.0, and Microsoft soon built its own version into Internet Explorer.","Because two companies were now writing their own versions, they needed shared rules so that the same code would work in every browser. In 1997 an organisation called Ecma International published a standard version of the language, officially named ECMAScript. JavaScript is still updated almost every year, and today it runs on nearly every website you visit. When a page shows a pop-up warning that your password is too short, slides photos past in a carousel or lets you play a game without loading a new page, JavaScript is usually doing the work.","At almost the same time, another problem was being solved: style. HTML was designed to describe what each part of a page is, such as 'this is a heading' or 'this is a list'. But website makers wanted to control how it looked: colours, fonts, spacing and layout. In the browser wars, companies kept adding new HTML tags for decoration, such as a font tag for choosing letter styles, which made pages messy and hard to change.","In October 1994 a Norwegian programmer working with Tim Berners-Lee at CERN, Hakon Wium Lie, proposed Cascading Style Sheets, or CSS. He soon teamed up with a Dutch engineer, Bert Bos. The idea was to keep the content of a page (in HTML) separate from its looks (in CSS). A style sheet could say 'every heading on this website is dark blue and large' in a single line, and if you wanted to change all the headings to green, you would change just that one line instead of hundreds of pages. The word 'cascading' means that several style rules can flow down and combine, with more specific rules winning over general ones.","The first official CSS recommendation was published at the end of 1996, though it took browsers years to support it properly. Today the three languages work together like a team. HTML is the skeleton and the words, CSS is the clothing and decoration, and JavaScript is the muscles that make things move and respond. Every page of a modern site, including the one you might be reading this on, is built from these three."]},{"heading":"A Club to Keep the Web Open","paragraphs":["The browser wars had a dangerous side. If each company kept inventing its own features that only worked in its own browser, the Web could split into separate 'Netscape webs' and 'Microsoft webs', and a page made for one might break in the other. Tim Berners-Lee had always wanted the Web to be one shared space that anybody could use with any computer.","So in October 1994 he founded the World Wide Web Consortium, known as W3C, at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in Cambridge, Massachusetts, working closely with CERN, where the Web had been born. Berners-Lee became its director, and he wanted the consortium to be international from the start: in April 1995 the French research institute INRIA became its European host, and in 1996 Keio University in Japan became its host in Asia.","A consortium is a group of organisations that agree to work together. W3C's members include browser makers, universities, technology companies, governments and charities. They meet, argue politely, test ideas and publish 'recommendations': detailed descriptions of how HTML, CSS and many other web technologies should work. Like the RFCs of the early Internet that you met in Part 4, these documents are open and free for anyone to read and use. W3C also works on accessibility guidelines that help make websites usable for people who are blind, deaf or have other disabilities, for example by giving every picture a written description that a screen reader can speak aloud.","W3C cannot force any company to obey it. Its power comes from agreement: if all the big browser makers follow the same standard, then web designers can write a page once and trust it to work everywhere. Today the Web's standards are shaped by W3C together with other groups, including one called WHATWG, formed in 2004 by browser makers, which now looks after the main HTML standard. It is a reminder of the big lesson of this whole story: the Internet and the Web keep working because rivals agree to share the same open rules."]},{"heading":"What Really Happens When You Click","paragraphs":["Let us slow down time and watch a browser at work. Imagine you type the address https://www.example.org/dinosaurs.html and press Enter. It feels instant, but in that blink a whole series of steps happens, using many of the ideas from earlier parts.","Step one: the browser reads the address, or URL. The part before the colon, https, says which protocol to use. The middle part, www.example.org, is the name of the computer to contact. The last part, /dinosaurs.html, is the particular page on that computer.","Step two: the browser needs a number, not a name. As you learned in Part 8, it asks the Domain Name System to turn www.example.org into an IP address. If your computer or your home router looked up that name recently, the answer may already be remembered, or 'cached', and arrive in under a millisecond. Otherwise DNS servers may need to be asked, which usually takes a few tens of milliseconds.","Step three: the browser opens a connection to that IP address using TCP, the protocol from Part 7 that makes sure packets arrive in order. Because the address begins with https, it also performs a quick secret handshake to set up encryption, so nobody in between can read what is sent. Part 17 explains how that padlock works.","Step four: the browser sends an HTTP request. It is a short message in plain words, something like 'GET /dinosaurs.html', plus extra lines saying which browser you are using and which languages you can read. Step five: the server replies. The reply starts with a status code; 200 means 'OK, here it is', while the famous 404 means 'Not Found'. Then comes the HTML of the page.","Step six is where the real work begins. The browser reads the HTML from top to bottom and builds a tree-shaped model of the page in its memory, with headings, paragraphs and pictures as branches. As it reads, it discovers more things it needs: a CSS style sheet, a few JavaScript files, a dozen images, maybe a font. For each one it sends another HTTP request, often several at once. A modern news site can ask for well over a hundred separate files before it is complete.","Step seven: the browser works out the layout, deciding exactly where every box, word and picture goes for your screen size, and then 'paints' the pixels. JavaScript may run and change the page while you look at it. On a fast connection the whole sequence can take well under a second. Every time you click a link, much of it happens again, and all of it rests on the layers of the Internet that took decades to build."]},{"heading":"Foxes, Chrome and Web Apps","paragraphs":["Netscape did not simply vanish. In 1998, losing badly to Internet Explorer, the company did something bold: it released the source code of its browser to the public, so that volunteers anywhere could read it and improve it. This became the Mozilla project, named after Netscape's old dinosaur-like mascot. In 2003 a non-profit organisation, the Mozilla Foundation, was set up to run it.","In November 2004 Mozilla released Firefox 1.0, a fast, lightweight browser with tabs, a pop-up blocker and add-ons that let users customise it. People who were fed up with pop-up adverts and security problems loved it. According to Mozilla, Firefox was downloaded more than 100 million times in less than a year. Fans even raised money to pay for a big advert in the New York Times listing the names of thousands of supporters. Apple had launched its own browser, Safari, in 2003, and competition was back.","At the same time, websites were turning into programs. In April 2004 Google launched Gmail, an email service that worked inside a browser and felt almost like software on your own computer. In 2005 Google Maps let you drag a map around smoothly with your mouse, fetching new map pieces in the background without reloading the page. A designer named Jesse James Garrett gave this style of website a name in 2005: Ajax, short for a group of techniques built around JavaScript. Soon people were writing documents, editing photos and playing games inside web pages. These are called web apps.","Web apps needed faster browsers, and in September 2008 Google released its own browser, Chrome. Its makers explained their thinking in a comic book drawn by the artist Scott McCloud. Chrome ran each tab as a separate process, so if one website crashed, the others kept going, and it had a very fast new JavaScript engine called V8. Google also released most of its code as an open-source project called Chromium. Chrome grew quickly and became the world's most used browser in the early 2010s, while Internet Explorer slowly faded; Microsoft finally retired it in 2022 in favour of its newer browser, Edge, which is itself built on Chromium code.","Meanwhile, the standards kept growing. A new version of HTML, called HTML5, was finished by W3C in 2014. It let browsers play video and audio and draw graphics without the extra plug-in programs, such as Adobe Flash, that people once had to install. Today a browser is really a small operating system: it can run games, video calls, maps and office software, all from a web address.","And the number of websites exploded. A researcher at MIT named Matthew Gray kept count in the early days by sending out a program to explore the Web. He found about 130 websites in June 1993, roughly 600 by the end of that year, about 10,000 by the end of 1994 and about 23,500 by the middle of 1995. Within a few more years there were millions. Shops, schools, museums, bands, governments and kids with their first homepage all joined in. The Web had become an enormous library with no librarian and no catalogue. With so many pages appearing every day, a new question became urgent: how on Earth would anyone find anything?"]}],"deepDive":"Why did a browser matter so much, when the Web already existed? Historians of technology point to a pattern: an infrastructure becomes popular when the 'last mile' to the ordinary user becomes easy. Before Mosaic, the Web was technically complete, with HTTP, HTML and URLs all working, but the experience was poor. Mosaic's inline images, simple installation and versions for Unix, Macintosh and Windows lowered the barrier enough for non-experts, and the number of websites jumped from roughly a hundred to tens of thousands in about two years. The browser wars that followed raise a debate economists still have about 'network effects' and bundling. Netscape gave its browser free to individuals and earned money from businesses, showing that software built on open standards could be profitable. Microsoft responded by bundling Internet Explorer with Windows, a strategy that a US federal judge found in 2000 to be part of an illegal effort to protect Microsoft's monopoly; an appeals court in 2001 upheld much of that finding while overturning the order to break the company up, and the case was settled. Some argue that the competition sped up innovation; others say Explorer's dominance in the early 2000s let the Web stagnate, since Microsoft had little reason to improve a browser that already had most of the market. A second debate concerns standards. Browser makers have often moved faster than standards bodies, inventing features first and standardising later, which is how JavaScript became ECMAScript and why WHATWG was formed by browser makers in 2004 when they felt W3C's plans for XHTML did not match what developers needed. Today a different concern is common among web experts: most major browsers are now built on just a few rendering engines, mainly Chromium's Blink, Apple's WebKit and Mozilla's Gecko, and Blink-based browsers hold a large majority of users. Critics worry this gives one company great influence over how the Web evolves, echoing the browser wars of the 1990s. Supporters reply that the engines are open source and that W3C and WHATWG standards still bind them. Finally, there is the human story behind the speed of change: JavaScript, written in about ten days, now runs on billions of devices, a striking example of how an early, rushed design decision can become permanent once millions of pages depend on it.","funFact":"Brendan Eich wrote the first version of JavaScript in about ten days in May 1995, and despite its name it is a completely different language from Java.","check":{"q":"What was the special feature that made the Mosaic browser so popular in 1993?","a":"It showed pictures inside web pages, right next to the text, and was easy to install","wrong":["It was the first program ever to connect two computers","It let people make video calls to each other","It was the first browser to use JavaScript"]},"sources":[{"title":"NCSA Mosaic","publisher":"National Center for Supercomputing Applications, University of Illinois","url":"https://www.ncsa.illinois.edu/research/project-highlights/ncsa-mosaic/"},{"title":"Browsers: Windows on the Web","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/revolution/the-web/20/388"},{"title":"Browser Wars","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/revolution/the-web/20/389"},{"title":"History of W3C","publisher":"World Wide Web Consortium (W3C)","url":"https://www.w3.org/about/history/"},{"title":"Mozilla's History","publisher":"Mozilla","url":"https://www.mozilla.org/en-US/about/history/"},{"title":"Popularity","publisher":"Brendan Eich (personal blog)","url":"https://brendaneich.com/2008/04/popularity/"},{"title":"Brief History of the Internet","publisher":"Internet Society","url":"https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/"},{"title":"Networking & The Web timeline","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/timeline/networking-the-web/"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/11-1.jpg","alt":"The NCSA Mosaic web browser showing a page with links and pictures mixed right into the text.","file":"NCSA Mosaic Browser Screenshot.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NCSA_Mosaic_Browser_Screenshot.png","author":"Charles Severance","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/11-2.jpg","alt":"Brendan Eich, who created the JavaScript programming language at Netscape in 1995.","file":"Brendan Eich Mozilla Foundation official photo.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brendan_Eich_Mozilla_Foundation_official_photo.jpg","author":"Darcy Padilla","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/11-3.jpg","alt":"A boxed copy of Netscape Navigator on display at the Computer History Museum.","file":"CHM Artifacts Boxed Netscape Navigator (2273126105).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHM_Artifacts_Boxed_Netscape_Navigator_(2273126105).jpg","author":"Marcin Wichary from San Francisco, Calif.","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":3937},{"n":12,"title":"Searching the Haystack","when":"1990 – today","summary":"As the Internet filled up with millions of files and pages, finding anything became like looking for a needle in a haystack. Clever students built search tools with funny names like Archie, Gopher and Yahoo!, and then two Stanford students invented a smart way to rank pages by their links. Their project became Google, but the first search tools came years before it.","sections":[{"heading":"A Library With No Catalogue","paragraphs":["Imagine walking into the biggest library in the world. There are millions of books, but they are not arranged on shelves by subject. They are piled up in thousands of separate rooms, each room run by a different person, and new rooms are being added every day. There is no catalogue, no librarian and no map. That is roughly what the Internet looked like to its users around 1990.","Back then, much of the useful material on the Internet was stored on computers called FTP servers. FTP, the File Transfer Protocol, was an old and reliable way of copying files from one computer to another. Universities and companies set up 'anonymous FTP' sites where anyone could log in and download free programs, documents and data. The problem was that you had to know which computer had the file you wanted and in which folder. People shared lists by email and newsgroups, and many files were simply lost in the crowd.","At McGill University in Montreal, Canada, a graduate student from Barbados named Alan Emtage had a job helping students and staff find software. He got tired of hunting through FTP sites by hand, so he wrote a program to do it for him. His program regularly visited a list of anonymous FTP sites, collected the names of every file stored there and put them all into one searchable list. Instead of asking around, you could type part of a file name and get back a list of where to download it.","The tool was called Archie, from the word 'archive' with the letter v removed, partly because short names suited the computer systems of the time. It was publicly released on 10 September 1990, and colleagues including Bill Heelan and Peter Deutsch helped develop and spread it. Archie is widely regarded as the first Internet search engine. It was simple by today's standards: it searched file names, not the words inside documents, and you had to type rather exact commands. But the central idea was brand new and powerful. A program could automatically go out across the network, gather information about what was out there and build an index that people could search.","Emtage did not patent Archie or make a fortune from it, and for years few people knew his name. Today he is often honoured as one of the pioneers of search, and in 2017 he was inducted into the Internet Hall of Fame. Every search engine you use still follows the basic pattern that Archie showed: gather, index, answer."]},{"heading":"Gophers, Veronica and Jughead","paragraphs":["In 1991, the same year Tim Berners-Lee announced the World Wide Web, a small team at the University of Minnesota released a different system for finding information. It was led by Mark McCahill and included programmers such as Farhad Anklesaria and Paul Lindner. They called it Gopher. The name was a joke with two meanings: the University of Minnesota's sports teams are the Golden Gophers, and a 'gofer' is someone who goes and fetches things for you.","Gopher worked like a giant set of menus. You started at a main menu with numbered choices, such as 'Campus news', 'Library catalogue' or 'Weather'. Choosing one opened another menu, and then another, like going through folders inside folders on a computer, until you reached a document. Crucially, a menu item could point to a different Gopher server anywhere in the world, so all the Gopher servers together formed one huge linked 'Gopherspace'.","For a while Gopher was more popular than the Web. It was simple, fast on slow modems, and easy for universities to set up. Search tools soon appeared for it, with names chosen to continue the Archie comic-book joke. Veronica, created in 1992 at the University of Nevada, Reno, searched the titles of menus on Gopher servers around the world. Jughead searched within a single Gopher site. (Archie, Veronica and Jughead are all characters in the long-running Archie comics.)","So why are you using the Web and not Gopher? Historians point to two big reasons. First, the Web was more flexible: a web page could mix text, links and later pictures in any arrangement, while Gopher was stuck with rigid menus. Second, in 1993 the University of Minnesota announced that it would charge licence fees to some users of its Gopher server software, especially businesses. In April of that same year, CERN put the Web's software into the public domain for anyone to use free. The Computer History Museum describes this moment as Gopher stumbling. Many developers decided the free, open Web was the safer bet, and Gopher faded. A few enthusiasts still run Gopher servers today, as a kind of living museum of the early Internet."]},{"heading":"Jerry and David's Guide","paragraphs":["Even before automatic search engines for the Web became good, people tried another approach: making lists by hand. In 1991 the Web team at CERN started the WWW Virtual Library, a catalogue of interesting sites arranged by subject and looked after by volunteer experts. It was a bit like the subject cards in an old library drawer.","In early 1994 two electrical engineering graduate students at Stanford University in California, Jerry Yang and David Filo, started their own list of favourite websites, partly to avoid working on their theses. They worked from a trailer on campus that served as their office. At first their list was called 'Jerry and David's Guide to the World Wide Web'. As it grew, they sorted sites into categories, such as Science, Entertainment and Sports, and then into smaller sub-categories within those, like branches on a tree.","Within a few months they renamed it Yahoo!. The founders said they liked the word because in Jonathan Swift's novel Gulliver's Travels, a 'yahoo' is a rude, uncivilised creature, and they jokingly thought of themselves that way. They also offered a playful explanation of the letters: 'Yet Another Hierarchical Officious Oracle', though this was added after the name was chosen. The exclamation mark was part of the brand.","Yahoo! became wildly popular because it solved a real problem in a friendly way. If you wanted websites about volcanoes, you could click Science, then Earth Sciences, then Geology, then Volcanoes, and find a tidy list checked by a human. Soon tens of thousands of people a day were visiting. Yang and Filo founded a company in 1995 and hired staff, nicknamed 'surfers', whose job was to look at websites and decide which category they belonged in. Yahoo! shares went on sale to the public in 1996, and it became one of the most famous companies of the dot-com era, adding news, email, games and more to become a 'portal', a starting page for everything online.","But human-made directories had a weakness. People can only look at so many sites, and by the late 1990s new pages were appearing far faster than any team could review them. A directory was like a well-organised bookshop; what the Web now needed was something that could read every book in the library."]},{"heading":"Robots That Read Everything","paragraphs":["The answer was to send out robots. Not metal robots with arms, but programs that travel from website to website, reading pages and following links, the way a spider moves along the threads of its web. That is why these programs are called crawlers or spiders.","In 1994 a student at the University of Washington in Seattle, Brian Pinkerton, launched WebCrawler. It was one of the first search engines to record every word on the pages it visited, not just their titles. That meant you could search for a word buried deep inside a page and still find it. The same year, a researcher at Carnegie Mellon University in Pittsburgh, Michael Mauldin, started Lycos, named after a family of wolf spiders that chase their prey instead of waiting in a web. Other engines with lively names soon followed: Excite, Infoseek, HotBot, and in 1996 Ask Jeeves, which invited people to type questions in ordinary English to an imaginary butler.","The giant of the mid-1990s was AltaVista, launched in December 1995 by researchers at Digital Equipment Corporation, or DEC, a big computer company. DEC wanted to show off how powerful its new Alpha computers were, and indexing the entire Web was a spectacular demonstration. AltaVista was fast, it indexed many millions of pages, and it let people use clever tricks such as putting words in quotation marks to search for an exact phrase. It quickly became one of the most visited sites on the Web. The Computer History Museum calls it the most popular web search engine before Google.","But these engines had a growing problem. They mostly ranked pages by looking at the words on them: if you searched for 'volcano', a page that said 'volcano' fifty times might come out on top. Website owners soon learned to cheat by stuffing pages with popular words, sometimes hidden in white text on a white background where readers could not see them. Search results became cluttered with junk. The engines could find millions of pages, but they were not good at telling which ones were actually useful. Something smarter was needed."]},{"heading":"From BackRub to Google","paragraphs":["In 1995 Larry Page, a young computer scientist from Michigan, was thinking of studying at Stanford. Sergey Brin, a Stanford student who had moved to the United States from Moscow as a child, was given the job of showing him around. According to Google's own telling of the story, the two disagreed about almost everything on that first day. But Page joined Stanford, and they became friends and research partners.","Page was interested in the links between web pages. On the Web, links only point one way: a page knows what it links to, but not which pages link to it. Page wondered what would happen if you collected all the links and turned them around. In 1996 he and Brin built a research search engine to explore this idea. They called it BackRub, because it studied 'back links', the links pointing back to a page. It ran on Stanford's network and was supported by the university's Digital Library Project. Their computers were cheap, assorted machines, and one early set of hard drives was famously housed in a case built from toy construction bricks.","Their big idea was PageRank. Think of the Web as a giant popularity contest where each link from one page to another is like a vote. A page with many incoming links is probably useful. But not all votes are equal: a link from an important page, one that has lots of good links pointing to it, counts for more than a link from an unknown page. This idea of judging pages by who links to them was similar to the way scientists judge research papers by how often other scientists cite them. Combined with the words on the page, it produced results that felt startlingly good. The name PageRank was also a small pun, since it ranked web pages and was named after Larry Page.","They renamed their engine Google, a play on the word 'googol', which is the number 1 followed by 100 zeros. The huge number suited their goal, which Google later described as organising the world's information and making it universally accessible and useful. For a while the search engine lived at a Stanford web address and was used mostly by students and teachers. It became so popular that it used a large share of the university's network capacity.","In August 1998 Andy Bechtolsheim, a co-founder of the computer company Sun Microsystems, saw a demonstration and wrote them a cheque for 100,000 dollars made out to 'Google Inc.', a company that did not yet legally exist. Page and Brin quickly set one up, and Google Inc. was officially founded in September 1998. Its first office was a rented garage and rooms in a house in Menlo Park, California, owned by Susan Wojcicki, who later became one of Google's most important leaders and ran YouTube.","Google's homepage was famously plain: a logo, a search box and two buttons, at a time when Yahoo! and other portals were crowded with news, adverts and links. Results appeared quickly and were usually relevant. People told their friends, and Google grew mostly by word of mouth. In 2000 it began showing small text adverts linked to the words people searched for, an idea it borrowed and improved from another company, GoTo.com, and these ads became its main source of money. Google was not the first search engine, not even close, but within a few years it became the most used one in much of the world, and 'to google' became a verb in dictionaries."]},{"heading":"Inside the Machine: Crawl, Index, Rank","paragraphs":["How does a modern search engine find an answer among billions of pages in less than a second? The secret is that it does almost all the hard work before you ever type anything. Google describes its search in three stages: crawling, indexing and serving results.","Stage one is crawling. There is no master list of all web pages, so crawler programs, such as Google's crawler, called Googlebot, find pages by following links from pages they already know and by reading lists of pages called sitemaps that website owners provide. A crawler downloads each page, notes its links and adds new ones to a to-do list. It also tries to be polite, slowing down so it does not overload a website. Site owners can put a small file called robots.txt on their site to ask crawlers not to visit certain parts of it.","Stage two is indexing. Think of the index at the back of a big book: you look up 'volcano' and it tells you every page number where that word appears. A search engine's index works the same way, but for the whole Web. For each word it stores a list of every page that contains it, along with extra details, such as whether the word is in a heading, what language the page is in and which other pages link to it. If many pages have nearly the same content, the engine groups them and picks one main version. Building this index takes enormous numbers of computers working all the time, in data centres around the world.","Stage three is ranking and serving. When you type 'how do volcanoes erupt', the engine does not search the Web at that moment. It looks in its prepared index, finds pages containing those words or words with similar meanings, and then sorts them. Google says it uses hundreds of factors to decide the order, including how well the page matches your words, how trustworthy and useful the page seems, how links point to it, your language, your rough location and whether you are on a phone. All of this happens in a fraction of a second.","Search engines are now far more complicated than the first PageRank. They try to understand meaning, not just matching words, so that a search for 'big cat with stripes' can find pages about tigers. They show maps, pictures, dictionary definitions and, more recently, summaries written by artificial intelligence. And it is a big business: Google has repeatedly said it does not take payment to rank a page higher in its ordinary results, but it does sell adverts, which are labelled as sponsored and usually appear at the top."]},{"heading":"Winners, Losers and Searchers Around the World","paragraphs":["The story of search is full of surprises about who wins. In the late 1990s, people thought AltaVista and Yahoo! were unbeatable. Yet for about a decade, as the Computer History Museum points out, web search mostly lost money: engines such as Lycos, Ask Jeeves, AltaVista and early Google earned some income from banner adverts but struggled to make a profit. Search only became a hugely profitable business once companies learned to show small adverts that matched exactly what each person was searching for.","The early leaders faded one by one. DEC, which owned AltaVista, was itself in trouble and was bought by another computer company in 1998, and AltaVista passed between several owners before it was finally switched off in 2013. Yahoo! did not build its own powerful search engine at first; for a few years around 2000 its search results were actually supplied by Google, which helped make Google famous. Yahoo! later used other technology and, from 2010, results from Microsoft. Its hand-made directory, the thing that started it all, was closed in 2014.","The size of the job grew at an astonishing rate. When Page and Brin described their prototype in a research paper in 1998, it had indexed about 24 million web pages. Two years later Google announced an index of about a billion pages. In 2008 Google engineers wrote that their systems had found more than a trillion unique web addresses, although many of these were duplicates or automatically generated pages that were not worth indexing. Today search engines index hundreds of billions of pages, and they must keep revisiting them, because pages change, appear and disappear all the time.","Google is not the only search engine in the world, and in some countries it is not the favourite. In China, where Google's services are blocked, the most used search engine is Baidu, co-founded in 2000 by Robin Li, who had worked on link-based ranking in the United States in the 1990s. In Russia the leading search engine is Yandex, launched in 1997, and in South Korea a portal called Naver has long been very popular. Microsoft launched its search engine Bing in 2009. Smaller engines such as DuckDuckGo, started in 2008, attract users by promising not to track what they search for.","Searching has also spread far beyond web pages. You can search inside maps for the nearest library, inside online shops for a particular toy, inside video sites for a science experiment, and inside your own email for a message from last year. Phones let you search by speaking, or by pointing the camera at a plant or a building. Each of these uses the same old pattern Alan Emtage's Archie showed in 1990: gather information, organise it into an index, and answer questions quickly."]},{"heading":"Searching Like a Detective","paragraphs":["Because search engines are so quick and confident, it is easy to think the top result must be the truth. It is not always. A search engine ranks pages by how relevant and popular they seem, which is not the same as being correct. Some websites are designed specifically to climb the rankings, a whole industry called search engine optimisation, or SEO. Adverts can sit at the top. And a false claim that many people repeat and link to can sometimes outrank a careful, accurate page.","Good searchers act like detectives. Here are some tricks worth remembering. Use a few precise keywords rather than a long sentence, for example 'Mosaic browser 1993' instead of 'what was that old browser thing'. Put words in quotation marks to search for an exact phrase. If you get the wrong kind of result, add a word: 'Python snake' or 'Python programming'. Many engines let you add site: followed by a website, such as site:nasa.gov, to search only that site.","Then judge what you find. Look at who made the page: a museum, a university, a government science agency or a respected newspaper is usually more reliable than an anonymous post. Check the date, because facts change. See if two or three different trustworthy sources agree. Notice whether a result is labelled as an advert. Be extra careful with AI-written summaries; they can be very helpful, but they sometimes state mistakes confidently, so click through to the original sources when it matters. Part 18 has more about spotting misinformation.","Search engines have changed how people think and learn. Facts that once needed a trip to the library now take seconds to find, and billions of searches are made every day. But the skill of judging what you find matters more than ever. A search box can show you the haystack and point to where the needles might be. Deciding which needle is real is still your job.","And here is something to think about next time you press Enter. When you search, your words do not simply float off into the air. They travel as packets out of your house, across your town, often through cables under the sea, to a data centre packed with humming computers, and the answers race all the way back. So what exactly are those wires and cables, and where do they go?"]}],"deepDive":"PageRank is a lovely piece of mathematics. Page and Brin imagined a 'random surfer' who starts on any web page and keeps clicking links at random. Every so often, the surfer gets bored and jumps to a completely random page instead; in their published description this happened with a probability of about 15 percent, so a 'damping factor' of about 0.85 described the chance of following a link. If the surfer does this forever, some pages are visited more often than others, and the fraction of time spent on each page is its PageRank. Mathematically, this is the stationary distribution of a Markov chain, which can be found by repeatedly passing rank along the links until the numbers settle down, a calculation that converges in a few dozen rounds even for very large graphs. The idea had relatives in earlier work: citation analysis in science had long judged papers by who cited them, and around the same time Jon Kleinberg at Cornell developed a related link-analysis method called HITS, while Robin Li, who later co-founded the Chinese search engine Baidu, had patented a link-based ranking system called RankDex in the mid-1990s. Historians therefore tend to see link analysis as an idea several people were reaching for at once, with Google turning it into the most successful product. PageRank also started an arms race. Once people knew links mattered, some created 'link farms' of fake pages linking to each other, and search engines responded with ever more complex ranking systems, now using machine learning and, by Google's account, hundreds of factors, of which the original PageRank is only one. There are lively debates about search today. One concerns market power: Google handles a large majority of web searches in many countries, and in 2024 a US federal judge ruled that it had illegally maintained a monopoly in general search, partly through payments to be the default search engine on phones and browsers. Another concerns 'filter bubbles', the worry that personalised results show people only what they already agree with; researchers have found the effect in search is real but often smaller than feared. The newest debate is about AI-generated answers shown above the links, which can save time but may reduce visits to the websites whose work they summarise, and which sometimes contain errors.","funFact":"The name Google comes from 'googol', the mathematicians' word for the number 1 followed by 100 zeros, and Archie, the first Internet search engine, got its name from 'archive' with the letter v taken out.","check":{"q":"What was the main idea behind PageRank?","a":"Treat links to a page like votes, with votes from important pages counting more","wrong":["Rank pages by how many times they repeat the search word","Rank pages by how recently they were made","Let humans sort every web page into categories by hand"]},"sources":[{"title":"Today in media history: The first Internet search engine is released in 1990","publisher":"Poynter","url":"https://www.poynter.org/reporting-editing/2014/today-in-media-history-the-first-internet-search-engine-is-released-in-1990/"},{"title":"Search","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/revolution/the-web/20/390"},{"title":"Our story","publisher":"Google","url":"https://about.google/our-story/"},{"title":"In-depth guide to how Google Search works","publisher":"Google Search Central","url":"https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/how-search-works"},{"title":"Networking & The Web timeline","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/timeline/networking-the-web/"},{"title":"Archie (search engine)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archie_(search_engine)"},{"title":"Yahoo","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yahoo"},{"title":"AltaVista","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AltaVista"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/12-1.jpg","alt":"Google's first production server rack from 1999, packed with cheap circuit boards, at the Computer History Museum.","file":"Google's First Production Server (1999) rack - Computer History Museum (2007-11-10 23.05.17 by Carlo Nardone).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Google%27s_First_Production_Server_(1999)_rack_-_Computer_History_Museum_(2007-11-10_23.05.17_by_Carlo_Nardone).jpg","author":"Carlo Nardone from Roma, Italy","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/12-2.jpg","alt":"Google founders Larry Page (left) and Sergey Brin, pictured in September 2003.","file":"Google page brin.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Google_page_brin.jpg","author":"Ehud Kenan","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/12-3.jpg","alt":"Alan Emtage, creator of Archie, the first Internet search engine, photographed in 2019.","file":"Alan Emtage July 2019.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alan_Emtage_July_2019.jpg","author":"Bajan64~commonswiki","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":3776},{"n":13,"title":"Wires, Glass and Undersea Cables","when":"1960s – today","summary":"The Internet is not a cloud floating in the sky. It is a real machine made of copper wires, glass threads thinner than a hair, and giant cables lying on the bottom of the ocean. Flashes of light race through that glass, carrying your videos and messages from one side of the world to the other.","sections":[{"heading":"You Can Trip Over the Internet","paragraphs":["People often talk about the Internet as if it were invisible, a kind of magic that lives everywhere and nowhere. It is easy to see why. You tap a screen, and a moment later a video from Japan or a message from your cousin in Canada appears. Nothing seems to move. But if you could shrink down to the size of an ant and follow that video back to where it came from, you would find yourself crawling through a very real, very physical world.","You would start at the little box in your home, the one with blinking lights. From there you would slide along a thin cable through the wall and out to the street, where it joins a fatter bundle of cables strung on poles or buried under the pavement. That bundle leads to a building owned by your Internet provider, full of humming metal cabinets. From there, thicker cables run along railway lines, beside motorways and under rivers to big cities. In some of those cities you would find buildings packed with thousands of computers, called data centres, and special meeting places where different networks plug into each other. And if your video came from another continent, your journey would take you down a beach, into the sea, and along the ocean floor for thousands of kilometres, through the dark, past sleeping fish, inside a cable about as thick as a garden hose.","So the Internet has a body. It has copper wires, the same kind of metal wires that carried telephone calls for more than a century. It has fibre-optic cables, which carry data as flashes of light through strands of incredibly pure glass. It has data centres, where websites, games and photos are stored and served. It has exchange points, where networks swap traffic. It has radio towers and, for some places, satellites. All of these parts are built, owned, repaired and paid for by real people and companies.","Remember the big idea from earlier parts of this story: the Internet is a network of networks. Thousands of separate networks, run by universities, phone companies, cable companies, governments and tech firms, agree to follow the same rules, TCP/IP, and to pass packets to one another. In this part we look at the physical roads those packets travel on. And the most important road of all turns out to be made of glass."]},{"heading":"Charles Kao and the Clearest Glass in the World","paragraphs":["In the early 1960s, telephone companies had a growing problem. More and more people wanted to make calls, and soon there would be television signals and computer data to carry too. Copper wires and radio links could only carry so much. Engineers knew that light waves wiggle far faster than radio waves, which meant that, in theory, a beam of light could carry an enormous amount of information. Lasers had just been invented in 1960. The question was: what could you send the light through? Air was no good, because fog, rain and dust block light, and a beam cannot go around corners. Glass seemed like an option, but the glass of the time was hopeless. Light shone into a glass fibre faded away to almost nothing after a few metres.","Charles Kuen Kao was born in Shanghai, China, on 4 November 1933. He studied electrical engineering in London and then worked at Standard Telecommunication Laboratories, a research centre in Harlow, a town north-east of London. By 1963 he was leading a group there studying how to send light through fibres. Together with his colleague George Hockham, he did something brave and careful: instead of accepting that glass was just too murky, he asked exactly why the light was disappearing.","Their answer, worked out in 1965 and presented in January 1966, changed the world. Kao and Hockham argued that the problem was not glass itself. The problem was the tiny amounts of impurities in it, such as specks of iron and other metals, which soaked up the light. If someone could make glass pure enough, Kao calculated, the losses could fall below a key target: 20 decibels per kilometre. That number sounds technical, but here is what it means. Losing 20 decibels means that after one kilometre, about one hundredth of the light is still left. That may not sound like much, but it would be enough for a sensitive detector to read the signal, and it would be wildly better than the glass of the day, which lost almost all of its light in a few metres.","Many experts were doubtful. Making glass that pure seemed almost impossible. But Kao travelled to glass factories and laboratories, tested samples and encouraged researchers to try. In 1970, a team at the Corning Glass Works company in New York State, including Robert Maurer, Donald Keck and Peter Schultz, made a fibre of extremely pure silica glass that lost only about 17 decibels per kilometre. Kao's target had been beaten. Within a few years, losses dropped even further. Today's best fibres lose only about 0.2 decibels per kilometre, so a pulse of light can travel around 15 kilometres before half of it fades away. If ocean water were as clear as this glass, you could see the sea floor kilometres below you.","Kao later became a professor and then head (vice-chancellor) of the Chinese University of Hong Kong, from 1987 to 1996. People often call him the father of fibre optics. In 2009 he shared the Nobel Prize in Physics, receiving half of the prize for his work on sending light through fibres for communication. The other half went to Willard Boyle and George Smith, who had invented the image sensor chip used in digital cameras. Kao died in Hong Kong in 2018, aged 84. Nearly every message you send today spends at least part of its journey inside the kind of glass he imagined."]},{"heading":"How Light Gets Trapped in a Glass Thread","paragraphs":["Try this the next time you go swimming. Dive under the water in a pool, look up at the surface at a low angle, and you may see something strange: the surface looks like a silvery mirror reflecting the pool below. That is the same trick that makes fibre optics work. It is called total internal reflection.","Light travels a little slower in water or glass than in air. Scientists measure this slowing with a number called the refractive index. When light inside a slower material hits the boundary with a faster material at a shallow, glancing angle, it cannot escape. Instead it bounces back completely, as if the boundary were a perfect mirror. That is the mirror you see from under the water.","A fibre-optic strand uses this trick on purpose. Each strand has two layers of glass. In the middle is the core, the path the light follows. Around it is the cladding, a layer of glass made with a very slightly lower refractive index. The difference is tiny: in a typical fibre, the core's index might be about 1.4475 and the cladding's about 1.444. But it is enough. Light zipping down the core at a shallow angle strikes the boundary with the cladding and is reflected back inward, again and again, thousands of times, following the fibre even when it curves gently around a corner. The light is trapped inside, like a ball rolling down a gutter that can never jump out.","These strands are astonishingly thin. A common type used for long distances has a core only about 8 micrometres wide (a micrometre is a thousandth of a millimetre) and cladding about 125 micrometres across, which is roughly the thickness of a human hair. Plastic coatings are added around each strand to protect it, and many strands are bundled into one cable.","How does light carry information? A tiny laser at one end flashes on and off, or changes the brightness and timing of its light, billions of times a second. Each flash or pattern stands for 1s and 0s, the bits that make up every photo, song and message. At the other end, a light detector turns the flashes back into electrical signals. The light used is usually invisible infrared, with a wavelength of about 1,550 nanometres, because the glass is clearest at that colour. Inside the glass, light travels at roughly 200,000 kilometres per second, about two-thirds of its speed in empty space, which is still fast enough to cross the Atlantic in about 30 thousandths of a second.","Two more clever ideas let fibres carry truly gigantic amounts of data. The first is called wavelength-division multiplexing, a long name for a simple idea: you can send many different colours of light down the same strand at the same time, and each colour carries its own separate stream of data, like many cars driving side by side in different lanes. Modern systems can squeeze dozens of colours into one strand. The second idea is the optical amplifier, developed in the late 1980s. Even the best glass slowly dims the light, so long cables need boosters. Older systems had to turn the light into electricity, clean it up and turn it back into light. Optical amplifiers made of fibre mixed with a rare element called erbium can boost the light directly, all colours at once. Together, these tricks mean a single modern cable can carry more data than the whole Internet carried a few decades ago."]},{"heading":"Cables on the Ocean Floor","paragraphs":["If you look at a map of the Internet's long-distance links, it looks like a giant spider web wrapped around the planet, and most of the threads run under the sea. Undersea cables, also called submarine cables, are the true superhighways of the Internet. The share of data travelling between continents that goes through them is often quoted as more than 95 percent. Satellites carry only a tiny slice. According to United States government figures reported by the research firm TeleGeography, satellites carried less than half of one percent of the country's international capacity. When you watch a video made on another continent, it almost certainly reached you through the sea.","The idea of laying cables on the sea bed is not new. As you read in Part 1, the first lasting telegraph cable across the Atlantic was laid in 1866 by the giant ship Great Eastern. Telephone cables followed: the first transatlantic telephone cable, TAT-1, opened in 1956 and could carry about 36 phone calls at once. Each new cable carried more, but they were still made of copper, and copper has limits.","Then came glass. TAT-8 was the first fibre-optic cable to cross the Atlantic. It was built by a team of phone companies led by AT&T in the United States, British Telecom and France Télécom, using technology developed at AT&T's Bell Laboratories. It came ashore at Tuckerton in New Jersey, at Widemouth Bay in Cornwall, England, and at Penmarch in Brittany, France, with the line splitting under the sea to reach both European countries. It entered service in 1988 and cost about 335 million US dollars. TAT-8 carried about 280 megabits per second, enough for roughly 40,000 telephone calls at the same time, many times more than TAT-1. It was so popular that it was nearly full within about eighteen months. TAT-8 was retired in 2002, and in 2025 a ship began hauling its old cable up from the sea floor so the materials could be recycled.","Today there are hundreds of submarine cable systems. TeleGeography tracks more than 600 that are either working or being planned, and together the working ones stretch for more than 1.5 million kilometres, enough to wrap around the Earth at the equator over 35 times. Some are short, like the roughly 131-kilometre cable between Ireland and Britain. Others are enormous: the Asia America Gateway runs for about 20,000 kilometres. Newer cables are often paid for by big technology companies that run search engines, video sites and cloud services, because they need to move so much data between their data centres.","What does such a cable look like? Out in the deep ocean, where it simply lies on the sea floor, it is surprisingly slim, often about the width of a garden hose. At its heart are the hair-thin glass fibres, surrounded by layers that keep out water and give it strength, plus a copper layer that carries electricity to power the amplifiers spaced along the cable every few dozen kilometres. Near the shore, where the water is shallow and the dangers are greater, cables are wrapped in thick steel armour and buried in a trench in the sea bed.","Laying a cable is a huge job. Special cable ships carry thousands of kilometres of cable coiled in giant tanks inside their hulls. Before laying begins, survey ships map the sea floor to find a safe route, avoiding underwater cliffs, volcanoes and busy fishing grounds. Then the cable ship sails slowly along the route, paying out the cable over its stern, carefully controlling the speed so the cable settles gently onto the bottom, sometimes thousands of metres down. Close to land, a sea plough towed behind the ship can cut a groove in the sea bed and bury the cable as it goes."]},{"heading":"Anchors, Earthquakes and the Truth About Sharks","paragraphs":["Cables do break. The International Cable Protection Committee, an organisation of cable owners, has counted roughly 200 faults around the world each year. That sounds like a lot, but remember there are hundreds of cables and over a million kilometres of them, and most breaks are fixed without most people ever noticing, because traffic is quickly rerouted along other cables. This is the Internet's old design idea of many paths working once again.","So what breaks them? Mostly, people do, by accident. About two-thirds of faults are caused by fishing boats dragging heavy nets and gear along the sea bed, or by ships dropping and dragging their anchors. Natural events cause some of the rest. Earthquakes and underwater landslides can snap cables or bury them, and a big earthquake off southern Taiwan at the end of 2006 damaged several cables at once, slowing Internet connections across parts of Asia for days, while full repairs took weeks. Equipment failures inside the cable also happen, but less often.","You may have heard stories about sharks biting undersea cables, and there is a grain of truth in them. In the mid-1980s, according to news reports from the time, an early trial fibre-optic cable laid by AT&T near the Canary Islands was bitten by sharks several times, possibly because the electric fields around the cable attracted them. Engineers responded by adding protective layers. But it is important to keep this in proportion. According to the cable protection committee's data reported by TeleGeography, fish bites caused zero cable faults between 2007 and 2014. Sharks make a fun headline, but the real dangers are anchors, fishing gear and earthquakes.","When a cable does break, a repair ship sets out on something like a deep-sea rescue. Engineers on shore send test signals down the cable and measure how long they take to bounce back, which tells them roughly where the break is. The ship sails to the spot and lowers a grapnel, a heavy hook dragged along the sea floor to catch the cable, or in shallower water uses a remotely operated underwater robot. The broken ends are hauled up to the deck one at a time. There, in a special clean room, technicians join the hair-thin glass fibres together with great precision, splicing a fresh piece of cable in between. Then the repaired cable is lowered back into the sea. A repair can take days or even weeks, depending on the weather, the depth and how far the ship must travel."]},{"heading":"From Screeching Modems to Fibre at Home","paragraphs":["The undersea cables are the motorways, but your home also needs a driveway to join them. The connection between your house and your Internet provider is often called the last mile, and for a long time it was the slowest part of the whole journey.","As you read in Part 9, the first home connections used dial-up modems. They turned data into screeching sounds and sent them down the ordinary telephone line, and the fastest ones managed about 56 kilobits per second. To understand what that means, we need an important word. Bandwidth is how much data a connection can carry each second. It is measured in bits per second. A kilobit is a thousand bits, a megabit is a million bits, and a gigabit is a billion bits. Internet speeds are usually written as Mbps (megabits per second) or Gbps. Watch out for one tricky detail: a byte is 8 bits, so a file of 5 megabytes contains 40 megabits.","Now let's do a real calculation. Imagine downloading a 5-megabyte song. On a 56-kilobit dial-up modem, that is 40 million bits divided by 56,000 bits per second, which takes about 714 seconds, or roughly 12 minutes, if everything goes perfectly. On a 100-megabit connection, the same song takes about 0.4 of a second. That difference, more than a thousand times faster, is why the modern Internet of streaming video and online games became possible.","The first big step up was broadband, a word meaning a connection that is always on and much faster than dial-up. One kind, called DSL (digital subscriber line), uses the same copper telephone wires as dial-up, but in a smarter way. A phone call uses only fairly low-pitched frequencies on the wire. DSL sends data using much higher frequencies on the same pair of wires, so you can be online and make a phone call at the same time. Its speed drops the farther you live from the telephone exchange, because high frequencies fade quickly in copper. Another kind, cable broadband, uses the thick coaxial cables that cable television companies had already laid to homes.","The best option is to bring fibre all the way to the home, often called fibre to the home or full fibre. Instead of copper for the last stretch, a glass strand runs right into your house, where a small box turns the light into signals your router can use. Fibre fades far less than copper, so homes far from the exchange can get the same speeds as homes next door to it, and many homes with fibre today can get speeds of one gigabit per second or more.","There is one more idea worth knowing. Bandwidth is not the same as delay. Engineers call the delay latency: the time it takes one small packet to travel from you to somewhere else. Think of a water pipe. Bandwidth is how wide the pipe is, how much water can flow through each second. Latency is how long it takes the first drop to reach the far end. A wide pipe is great for downloading a huge film. A short pipe matters more for an online game, where every split second counts. Both depend on the physical wires and how far the data must travel."]},{"heading":"Where Networks Meet: Internet Exchange Points","paragraphs":["Here is a puzzle. Your Internet provider runs one network. The video site you visit is connected to a different network. Your friend down the road might use a third. How do packets jump from one company's network to another? The answer is that networks have to physically connect somewhere, with real cables plugged into real machines.","Sometimes two networks run a cable directly between their equipment. But it would be impossible for thousands of networks to each run a separate cable to every other network. So they meet at places called Internet exchange points, or IXPs. An IXP is usually a room or set of rooms in a data centre full of powerful network switches. Each member network runs a connection into the switch, and from there it can swap traffic with any other member that agrees. This swapping is called peering, and it is often done without either side paying the other, because both benefit.","IXPs grew out of the history you read in Part 9. When the government-funded NSFNET backbone was retired in 1995 in the United States, it was replaced by a handful of official meeting points called Network Access Points, alongside exchanges such as MAE-East near Washington DC. Since then, hundreds of exchange points have opened around the world. Some of the biggest are in Frankfurt, Amsterdam and London, where hundreds of networks connect and enormous amounts of traffic flow through the switches every second. Many IXPs are run as non-profit associations owned by their members.","Why do exchange points matter so much? Imagine two neighbours in the same city, each with a different Internet provider. If those providers do not connect locally, an email from one neighbour to the other might have to travel to a distant city, or even to another continent, before coming back, a bit like posting a letter to the house next door by sending it overseas first. In some parts of the world this really used to happen. A local IXP lets the traffic take the short route. That makes connections faster, cheaper and more reliable, and it means a country's Internet keeps working better even if an international cable breaks. This is why building local exchange points has been an important way to improve Internet access in many countries."]},{"heading":"Up in Space: Satellites and Their Long Delay","paragraphs":["If cables are so important, why use satellites at all? Because there are places cables cannot easily reach: ships in the middle of the ocean, aeroplanes, research stations in Antarctica, remote mountain villages and small islands far from any cable route. For them, a link through space can be the only option.","Satellites began relaying communications long before the Internet. In July 1962, the satellite Telstar 1 sent live television pictures across the Atlantic for the first time. In 1965, Intelsat I, nicknamed Early Bird, became the first commercial communications satellite in a special kind of orbit called geostationary orbit. A satellite in this orbit sits about 35,786 kilometres above the equator. At that height it takes exactly as long to circle the Earth as the Earth takes to spin once, so it seems to hang still in the sky. That is useful, because a dish on the ground can point at the same spot all the time.","But that great height comes with a cost: delay. Radio signals travel at the speed of light, about 300,000 kilometres per second, which sounds instant. However, for a packet to go from a dish near you up to a geostationary satellite and back down to a ground station, it must travel at least about 71,600 kilometres. That takes roughly a quarter of a second. If you send a request and wait for a reply, the signal has to make that trip twice, so it is about half a second before an answer arrives, even before any other delays are added. You could hear this kind of delay in old live television broadcasts by satellite, when a news presenter asked a question and the reporter far away seemed to pause awkwardly before answering. For a phone call, half a second feels strange. For a fast online game, it is a disaster.","Compare that with a fibre-optic cable under the Atlantic. Even though light travels slower in glass, the route is so much shorter that a round trip across the ocean takes well under a tenth of a second. A single modern undersea cable can also carry far more data than a satellite. This is why the Internet is not mostly in the sky, whatever the word cloud might make you think. It lives mostly underground and under the sea. Newer satellites in much lower orbits have far shorter delays, and you will meet them in Part 20.","Glass threads under the sea, copper wires in old streets, fibre plugged into homes, switches humming in exchange points: the Internet is one of the largest machines ever built, and you can touch it. But there is still one place where all those cables come to an end, and that is the socket in your wall. Wires are wonderful, yet they tie you to one spot. What if your computer, or your phone, could connect to the Internet without any cable at all?"]}],"deepDive":"Why did glass win? The answer lies in physics and economics. Copper carries electrical signals, but at high frequencies the signal fades quickly and picks up interference, so long copper lines need frequent boosters and carry limited data. Infrared light near 1,550 nanometres has a frequency of roughly 190 terahertz, leaving enormous room for information. Kao and Hockham's insight in 1965–66 was that the unavoidable losses in silica are very low and that most of the loss in the glass of their day came from impurities such as metal ions, which could be removed. Their target of 20 dB/km (about 1 percent of the light left after 1 km) was beaten by Corning in 1970, and modern fibre reaches about 0.15–0.2 dB/km near 1,550 nm, close to the physical limit set mainly by scattering from tiny density variations frozen into the glass. The decibel scale is logarithmic: every 10 dB of loss divides the power by ten, so at 0.2 dB/km a link can run tens of kilometres before it needs boosting. Undersea systems use erbium-doped fibre amplifiers, developed around 1986–87, in which erbium ions energised by a pump laser release extra light in step with the signal across a band of wavelengths in the 1,530–1,565 nm region. Because one amplifier boosts that whole band, dense wavelength-division multiplexing became practical: dozens of channels share one fibre. Engineers also fight dispersion, where parts of a pulse arrive at slightly different times and smear the bits together; modern receivers use powerful digital signal processing to undo much of it. Historians and policy experts debate some consequences. Cables follow geography and money, so they bunch up at a few landing points and narrow passages, which raises worries about resilience when several break at once, as happened after the earthquake off Taiwan in December 2006. Ownership has also shifted: early cables were built by groups of national telephone companies, while many new ones are funded by large technology firms. Finally, the oft-quoted figure that 95 to 99 percent of intercontinental traffic travels by cable is widely repeated and broadly right in spirit, but exact shares are hard to measure, which is why careful writers say the vast majority.","funFact":"In the mid-1980s, according to news reports, an experimental AT&T fibre-optic cable near the Canary Islands was bitten by sharks several times, yet according to industry data reported by TeleGeography, fish bites caused no cable faults at all between 2007 and 2014.","check":{"q":"What did Charles Kao realise was the main reason light faded so quickly in early glass fibres?","a":"Impurities in the glass soaked up the light","wrong":["Glass can never carry light more than a few metres","The fibres were far too thick","Sharks kept biting the cables"]},"sources":[{"title":"Charles K. Kao","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_K._Kao"},{"title":"Optical fiber","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optical_fiber"},{"title":"TAT-8","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TAT-8"},{"title":"Submarine Cable Frequently Asked Questions","publisher":"TeleGeography","url":"https://www2.telegeography.com/submarine-cable-faqs-frequently-asked-questions"},{"title":"Internet exchange point","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_exchange_point"},{"title":"Types of orbits","publisher":"European Space Agency (ESA)","url":"https://www.esa.int/Enabling_Support/Space_Transportation/Types_of_orbits"},{"title":"Submarine communications cable","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Submarine_communications_cable"},{"title":"2006 Hengchun earthquakes","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2006_Hengchun_earthquakes"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/13-1.jpg","alt":"Charles K. Kao, the fibre-optics pioneer, smiling at a lecture in October 2003.","file":"Charles K. Kao (22 Oct 2003).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_K._Kao_(22_Oct_2003).jpg","author":"C-Ya-gg","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/13-2.jpg","alt":"Cross-section of an undersea fibre-optic cable: tiny glass fibres in the middle, wrapped in steel wires and plastic.","file":"Submarine cable cross-section.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Submarine_cable_cross-section.png","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/13-3.jpg","alt":"A green laser zig-zags along a clear plastic rod, bouncing off its walls just like light inside an optical fibre.","file":"Laser in fibre.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laser_in_fibre.jpg","author":"Timwether","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4372},{"n":14,"title":"Cutting the Cord: Wi-Fi and Mobile Internet","when":"1940s – today","summary":"Invisible radio waves fill the air around you, carrying videos, games and messages without a single wire. A film star, a group of radio astronomers in Australia and a committee of engineers all helped make it happen. Today most people in the world get online through a phone in their pocket.","sections":[{"heading":"Messages Riding on Invisible Waves","paragraphs":["Hold your hand up in the air. Right now, passing through your fingers, there are radio waves from Wi-Fi routers, phone masts, radio stations and maybe a neighbour's baby monitor. You cannot see them, hear them or feel them, but they are there, carrying conversations, music and data. Wireless Internet is the art of using those invisible waves to carry packets instead of sending them down a wire.","Radio waves are a kind of electromagnetic wave, the same family as visible light, microwaves and X-rays. They all travel at the speed of light. What makes them different is how fast they wiggle. Scientists call the number of wiggles per second the frequency, measured in hertz. One hertz means one wiggle per second. Wi-Fi often uses waves at about 2.4 gigahertz, which means about 2.4 billion wiggles every second. Each wave is about 12.5 centimetres long from one peak to the next, roughly the length of your hand.","The German scientist Heinrich Hertz first made and detected radio waves in a laboratory in the late 1880s, and the unit of frequency is named after him. By 1901, the Italian inventor Guglielmo Marconi had sent a radio signal across the Atlantic Ocean. For most of the twentieth century, radio carried voices and music. But a radio wave can carry anything that can be turned into a pattern. To send data, a transmitter changes something about the wave, such as its strength, its exact frequency or the timing of its peaks, in a pattern that stands for 1s and 0s. A receiver tuned to the same frequency spots those changes and turns them back into bits.","There is one big problem with radio, and it is the same problem you face in a noisy playground. Everyone shares the same air. If two people shout at the same moment, nobody can understand either of them. Radio signals can also be blocked, bounced off walls, mixed up by echoes or deliberately jammed. Much of the story of wireless Internet is the story of clever people solving those problems."]},{"heading":"ALOHAnet: Hawaii Goes Wireless","paragraphs":["You met ALOHAnet briefly in Part 5. Let's look more closely at how it worked, because its simple trick still echoes in wireless networks today. In September 1968, Norman Abramson and Franklin Kuo began a project at the University of Hawaii. The university had campuses spread across several islands, and running telephone wires between them across the sea was expensive and unreliable. So Abramson's team decided to use radio.","ALOHAnet began working in June 1971. It is often described as the first public demonstration of a wireless packet data network. Terminals on different islands sent packets by UHF radio to a central computer in Honolulu, at 9,600 bits per second. It used two radio channels: one for the terminals to talk to the hub, and one for the hub to broadcast replies to everyone.","Here is the clever part. All the terminals shared the same channel to the hub, so what happened if two of them sent a packet at the same time? The two signals would crash into each other, which engineers call a collision, and both would be garbled. ALOHAnet's answer was beautifully relaxed. Each terminal simply sent its packet whenever it was ready. If the hub received it correctly, it sent back an acknowledgement. If no acknowledgement came, the terminal assumed there had been a collision, waited a random amount of time and tried again. Because each terminal picked a different random wait, they were unlikely to crash into each other a second time.","This idea had a cost. When lots of terminals were busy, collisions wasted a big share of the channel's time. Mathematicians worked out that in the simplest version only about 18 percent of the channel's capacity carried useful data at best. A later improvement, called slotted ALOHA, split time into fixed slots and roughly doubled that to about 37 percent. But the core idea, sharing the air, detecting failures, and backing off for a random time before trying again, inspired Bob Metcalfe when he designed Ethernet in the 1970s, and versions of it live on in Wi-Fi and mobile phone networks today."]},{"heading":"Hedy Lamarr, a Piano Roll and a Secret Signal","paragraphs":["Now we travel back to Hollywood in 1940. Hedy Lamarr was one of the most famous film stars in the world. She had been born Hedwig Kiesler in Vienna, Austria, on 9 November 1914, and had moved to Hollywood in 1938. What most cinema fans did not know was that she loved inventing. She kept a workbench at home and enjoyed tinkering with ideas.","The Second World War was raging. Lamarr wanted to help the Allies, and she became interested in a problem with radio-controlled torpedoes. A torpedo steered by radio could be thrown off course if the enemy found its frequency and jammed it by blasting noise on that same frequency. Lamarr had an idea: what if the radio signal kept jumping from one frequency to another in a secret pattern that only the sender and the torpedo knew? An enemy listening on one frequency would hear only a tiny blip before the signal hopped away.","To make it work, she teamed up with George Antheil, an American composer famous for wild modern music. Antheil had written a piece called Ballet Mécanique that used several player pianos playing in step. A player piano is a piano that plays itself by reading a long paper roll punched with holes. Antheil knew how to keep machines synchronised, and the pair realised that two matching punched rolls, one in the ship and one in the torpedo, could tell both radios to hop to the same new frequency at exactly the same moment.","They filed their patent on 10 June 1941. It was granted on 11 August 1942 as US patent 2,292,387, titled 'Secret Communication System', under the names Hedy Kiesler Markey (her married name at the time) and George Antheil. The patent describes perforated strips like piano rolls, kept in step by clock motors, and notes that a standard 88-note piano roll could control 88 different frequencies, one for each key on a piano.","The US Navy did not use their invention during the war. It was considered too bulky and complicated to fit inside a torpedo. The patent expired, and for years few people knew about it. Later, as electronics improved, frequency-hopping and related ideas, together called spread spectrum, became important in military radios and then in everyday technology. Systems using similar frequency-hopping ideas were in operational use from about 1962. Lamarr and Antheil were finally honoured with a Pioneer Award from the Electronic Frontier Foundation in 1997, and Lamarr was added to the US National Inventors Hall of Fame in 2014.","So did Hedy Lamarr invent Wi-Fi? No, and it is important to be fair here. Her patent was an early and clever version of frequency hopping, an ancestor idea of the spread-spectrum radio that later wireless systems used. The very first Wi-Fi standard did include a frequency-hopping option, and Bluetooth still hops between frequencies today. But Wi-Fi itself was built decades later by many engineers, using different techniques, and other inventors had also thought about hopping frequencies. Lamarr deserves real credit as an inventor who saw a smart idea early, and her story is a great reminder that inventors can come from anywhere, even a film set. She simply was not the inventor of Wi-Fi."]},{"heading":"A Committee, a Standard and a Catchy Name","paragraphs":["For decades, most radio frequencies could only be used with a government licence, which made sense: you would not want random people broadcasting on the same frequencies as air traffic control. But in 1985, the US Federal Communications Commission (FCC) made a big decision. It allowed certain bands of frequencies, including one around 2.4 gigahertz, to be used by anyone for spread-spectrum radio without a licence, as long as the devices were low-powered. These bands were already used by things like microwave ovens, so they were considered a bit of a junk drawer. Engineers saw an opportunity.","Companies began building wireless networking products, but each company's equipment worked differently. One early example was WaveLAN, developed in 1991 by engineers at NCR, later part of AT&T, in the Netherlands, which ran at 1 to 2 megabits per second. The trouble was that a card from one company could not talk to a base station from another. It was like the early days of networking all over again, when different networks could not talk to each other, and the solution was the same: an agreed set of rules.","That job fell to a committee of the IEEE (the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers), a huge organisation of engineers that writes many technical standards. Its working group was called 802.11, and its chair for about ten years was a Dutch engineer named Vic Hayes. Getting dozens of rival companies to agree on one design took years of meetings and arguments. Hayes is often called the father of Wi-Fi for steering the group through it. The first 802.11 standard was published in 1997. It worked at 2.4 gigahertz and offered speeds of up to 2 megabits per second.","Two years later, in 1999, came two upgrades. 802.11b ran at up to 11 megabits per second at 2.4 gigahertz, and 802.11a reached up to 54 megabits per second at 5 gigahertz. It was 802.11b that really took off. That same year, Apple put wireless networking into its iBook laptop under the name AirPort, helping to show ordinary people that a laptop without a network cable was possible.","But 'IEEE 802.11b Direct Sequence' is not a name that makes people rush to the shops. In 1999, several companies formed a group to test products and make sure equipment from different makers really worked together. The group was first called the Wireless Ethernet Compatibility Alliance. They hired a branding company, Interbrand, to come up with something catchier. Interbrand suggested Wi-Fi, a name that rhymes with hi-fi, the old word for good-quality music systems. The group soon renamed itself the Wi-Fi Alliance, and today it certifies thousands of products every year.","You may hear people say Wi-Fi stands for wireless fidelity. It does not. Wi-Fi is a brand name chosen because it sounded friendly and memorable, and the Wi-Fi Alliance does not treat it as short for anything. In 2018 the Alliance also introduced simpler names for the versions: Wi-Fi 4 (802.11n), Wi-Fi 5 (802.11ac) and Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax), followed by later versions such as Wi-Fi 7."]},{"heading":"Radio Astronomers Solve the Echo Problem","paragraphs":["Early wireless networks had a frustrating problem indoors. Radio waves do not just travel in a straight line from the router to your laptop. They bounce off walls, ceilings, furniture and metal cupboards. Your laptop receives the direct signal plus many slightly delayed copies, like hearing your own voice echo around a big empty hall. At slow speeds this did not matter much. But the faster you try to send data, the shorter each bit becomes, and the echoes of one bit start smearing into the next, turning the signal into mush. Engineers call this multipath, and the scientists who tackled it described it as reverberation.","Some of the people who cracked this problem came from an unexpected field: radio astronomy, the science of studying space by listening to the radio waves that stars and galaxies give off. At CSIRO, Australia's national science agency, a team in its Radiophysics Division in Sydney included John O'Sullivan, Terry Percival, Diet Ostry, Graham Daniels and John Deane. O'Sullivan had earlier worked on ways to pick faint, smeared-out radio signals from space out of the noise, and the team realised that some of the same mathematics could help clean up wireless signals bouncing around a room.","Their solution used a powerful mathematical tool called the fast Fourier transform, which can split a complicated signal into lots of simple waves and put it back together again, very quickly, on a computer chip. Instead of sending one fast stream of data on one frequency, the system could send many slower streams side by side on many closely spaced frequencies at once. Because each stream is slow, the echoes have time to die away before the next piece of data arrives. All the streams together still add up to a fast connection. The team built a prototype in the early 1990s and patented their invention, with the key US patent granted in 1996.","This kind of approach became part of the faster Wi-Fi standards that followed, starting with 802.11a and later 802.11g, 802.11n and beyond. CSIRO argued that companies selling Wi-Fi products were using its patented ideas, and after long legal battles, many companies agreed to pay licence fees. Reports describe settlements worth about 200 million dollars in 2009 and about 220 million dollars in 2012, and CSIRO says it has earned around 430 million Australian dollars from more than 20 licence agreements. O'Sullivan received the Australian Prime Minister's Prize for Science in 2009, and in 2012 the CSIRO team received the European Patent Office's European Inventor Award in the non-European countries category.","It is fair to say that Wi-Fi has many parents. American engineers at the FCC opened the airwaves, Dutch engineers at NCR built early products, an international IEEE committee wrote the rules, Australian scientists solved a key speed problem, and branding experts gave it a name. Australia, the Netherlands and the United States all have good reasons to be proud. Like the Internet itself, Wi-Fi was a team effort spread across countries and decades."]},{"heading":"Inside Your Wi-Fi Router","paragraphs":["Let's open up the box in your home. In many homes, one box does several jobs at once. The modem part talks to your Internet provider over the fibre, cable or telephone line. The router part directs packets between the Internet and all the devices in your home, giving each one its own local address and remembering which reply belongs to which device. And the wireless access point part is a small radio transmitter and receiver with antennas that sends packets through the air to your phone, laptop, games console and smart speaker.","Wi-Fi mainly uses two bands of frequencies, around 2.4 gigahertz and 5 gigahertz, and newer equipment can also use a band around 6 gigahertz. Each band is divided into channels, like lanes on a road. The 2.4 gigahertz band is crowded and narrow: in many countries it has only three channels that do not overlap each other, numbered 1, 6 and 11. It is also shared with Bluetooth gadgets, cordless phones and microwave ovens, which work at about the same frequency. That is why your video might stutter when someone heats up their lunch. The 5 gigahertz band has many more channels and is usually much faster, but higher-frequency waves are weakened more by walls and floors. So 2.4 gigahertz reaches farther, while 5 gigahertz goes faster over shorter distances.","Range matters too. Indoors, a typical Wi-Fi signal works well within about 20 metres, depending on what is in the way. Thick brick or concrete walls, metal, mirrors and even water soak up or block the signal, and since people are mostly water, a crowded room can weaken it too. A fish tank between you and the router is bad news. Big homes sometimes use several linked access points, called a mesh network, to spread the signal around.","Because radio waves spread out in all directions, anyone nearby with the right equipment could pick up your Wi-Fi signal. That is why Wi-Fi networks use passwords and encryption, which scrambles the data so only devices with the right key can read it. The first Wi-Fi security system, called WEP, turned out to be weak and easy to break. It was replaced by WPA in 2003, then WPA2 and, from 2018, WPA3. A good rule: always use a strong password on your home Wi-Fi, and be careful about typing secret information when you are on a free public network in a café or airport.","Speeds have shot up since 1997. The first standard managed 2 megabits per second; 802.11n, or Wi-Fi 4, from 2009 could reach several hundred megabits per second by sending several streams at once from multiple antennas; and the newest versions can, under ideal conditions very close to the router, reach many gigabits per second. Real-world speeds are always lower than these headline numbers, because walls, distance and other users all take their share."]},{"heading":"From Bricks to Smartphones: Mobile Generations","paragraphs":["Wi-Fi covers your home or school. But what about when you are on a bus, at the beach or walking through a city? For that, we use mobile networks. A mobile network divides the land into areas called cells, each served by a mast with antennas. As you move, your phone is handed from one cell to the next without dropping the connection. Engineers talk about generations of mobile technology, usually shortened to G.","The first generation, 1G, arrived in the late 1970s and 1980s. It was analogue, meaning it sent voices as smoothly varying radio waves, a bit like an old radio broadcast. The phones were heavy and expensive, sometimes nicknamed bricks, and they were for talking only. The second generation, 2G, was digital: voices were turned into bits. The most popular 2G system, GSM, began in Europe, and the first GSM call was made in Finland in 1991. GSM brought a fun extra: text messages. On 3 December 1992, an engineer named Neil Papworth sent the first text message, 'Merry Christmas', from a computer to a Vodafone phone in Britain. Texts were limited to 160 characters, a size planned back in the 1980s by Friedhelm Hillebrand and Bernard Ghillebaert. Later 2G add-ons such as GPRS and EDGE allowed slow mobile Internet, enough for email and simple pages.","Third-generation, or 3G, networks arrived in the early 2000s and made mobile Internet properly usable for browsing and downloading. 4G networks, based on a technology called LTE, started appearing around 2009 and 2010 and were fast enough for smooth video streaming, video calls and maps that update as you walk. 5G networks began commercial service in 2019. They can offer much higher speeds and shorter delays, and they can handle huge numbers of connected devices in one place, such as a packed stadium. The International Telecommunication Union, the United Nations agency for communications, reported that in 2024 about 51 percent of the world's population was covered by 5G, about 92 percent by 4G, and about 96 percent by 3G or better. Coverage is very uneven, though: 5G reached 84 percent of people in high-income countries but only about 4 percent in low-income countries.","Then came the moment that changed everything. On 9 January 2007, Steve Jobs stood on stage at the Macworld conference in San Francisco and announced the iPhone, calling it a phone, an iPod and an Internet device in one. It went on sale in the United States in June 2007, costing 499 dollars for the 4 gigabyte model and 599 for the 8 gigabyte model. Surprisingly, the first iPhone could not use 3G. It used Wi-Fi and the slower 2G EDGE network. But its big touchscreen and real web browser showed people what a pocket computer could be. Other smartphones already existed, such as BlackBerry devices that were popular for email, and Japan had offered mobile Internet services on phones since 1999. The first phone running Google's Android software followed in 2008, the same year Apple opened its App Store. Soon, smartphones from many companies were everywhere."]},{"heading":"The Internet in Your Pocket","paragraphs":["Today, for most people on Earth, the Internet is something that lives in a phone. Studies of web traffic have found that since around the mid-2010s, more web pages around the world have been loaded on mobile phones than on desktop and laptop computers. In many countries, especially in Africa and Asia, millions of people skipped computers and landline connections entirely and went online for the first time with a smartphone. A farmer can check crop prices, a student can watch a science lesson, and a family can video-call a relative working in another country, all from a device that fits in a pocket.","Think about how much that phone depends on everything you have read in this story. When you send a photo from a park, it travels by radio to the nearest mast, then down cables, often fibre, to your mobile company's network, across exchange points, maybe under the sea, and finally out through someone else's Wi-Fi to your friend's phone. Wireless is really only the first and last few metres or kilometres of the journey. The heavy lifting is still done by the glass cables from Part 13. The packets are still chopped up and reassembled using the TCP/IP rules from Part 6.","There are still gaps. Mobile signals are weaker in remote countryside, mountains and deserts, and many people cannot afford a smartphone or a data plan. You will read more about this digital divide in Part 18. But the change from wired to wireless has been astonishingly fast. In 1997, the first Wi-Fi standard carried 2 megabits per second and hardly anyone had heard of it. Twenty years later, billions of people carried a powerful Internet computer everywhere they went.","And once the Internet was in every home, office and pocket, the world of business took notice. If people were online all the time, then shops, banks, newspapers and music companies wanted to be online too. In the late 1990s, investors poured money into almost any company with '.com' in its name, sometimes far too fast. What happened next was a boom, a crash and the birth of a brand-new economy."]}],"deepDive":"Why did spread spectrum and the fast Fourier transform matter so much? Radio channels are limited in two ways: by bandwidth (how wide a slice of frequencies you may use) and by noise. Claude Shannon's 1948 theorem showed that the maximum error-free data rate of a channel grows with both its bandwidth and its signal-to-noise ratio, so engineers try to squeeze every drop from a crowded, noisy band. Spread-spectrum methods deliberately spread a signal across a wider band than strictly needed. Frequency hopping, the family that includes the Lamarr–Antheil patent, jumps among channels in a pseudo-random pattern; direct-sequence spread spectrum, used by 802.11b, multiplies each bit by a fast code. Both make a signal resistant to narrow interference and jamming and let many users share a band. The higher-speed Wi-Fi generations instead rely on OFDM (orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing): the band is split into dozens or hundreds of narrow subcarriers, each carrying slow symbols, and a short guard interval absorbs echoes. FFT chips make the splitting and recombining cheap. Historians debate credit here. OFDM's mathematics was explored at Bell Labs and elsewhere in the 1960s and 1970s, so CSIRO did not invent OFDM itself; its patent concerned a particular way of combining such techniques for fast indoor wireless networking, and its validity was contested in court before companies settled. Lamarr's story raises a different debate: her patent was real and clever, but it was not adopted at the time, and the spread-spectrum systems built later drew on many independent lines of work. Mobile networks add another layer: the shift from 3G to 4G LTE also adopted OFDM-based methods, plus MIMO, which uses several antennas to send separate streams through the same frequencies by exploiting the different paths the waves take. In other words, echoes that once wrecked signals are now used to multiply capacity. Today, mobile data and Wi-Fi together carry a large share of the world's last-hop traffic, yet nearly all of it is handed to fibre within a few kilometres.","funFact":"Hedy Lamarr and George Antheil's 1942 patent suggested using a standard 88-note player-piano roll so a radio signal could hop among 88 frequencies, the same number as the keys on a piano.","check":{"q":"Does the name 'Wi-Fi' officially stand for 'wireless fidelity'?","a":"No, it is a brand name created in 1999 that does not officially stand for anything","wrong":["Yes, it was named by Hedy Lamarr in 1942","Yes, the IEEE chose it as the official name in 1997","No, it stands for 'wide frequency'"]},"sources":[{"title":"US Patent 2,292,387: Secret communication system","publisher":"Google Patents","url":"https://patents.google.com/patent/US2292387A/en"},{"title":"Wireless LAN","publisher":"CSIRO","url":"https://www.csiro.au/en/research/technology-space/it/wireless-lan"},{"title":"Who we are: History","publisher":"Wi-Fi Alliance","url":"https://www.wi-fi.org/who-we-are/history"},{"title":"IEEE 802.11","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_802.11"},{"title":"ALOHAnet","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ALOHAnet"},{"title":"Apple Reinvents the Phone with iPhone","publisher":"Apple Newsroom","url":"https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2007/01/09Apple-Reinvents-the-Phone-with-iPhone/"},{"title":"Facts and Figures 2024: Mobile network coverage","publisher":"International Telecommunication Union (ITU)","url":"https://www.itu.int/itu-d/reports/statistics/2024/11/10/ff24-mobile-network-coverage/"},{"title":"SMS","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SMS"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/14-1.jpg","alt":"Hedy Lamarr in a 1940 publicity photo by MGM. She was a film star and an inventor.","file":"Hedy lamarr - 1940.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hedy_lamarr_-_1940.jpg","author":"MGM / Clarence Bull","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/14-2.jpg","alt":"Drawing sheet from patent 2,292,387 (1942), the 'Secret Communication System' by Hedy Lamarr and George Antheil.","file":"Patent Case File No. 2,292,387, Secret Communication System, Inventors Hedy Kiesler Markey and George Antheil - DPLA - 128f022cfd9421aa10de72958a7edf90 (page 7).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Patent_Case_File_No._2,292,387,_Secret_Communication_System,_Inventors_Hedy_Kiesler_Markey_and_George_Antheil_-_DPLA_-_128f022cfd9421aa10de72958a7edf90_(page_7).jpg","author":"Department of Commerce. Patent and Trademark Office. (1975)","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/14-3.jpg","alt":"The back of a first-generation Apple iPhone (8 GB), the model launched in 2007.","file":"IPhone First Generation 8GB (3680480234).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IPhone_First_Generation_8GB_(3680480234).jpg","author":"Carl Berkeley from Riverside California","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3912},{"n":15,"title":"Boom, Bust and a Brand-New Economy","when":"1994 – 2010s","summary":"In the 1990s people started using the Internet to buy books, sell old toys, bank, learn and chat face to face across oceans. Investors got so excited that they poured money into almost any company with '.com' in its name, until the bubble went POP in 2000. But the best ideas survived, and some of the most useful things online, like Wikipedia and Linux, were built by volunteers for free.","sections":[{"heading":"A Bookshop With No Shelves","paragraphs":["In the spring of 1994, a 30-year-old computer scientist named Jeff Bezos was working at a New York investment firm called D. E. Shaw. He came across a striking number: use of the World Wide Web was growing at a rate of more than 2,000 percent a year. Bezos decided that something growing that fast was a chance he could not ignore. He made a list of products that might sell well online and picked books.","Why books? A normal bookshop, even a huge one, can only hold perhaps 100,000 or so different titles on its shelves. But millions of different books were in print. A website does not need shelves. It can list every book that exists and then order the right copy from a warehouse once a customer has paid. Books were also easy to describe, easy to post, and every copy of a particular book is identical, so customers did not need to touch them before buying.","Bezos quit his job, and he and his wife MacKenzie moved across the United States to the Seattle area. Seattle had plenty of skilled computer programmers and was close to a big book distribution warehouse in Roseburg, Oregon. On 5 July 1994 he founded the company in Bellevue, Washington, working out of the garage of a rented house. Its first name was Cadabra, as in 'abracadabra', but people kept hearing it as 'cadaver', which is a dead body. By November he had renamed it Amazon, after the river that carries more water than any other on Earth. The name suggested something enormous, and it also came near the top of alphabetical lists.","The website opened to the public on 16 July 1995 and boldly called itself 'Earth's Biggest Bookstore'. The tiny team built desks out of wooden doors screwed onto legs, because doors were cheaper than real desks. In the early days a bell rang every time an order came in, until there were so many orders that the ringing became unbearable and the bell was switched off. Within its first two months, orders arrived from every US state and from more than 45 other countries. One of the very first books bought is often said to have been a book about how computers might think up ideas, called 'Fluid Concepts and Creative Analogies' by Douglas Hofstadter.","Amazon sold shares to the public on the stock market in May 1997. Soon it was selling music and videos, then toys, electronics and almost anything else. Yet for years it made no profit at all, because it spent every dollar on growing. Its first profitable quarter did not come until the end of 2001. Many people laughed at it as an example of a company that was all hype. As you will see, the laughter turned out to be premature."]},{"heading":"A Broken Laser Pointer and a Pez Tale","paragraphs":["Amazon sold new things. A different idea was to help ordinary people sell things to each other. On the Labor Day weekend of 1995, a 28-year-old French-born Iranian-American programmer called Pierre Omidyar wrote some code at his home in San Jose, California, and launched a page called AuctionWeb. It was a hobby, a side project on his personal website. Anyone could list an item, and other people could bid for it, just like at an auction, except the auction lasted days and the bidders could be anywhere in the world.","One of the first things Omidyar listed was a broken laser pointer. To his amazement, it sold for 14 dollars and 83 cents. He emailed the buyer to make sure they understood that it did not work. The buyer replied that he collected broken laser pointers. Omidyar realised that the Internet could connect people with very unusual interests: somewhere out there, for almost anything, there was someone who wanted it.","AuctionWeb made about 1,000 dollars in its first month, mostly from small fees on sales. Soon it was so busy that Omidyar's web host made him upgrade to a business account, and he began charging sellers properly. He hired his first employee, Chris Agarpao, and then a president, Jeff Skoll. In September 1997 the site was renamed eBay. The name came from Omidyar's consulting company, Echo Bay Technology Group. He had wanted echobay.com, but a gold-mining company already had it, so he shortened it to eBay.com. In March 1998 Meg Whitman became chief executive, and that September eBay sold shares to the public. On their first day the shares jumped from 18 dollars to 53 dollars.","You may have heard that eBay was started so that Omidyar's girlfriend (later his wife) could trade Pez sweet dispensers with other collectors. It is a charming story, but it is not how eBay began. It was invented in 1997 by a member of eBay's public relations team, who thought reporters would find a love story about sweet dispensers more interesting than a programmer experimenting with an auction website. She was right: newspapers repeated it for years. It is a good example of why historians check where a story first came from.","eBay had a real problem to solve: how could strangers trust each other? A buyer in Texas might send money to a seller in Scotland and never receive the item. eBay's answer was the feedback system. After each sale, buyer and seller could rate each other as positive, neutral or negative and leave a comment. Over time, each person built a public score, a reputation. Honest sellers with hundreds of happy customers became trusted. This idea of ratings from strangers spread everywhere: today taxi apps, holiday rentals and online shops all use descendants of it.","Paying was the other headache. Early eBay buyers often posted paper cheques or money orders, which took days. A company called PayPal made it possible to pay by email address, and it became so popular among eBay users that eBay bought PayPal in October 2002 for about 1.4 billion dollars."]},{"heading":"The Gold Rush: The Dot-Com Boom","paragraphs":["To understand what happened next, you need to know a little about shares. When a company 'goes public', it sells small pieces of itself, called shares or stocks, on a stock market. People buy shares hoping the company will grow and the shares will become worth more. The price of a share depends on what buyers believe the company will be worth in the future, not just on what it earns today. That means excitement can push prices very high, and fear can push them very low.","Many people date the start of the boom to 9 August 1995. That day Netscape, the company that made the popular Netscape Navigator browser you met in Part 11, sold its shares to the public. They had been priced at 28 dollars each, but so many people wanted them that on the first day they shot up to around 75 dollars before settling at about 58. Netscape had barely made any profit. Suddenly the whole world noticed that a young Internet company could be worth billions on paper.","Over the next five years, investors rushed to buy shares in anything connected to the Internet. New companies were called 'dot-coms', because their names often ended in '.com'. Many of them had a simple plan called 'get big fast': spend huge amounts of money on advertising to attract as many visitors, or 'eyeballs', as possible, and worry about making a profit later. Some companies added '.com' to their names, and their share price jumped just because of it.","The NASDAQ is a US stock market index (a kind of average) that includes lots of technology companies. It rose by several hundred percent between 1995 and early 2000. In January 2000, the American football Super Bowl showed more than a dozen adverts from dot-com companies (counts range from about 12 to 19), and each 30-second advert cost around 2 million dollars. The same month, the online service AOL announced it would merge with the giant media company Time Warner, the biggest merger in history at that time. It seemed as if Internet companies were about to swallow the old world.","Some of the new companies were brilliant ideas that were simply too early. Webvan delivered groceries ordered online and built huge, expensive automated warehouses before it had enough customers. Pets.com sold pet food and supplies online, and became famous for its talking sock-puppet mascot. The trouble was that heavy bags of dog food are expensive to ship, and the company often sold items for less than they cost to buy and deliver. A European fashion website called Boo.com spent well over 100 million dollars in less than two years.","Telephone and cable companies joined the rush too. They believed Internet traffic would keep doubling at incredible speeds, so they borrowed hundreds of billions of dollars and laid enormous amounts of new fibre-optic cable, the glass threads you met in Part 13. Much of it was never switched on at first. Engineers called the unused strands 'dark fibre'."]},{"heading":"Pop! The Bubble Bursts","paragraphs":["Imagine blowing a soap bubble. It grows bigger and shinier, but the skin gets thinner and thinner. Economists use the word 'bubble' for a time when prices climb far above what things are really worth, because sooner or later the skin breaks.","On 10 March 2000 the NASDAQ index reached 5,048.62, the highest it had ever been. Then it started to fall. At first people hoped it was just a wobble. It was not. Investors began asking awkward questions: when will these companies actually make money? Many of them were spending their investors' cash so fast that they would run out within months. Once a few big investors started selling, others panicked and sold too, and prices tumbled.","The fall lasted more than two years. By October 2002 the NASDAQ had lost about 78 percent of its value from the peak. That means a share worth 100 dollars at the top might have been worth only about 22 dollars at the bottom. Estimates suggest investors lost around 5 trillion dollars on paper. Pets.com closed in November 2000, only nine months after selling its shares to the public. Webvan went bankrupt in 2001. Thousands of people who had joined exciting start-ups found themselves out of work.","Even strong companies were battered. Amazon's share price fell by more than 90 percent between late 1999 and 2001, and newspapers wondered whether it would survive. It did, by cutting costs and focusing on customers, and it finally reported a profit for a whole year in 2003. eBay, which charged fees on every sale and so made real money from the start, kept growing through the crash. Google, founded in 1998, was still a small private company and quietly kept improving its search engine.","The NASDAQ did not climb back above its March 2000 high until 2015, fifteen years later. Yet the crash did not mean the Internet had failed. People did not stop emailing, shopping or searching. What failed was the idea that any website with a catchy name would automatically become rich. A study of the period found that about half of the dot-com companies were still in business in 2004, usually smaller and humbler.","The bust even left behind useful gifts. All that dark fibre laid by bankrupt telecom companies was still in the ground. Other companies bought it cheaply, and in the following years it carried the floods of video, music and photos that people began sharing. Some of the Internet's later success was built on the leftovers of its first great crash."]},{"heading":"Banking, Booking and Homework: Daily Life Moves Online","paragraphs":["While the stock market rose and fell, ordinary life was quietly changing. In 1994, a group of pizza restaurants in Santa Cruz, California tested a service called PizzaNet that let people order Pizza Hut pizza through a web page. Around the same time, Stanford Federal Credit Union is often credited with offering some of the first Internet banking to its members. In 1995 Security First Network Bank opened as one of the first banks that existed only online, with no branches at all.","Online banking meant you could check your balance at midnight, move money between accounts, or pay a bill without queuing at a counter. To make it safe, banks relied on the encryption you will meet in Part 17, which scrambles information so that people snooping on the network cannot read it. At first many customers were nervous about typing bank details into a computer. Within about a decade, millions of people did it every day, and today many banks have closed branches because so few customers visit.","Travel changed just as much. Before the Internet, booking a flight usually meant phoning an airline or visiting a travel agent, who looked up seats on a special computer system that ordinary people could not use. In 1996 Microsoft launched Expedia, and other travel websites followed. Suddenly anyone could compare prices from many airlines and hotels at once. Booking concert tickets, cinema seats and train journeys moved online in the same way.","School and libraries changed too. Libraries replaced wooden drawers full of index cards with online catalogues, so you could find out from home whether a book was on the shelf. Project Gutenberg, started by Michael Hart back in 1971, typed up books that were old enough to be free of copyright and shared them online, and by the 2000s it offered thousands of free e-books. Pupils who once relied on a printed encyclopedia at home could now look up information on the Web, although, as Part 12 warned, they also had to learn to judge whether it was trustworthy.","Work began to move as well. Email replaced many letters and faxes. Some people started working from home a few days a week, sending files over the Internet instead of carrying them into an office. Companies discovered they could hire a programmer in India or a designer in Poland as easily as one down the street. During the COVID-19 pandemic of 2020, these habits were tested on a giant scale, when hundreds of millions of children around the world attended school through screens for weeks or months. Not everyone benefited equally, though: people without a computer or a good connection were left out, a problem Part 18 explores."]},{"heading":"An Encyclopedia Anyone Can Edit","paragraphs":["In March 2000, an Internet entrepreneur named Jimmy Wales and a philosophy PhD named Larry Sanger started an online encyclopedia called Nupedia. It was free to read, but it worked like a traditional encyclopedia: experts wrote articles, and each article went through a long, careful review by other experts. That was good for accuracy but painfully slow. After about a year, only a tiny number of articles had been completed.","Sanger heard about a kind of website called a wiki. The first wiki, WikiWikiWeb, had been created in 1995 by a programmer named Ward Cunningham. He named it after the 'Wiki Wiki' shuttle buses at Honolulu airport in Hawaii; 'wiki' is a Hawaiian word meaning 'quick'. On a wiki, any visitor can click 'edit' on a page, change it and save it immediately. Every earlier version is kept, so mistakes and vandalism can be undone.","On 10 January 2001 Sanger suggested using a wiki as a side project to feed draft articles into Nupedia. Five days later, on 15 January 2001, Wikipedia went live. The name joins 'wiki' and 'encyclopedia'. Wales is credited with the goal of a free encyclopedia anyone could edit, and Sanger with proposing the wiki method and leading the early community; the two later disagreed about who deserved how much credit, and Sanger left the project in 2002.","The side project quickly swallowed the main one. Thousands of volunteers began writing about everything from black holes to Bulgarian folk dances. Editions in other languages started in March 2001, and by the end of 2004 there were 161 of them. The English edition passed 2 million articles in 2007, and today it has more than 7 million, while Wikipedia as a whole exists in over 300 languages. Since 2003 it has been run by a non-profit charity, the Wikimedia Foundation, and it is paid for mostly by small donations rather than adverts.","How can an encyclopedia that anyone can change be reliable? Wikipedia's volunteers built rules and habits to help. Articles should take a 'neutral point of view' and every important fact should be backed up by a published, reliable source, listed in the footnotes. Editors watch lists of pages for changes, and automatic programs called bots spot obvious vandalism, like a page replaced with rude words, and reverse it within seconds. Every disagreement is argued out on a 'talk page' next to the article, so anyone can see why a decision was made.","In 2005 the science journal Nature asked experts to check a sample of science articles from Wikipedia and from the Encyclopaedia Britannica. Both had errors, and the experts found only somewhat more in Wikipedia than in Britannica. Britannica disputed the study's methods, and the argument made headlines. Most teachers today give a sensible middle answer: Wikipedia is a great place to start learning about a topic, but for important work you should follow its footnotes to the original sources and check them yourself."]},{"heading":"Free Code for Everyone: Linux and Apache","paragraphs":["Wikipedia was not the first big project built by volunteers sharing their work. Programmers had been doing it for years. To see how, you need to know about the 'kernel'. Every computer runs an operating system, the master program that starts when you switch it on and shares out the processor, memory and screen among all the other programs. At the very heart of the operating system is the kernel, the part that talks directly to the hardware.","In 1991 a 21-year-old student at the University of Helsinki in Finland, Linus Torvalds, bought a new PC and wanted to run a Unix-style operating system on it. The good ones were expensive, so he started writing his own kernel. On 25 August 1991 he posted a message to an online discussion group called comp.os.minix. He described it as 'just a hobby, won't be big and professional like gnu'. GNU was a project, begun by Richard Stallman in 1983, to create a complete free operating system, and it had produced many tools but did not yet have a working kernel of its own. Torvalds' kernel fitted the gap.","Torvalds wanted to call it Freax, but the administrator of the computer where he uploaded the files named the folder 'Linux', and the name stuck. In 1992 he released it under the GNU General Public License. This licence says that anyone may use, copy, study and change the code for free, as long as they share their improvements under the same rules. Programmers all over the world began sending in fixes and new features, using the Internet itself to coordinate their work. In 1996 Linux got its famous mascot, a cheerful penguin named Tux.","This way of working became known in 1998 as 'open source': the source code, the human-readable instructions of a program, is open for anyone to read and improve. It sounds as if it could never work. Why would people give away their work? Some did it for fun or to learn, some to build a reputation, and many because their employers found it cheaper to improve a shared version than to build a private one.","The results were astonishing. Linux now runs a huge share of the servers that power websites and online services. Since 2017, every one of the world's 500 fastest supercomputers has run Linux. And Android, the most widely used phone operating system on Earth, is built on the Linux kernel, so a hobby from a student's bedroom in Finland is now inside billions of pockets.","The Web had its own open-source hero. In early 1995 the most popular web server program was one written by Rob McCool at the NCSA in Illinois, the same centre that made the Mosaic browser. When McCool left, development stopped, so webmasters began swapping their own fixes, called 'patches', by email. In February 1995 eight of them formed the Apache Group, combined their patches, and released Apache 1.0 on 1 December 1995. In less than a year it became the most popular web server on the Internet, according to the Netcraft surveys that counted web servers, and it stayed on top for many years. People often joke that the name is a pun on 'a patchy server', because it was built from patches."]},{"heading":"Songs, Lawsuits and Calls Across the World","paragraphs":["Music was one of the first things to be turned upside down. A song on a CD takes up a lot of data, far too much to download over a dial-up modem. But a format called MP3 could squeeze a song to roughly a tenth of its size by throwing away sounds that human ears barely notice. A three-minute song became a file of about 3 megabytes, which a university network or a fast home connection could download in minutes.","In June 1999 a college student named Shawn Fanning, working with Sean Parker, launched Napster. It was a peer-to-peer program, which means that instead of downloading songs from one central website, users downloaded them directly from each other's computers. Napster just kept a giant list of who had which song. Type in the name of a band, and you could find almost any song ever recorded, including rare live recordings, for free. Students loved it. At its peak in February 2001 it had about 26 million users.","The problem was that most of the songs were copied without permission from the musicians and record companies who owned them, which is against copyright law. Record labels sued Napster in December 1999 in a case known as A&M Records v. Napster. In 2000 the heavy metal band Metallica and the rapper Dr. Dre sued too, after finding their unreleased songs being shared. Some musicians defended Napster as a way for fans to discover music, while others said it was simply taking their work without paying. In July 2001, following a court order, Napster shut down its network.","Napster lost, but it had proved that people wanted music instantly over the Internet. In April 2003 Apple opened the iTunes Music Store, selling legal downloads for 99 cents a song. A few years later, streaming services, which you will meet in Part 19, let people listen to millions of songs for a monthly fee or with adverts, without owning the files at all. The music business today earns much of its money from streaming, a model that grew out of the chaos Napster started.","Distance itself was shrinking too. In August 2003 a small team released the first public test version of Skype. It was founded by Niklas Zennström from Sweden and Janus Friis from Denmark, and the software was written by Estonian programmers Ahti Heinla, Priit Kasesalu, Jaan Tallinn and Toivo Annus. The name came from 'sky' and 'peer-to-peer'. Skype turned a person's voice into packets and sent them over the Internet, so a call from London to Sydney cost nothing beyond the Internet connection, while an ordinary long-distance phone call could cost a lot per minute.","Soon Skype added video calls through a webcam. For families split between countries, it was life-changing: grandparents could watch a grandchild take first steps on a screen, and soldiers and sailors far from home could see their children's faces. eBay bought Skype in 2005 for about 2.6 billion dollars, and Microsoft bought it in 2011 for 8.5 billion. Skype itself was finally retired in May 2025, with users moved to Microsoft Teams, but by then video calling had become something billions of people did without a second thought.","By the end of the 2000s, the shape of a new economy was clear. Shopping, money, music, knowledge and conversation had all moved online, built by companies, by volunteers, and by a few rule-breakers. But almost all of it still had a familiar pattern: a company or an organised project made the content, and everyone else read, watched or bought it. A new idea was about to flip that around. What if ordinary people, not companies, created most of what was on the Web?"]}],"deepDive":"Economists still argue about why the dot-com bubble grew so large. One explanation is 'irrational exuberance', a phrase used by US Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan in a speech in December 1996: investors convinced themselves that old rules about profits no longer applied to a 'New Economy'. Another points to cheap borrowing, to the many ordinary people trading shares online for the first time through cheap Internet brokers, and to the way bankers earned large fees for bringing new companies to the stock market. A third view says investors were not entirely foolish: they were right that the Internet would change everything, but wrong about which companies would win and how quickly. The numbers are dramatic. The NASDAQ Composite rose several hundred percent between 1995 and its close of 5,048.62 on 10 March 2000, then lost about 78 percent by October 2002, wiping out an estimated 5 trillion dollars of paper wealth. By November 2000 Internet stocks alone had fallen about 75 percent from their highs. Telecom equipment makers had invested over 500 billion dollars, largely with borrowed money, in the five years after the US Telecommunications Act of 1996. Yet roughly half of dot-com firms still existed in 2004, and the survivors, Amazon and eBay among them, turned out to be far more valuable than their peak prices suggested. Historians of technology note a recurring pattern, seen also in the British 'railway mania' of the 1840s: a genuine new network attracts a speculative frenzy, the frenzy collapses, and the over-built infrastructure is then used cheaply by the next generation. Open source raises its own debates. Economists once struggled to explain why skilled programmers would donate work worth millions. Studies point to a mix of motives: learning, reputation that leads to jobs, the satisfaction of solving problems, and companies' self-interest in sharing the cost of common tools. Wikipedia's reliability is likewise debated; its strength is transparency, since every edit and argument is public, but coverage is uneven, and topics favoured by its mostly volunteer editors receive more attention than others.","funFact":"The first item sold on eBay's predecessor, AuctionWeb, was a broken laser pointer that went for 14 dollars and 83 cents to a buyer who said he collected broken laser pointers.","check":{"q":"On what date did Wikipedia launch?","a":"15 January 2001","wrong":["4 February 2004","16 July 1995","10 March 2000"]},"sources":[{"title":"Dot-com bubble","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dot-com_bubble"},{"title":"Amazon (company)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_(company)"},{"title":"eBay","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EBay"},{"title":"Wikipedia","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia"},{"title":"History of Linux","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Linux"},{"title":"About the Apache HTTP Server Project","publisher":"Apache Software Foundation","url":"https://httpd.apache.org/ABOUT_APACHE.html"},{"title":"Napster","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Napster"},{"title":"Skype","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skype"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/15-1.jpg","alt":"The NASDAQ Composite stock index from 1994 to 2004, soaring to a peak in 2000 and then crashing.","file":"NASDAQ IXIC - dot-com bubble.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASDAQ_IXIC_-_dot-com_bubble.png","author":"Made by ed g2s • talk.","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/15-2.jpg","alt":"Linus Torvalds, creator of the Linux kernel, speaking at LinuxCon Europe 2014.","file":"LinuxCon Europe Linus Torvalds 03 (cropped).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LinuxCon_Europe_Linus_Torvalds_03_(cropped).jpg","author":"Krd (photo)Von Sprat (crop/extraction)","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/15-3.jpg","alt":"Tux the penguin, the friendly mascot of Linux, first drawn by Larry Ewing in 1996.","file":"Tux.svg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tux.svg","author":"Larry Ewing, Simon Budig, Garrett LeSage","license":"Attribution"}],"words":4341},{"n":16,"title":"Everyone's a Publisher: Social Media","when":"1997 – today","summary":"For hundreds of years, only newspapers, TV stations and book publishers could share news and stories with millions of people. Then the Internet let anyone make a homepage, write a blog, post a video or share a photo with the whole world. That brought families closer and helped people learn and help each other, but it also brought new problems, like fake news and apps designed to keep you scrolling.","sections":[{"heading":"A Little Home on the Web","paragraphs":["Think about how news travelled in 1990. If you wanted thousands of people to read your story, you needed a newspaper, a magazine, a radio station or a TV channel to agree to publish it. Printing presses and broadcast towers were expensive, so a small number of editors decided what most people saw and heard. Ordinary people could write letters to the editor, and hope.","The World Wide Web changed that. As you saw in Part 10, Tim Berners-Lee designed the Web so that anyone with a server could publish pages. But in the early 1990s, running your own web server was difficult. What ordinary people needed was a free place to put a page.","In 1994 David Bohnett and John Rezner started a company in California called Beverly Hills Internet, which soon became GeoCities. It gave people free space to build their own web pages, called 'homepages'. GeoCities organised them like a city. Pages were grouped into themed 'neighbourhoods': one for science fiction fans, one for computer topics, one for families, and so on. Each page had an address, almost like a house number on a street, and users were called 'homesteaders', the old American word for settlers who claimed land.","Visiting a GeoCities page in the late 1990s was an adventure. People wrote in bright colours on starry backgrounds, added spinning animated pictures, flashing 'Under Construction' signs, music that started playing automatically, and little counters showing how many visitors had come. Some pages were about a pet hamster, others were detailed fan guides to video games, or family histories carefully typed out by grandparents. There were millions of them. In 1999 the search company Yahoo! bought GeoCities for about 3.6 billion dollars in shares.","Around that time GeoCities taught an early lesson about privacy. The US Federal Trade Commission found that the company had told members their personal information would be used in one way, while actually sharing it in other ways, and ordered it to stop. Later, when Yahoo! changed the rules to claim rights over what users had written, homesteaders protested loudly and Yahoo! backed down. Even in the 1990s, people cared about who controlled what they had made.","Yahoo! closed GeoCities in the United States on 26 October 2009. Volunteers from the Internet Archive and a group called Archive Team raced to copy as many pages as they could before the deadline, and Archive Team later shared about 641 gigabytes of rescued pages. That is why you can still visit some of those sparkling, chaotic homepages today. It is a reminder that what we put online is not automatically kept forever, and not automatically deleted either."]},{"heading":"From Weblogs to Blogs","paragraphs":["A homepage was a bit like a poster: you made it and then mostly left it alone. Some people wanted something more like a diary, updated every day with new thoughts and links. In 1994 a college student named Justin Hall started a website called links.net, where he wrote about his life and pointed to interesting things he had found on the Web. It is often called one of the very first blogs, though, as with many 'firsts', other people were doing similar things around the same time.","In 1997 a writer named Jorn Barger called this kind of site a 'weblog', a log, or record, of the web. In 1999 a designer named Peter Merholz jokingly split the word as 'we blog', and the short word 'blog' stuck. A person who writes one is a blogger, and each entry is a post. Posts appear with the newest at the top, and readers can usually leave comments underneath.","At first you needed to know some HTML to keep a blog. Then, in 1999, a small company called Pyra Labs, founded by Evan Williams and Meg Hourihan, launched Blogger, which let anyone start a blog by filling in a simple form. The same year LiveJournal appeared, and in 2003 the free blogging software WordPress was released. WordPress is open source, like the Linux and Apache software in Part 15, and today it powers a very large share of all websites.","Blogs gave a voice to people who had never had one. A teacher could share lesson ideas with teachers on other continents. Someone with a rare illness could find others with the same condition. Hobbyists who knew more about old trains, knitting or beetles than any newspaper reporter could write about them in detail. Some bloggers became journalists, uncovering stories that big news organisations had missed, while others simply wrote about their cats.","There was a catch that would become important later. A newspaper has editors who check facts before printing, and it can be sued if it publishes lies about people. A blog might have no editor at all. Readers suddenly had to do more of the checking themselves: who wrote this, how do they know, and do other trustworthy sources agree? Those questions, which we will come back to in Part 18, became far more urgent as social media grew."]},{"heading":"The First Social Networks","paragraphs":["Blogs were about publishing. Social networks added something new: a map of who knows whom. You may have heard of the idea of 'six degrees of separation', the claim that any two people on Earth are linked by a short chain of friends of friends, perhaps about six steps long. In 1997 an entrepreneur in New York named Andrew Weinreich launched a website named after that idea: SixDegrees.com.","SixDegrees let you create a profile and list your friends, family and acquaintances, and even invite people who were not members yet. You could then send messages and post on bulletin boards that reached people up to three steps away from you in the network. At its height it had about 3.5 million registered members, and in December 1999 a company called YouthStream Media Networks bought it for 125 million dollars. But most people were still using slow dial-up connections, few of their friends were online, and digital cameras were rare, so there was not much to do there. SixDegrees shut down around the end of 2000.","The next attempt came from a Canadian programmer in California called Jonathan Abrams. He founded Friendster in 2002 and launched it in 2003. Friendster let you see your friends' friends and was meant partly to help people meet new people. It grew to about 3 million users within months, and in 2003 Google offered to buy it for 30 million dollars, an offer Friendster turned down. In the United States it was soon overtaken by rivals, but it became hugely popular in Southeast Asia, where most of its later visitors came from, before finally closing in 2015.","Myspace launched on 1 August 2003. It was built in about ten days by a team at a Los Angeles internet company, and its early leaders included Chris DeWolfe and Tom Anderson. When you joined Myspace, Tom was automatically your first friend, so millions of people knew his smiling profile picture. Myspace let users decorate their profile pages with their own colours, backgrounds and songs, a little like GeoCities pages, and it became especially important for musicians, who could upload songs and gather fans without a record deal.","Myspace exploded. In July 2005 the media company News Corporation bought it for 580 million dollars. In June 2006 it became the most visited website in the United States, ahead even of Yahoo! Mail and Google Search, and by 2008 it had over 100 million visitors a month. Then, just as quickly, people began leaving for a newer network with a plainer blue design. The lesson for social networks was clear: people go where their friends are, and if their friends move, they move too."]},{"heading":"The Facebook, the Feed and the Little Blue Bird","paragraphs":["In February 2004, a 19-year-old Harvard University student named Mark Zuckerberg launched a website called TheFacebook from his dormitory room, together with fellow students Eduardo Saverin, Andrew McCollum, Dustin Moskovitz and Chris Hughes. Many American colleges printed 'face books', directories with a photo and a few details about each student. TheFacebook put that idea online, and let students connect as friends and see each other's profiles.","At first only people with a Harvard email address could join. That made it feel exclusive and trustworthy, because everyone was a real student using their real name. Within a month it opened to Stanford, Columbia and Yale, then to hundreds of other universities. In 2005 the company dropped 'The' from its name and opened a version for high schools. In September 2006 it opened to anyone aged 13 or over with an email address. The same year it introduced the News Feed, a constantly updated list of what your friends were doing. Some users protested that it felt like spying, but the feed became the heart of Facebook, and of almost every social app since.","Facebook kept growing at an almost unbelievable speed. It passed Myspace in worldwide visitors around 2007 or 2008, reached 500 million users in 2010 and one billion monthly users in October 2012, the same year it sold shares to the public. In 2021 the company renamed itself Meta, though the Facebook website and app kept their names.","Meanwhile in San Francisco, a podcasting company called Odeo was struggling. In 2006 a small team there, including Jack Dorsey, Noah Glass, Biz Stone and Evan Williams (the same Evan Williams who had helped create Blogger), built a service first spelled 'twttr'. The idea was to share short status updates, like a text message sent to everyone who followed you. Posts were limited to 140 characters, short enough to fit in a mobile phone text message with room left for a username. Twitter launched publicly in July 2006. Its big breakthrough came at a technology festival in Austin, Texas, in March 2007, when the number of messages per day jumped from about 20,000 to about 60,000 as people used it to tell each other which talks and parties to go to.","Twitter users invented many of its rules themselves. In 2007 a user named Chris Messina suggested putting the # symbol in front of a word, like #science, to group posts on the same topic. The 'hashtag' spread to almost every other social network. Twitter doubled its post limit to 280 characters in 2017 and was renamed X in 2023.","Photos became the next big thing once phones had good cameras. Kevin Systrom and Mike Krieger had built a check-in app called Burbn, but noticed that people mostly liked its photo features. They stripped everything else away and on 6 October 2010 released Instagram for the iPhone, with filters that made phone snapshots look like old-fashioned prints. It gained a million users in about two months. Facebook bought Instagram in 2012 for about 1 billion dollars, when the company had only around a dozen employees.","Later apps kept changing the shape of social media. Snapchat, launched in 2011, made photos that disappeared after being viewed. TikTok, launched internationally in 2017 by the Chinese company ByteDance, built its whole design around short videos chosen for you by a computer program called a recommendation algorithm, rather than mainly by who your friends are. Each new app borrowed ideas from the last and added a twist."]},{"heading":"Nineteen Seconds at the Zoo","paragraphs":["Sharing videos was much harder than sharing words or pictures. Video files were huge, and different computers needed different programs to play them. In early 2005 three former employees of PayPal, the payment company you met in Part 15, decided to fix that. Chad Hurley, Steve Chen and Jawed Karim registered the name youtube.com in February 2005. Their website would convert any uploaded video into a format that played straight away in a web browser, and let anyone copy a bit of code to show the video on their own blog or Myspace page.","On 23 April 2005, Jawed Karim uploaded the very first YouTube video. It is called 'Me at the zoo'. His high school friend Yakov Lapitsky filmed him standing in front of two elephants at the San Diego Zoo in California. Karim, wearing a dark jacket, turns to the camera and explains that the cool thing about these animals is that they have really, really, really long trunks. That is pretty much it. The whole video lasts 19 seconds.","It does not sound like a historic moment, but in a way it was perfect. It was not made by a film studio or a TV channel. It was made by an ordinary person, with an ordinary camera, about whatever he felt like. That is exactly the kind of video YouTube would soon be full of. 'Me at the zoo' is still online, and it has been watched hundreds of millions of times.","YouTube grew so quickly that by October 2006 Google bought it for 1.65 billion dollars. Since then it has become one of the most visited websites in the world. People upload cooking lessons, science experiments, music, video-game walkthroughs, lectures from top universities and videos explaining how to fix almost anything, from a bicycle chain to a leaking tap. In the United States, about 84 percent of adults say they use YouTube, more than any other social platform, according to the Pew Research Center.","Something else quietly happened too. YouTube and similar sites let creators earn money by sharing in the income from adverts shown alongside their videos. A teenager filming in a bedroom could build an audience bigger than some TV channels. 'YouTuber' and 'content creator' became real jobs, and many children today name them as their dream careers. Remember those dark fibres from the dot-com crash? The huge amounts of cheap bandwidth they provided helped make streaming billions of videos possible."]},{"heading":"The Bright Side: Connection, Creativity and Help","paragraphs":["Social media has done a great deal of good. For families spread across countries, it means grandparents can see new baby photos minutes after they are taken, and cousins on different continents can grow up knowing each other. People who feel alone because of an unusual illness, a disability, or simply an unusual hobby can find others who understand them.","It has become a giant classroom. You can learn to play the guitar, solve a Rubik's cube, draw comics or understand black holes from people who are passionate about those things. Museums, space agencies and scientists post their discoveries directly. When NASA lands a rover on Mars, millions of people watch it live online and ask questions in the comments.","Creativity has exploded. Musicians who once needed a record company can release songs directly to fans. Young animators, writers, game designers and filmmakers can show their work to the world, get feedback and improve. Many famous singers, comedians and authors today were first noticed online.","Social media can also help in emergencies. After the earthquake in Haiti in January 2010, people in the United States gave millions of dollars to the Red Cross simply by sending a text message, setting a record for mobile donations, with the appeal spread widely through social networks. In 2014 the 'Ice Bucket Challenge', in which people filmed themselves being drenched in icy water and challenged friends to do the same, raised about 115 million dollars for research into the disease ALS in about two months, passing 100 million dollars in its first month. Facebook added a 'Safety Check' feature in 2014 so that people near a disaster could tell their friends and family with one tap that they were safe. Volunteers use online maps and posts to organise rescues, share where shelters are, and find missing pets.","Social media also gives people a way to speak up. Communities can draw attention to problems that newspapers ignore, and citizens can film events as they happen. That power to organise is one of the most debated parts of the story, as the next section shows."]},{"heading":"The Hard Side: Hooks, Comparisons and Fake News","paragraphs":["Most social media apps are free to use. That raises a fair question: how do the companies make billions of dollars? The main answer is advertising. The longer you spend in an app, the more adverts you see and the more the company learns about what you like, so it can show you adverts you are more likely to click. This creates a strong reason for companies to design apps that are very hard to put down.","Some of these designs are easy to spot once you know about them. A feed that never ends, so there is no natural place to stop, is called 'infinite scroll'. Its inventor, the designer Aza Raskin, has said he regrets how it is used. Videos that start playing automatically, red notification badges, streaks you lose if you skip a day, and counts of likes all work on the same part of the brain that enjoys surprises and rewards. You never know whether the next refresh will bring something exciting, which is a bit like a slot machine.","Comparing yourself to others is another problem. People usually post their best moments: the holiday, the perfect photo, the prize. Scrolling through everyone else's highlights can make your normal day feel dull, even though their normal days are just as ordinary. Filters can change faces and bodies so that photos are not even real. In 2023 the US Surgeon General, America's top public health official, issued an advisory warning that heavy social media use can carry real risks for young people's mental health and sleep, while also noting benefits such as finding friends and support. Taking breaks, turning off notifications and talking about how apps make you feel are all sensible habits.","Then there is misinformation, false information spread as if it were true. In 2018 researchers at MIT studied over a decade of stories shared on Twitter and found that false news spread faster and reached more people than true news, often because false stories were more surprising and emotional. A lie can travel around the world before anyone has checked it. Fake news has spread about elections, about health and vaccines, and about disasters while they were still happening.","Social media's role in big political events is debated too. The 'Arab Spring' was a wave of protests across North Africa and the Middle East from late 2010 to 2012. In Tunisia, videos filmed on phones and shared online helped spread news of the protests. In Egypt, a Facebook page called 'We Are All Khaled Said' helped call people to demonstrations in January 2011, and the government shut down most Internet access for several days, which did not stop the protests. Some reporters called these 'Facebook revolutions' or 'Twitter revolutions'. Many historians and political scientists think that goes too far. People protested because of deep problems such as unemployment, high prices and unfair governments. Social media helped spread information and organise faster, but it did not cause the protests, and in some countries where few people used it, it played only a small part. Governments, too, learned to use the same tools to watch and track protesters."]},{"heading":"Why Thirteen? A Law About Children's Data","paragraphs":["If you have ever tried to sign up for a social media app, you have probably been asked your age, and told you must be at least 13. Why 13, and not 10 or 16? The answer is a US law passed in 1998, the Children's Online Privacy Protection Act, usually shortened to COPPA.","In the late 1990s, people noticed that some websites aimed at children were asking them for names, home addresses, email addresses and other personal details, sometimes in exchange for games or prizes, and then using that information for marketing. Congress passed COPPA in 1998 and asked the Federal Trade Commission, the government agency that protects consumers, to write detailed rules. The rules came into force on 21 April 2000 and were updated in 2013 and again in 2025.","COPPA says that websites and apps aimed at children under 13, or that know a user is under 13, must tell parents what information they collect and get a parent's verifiable permission before collecting personal information from that child. Getting reliable parental permission for millions of users is complicated and expensive. So most social media companies decided simply not to allow users under 13 at all. That is why 13 became the common minimum age around the world, even in countries with their own laws. Some countries now set higher ages for some services; under Europe's data rules, for example, the age can be as high as 16 depending on the country.","It is worth being clear about what the rule means. The age limit is mainly about protecting children's personal data, not a promise that apps are perfectly safe for everyone over 13. Lying about your age to get in removes the extra protections the law was meant to give. Some companies have built special versions for younger users, such as YouTube Kids, launched in 2015, with stricter controls and parents involved.","Social media turned the Internet from a library into a giant town square, where billions of people talk, share, argue, joke and help each other at once. It made everyone a publisher. But town squares attract more than friendly neighbours. Where lots of people gather, so do tricksters and thieves, looking for passwords, money and personal information. And the Internet, designed by researchers who mostly trusted each other, was not built with security in mind."]}],"deepDive":"Researchers who study social media often describe a shift from 'Web 1.0', where a relatively small number of organisations published and most people only read, to 'Web 2.0', a term popularised around 2004 by the publisher Tim O'Reilly, where the users themselves supply most of the content. The economics behind that shift matter. Social platforms are 'two-sided markets': users get the service free, and advertisers pay to reach them. Revenue grows with attention, so platforms measure and optimise engagement, the time and interactions users give them. Recommendation algorithms rank posts by predicted engagement, and critics argue that this tends to reward content that provokes strong emotions, including anger. A widely cited 2018 study in the journal Science by Soroush Vosoughi, Deb Roy and Sinan Aral analysed about 126,000 story cascades on Twitter between 2006 and 2017 and found that false stories spread farther, faster and more broadly than true ones, and that humans, not automated bots, were mainly responsible. Network effects explain both the rapid rise and the sudden fall of networks such as Friendster and Myspace: a network's value to each member grows with the number of their friends on it, so once people start leaving, the decline can speed up. Scholars disagree about how much social media affects mental health. Many studies find small average links between heavy use and lower well-being, while noting that it is hard to prove cause and effect and that effects differ greatly between individuals; this is why the 2023 US Surgeon General's advisory called for more research while urging caution. The Arab Spring debate is similar. Writers such as Evgeny Morozov warned against 'cyber-utopianism', the belief that technology by itself spreads freedom, and pointed out that authoritarian governments also use social media for surveillance and propaganda. Most researchers now see platforms as amplifiers and accelerators of movements whose causes lie offline. Finally, COPPA's under-13 threshold was a policy compromise of the 1990s; legislators have since debated raising protections for older teenagers, and several countries have introduced or proposed higher minimum ages for social media.","funFact":"The first video ever uploaded to YouTube, 'Me at the zoo', is just 19 seconds long and shows co-founder Jawed Karim talking about elephants' long trunks at the San Diego Zoo.","check":{"q":"Why do most social media apps require users to be at least 13?","a":"Because of the US COPPA law (1998), which requires parental permission to collect personal data from children under 13","wrong":["Because the Internet was invented 13 years after the telephone","Because computers cannot recognise faces of children under 13","Because a United Nations treaty bans children under 13 from using phones"]},"sources":[{"title":"GeoCities","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GeoCities"},{"title":"Myspace","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myspace"},{"title":"History of Facebook","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Facebook"},{"title":"Twitter","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twitter"},{"title":"Me at the zoo","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Me_at_the_zoo"},{"title":"Social media's role in the Arab Spring","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_media%27s_role_in_the_Arab_Spring"},{"title":"Complying with COPPA: Frequently Asked Questions","publisher":"US Federal Trade Commission","url":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-coppa-frequently-asked-questions"},{"title":"Social Media Fact Sheet","publisher":"Pew Research Center","url":"https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/fact-sheet/social-media/"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/16-1.jpg","alt":"Jawed Karim, one of the three founders of YouTube, photographed in 2008.","file":"Jawed Karim 2008.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jawed_Karim_2008.jpg","author":"Picture taken by Robin BrownOriginal uploaded by Jawed (Transfered by Quibik)","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/16-2.jpg","alt":"A huge crowd fills Tahrir Square in Cairo at night during the Egyptian protests of February 2011.","file":"Tahrir Square - February 10, 2011.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tahrir_Square_-_February_10,_2011.png","author":"Jonathan Rashad","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":3869},{"n":17,"title":"Hackers, Viruses and Secret Codes","when":"1970s – today","summary":"The early Internet trusted everybody, so tricksters soon found ways to sneak in with viruses, worms and fake messages. Clever people fought back with secret codes that anyone can lock but only you can unlock. That little padlock in your browser is one of those codes at work!","sections":[{"heading":"A Network Built on Trust","paragraphs":["Imagine a club so small that every member knows every other member by name. Nobody locks the clubhouse door, nobody checks who is coming in, and if a note is pinned to the noticeboard signed \"Bob\", everyone assumes Bob wrote it. That is roughly what the early ARPANET was like. In 1971 it linked only a couple of dozen computers, mostly at universities and research labs, and the people using them were scientists, engineers and students who often knew each other personally or had met at conferences.","The builders of the network had a very clear job: move packets from one place to another, reliably and cheaply. They were brilliant at it. But security was not on their list of goals. The packet-switching rules you met earlier in this story, and later TCP/IP, carried a sender's address on every packet, yet nothing checked that the address was true. Email worked the same way. When you sent a message, your computer simply wrote who it was from, and the receiving computer believed it.","Why would clever people design things like that? Computers were rare and lived in locked machine rooms, and anyone using the network already had permission to be in a university or government lab. Extra checks would have made everything slower and more complicated for an experiment that might not even work. The 'end-to-end' idea also meant the middle of the network was kept simple on purpose: routers just passed packets along, and any clever work, including security, was left to the computers at the edges.","Even in this friendly world, the first hints of trouble appeared early. In 1971 Bob Thomas, a programmer at BBN, the company that had built the ARPANET's message-switching computers, wrote an experimental program called Creeper. It hopped from one DEC PDP-10 computer to another across the network and printed a cheeky message on the screen: \"I'M THE CREEPER : CATCH ME IF YOU CAN.\" Ray Tomlinson, the same engineer who had just sent the first network email, improved it so that it copied itself instead of just moving. Then he wrote a second program, Reaper, which travelled around the network looking for Creeper and deleting it.","Creeper did no damage. At most a few dozen machines could have been visited, and it was more of a playful test than an attack. But it proved something important: a program could travel across a network all by itself. Many historians call Creeper the first computer worm, and Reaper the first antivirus program.","As the years passed, the club grew into a city, and then into a planet; in 1988 the Morris worm, which you read about earlier, slowed thousands of computers to a crawl. Today about six billion people use the Internet. Most are honest, but not everyone. The trusting design is still underneath everything, so engineers have spent decades adding locks, alarms and guards on top. This part is the story of those locks, and of the tricksters they are meant to stop."]},{"heading":"A Zoo of Malware","paragraphs":["Harmful software has a general name: malware, short for 'malicious software'. Malicious means meant to cause harm. Just as animals in a zoo are sorted into groups, security experts sort malware by how it spreads and what it does. Knowing the names helps you understand the news.","A virus is a piece of code that attaches itself to something else, such as a program or a document, a bit like a burr sticking to a dog's fur. It only spreads when that thing is shared and opened. One of the earliest known personal-computer viruses, Elk Cloner, was written in 1982 by Rich Skrenta, a 15-year-old in Pennsylvania, as a prank. It travelled on Apple II floppy disks, and every 50th time an infected disk started up, it showed a short silly poem on the screen. In 1986 two brothers in Lahore, Pakistan, Basit and Amjad Farooq Alvi, wrote a program called Brain that spread on floppy disks for IBM PCs. It is often listed as the first PC virus.","A worm is different: it does not need a person to share anything. It searches the network for computers with a weak spot and copies itself onto them directly, then uses each new computer to look for more. That is why worms can spread astonishingly fast. Creeper was a gentle worm. The Morris worm of 1988 was an accidental disaster. In May 2000 the ILOVEYOU worm, which began in the Philippines, arrived as an email with the subject line 'ILOVEYOU' and an attachment pretending to be a love letter. When people opened it, it mailed itself to everyone in their address book. Within days it had reached millions of computers around the world.","A Trojan, or Trojan horse, is named after an ancient Greek legend. In the story, Greek soldiers hid inside a giant wooden horse that the people of Troy dragged into their city as a gift. A Trojan program works the same way: it pretends to be something useful or fun, like a free game or a 'speed booster', but secretly does something else, such as letting a stranger control the computer.","Ransomware is malware that scrambles a person's or company's files and then demands money, a 'ransom', to unscramble them. The most famous example struck on 12 May 2017. It was called WannaCry, and within a day it had hit something like 200,000 to 300,000 computers in about 150 countries. In Britain, parts of the National Health Service had to cancel appointments and send some patients to other hospitals while staff worked with pen and paper. There is a twist in that story you will meet later in this part: Microsoft had already released a fix for the weak spot WannaCry used, about two months earlier. Many of the computers it hit simply had not installed it.","The zoo also holds spyware, which secretly watches what you do, and keyloggers, which record every key you press. The good news is that the same few habits protect against almost all of them: keep software updated, install apps only from trusted places, and think before opening anything unexpected."]},{"heading":"White Hats, Black Hats","paragraphs":["When people hear the word 'hacker', they often picture a villain in a dark hoodie. But the word started out meaning something much nicer. At MIT in the late 1950s and 1960s, a 'hack' was a clever trick or an elegant solution, and a hacker was someone who loved exploring how systems worked and making them do surprising things. Many of the people who built the early Internet proudly called themselves hackers in exactly that sense.","Security experts borrowed an idea from old cowboy films, where the hero often wore a white hat and the villain a black one. A 'black hat' breaks into computers to steal, spy or cause damage. A 'white hat' uses the very same skills to find weaknesses so they can be fixed before criminals find them. In between are 'grey hats', who sometimes break rules but without meaning harm. White-hat work is a real, respected career. Companies hire 'penetration testers' to attack their own systems, with permission, and write reports on every hole they find.","One of the most dramatic white-hat moments came during the WannaCry outbreak in 2017. A young British researcher named Marcus Hutchins was studying the malware's code and noticed that it kept checking whether a strange, unregistered web address existed. He registered that address himself, which reportedly cost only about ten dollars. To everyone's surprise, that act worked like an emergency off switch: the worm checked the address, found it existed, and stopped spreading to new computers. It did not rescue machines already hit, but it slowed the outbreak enormously. Security people call this a 'kill switch'.","White hats also follow a code of conduct called responsible disclosure, or coordinated disclosure. If a researcher finds a serious bug, they quietly tell the company first and give it time to fix the problem before announcing it publicly. That way users get a fix before criminals learn how to exploit the flaw. Teams such as CERT, set up after the Morris worm in 1988, help coordinate this."]},{"heading":"Phishing: A Hook in Your Inbox","paragraphs":["Most attacks today do not start with super-clever code at all. They start with a message. Criminals have found that it is often far easier to trick a person into handing over a password than to break through a computer's defences. This kind of trickery is called social engineering, and its most common form is phishing.","The odd spelling, with 'ph' instead of 'f', comes from hacker slang of the 1990s, when tricksters on services such as AOL sent messages pretending to be staff and asking users to 'confirm' their passwords. The idea is just like fishing: send out lots of bait and wait for somebody to bite. Phishing messages can arrive as emails, text messages, messages inside games and apps, or even phone calls.","Here is a typical scene. A message pops up that seems to come from a well-known company or bank, with the right logo and colours. It says there is a problem with your account, maybe a 'suspicious login' or a 'failed payment', and that your account will be closed in 24 hours unless you click a link and sign in. The link leads to a page that looks exactly like the real login page. But it is a fake, and anything typed into it goes straight to the criminal. The United States Federal Trade Commission lists messages like these, along with fake invoices, pretend government refunds and coupons for free stuff, as some of the most common phishing tricks.","Phishing messages often share the same warning signs. Security agencies such as CISA in the United States point out a few to look for. First, urgency or strong emotion: threats, countdowns, or prizes that sound too good to be true. Second, a request for passwords, codes, money or personal details, which real companies almost never ask for by message. Third, links or email addresses that are slightly wrong, perhaps with a letter swapped or an extra word added to a famous brand name. Fourth, shortened links from senders you cannot check. Bad spelling used to be a clue too, but CISA now warns that some phishing messages have perfect spelling and grammar, so a well-written message is not automatically safe.","The best response is calm and simple. Do not click the link or open the attachment. Do not reply. If you think the message might be real, check another way: open the app or type the company's website address yourself, or ask the person using a phone number you already know. Then report the message using the 'report' or 'spam' button, and delete it. For young readers, the very best move is to show any strange message to a trusted adult. 'Spear phishing' aims at one particular person using real details about them, one more reason to be careful about what you share online."]},{"heading":"The Problem With Secret Keys","paragraphs":["People have been hiding messages for thousands of years. Roman writers described Julius Caesar shifting each letter a few places along the alphabet, so that A became D. In the Second World War, German forces scrambled messages with Enigma machines, and codebreakers at Bletchley Park in Britain worked for years to crack them. Turning a readable message into scrambled nonsense is called encryption, and turning it back is decryption. The secret recipe that controls the scrambling is called the key.","For almost all of history, encryption had one big rule: the sender and the receiver needed the same secret key. This is called symmetric encryption, because both sides hold an identical key, like two copies of the same front-door key. It works well, but it has a nasty problem hidden inside it. How do you get the key to the other person in the first place? If you send it along the same channel as your messages, a spy could copy it. Armies and governments solved this the expensive way, with trusted couriers carrying codebooks in locked briefcases, sometimes handcuffed to their wrists.","Now think about the Internet. You might want to buy something from an online shop in another country, chat privately with a cousin, or log in to your school's website. You have never met these computers. There is no courier. Every packet you send passes through many routers owned by strangers, and any of them could, in principle, copy it. How could two computers that have never met agree on a secret key while everyone else is listening? For a long time, most experts thought this was simply impossible. This is called the key distribution problem.","In the early 1970s a young American named Whitfield Diffie became obsessed with this puzzle, at a time when cryptography was mostly a secret subject for spy agencies. He teamed up with Martin Hellman, a professor at Stanford University in California, while a student named Ralph Merkle was working on similar ideas at Berkeley. In November 1976 Diffie and Hellman published a paper called \"New Directions in Cryptography\" in a journal named IEEE Transactions on Information Theory. It opened with the bold claim that cryptography was on the brink of a revolution, and they were right. The paper described a way for two people to build a shared secret key out in the open, even with an eavesdropper listening to every word. It also introduced the idea of public-key cryptography: a system in which each person has two keys that work as a pair, one that they can show to the whole world and one that they keep completely private.","Here is a famous way to picture their key-exchange trick, using paint. Alice and Bob agree, in public, on a starting colour, say yellow. Each then secretly picks a private colour, mixes it into some yellow, and sends the mixture to the other. Now each adds their own private colour to the mixture they received. Both end up with exactly the same final colour, made of yellow plus both secret colours. A spy saw the yellow and the two mixtures, but un-mixing paint is practically impossible, so the spy cannot work out the final colour. Real computers use enormous numbers instead of paint. In 2006 Hellman suggested the method should really be called Diffie-Hellman-Merkle key exchange, to give Merkle fair credit. In 2015 Diffie and Hellman received the Turing Award, often called the Nobel Prize of computing, for their work."]},{"heading":"RSA, Prime Numbers and a Secret Kept for 24 Years","paragraphs":["Diffie and Hellman had described what public-key encryption should do, but they had not found a complete practical recipe for sending scrambled messages with it. At the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, three young researchers took up the challenge: Ron Rivest and Adi Shamir, who were computer scientists, and Leonard Adleman, a mathematician. For about a year Rivest and Shamir kept inventing possible schemes, and Adleman kept finding ways to break them.","Then, in April 1977, after an evening at a Passover meal, Rivest reportedly could not sleep, kept thinking about the problem, and worked out the key idea overnight. By morning he had most of a paper written. The method was named RSA after the three inventors' initials. In August 1977 the writer Martin Gardner described it in his popular 'Mathematical Games' column in Scientific American magazine.","RSA's magic depends on prime numbers, numbers such as 2, 3, 5, 7 and 13 that can only be divided evenly by 1 and themselves. Multiplying two primes is easy: 13 times 17 is 221, which you can work out in your head. But going backwards is much harder. If someone gives you 221 and asks which two primes made it, you have to try divisions one by one. With small numbers that takes seconds. With two primes each hundreds of digits long, multiplied into a number more than 600 digits long, even the fastest computers on Earth would need an unimaginably long time. In RSA, your public key contains that giant product, and your private key depends on knowing the two secret primes. Anyone can use your public key to lock a message, but only the private key can unlock it.","RSA also allows something called a digital signature. If you scramble a message with your private key, anyone can check it with your public key, which proves that it really came from you and was not changed on the way. This solves the 'is this email really from Bob?' problem that the early network ignored. MIT received a US patent for RSA in 1983, and in September 2000, just before the patent ran out, the company RSA Security released the method for anyone to use freely. Rivest, Shamir and Adleman received the Turing Award in 2002.","And now for one of the best plot twists in computer history. In 1997 the British government revealed that its own codebreakers at GCHQ, the Government Communications Headquarters, had discovered the same ideas years earlier and kept them secret. Around 1970 a GCHQ engineer named James Ellis wrote an internal report arguing that 'non-secret encryption' should be possible, but he could not find the maths to make it work. In September 1973 a young mathematician named Clifford Cocks joined GCHQ, fresh from Cambridge University. A colleague told him about Ellis's puzzle, and Cocks, by his own later account, worked out a method in an evening, essentially the same idea as RSA, four years before Rivest, Shamir and Adleman. Soon afterwards, his school friend Malcolm Williamson, also at GCHQ, worked out a method like Diffie-Hellman key exchange.","Because the work was classified, none of the three could tell anyone, not even to claim credit. Cocks finally described the history in a public talk in December 1997. Sadly, James Ellis had died just a month before. Historians today give credit to both groups: the GCHQ team were first, but the American researchers discovered the ideas independently and published them, which is why the whole world could build on their work. It is a perfect example of something you have seen again and again in this story: great ideas often appear in more than one place at once."]},{"heading":"The Little Padlock in Your Browser","paragraphs":["Look at the address bar of almost any website and you will see 'https' at the start. That 's' stands for secure, and it is public-key cryptography quietly working for you. Here is a way to imagine it. Picture a shop that hands out free open padlocks to anyone who asks, but keeps the only key in a vault. You want to send the shop a secret. You put your secret in a box, snap one of the shop's padlocks shut on it, and post it. Now nobody, not even you, can open that box, except the shop with its key. That padlock is the public key, and the key in the vault is the private key. In practice, your browser uses this trick at the start of a visit to agree a fresh secret key with the website, and then both sides switch to fast symmetric encryption for the rest of the conversation, because the public-key maths is too slow to use for every single packet.","This system began with the company Netscape, which made the most popular web browser of the mid-1990s. Netscape wanted people to feel safe typing credit card numbers into websites, so in 1994 it created HTTPS and a set of rules called SSL, the Secure Sockets Layer. The first version was never released because it had flaws. SSL 2.0 came out in 1995, and a redesigned SSL 3.0 followed in 1996; Netscape's chief scientist, the cryptographer Taher Elgamal, is often called the 'father of SSL'. In 1999 the Internet's standards body took over the job and renamed the improved version TLS, Transport Layer Security. The latest major version, TLS 1.3, was published in 2018.","But how does your browser know that the public padlock really belongs to your bank and not to an impostor? The answer is a certificate, a kind of digital ID card for a website, signed by a trusted organisation called a certificate authority. Your browser comes with a built-in list of certificate authorities it trusts, and it checks each website's certificate against that list. If anything looks wrong, you see a big warning page.","For years, certificates cost money and were fiddly to set up, so many smaller websites never bothered. That changed in 2016 when a non-profit project called Let's Encrypt began giving out certificates free and automatically. Browser makers also started labelling plain 'http' pages as 'Not secure'. The result was a huge shift. As recently as 2018, only around a third of the top million websites used HTTPS by default, yet today most web pages loaded in major browsers use HTTPS.","One important warning: the padlock means your connection is private, not that the website is honest. A phishing site can have a perfectly good padlock too. The padlock stops strangers in the middle from reading or changing what you send, but it cannot tell you whether the person at the other end is trustworthy. For that, you still need your own good judgement."]},{"heading":"Walls, Patches and Bug Bounties","paragraphs":["In old cities, a firewall is a thick wall built between houses so that a fire in one cannot spread to the next. In the 1980s engineers borrowed the name for a computer that sits between a private network and the outside world and decides which traffic may pass. The earliest firewalls were packet filters. Remember that every packet carries a header with addresses and other labels. A packet filter reads those labels and checks them against a list of rules, a bit like a security guard with a clipboard. Packets from approved places, heading to approved services, are waved through. Others are silently dropped. Engineers at Digital Equipment Corporation published some of the first work on this in the late 1980s, and at Bell Labs, Bill Cheswick and Steven Bellovin built and wrote about early firewalls too. Today almost every home Wi-Fi router has a simple firewall built in, and so do most laptops.","Even the best wall is useless if there is a hole in it, and software is full of holes. Large programs contain millions of lines of code written by thousands of people, and some mistakes are almost certain to slip through. When a company discovers a security hole, it writes a fix called a patch and sends it out as a software update. This is why those update reminders on your phone and computer matter so much. Remember WannaCry? Microsoft released the patch for the weakness it used on 14 March 2017, about two months before the outbreak. The computers that had installed the update were protected. Many of the ones that were hit had not.","Because holes are so hard to find, companies have started paying outsiders to help. These rewards are called bug bounties, after the 'wanted' posters and rewards of the old American West. One of the earliest examples came in 1983, when a software company called Hunter and Ready offered a Volkswagen Beetle car, a 'bug', to anyone who found a bug in its operating system. In 1995 Netscape launched a bug bounty for a test version of its browser. Today Google, Microsoft, Apple, Mozilla and many others run bounty programmes, and in 2016 the US Department of Defense ran one called 'Hack the Pentagon', inviting approved researchers to find weaknesses in some of its public websites.","Put all these tools together and you get layers of protection, like a castle with a moat, walls and guards. Engineers call this defence in depth. Yet the most successful attacks still sneak past all of it by tricking a human being into opening the gate. The strongest lock in the world cannot help if someone gives away the key. So the final and most important layer of protection is not a machine at all. It is you, and the habits you choose every time you go online. That is where the next part begins."]}],"deepDive":"Public-key cryptography rests on 'trapdoor one-way functions': operations that are easy to compute but practically impossible to reverse unless you know a secret. RSA uses multiplication of two large primes, p and q. The public key is their product n together with an exponent e; the private key d is computed from p and q so that raising a number to the power e and then to the power d, both 'modulo n' (keeping only the remainder after dividing by n), returns the original number. Anyone who could factor n back into p and q could compute d, so RSA's security depends on factoring being hard. In practice, n is usually 2,048 bits or more, a number over 600 decimal digits long. Diffie-Hellman key exchange relies on a related problem, the 'discrete logarithm': computing g to the power x modulo a prime is quick, but recovering x from the result is extremely slow for well-chosen numbers. Modern systems increasingly use elliptic-curve versions of these ideas, which give similar security with much shorter keys. Two debates shape the field today. The first is about credit. GCHQ's James Ellis, Clifford Cocks and Malcolm Williamson reached the key ideas between about 1970 and 1974, yet because their work stayed classified until 1997 it had no influence on the open world, while the published papers of Diffie, Hellman, Merkle, Rivest, Shamir and Adleman launched an entire industry. Historians usually credit both, separately. The second debate is about the future. A large enough quantum computer, running an algorithm Peter Shor published in 1994, could factor big numbers and solve discrete logarithms quickly, which would break RSA and Diffie-Hellman. No such machine exists yet, but in 2024 the US National Institute of Standards and Technology published its first 'post-quantum' encryption standards, built on different maths, and browsers and messaging apps have begun adopting them. There is also a long-running argument about 'backdoors': some governments want special access to encrypted messages to investigate crimes, while most security experts argue that any secret door built for the good guys will eventually be found by the bad guys too. Finally, remember that cryptography protects data in transit, but most real-world breaches come from phishing, stolen or reused passwords, and unpatched software rather than from anyone breaking the maths.","funFact":"The RSA method was first explained to the public in August 1977 in Martin Gardner's 'Mathematical Games' column in Scientific American, before the inventors' formal paper appeared in a journal.","check":{"q":"Which British mathematician secretly worked out a method like RSA at GCHQ in 1973, years before it was revealed?","a":"Clifford Cocks","wrong":["Tim Berners-Lee","Alan Turing","Ron Rivest"]},"sources":[{"title":"Dr Clifford Cocks CB (honorary graduate, 2008)","publisher":"University of Bristol","url":"https://www.bristol.ac.uk/alumni/our-alumni/honorary-degrees/honorary-graduates/2008/cocks.html"},{"title":"Recognize and Report Phishing","publisher":"CISA (US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency)","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/recognize-and-report-phishing"},{"title":"How To Recognize and Avoid Phishing Scams","publisher":"US Federal Trade Commission","url":"https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/how-recognize-and-avoid-phishing-scams"},{"title":"Diffie–Hellman key exchange","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diffie%E2%80%93Hellman_key_exchange"},{"title":"RSA cryptosystem","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RSA_cryptosystem"},{"title":"Transport Layer Security","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transport_Layer_Security"},{"title":"WannaCry ransomware attack","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WannaCry_ransomware_attack"},{"title":"Creeper and Reaper","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Creeper_and_Reaper"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/17-1.jpg","alt":"A 2008 cryptographers' panel: Whitfield Diffie (on the big screen), Martin Hellman, Ron Rivest and Adi Shamir on stage.","file":"Cryptographers-2008.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cryptographers-2008.jpg","author":"Dan Spisak","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/17-2.jpg","alt":"Ron Rivest, Adi Shamir and Len Adleman, the R, S and A of RSA, together at the CRYPTO 2017 conference.","file":"2017-08-Ron Rivest Adi Shamir and Len Adleman on CRYPTO 2017 in UCSB.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2017-08-Ron_Rivest_Adi_Shamir_and_Len_Adleman_on_CRYPTO_2017_in_UCSB.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/17-3.jpg","alt":"Diffie-Hellman key exchange explained with paint: Alice and Bob mix secret colours and end up with the same shared secret.","file":"Diffie-Hellman Key Exchange.svg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diffie-Hellman_Key_Exchange.svg","author":"Original schema: A.J. Vinck, University of Duisburg-Essen SVG version: Flugaal","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4365},{"n":18,"title":"Being Safe, Smart and Kind Online","when":"Today","summary":"The Internet is amazing, but you need a few good habits to enjoy it safely. Learn how to make super-strong passwords, keep your secrets secret, spot fake stories and be kind to everyone online. And if anything ever feels wrong, tell a grown-up you trust!","sections":[{"heading":"Passwords: The Keys to Your Online Life","paragraphs":["In the last part you saw how engineers built locks into the Internet: encryption, certificates, firewalls and patches. All of them can be undone in a second if someone gets hold of your password. So let us start with the key that you control yourself.","Every year, security companies publish lists of the most common passwords found in leaked data, and every year the same embarrassing favourites appear near the top: '123456', 'password', 'qwerty', and names of football teams. Britain's National Cyber Security Centre, the NCSC, points out that attackers do not guess passwords one at a time like a person would. They use computer programs that can try enormous numbers of guesses, starting with lists of common passwords and dictionary words, then trying every common trick, like swapping the letter 'a' for '@' or adding '1!' at the end. A password such as 'Pa55word!' might satisfy a website's rules, but it is one of the first things those programs try.","So what makes a password strong? CISA, the American cybersecurity agency, gives three tests. First, it should be long: at least 16 characters, because every extra character multiplies the number of possible guesses. Second, it should be random, not your name, your pet, your birthday or your favourite band, which anyone who knows you, or who can see your posts, might guess. Third, it should be unique, meaning you never use the same password on two different sites.","That third rule matters more than most people realise. Websites get broken into all the time, and lists of stolen email addresses and passwords are traded by criminals. If you reuse a password, a break-in at a small games forum could let a stranger try the same password on your email account. Security people call this 'credential stuffing', and it works because so many people reuse passwords. Your email account is especially precious, because it is usually where 'reset your password' messages go. Whoever controls your email can often take over everything else.","A clever way to get long, random and memorable is a passphrase. The NCSC recommends joining three random words, such as 'coffeetrainfish', while CISA suggests four to seven unrelated words, like its example 'HorsePurpleHatRun'. The trick is that the words must be truly random, not a famous phrase or song lyric. A good method is to imagine a silly picture: a purple horse wearing a hat and running. It is easy for you to remember, and very hard for a computer to guess.","But nobody can remember fifty different passphrases. That is where a password manager comes in. It is a program, often built into your browser or phone, that creates strong random passwords for every site, stores them in an encrypted vault, and fills them in for you. You only need to remember one very strong master passphrase. Many security experts now also recommend 'passkeys' where websites offer them. A passkey uses the public-key cryptography you met in the last part, so there is no password to type, steal or phish at all. And one more rule that never changes: a password is like a toothbrush. Do not share it with friends, even your best friend. The only people who might need to know a young person's passwords are their parents or carers."]},{"heading":"Two Keys Are Better Than One","paragraphs":["Imagine a treasure chest with two different locks. A thief who steals one key still cannot open it. That is the idea behind two-factor authentication, often shortened to 2FA, or multi-factor authentication, MFA, when there are two or more.","A factor is a type of proof that you are really you. Security experts sort them into three kinds. The first is something you know, such as a password or a PIN. The second is something you have, such as your phone or a small security key that plugs into a computer. The third is something you are, such as your fingerprint or face. Two-factor authentication means using two different kinds together, usually a password plus a code sent to, or created on, your phone.","Here is how it feels in practice. You type your password, and then the website asks for a six-digit code. You open an authenticator app on your phone, which shows a fresh code that changes every 30 seconds or so, and type it in. Even if a criminal has stolen your password in a data breach, they do not have your phone, so they are stuck.","Not all second factors are equally strong. CISA ranks them. At the top are physical security keys, small devices that you tap or plug in, and which cannot be fooled by fake websites. Next come authenticator apps, especially ones that ask you to match a number shown on screen. Codes sent by text message or email are the weakest kind, because criminals sometimes trick phone companies into moving a person's number to a new SIM card. Even so, CISA's advice is clear: any second factor is better than none. A sensible plan is to switch it on first for your most important accounts, such as email.","Two-factor authentication has one weak spot of its own: you. Tricksters sometimes phone or message people pretending to be from a company, saying 'We have sent you a code, please read it out to us to confirm your account.' Never do this. A real company will never ask you to tell them a login code. The code is meant for the website only, the same way your house key is meant only for your own front door.","If you are young, the grown-ups in your life may manage these settings for your accounts, which is perfectly sensible. The important thing is to understand why the extra step exists. It is a little moment of inconvenience that blocks a huge number of attacks."]},{"heading":"Your Digital Footprint","paragraphs":["Walk across a sandy beach and you leave footprints. Online, almost everything you do leaves a trail too: posts, comments, photos, likes, searches, game chats and the places your phone has been. Together these make up your digital footprint. Some of it you create on purpose, by posting things. Some of it is collected quietly in the background, as you will see later in this part.","The big difference from a beach is that the sea does not wash online footprints away. A photo you share can be copied, screenshotted and re-shared in seconds, by people you have never met. Even if you delete the original, copies may survive for years. Many universities and employers look at applicants' public profiles, and a silly or unkind post made at 12 can be found when you are 22. That is not a reason to be afraid of the Internet, just a reason to pause and ask, before posting anything: would I be happy for my teacher, my grandparents and a stranger to see this, now and in ten years?","Some information should almost never be shared publicly, especially by young people: your full home address, your phone number, the name of your school, your passwords, and where you are right now or will be later. Be careful with photos, too. A picture of you in a school uniform, in front of your house with the number on the door, or next to a street sign, can reveal where you live or study without a single word being written. Many phones also hide the place where a photo was taken inside the picture file, unless location settings are turned off.","Privacy settings help you choose who can see what. Many apps let you make your account private, so only people you approve can see your posts. It is worth checking these settings with a parent or carer, because apps change them from time to time. Remember too that 'friends' online are not always who they say they are. A person who is 'a 12-year-old from your town' might be someone else entirely. If an online contact asks for photos, asks you to keep secrets, or wants to meet in person, tell a trusted adult straight away. That is always the right thing to do, and you will never be in trouble for asking.","Finally, other people's footprints matter as much as your own. Ask before posting a photo of a friend or a family member, and respect it if they say no. Kindness and privacy often go hand in hand."]},{"heading":"Kindness, Cyberbullying and Asking for Help","paragraphs":["Behind every username is a real person with real feelings. That sounds obvious, but screens make it surprisingly easy to forget. When you talk to someone face to face, you see their expression change if your words hurt. Online, you only see text, and a joke that seems funny to you can land very differently on the other side.","When unkindness online happens again and again, on purpose, to scare, anger or shame someone, it is called cyberbullying. It can happen through messages, social media, group chats, online games or even shared documents. It might mean nasty comments, spreading rumours, leaving someone out of a group on purpose, sharing embarrassing photos, or pretending to be someone else. Because phones are always nearby, cyberbullying can follow a person home, which is one reason it can feel so heavy.","UNICEF, the United Nations children's agency, gives clear advice for anyone being bullied online. First, tell someone you trust: a parent, relative, teacher, school counsellor or coach. This is usually the most important step. Second, save evidence, such as screenshots, because online bullying leaves a record that can show what happened. Third, block the person and report the behaviour using the app's report button. UNICEF notes that on big platforms such as Instagram and TikTok, reports are anonymous or confidential, so the person will not be told who reported them. It is also wise not to reply angrily, which can make things worse. Many apps also let you mute, restrict or filter comments if you want less contact without a big confrontation.","UNICEF also makes an important point: you did nothing wrong, so you should not have to give up the Internet to escape bullying. The aim is to make the bullying stop, not to make you disappear. If someone is ever in immediate danger, the right step is to contact emergency services, and many countries have free child helplines staffed by people who are trained to listen.","What if you see someone else being bullied? You can be an 'upstander' instead of a bystander. That does not mean fighting back in the comments. It can be as simple as sending the person a kind private message, refusing to share or like the cruel post, reporting it, and telling an adult. Small actions like these show the person being targeted that they are not alone.","A good test before you post or send anything is to ask three questions. Is it true? Is it kind? Is it necessary? Not every message has to pass all three, because jokes and silly memes are part of the fun of the Internet, but if a post fails the 'kind' test, it is probably best left unsent."]},{"heading":"Be a Detective: Spotting Misinformation","paragraphs":["The Internet lets anyone publish anything, which is wonderful, and also means that false information can spread just as fast as true information, sometimes faster. Misinformation means false or misleading information shared by mistake. Disinformation means false information spread on purpose to fool people. Either way, a good online citizen learns to think like a detective.","Start with three questions. Who made this? Look for a named author or organisation, and ask whether they are experts on the subject and what they might want you to believe or buy. What is the evidence? Good information usually tells you where its facts came from, with links to studies, official data or named witnesses. What do other trusted sources say? This is the most powerful trick of all. Professional fact-checkers often open new tabs and search for the claim or the website's name to see what reliable news organisations, museums, universities or science agencies say about it. This is sometimes called 'reading laterally', because you look sideways across the web instead of only scrolling down the one page.","Watch out for your own feelings too. Fake stories are often designed to make you shocked, scared or furious, because people share strong emotions quickly without checking. If a post makes you think 'I must share this right now!', that is exactly the moment to slow down. Also check the date: an old story or photo can be re-shared years later as if it just happened, and that is one of the most common tricks of all.","Pictures and videos used to feel like proof, but no longer. A deepfake is a video, picture or sound recording created or altered with artificial intelligence to show someone doing or saying something they never did. Today AI tools can also make realistic images of events that never happened, and imitate a person's voice from a short recording. Clues can include strange hands or teeth, blurry edges, lighting that does not match, or lips that do not quite fit the words, but the best fakes have no obvious clues at all. That is why checking the source and finding other trusted reports matters more than staring at pixels. A 'reverse image search' tool can sometimes show where a picture first appeared online.","Remember the lesson from the search engines part too: the top result is not automatically the truest one, and a website with a padlock is not automatically honest. Being a detective does not mean believing nothing. It means checking before you trust, and being willing to change your mind when good evidence appears."]},{"heading":"Who Is Watching? Cookies, Ads and Your Data","paragraphs":["Why are so many websites and apps free? Building and running them costs a lot of money, so many companies earn it by showing advertisements. And adverts are worth more when they are aimed at the right people. That is why many websites collect information about what visitors read, click and buy.","One of the main tools is the cookie, and it has a surprisingly friendly origin. In June 1994 a Netscape programmer named Lou Montulli proposed cookies to solve a simple problem: websites forgot who you were each time you clicked to a new page, so a shop could not remember what was in your shopping basket. A cookie is a small piece of text a website asks your browser to store and send back next time, like a cloakroom ticket. The name came from 'magic cookie', an older programmers' term for a little piece of data that is passed back and forth unchanged. Cookies are still what keep you logged in and remember your basket today.","The trouble came with 'third-party cookies'. These belong not to the site you are visiting, but to another company, often an advertising network whose ads appear on thousands of websites. Each time you visit one of those sites, the ad company can recognise its cookie and add a note to its record of your browsing. Over time, it can build a detailed profile of your interests to choose which ads to show you. Most people did not even know cookies existed until a newspaper article explained them in 1996. Modern apps use many other tracking methods as well, including information about your device and your location.","Governments have responded with privacy laws. Europe's ePrivacy rules from 2002, strengthened in 2009, are the reason so many websites show a cookie banner asking for permission. The biggest law is the General Data Protection Regulation, or GDPR, which the European Union adopted in 2016 and which took effect on 25 May 2018. It gives people rights over their personal data, including the right to see what a company holds about them and, in many cases, to have it deleted. Companies that break the rules can face very large fines. Because so many international companies have European customers, GDPR changed how websites work around the world, and many other countries have since passed similar laws. In the United States, the COPPA law of 1998, which you met earlier, gives special protection to children under 13.","You have choices too. When a cookie banner appears, look for a 'reject' or 'necessary only' button. Check which apps can see your location, microphone and contacts, and switch off anything that does not need them. A good rule: if a service is free, ask yourself how it is paying for itself."]},{"heading":"Bigger Than One Screen: Divides, Shutdowns and Screen Time","paragraphs":["It is easy to imagine that everyone is online. They are not. The International Telecommunication Union, the United Nations agency for digital technology, estimated that about 6 billion people used the Internet in 2025, around three-quarters of humanity. That still left about 2.2 billion people offline, and the ITU has found that most people without the Internet live in rural areas. The gap between people who can get online easily and those who cannot is called the digital divide. It depends on where you live, how much money your family has, whether there is electricity and a signal nearby, and whether people have had the chance to learn digital skills. When homework, job applications, banking and even doctors' appointments move online, being disconnected can mean being left behind.","In some places the Internet is switched off on purpose. An Internet shutdown is a deliberate cutting off or slowing down of Internet or phone services, usually ordered by a government, sometimes for a whole country and sometimes for one region or one app. The digital rights group Access Now, which tracks them through its KeepItOn campaign, counted 313 shutdowns in 52 countries in 2025, the highest number since 2016. Shutdowns often happen during protests, elections or conflicts, and some countries have even cut the Internet during national school exams to stop cheating. Human rights groups argue that shutdowns harm people's ability to get news, contact families, run businesses and reach emergency help.","Other governments use censorship, blocking particular websites, apps or topics while leaving the rest of the Internet working. Debates about where to draw the line between keeping people safe and allowing free speech are some of the hardest questions societies face today, and different countries answer them in very different ways.","Then there is a challenge much closer to home: screen time. The Internet is full of brilliant things, from coding tutorials to museum tours to video calls with grandparents, and not all screen time is equal. Learning and creating are different from scrolling endlessly. But many apps are designed to keep you watching for as long as possible, with endless feeds and autoplaying videos. Sleep, exercise, time outdoors and time with friends and family in person all matter for a healthy brain and body. Simple habits help: keep phones out of bedrooms at night, switch off unnecessary notifications, and notice how different apps make you feel."]},{"heading":"Your Online Safety Checklist","paragraphs":["That was a lot of advice, so here it is squeezed into a checklist you can remember. Think of the word SMART. S is for Secrets: keep your passwords, address, school and location private, and use long passphrases, a password manager and two-factor authentication. M is for Messages: be suspicious of urgent messages, unexpected links and attachments, and anyone asking for codes or personal details. A is for Ask: if something feels wrong, scary or confusing, ask a trusted adult, and remember you will never be in trouble for asking. R is for Real or fake: check who made it, look for evidence and see what other trusted sources say before you believe or share. T is for Treat others well: be kind, think before you post, ask before sharing photos of others, and stand up for people being bullied.","Add two quick habits to that list. Install updates when your device asks, because they patch the holes criminals use. And take breaks: the best Internet users know when to log off.","Every one of these habits is small, but together they make you far safer than any single piece of technology could. They also make the Internet a better place for everyone else, because every account that is hard to break into, and every rumour that is not passed on, makes life harder for tricksters.","And now, a question to carry into the next part. Every day you save photos, send messages, edit school documents and watch videos. When you take a picture and it appears on your tablet too, or a friend can open a document you just wrote, where exactly is all that stuff kept? People say it lives in 'the cloud'. But clouds are made of water droplets, and your photos are definitely not floating in the sky. So where do they really live? Let us go and find out."]}],"deepDive":"Online safety is full of real trade-offs that experts still argue about. Take passwords. For years, organisations forced users to include symbols and change passwords every few months. Research found this backfired: people chose predictable patterns such as 'Summer2024!' and then 'Autumn2024!'. Guidance from the US National Institute of Standards and Technology and Britain's NCSC shifted towards long passphrases, checking new passwords against lists of known breached ones, and not forcing regular changes unless there is evidence of a breach. The next shift is passkeys, which replace passwords with public-key cryptography tied to a device, so there is nothing reusable for a phishing site to steal. Two-factor authentication has its own ranking: text-message codes can be intercepted through 'SIM swapping', while hardware security keys based on the FIDO standards are resistant to phishing because they check the real website's address themselves. Privacy raises bigger questions. Targeted advertising pays for many free services, but it relies on collecting data about behaviour across sites and apps. Laws such as Europe's GDPR, which took effect on 25 May 2018, are built on principles like data minimisation (collect only what you need) and lawful basis (have a valid reason to process data). Critics say cookie banners have turned consent into a box people click without reading, and regulators have fined some large companies hundreds of millions of euros. Browsers have moved to restrict third-party cookies, though the industry has struggled to agree on replacements. Misinformation research is also lively. Studies of professional fact-checkers by Stanford researchers found they were better than many students and even academics at judging websites, largely because they left the page to check what other sources said about it, a habit called lateral reading. Generative AI has made convincing fake images, voices and text cheap, which shifts emphasis from spotting flaws in content to checking provenance: where something came from. Finally, the digital divide is about more than cables. The ITU's estimate of about 2.2 billion people still offline in 2025 hides gaps in affordability, skills, language and gender, and closing them is one of the UN's development goals.","funFact":"The web cookie got its name from 'magic cookie', an older programmers' term for a small piece of data passed back and forth unchanged; Netscape's Lou Montulli brought the idea to the Web in 1994 to let online shops remember shopping baskets.","check":{"q":"On what date did Europe's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) take effect?","a":"25 May 2018","wrong":["1 January 1983","15 January 2001","6 August 1991"]},"sources":[{"title":"Use Strong Passwords","publisher":"CISA (US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency)","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/use-strong-passwords"},{"title":"Require Multifactor Authentication","publisher":"CISA (US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency)","url":"https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-multifactor-authentication"},{"title":"Three random words or #thinkrandom","publisher":"UK National Cyber Security Centre (NCSC)","url":"https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/blog-post/three-random-words-or-thinkrandom-0"},{"title":"Cyberbullying: What is it and how to stop it","publisher":"UNICEF","url":"https://www.unicef.org/end-violence/how-to-stop-cyberbullying"},{"title":"Data protection in the EU","publisher":"European Commission","url":"https://commission.europa.eu/law/law-topic/data-protection/data-protection-eu_en"},{"title":"Facts and Figures 2025: Internet use","publisher":"International Telecommunication Union (ITU)","url":"https://www.itu.int/itu-d/reports/statistics/2025/10/15/ff25-internet-use/"},{"title":"#KeepItOn: fighting Internet shutdowns around the world","publisher":"Access Now","url":"https://www.accessnow.org/campaign/keepiton/"},{"title":"HTTP cookie","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HTTP_cookie"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/18-1.jpg","alt":"YubiKey hardware security keys: one on a keyring and a new one in its packet, used for two-factor login.","file":"YubiKey 5C NFC.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YubiKey_5C_NFC.jpg","author":"Daniel Aleksandersen","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/18-2.jpg","alt":"A bank's two-factor token showing a six-digit code that changes every minute, typed in along with your password.","file":"Wells Fargo 2FA RSA Token - Two Factor Authentication - Six Digit Code (53370207137).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wells_Fargo_2FA_RSA_Token_-_Two_Factor_Authentication_-_Six_Digit_Code_(53370207137).jpg","author":"Tony Webster","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/18-3.jpg","alt":"Map of 2011 Internet use: each country sized by its number of users and coloured by the percentage of people online.","file":"InternetPopulation2011 DeSabbata Graham OII.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:InternetPopulation2011_DeSabbata_Graham_OII.png","author":"Stefano De Sabbata and Mark Graham","license":"CC BY 3.0"}],"words":3791},{"n":19,"title":"Living in the Cloud","when":"1999 – today","summary":"Your photos, songs and saved games don't float in the sky. They live on real computers in huge, humming buildings called data centres. Even a coffee pot and a fizzy-drink machine got connected to the Internet, long before smart speakers!","sections":[{"heading":"Other People's Computers","paragraphs":["At the end of the last part we asked a simple question: when you take a photo on a phone and it magically appears on your tablet, or when a game remembers your progress on a different console, where is all that stuff actually living? The popular answer is 'in the cloud'. It sounds soft and floaty, like your holiday pictures are drifting somewhere above the rooftops. The truth is much more solid, and much more interesting.","The cloud is other people's computers. That is the whole secret. When you save something 'to the cloud', your device chops it into packets, sends it across the Internet exactly the way we followed in Part 7, and it lands on a computer owned by a company such as Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Apple or one of many smaller firms. That computer sits in a building called a data centre, together with thousands or even hundreds of thousands of other computers. Usually your file is copied onto several machines, often in different buildings, so that if one hard drive breaks your photo is still safe somewhere else.","So why the fluffy name? For decades, engineers drawing network diagrams on whiteboards had a habit. When they wanted to show 'the big messy network out there that we don't need to draw in detail', they sketched a lumpy cloud shape. Data went into the cloud on one side and came out on the other. Over time, people started using the word for the services living inside that lumpy shape too. By the mid-2000s, 'cloud computing' had become the fashionable name.","Here is a fun twist: the cloud is actually an old idea coming back. Remember time-sharing from Part 2, when lots of people sat at terminals sharing one giant computer in the 1960s? Back then, some thinkers dreamed of 'computer utilities', where you would plug into computing power the way you plug into the electricity grid. Then personal computers arrived in the 1970s and 1980s, and everyone got their own machine on their own desk. The Computer History Museum describes the cloud as the computer utility returning: once the Web made huge, shared servers cheap and reliable, the balance swung back toward big central computers that many people use at once.","The cloud does three main jobs for you. First, it stores things: photos, videos, school documents, saved games and backups. Second, it runs software for you: when you write in an online document or play a browser game, much of the thinking happens on a faraway computer, and your device mostly shows the results. Third, it lends computing power to businesses and scientists, who can rent hundreds of computers for an hour instead of buying them. Grown-ups sometimes describe these as 'software as a service', 'platform as a service' and 'infrastructure as a service', but the idea underneath is the same. Instead of owning the machine, you borrow it over the Internet."]},{"heading":"Selling Computing Like Electricity","paragraphs":["One of the first companies to bet everything on this idea started in a one-bedroom apartment on Telegraph Hill in San Francisco. In 1999, Marc Benioff, Parker Harris, Frank Dominguez and Dave Moellenhoff founded Salesforce. Their product helped businesses keep track of customers, which does not sound thrilling, but the way they delivered it was. Normally a company would buy big boxes of software discs, install them on its own computers and pay experts to keep everything running. Salesforce said: just open your web browser, log in and use it. All the real work happens on our computers.","When Salesforce officially launched in February 2000, it held an event built around the slogan 'The End of Software'. Of course, it was not really the end of software; it was the end of installing software yourself. Even the dot-com crash that soon followed, which forced the young company to lay off about a fifth of its staff, did not kill the idea. Paying a monthly fee to use software over the Web slowly became normal.","The giant step came from a company most people then knew as an online bookshop. Amazon had spent years building enormous computer systems to run its own website, and it learned how to manage storage and servers on a huge scale. Its engineers realised that other businesses would pay to use the same kind of systems. On 13 March 2006, Amazon Web Services announced Amazon S3, the Simple Storage Service, which it described as storage for the Internet. Anyone with a credit card could store almost any amount of data and fetch it back from anywhere on the Web, paying only for what they used.","A few months later, on 24 August 2006, Amazon opened a test version of EC2, the Elastic Compute Cloud. EC2 let you rent virtual computers by the hour. 'Virtual' means the computer you rent is really a slice of a much bigger physical machine, carefully walled off so that it behaves like a computer of its own. Need ten computers for an afternoon to test a new app? Rent them. Need ten thousand for a big launch day? Rent those too, then hand them back. That word 'elastic' was the magic: computing could now stretch and shrink like a rubber band.","This changed how new companies were born. Before, a small team with a big idea had to spend lots of money on servers before they even knew whether anyone would use their service. After 2006, two friends with laptops could launch a website and let Amazon's machines carry the load. Many famous apps of the following years grew up on rented cloud computers. Microsoft and Google soon built their own cloud businesses, and today those three companies run a large share of the world's rented computing.","Ordinary people met the cloud in friendlier ways. In April 2004 Google launched Gmail, offering what then seemed like a ridiculous 1 gigabyte of free storage, hundreds of times more than many rival free email services. In 2006 Google bought a small online word processor called Writely and turned it into Google Docs. Suddenly two classmates in different towns could type in the same document at the same time, watching each other's words appear. The document did not live on either of their computers. It lived in the cloud, and both of them were simply looking at it through a window."]},{"heading":"Press Play: Streaming Takes Over","paragraphs":["Imagine it is 2006 and you want to watch a film at home. You might go to a video shop and rent a DVD. Or, if your family subscribed to a company called Netflix, a red envelope with a DVD inside might arrive in your letterbox. Netflix had been founded in California in 1997 by Reed Hastings and Marc Randolph, and its first big business was posting discs to people and letting them send them back by mail.","In January 2007, Netflix added a feature called Watch Now, which let subscribers stream films and shows over the Internet to their computers. Streaming means you start watching while the video is still arriving, a few seconds at a time, instead of downloading the whole file first. At the beginning the choice was small and the pictures were often blurry, because many home connections were still slow. But broadband was spreading, and within a few years streaming became the main way many families watched television.","How does streaming keep a show playing smoothly? Your video app downloads a little bit ahead of where you are watching and keeps it in a store called a buffer. If the connection hiccups, the app plays from the buffer while it catches up. Clever streaming services also prepare each show at several quality levels, from fuzzy to crystal sharp. Your app keeps measuring how fast data is arriving and quietly switches between them. That is why a film sometimes turns blocky for a moment and then sharpens again. Netflix has said that streaming in high definition can use roughly 3 gigabytes of data an hour.","If everyone in the world had to fetch the same popular film from one building in California, the Internet's long-distance cables would be jammed. So streaming companies use content delivery networks, or CDNs: thousands of servers spread around the world that keep copies of popular videos close to where people live. Netflix even places its own boxes of storage inside Internet providers' buildings. When you press play, the video may travel only a few dozen kilometres instead of across an ocean.","Music made a similar journey. Remember Napster from Part 15 and the arguments about copying songs? In 2006 in Stockholm, Sweden, Daniel Ek and Martin Lorentzon founded a company that wanted to make legal music easier than piracy. Spotify launched in 2008, letting people stream songs instantly from a giant library, paid for either by adverts or a monthly subscription. Spotify now says it has about 788 million users in about 184 markets. Instead of owning a shelf of CDs, most young listeners today rent access to almost all recorded music.","Games moved into the cloud too. Online multiplayer games run on servers in data centres that keep track of every player's position many times a second, then send updates to everyone. Saved progress is often kept in the cloud so you can pick up on another device. Some services even run the whole game on a powerful faraway computer and stream the pictures to you like a video, while your button presses travel back the other way. For that to feel fair, the delay, called latency, has to stay to a few tens of milliseconds.","All this watching and listening adds up. Industry studies by network companies regularly estimate that video makes up well over half of all the data flowing across the Internet, and some put it at around two-thirds. Text messages and web pages are tiny by comparison. A single minute of high-definition video can contain more data than thousands of pages of a book. When we talk about the Internet getting busier every year, a lot of that growth is people pressing play."]},{"heading":"Inside a Data Centre","paragraphs":["Let us visit one. Picture a long, low building, often windowless, sitting in a field near a power station or a big river. There are tall fences, cameras, security guards and gates that open only for the right badge. Inside, you might have to pass a fingerprint or eye scanner before you even reach the computer halls. Companies guard these places carefully, because they hold the data of millions of people.","Now open the door to a computer hall. The first thing you notice is the noise: a steady roar from thousands of fans. The second is the size. Rows of tall metal cabinets called racks stretch away like library shelves, each about two metres tall. Each rack holds dozens of flat computers called servers, stacked like pizza boxes, with blinking green and blue lights and thick bundles of cables running out of the back. A big data centre can contain tens of thousands of servers or more. Big cloud companies even design their own servers, leaving out anything they do not need, such as screens or fancy cases.","Every one of those computers turns electricity into heat, just as your laptop gets warm on your knees. Packed together, they would quickly cook themselves, so cooling is one of the biggest jobs in the building. Many halls are arranged into 'cold aisles' and 'hot aisles'. Cool air is blown into the aisle at the front of the racks, the servers suck it in, and hot air comes out the back into a separate aisle, where it is carried away. Some data centres use giant air-conditioning machines. Others cool air by evaporating water, a bit like the way sweat cools your skin. In chilly places, engineers can simply use cold outside air for much of the year. Google's data centre at Hamina in Finland uses cold seawater from the Gulf of Finland, and a project there is planned to send waste heat to warm local homes and businesses.","A data centre must never simply switch off. If the power from the grid flickers, rooms full of batteries take over instantly. Within seconds, enormous diesel generators, each one the size of a lorry, roar into life and keep everything running until the grid comes back. Engineers also practise for disasters: networks are connected by more than one fibre cable, important data is copied to other buildings far away, and broken parts are swapped out every day. In a building with hundreds of thousands of hard drives, some will fail on any given day, so the system is designed to shrug it off.","Now the important part: the cloud is not weightless, and it certainly does not run on nothing. The International Energy Agency (IEA) estimated that data centres around the world used about 415 terawatt-hours of electricity in 2024, roughly 1.5% of all the electricity the world used. That is in the same range as the yearly electricity use of a large country such as France. The IEA found that this use had been growing by about 12% a year over the previous five years, and expected it to roughly double by 2030, mainly because of artificial intelligence.","Water matters too. Data centres that cool themselves by evaporation can use large amounts of water, which is a real concern in dry regions. That is why engineers keep inventing better designs. They measure efficiency with a number called PUE, power usage effectiveness: the total electricity the building uses divided by the electricity that actually reaches the computers. A perfect score would be 1.0, meaning no energy at all is spent on cooling or lighting. The best modern data centres come quite close to that, while older ones can use far more. Many cloud companies also buy electricity from wind and solar farms, though making every hour of every day clean is still a hard puzzle."]},{"heading":"A Fizzy Drink and a Coffee Pot Go Online","paragraphs":["The cloud is not only about people storing files. It is also about things talking to the Internet by themselves. And this story begins, as so many good computer stories do, with thirsty programmers.","At Carnegie Mellon University in Pittsburgh, the computer science department had a Coca-Cola vending machine that was popular because it sold bottles cheaply. The trouble was that it sat a long walk from many offices, and freshly loaded bottles stayed warm for a while. Nobody wanted to trek over only to find the machine empty, or to get a warm drink. So in 1982, a group of students and staff, among them Mike Kazar, David Nichols, John Zsarnay and Ivor Durham, wired it up. Small switches in the machine's six columns sensed the bottles, and a program kept track of how long each one had been inside, so it knew which were cold.","Anyone on the university's computers could then type a short command, 'finger coke' followed by the machine's network name, and get a report on which columns had bottles and whether they were cold yet. Because the university's computers were connected to the ARPANET, people elsewhere on the network could ask too. It is often called one of the very first Internet-connected appliances. The university says the department was drinking about 120 bottles a day at one point, so the information was genuinely useful.","Over in England, at the University of Cambridge Computer Laboratory, a different drink caused a different problem. In 1991, about fifteen researchers working in and around a room called the Trojan Room shared a single coffee machine in the corridor. People on other floors would climb the stairs only to find the pot empty. Quentin Stafford-Fraser and Paul Jardetzky came up with a clever fix. They pointed a camera at the pot, ran cables to a computer with a 'frame grabber' that could capture pictures, and wrote a little program called XCoffee. It showed a tiny, grey, low-resolution image of the pot in the corner of their screens, updated about three times a minute.","In November 1993, Daniel Gordon and Martyn Johnson connected the coffee camera to the World Wide Web, which by then had just become free for everyone (Part 10). Now anyone, anywhere in the world, could check the coffee level in a Cambridge corridor. Hundreds of thousands of people did, which is funny, because none of them could drink it. It is often described as the world's first webcam. When the lab moved buildings in 2001, the camera was switched off at 10:54 on 22 August 2001, and the coffee machine was sold on eBay for £3,350 to the German news website Spiegel Online.","In 1999, a British technologist named Kevin Ashton gave a name to the bigger idea. He was working with tiny radio tags, called RFID tags, that could let products such as lipsticks and shampoo bottles report where they were in shops and warehouses. In a presentation he gave while working at the company Procter and Gamble, he called this the 'Internet of Things', and the phrase stuck. The idea is simple: not just people, but objects, could have sensors, collect information and share it over the Internet without a human typing anything.","Today the Internet of Things, or IoT, is all around us. Smart thermostats learn when a family is home and adjust the heating. Video doorbells send a picture to a phone when someone rings. Fitness watches count steps and heartbeats. Farm sensors measure how wet the soil is so that crops get exactly the water they need, and city sensors track traffic and air pollution. Many of these gadgets send their data to the cloud, where it is stored and analysed, and then send instructions back.","There is a catch, and it connects to Part 17. Lots of cheap gadgets were sold with weak, built-in passwords that owners never changed. In 2016, a piece of malware called Mirai took over a huge number of internet-connected cameras and home routers and used them, all at once, to flood a company that helps run the Domain Name System. For several hours many popular websites were hard to reach for people in parts of the United States and Europe. The lesson: every connected thing, even a toaster or a baby monitor, is a little computer that needs updates and a good password."]},{"heading":"Giant Brains in the Cloud","paragraphs":["When you ask a voice assistant what the weather will be, or use a chatbot to help explain a maths problem, the thinking usually is not happening inside your phone. Your words are turned into data, sent over the Internet to a data centre, processed by powerful computers, and the answer is sent back, often in well under a second. Modern artificial intelligence, or AI, is one of the biggest jobs the cloud has ever been given.","Why does AI need so much computing? Today's large AI systems are built from what are called neural networks, loosely inspired by brain cells. A big one contains billions of adjustable numbers, called parameters. To train the system, computers show it enormous amounts of text, pictures or sound and nudge all those numbers, again and again, so that its guesses get a little better each time. That means doing a mind-boggling number of simple multiplications and additions. Training a leading AI model can keep thousands of specialised chips busy for weeks or months.","The chips most used for this are graphics processing units, or GPUs. They were first designed to draw the pictures in video games, which also means doing millions of small calculations at the same time. Engineers discovered that this skill was perfect for AI. Some companies have designed their own AI chips too; Google, for example, announced chips it calls Tensor Processing Units in 2016. These chips are packed into racks so tightly, and run so hot, that some new data centres cool them with liquid flowing through pipes right next to the chips instead of with air alone.","Training is only half the story. Every time someone asks an AI a question, the system has to run again to produce an answer. Engineers call this 'inference'. One question uses only a little energy, but when hundreds of millions of people ask questions every day, it adds up. This is the main reason the IEA expects data centre electricity use to keep climbing, and why technology companies are building new data centres and signing deals for more power stations, including wind, solar and even nuclear power.","There are good sides to having AI in the cloud. A small company or a school does not need to buy a supercomputer to use powerful tools; it can rent them. Scientists can use cloud computers to study weather, design medicines or sort through millions of telescope images. But it also raises fair questions that thoughtful people are still debating: how much energy and water should go into AI, who owns the data the systems learn from, and how can we tell whether an answer is actually true? We will return to some of these big questions in the final part."]},{"heading":"The Cloud's Good Days and Bad Days","paragraphs":["Living in the cloud has real advantages. If your tablet falls in a swimming pool, your photos may already be safely copied elsewhere. Teachers can share work with a whole class in seconds. Families on different continents can edit the same holiday plan. A tiny start-up can serve millions of people without owning a single server. And because big companies spread data over several buildings, the cloud can be more reliable than one computer under somebody's desk.","But putting so much in so few places has risks too. On 28 February 2017, an engineer at Amazon Web Services was fixing a problem with S3 and typed a command meant to take a small number of servers offline. A mistake in the command removed far more than intended. For several hours, a large part of S3 in one region of the eastern United States stopped working properly, and many popular websites and apps that relied on it broke too. Amazon explained what went wrong and changed its tools so one command could no longer remove so much at once. The lesson was clear: when lots of services rely on the same cloud, a single slip can be felt by millions.","There are other questions to think about. If the cloud company closes down or changes its rules, what happens to your files? Who can see your data, and which country's laws apply to it? What happens when the Internet connection fails and your documents are all stored far away? Sensible people keep important things in more than one place, read the privacy settings and remember that 'free' cloud services are often paid for with adverts and data, as we saw in Part 18.","So the next time you upload a drawing or stream a song, picture its real journey: through your Wi-Fi, along fibre cables, into a humming hall full of blinking racks cooled by chilled air or water, copied onto several drives, ready to be sent back the instant you ask. The cloud is not fluffy at all. It is one of the biggest and most carefully built machines on Earth.","And yet, not everyone can reach it. Remember the billions of people still offline from Part 18, many in remote villages, on islands, or in mountains where laying cables is hard and expensive. To reach them, engineers are now looking upwards. Right now, above your head, thousands of new satellites are circling the Earth, and rockets keep launching more. Could the Internet's next big leap come from the sky?"]}],"deepDive":"Historians and economists often describe cloud computing as a shift from owning computers to renting computation, much like factories switched from running their own steam engines to buying electricity from the grid. Writer Nicholas Carr made this comparison famous in his 2008 book 'The Big Switch'. The comparison is helpful but not perfect. Electricity is the same wherever it comes from, while computing services differ in software, privacy rules, prices and the difficulty of moving your data elsewhere, a problem engineers call 'lock-in'. Because a handful of companies (Amazon, Microsoft and Google above all) run a large share of the world's rented computing, regulators in Europe and elsewhere have studied whether customers can switch providers easily and whether outages at one company are a risk to whole economies. Energy is the other big debate. The International Energy Agency estimated that data centres used about 415 terawatt-hours of electricity in 2024, about 1.5% of global demand, and projected roughly 945 terawatt-hours by 2030, with AI the main driver. Estimates vary widely, partly because companies do not publish all their data and partly because efficiency keeps improving: better chips, smarter cooling and higher server use can mean far more computing for the same energy. Engineers track efficiency with power usage effectiveness (PUE), the ratio of total facility power to the power used by computing equipment; leading hyperscale data centres report figures close to 1.1, while older facilities can be much higher. PUE says nothing about whether the electricity is clean, or about water, which is why researchers also measure carbon emissions and water use. Some argue that moving work from many small, inefficient office server rooms into large, well-run cloud data centres saves energy overall; others warn of a 'rebound effect', where cheaper computing simply leads people to use much more of it. Both effects are probably happening at once, and the balance between them is one of the open questions of this century.","funFact":"The Trojan Room coffee pot at Cambridge, often called the subject of the world's first webcam, was sold on eBay in 2001 for £3,350.","check":{"q":"What is 'the cloud' really?","a":"Computers in data centres, owned by companies, that you use over the Internet","wrong":["Data stored in water droplets in the sky","A satellite that holds everyone's photos","A special part inside your phone that stores files forever"]},"sources":[{"title":"Announcing Amazon S3 - Simple Storage Service","publisher":"Amazon Web Services","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2006/03/13/announcing-amazon-s3---simple-storage-service/"},{"title":"Announcing Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (Amazon EC2) - beta","publisher":"Amazon Web Services","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2006/08/24/announcing-amazon-elastic-compute-cloud-amazon-ec2---beta/"},{"title":"The History of Salesforce","publisher":"Salesforce","url":"https://www.salesforce.com/news/stories/the-history-of-salesforce/"},{"title":"Timeline of Computer History: Networking and the Web","publisher":"Computer History Museum","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/timeline/networking-the-web/"},{"title":"The CMU SCS Coke Machine","publisher":"Carnegie Mellon University School of Computer Science","url":"https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~coke/"},{"title":"Data center (energy use, citing IEA estimates)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_center"},{"title":"Trojan Room Coffee Pot timeline","publisher":"University of Cambridge Computer Laboratory","url":"https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/coffee/qsf/timeline.html"},{"title":"Spotify company info","publisher":"Spotify Newsroom","url":"https://newsroom.spotify.com/company-info/"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/19-1.jpg","alt":"A Logitech QuickCam Express, one of the small webcams that let home computers send live pictures over the Internet.","file":"Logitech QuickCam Express -8477.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logitech_QuickCam_Express_-8477.jpg","author":"Raimond Spekking","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/19-2.jpg","alt":"A row of equipment racks in a data centre room, standing on a raised floor with air vents for cooling.","file":"Datacenter Server Racks (22370909788).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Datacenter_Server_Racks_(22370909788).jpg","author":"Carl Lender from Sunrise, USA","license":"CC BY 2.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/19-3.jpg","alt":"Aerial view of Google's data centre in Council Bluffs, Iowa, with rows of cooling equipment along its side.","file":"Google Data Center, Council Bluffs Iowa (49062863796).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Google_Data_Center,_Council_Bluffs_Iowa_(49062863796).jpg","author":"Chad Davis","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4240},{"n":20,"title":"The Next Hop: The Future of the Internet","when":"Today – the future","summary":"Thousands of new satellites are circling the Earth to bring the Internet to faraway places, and scientists are building a network that can reach all the way to Mars. The Internet began with just two letters, 'LO'. What will you send next?","sections":[{"heading":"A Necklace of Satellites","paragraphs":["On a clear night, people around the world have started noticing something strange: a neat line of bright dots gliding across the sky one after another, like a string of pearls. They are not aliens. They are Internet satellites, freshly launched and still climbing to their working orbits.","In Part 13 we met the older kind of Internet satellite. Those sit in geostationary orbit, about 35,786 kilometres above the equator, where one trip around the Earth takes exactly a day, so the satellite seems to hover over the same spot. That is handy, because a dish on your roof can stay pointed at it. But it is a very long way up. A signal must travel up and back down, and a reply must make the same trip, so each request and answer can take around half a second or more. That is fine for watching a film, but painful for video calls and games.","The new idea is to fly much lower. On 24 May 2019, the company SpaceX launched a Falcon 9 rocket carrying 60 flat-packed satellites for a network called Starlink, after two test satellites the year before. Most Starlink satellites circle roughly 500 to 600 kilometres up, in what is called low Earth orbit, about sixty times closer than the geostationary ones. At that height, signals can make the round trip in a few tens of milliseconds, similar to many ground connections.","There is a catch to flying low. A low satellite whizzes around the planet in about an hour and a half, so it is only above your head for a few minutes. To give continuous service, you need a huge crowd of them, so that as one disappears over the horizon, another is already rising. That is why Starlink has kept launching, often dozens at a time. By late 2026, more than 10,000 Starlink satellites were in orbit, by far the largest group of satellites ever flown, and the service reported well over 10 million customers. Many newer satellites pass data to each other using laser beams, so a message can hop across space before coming down to a ground station.","On the ground, customers use a flat dish about the size of a pizza box or a small suitcase. Instead of turning to follow the satellites, many of these dishes steer their radio beam electronically, using hundreds of tiny antennas working together. Other companies are building rival constellations, such as OneWeb, and Amazon's Project Kuiper, which began launching production satellites in 2025 and was renamed Amazon Leo later that year.","Why does this matter? Low-orbit satellites can bring the Internet to places where cables are too hard or too expensive to lay: remote farms, mountain villages, islands, research stations in Antarctica, ships at sea and aeroplanes in flight. After hurricanes or earthquakes knock out local networks, satellite dishes can be flown in to reconnect hospitals and rescue teams. For some of the roughly 2.2 billion people still offline that we met in Part 18, this could be a real door to the world, though the dishes and monthly fees are still too costly for many families.","Not everyone is cheering. Astronomers have found bright satellite streaks spoiling long-exposure photos of the night sky, and radio astronomers worry about interference. SpaceX has tried darker coatings and sunshades to make its satellites dimmer. Space scientists also worry about crowding: with tens of thousands of satellites planned by several companies, orbits must be carefully managed so that satellites dodge each other and old ones are steered down to burn up in the atmosphere instead of becoming space junk. And remember from Part 13: even with all these satellites, the great majority of the world's Internet traffic still travels through cables on land and under the sea."]},{"heading":"Faster, Wider, Everywhere: IPv6, 5G and Beyond","paragraphs":["Back in Part 8 we saw that the original Internet addresses, IPv4, offered about 4.3 billion numbers, and that the central pool of free ones ran out in 2011. The replacement, IPv6, has 128-bit addresses, enough for about 340 undecillion devices, a number with 39 digits. That is so many that every grain of sand on every beach on Earth could have trillions of addresses of its own.","Switching the whole Internet to a new address system is like changing the tyres on a car while it is driving down the motorway. For years, almost nobody used IPv6, because IPv4 still worked thanks to clever tricks that let a whole household share one address. But adoption has crept up steadily. Google publishes a running chart of how many of its users reach it using IPv6, and in 2026 that figure has been somewhere around 45 to 50 percent, briefly passing 50 percent for the first time in March 2026, with some countries much higher than others. For a long time both systems will run side by side.","Mobile networks keep getting faster too. The first commercial 5G networks switched on in 2019 in South Korea and the United States, and 5G has since spread across much of the world. 5G can use higher radio frequencies than 4G, which carry more data but do not travel as far or through walls as well, so engineers fill cities with many small antennas on lamp posts and rooftops as well as big towers. 5G is not only about faster phones: it is designed to connect huge numbers of sensors and machines at once, and to give very low delays for things like remote-controlled robots.","Researchers are already working on 6G, which the International Telecommunication Union and phone companies hope to see in the 2030s. Ideas being studied include even higher frequencies, networks that can sense their surroundings like radar, and closer links between phones and satellites, so that one day your phone might connect straight to space when you are far from any tower. Nobody yet knows exactly what 6G will look like, which is part of the fun.","Wi-Fi, which we met in Part 14, keeps leaping ahead as well. Newer generations called Wi-Fi 6, Wi-Fi 6E and Wi-Fi 7 can share the air more cleverly among dozens of devices in a busy home, and some can use a new radio band around 6 gigahertz that is far less crowded. In 1997 the first Wi-Fi standard offered 2 megabits per second; the latest equipment can, in ideal lab conditions, reach speeds thousands of times higher. Your own home will never quite hit those numbers, because walls, distance and neighbours' networks all get in the way, but the direction is clear.","Underneath all the radio waves, the backbone keeps getting stronger too. Each new generation of undersea cable carries more than the last, because engineers squeeze more fibre pairs into each cable and send many colours of laser light down each fibre at the same time, a trick called wavelength-division multiplexing. Big technology companies now pay for many of the new cables themselves, so that their data centres on different continents can stay tightly linked. The everyday result is easy to miss: a video call to the other side of the world that would have seemed like magic in 1990 now feels completely normal.","Engineers also keep improving the rules that packets follow. A newer transport protocol called QUIC, first developed at Google and then turned into an open Internet standard in 2021, can set up secure connections more quickly than the older combination of TCP and encryption, and copes better when your phone hops from Wi-Fi to mobile data. Today a large share of web traffic, including many videos and searches, already travels using it, even though most people have never heard its name. That is how the Internet usually changes: quietly, one agreed upgrade at a time."]},{"heading":"An Internet for the Solar System","paragraphs":["Now let us think much, much bigger. Imagine an astronaut on Mars wants to send a message home. Radio signals travel at the speed of light, but Mars is so far away that, depending on where the two planets are in their orbits, a signal takes between about 3 and 22 minutes to make the one-way trip. Every couple of years the Sun moves directly between Earth and Mars, and for about two weeks communication becomes nearly impossible.","The normal Internet's rules cannot cope with that. TCP, which we followed in Part 7, expects to hear an acknowledgement back fairly quickly. If one does not arrive, it assumes something went wrong and starts resending or gives up. On a link where every 'got it!' takes forty minutes to come back, and where the connection vanishes for hours whenever a planet rotates a spacecraft out of sight, TCP would be hopelessly confused.","Vint Cerf, the co-designer of TCP/IP from Part 6, became fascinated by this problem. Around 1998 he began working with engineers at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in California, including Adrian Hooke, on what they called the Interplanetary Internet. Their answer became known as delay- and disruption-tolerant networking, or DTN.","The key idea of DTN is called 'store and forward'. Data is packed into bundles. Instead of expecting a clear path all the way from start to finish, each machine along the way, whether a rover, an orbiter or a ground station, holds onto a bundle safely in its memory until the next link opens up, then passes it on. It is a bit like a relay race in which runners may have to wait at the handover point until the next runner appears, but nobody ever drops the baton. As Cerf has pointed out, every new spacecraft that carries the software becomes another possible node in a growing network.","In October 2008, NASA tested the idea for real. Engineers uploaded DTN software to the EPOXI spacecraft (formerly Deep Impact), which was then about 20 million miles (about 32 million kilometres) from Earth on its way to a comet, and used it to send images to and from the spacecraft as if it were a node on a space network. Later, DTN was tested aboard the International Space Station. The rules were written up as open Internet documents: the Bundle Protocol was first described in RFC 5050 in 2007 and updated as Bundle Protocol Version 7 in RFC 9171 in January 2022.","Picture how a message from Mars might really travel. A rover takes a photo and packs it into bundles. When an orbiter passes overhead, the rover hands the bundles up and the orbiter stores them. Hours later, when the orbiter can see Earth and a giant dish antenna is free, it beams the bundles home. NASA's Deep Space Network has three main sites, in California, near Madrid in Spain and near Canberra in Australia, spread roughly a third of the way around the world from each other so that, as the Earth spins, at least one of them can always point toward a distant spacecraft. Their biggest dishes are 70 metres across, wider than an Olympic swimming pool is long. From the ground station, the bundles finally join the ordinary Internet and race to scientists' laptops in milliseconds after a journey of many minutes through space.","The idea has now moved from experiment to everyday tool. NASA reports that its PACE Earth-watching satellite began using DTN for routine data in 2024 and has sent more than 34 million bundles without losing one, and that DTN became an operational service on NASA's Near Space Network and Deep Space Network. DTN is part of the plans for the Artemis missions to the Moon and for future Mars relay satellites. One day, a photo taken by a child living on a Moon base might travel home through exactly this kind of network, a direct descendant of those four ARPANET computers in 1969."]},{"heading":"Who Is in Charge? Big Questions Still Open","paragraphs":["Here is a surprising fact: nobody owns the whole Internet, and no single government runs it. Different parts belong to different people: phone and cable companies own wires, cloud companies own data centres, universities own their campus networks, and you probably own a Wi-Fi router. The rules that let them all work together are looked after by a patchwork of groups. The Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) writes technical standards, the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) looks after the Web's rules, and ICANN coordinates domain names and the numbering system that Jon Postel once managed almost by himself (Part 8). In 2016, the United States government formally handed over its last oversight role in that numbering work to the global Internet community. Experts call this way of sharing control 'multistakeholder' governance: governments, companies, engineers and ordinary users all get a voice.","That does not mean everyone agrees. One long-running argument is about net neutrality, a phrase coined by law professor Tim Wu in 2003. It is the idea that Internet providers should treat all data fairly, and not, for example, slow down one video service to push customers toward another that paid them extra. Supporters say it keeps the Internet a level playing field where a tiny new website has the same chance as a giant. Critics say providers need freedom to manage their networks and charge for heavy use. In the United States, national rules were introduced in 2015, repealed in 2017, restored in 2024 and then struck down by a court in early 2025. The European Union adopted its own open-Internet rules in 2015. The debate is likely to continue.","Privacy is another big question. As we saw in Parts 18 and 19, many free services are paid for by collecting data about what we do. Some countries have passed strong laws such as Europe's GDPR; others have fewer rules. Engineers are also making privacy part of the plumbing: most web traffic is now encrypted, and newer protocols even hide which website names your device is looking up from people snooping along the way.","Then there is the question of who can reach what. Some governments block websites or switch off the Internet entirely during protests or exams, cutting off millions of people at once. Digital rights groups count many such shutdowns around the world every year. Others worry that the Internet could split into separate national networks with different rules, sometimes called the 'splinternet'. The original idea of one open network that anyone can join is not guaranteed; it has to be defended.","Finally, a brand-new puzzle: artificial intelligence can now write articles, draw pictures, copy voices and make realistic videos in seconds. That can be wonderful for creativity and learning, but it can also flood the Web with low-quality pages, fake reviews and convincing deepfakes. Researchers and companies are working on ways to label AI-made content and to trace where a picture really came from. Meanwhile, the skills from Part 18 matter more than ever: check who made something, look for other trusted sources, and be extra careful with anything that makes you feel very angry or very excited."]},{"heading":"Looking Back Along the Wire","paragraphs":["Before we take the next hop, let us look back at how far we have come. Our journey began with riders, ships and Claude Chappe's semaphore towers waving signals across France in the 1790s. Then Samuel Morse tapped out 'What hath God wrought' in 1844, cables crossed the Atlantic, and telephone operators plugged wires into switchboards to connect one call at a time.","Next came the dreamers. Vannevar Bush imagined a desk full of linked knowledge. J.C.R. Licklider joked about an Intergalactic Computer Network, and ARPA was ready to pay for bold ideas. Paul Baran, Donald Davies and Leonard Kleinrock each worked out, in different ways, why messages should be chopped into packets that share the lines instead of hogging them.","In 1969 the ARPANET was born, and its very first message crashed after two letters. Soon Ray Tomlinson was sending email with an @ sign, and Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn designed TCP/IP so that totally different networks could join into one network of networks. On 1 January 1983 the ARPANET switched over, and the Internet as we know it began. DNS gave computers names, NSFNET carried the network to universities, and modems screeched it into homes.","In 1989 Tim Berners-Lee proposed the World Wide Web, a service running on top of the Internet, and CERN gave it away free in 1993. Mosaic and Netscape made it colourful, search engines made it findable, and fibre-optic cables under the oceans carried it between continents. Wi-Fi and smartphones cut the cord. Online shops, Wikipedia, open-source software, social media and video calls changed how people buy, learn, make friends and keep in touch, and also brought new problems: viruses, scams, misinformation and unkindness, which encryption, careful laws and wise habits help to fight. And finally, the cloud turned the Internet into a giant shared computer, humming away in data centres around the planet.","Look at that list and notice something important. There is no single inventor. Hundreds of key people, and millions of helpers, each added a piece over more than two hundred years of communication history. The Internet is less like a single invention and more like a city, built and rebuilt by many hands."]},{"heading":"The Secret Ingredient: People Who Agreed","paragraphs":["So why does it all work? Why can a cheap phone made in one country send a photo through cables owned by dozens of companies, across an ocean, to a laptop made in another country running completely different software? The real secret is not any one cable or chip. It is agreement.","Remember Steve Crocker in April 1969, nervously writing RFC 1 and calling it a 'Request for Comments' so that nobody would think he was giving orders? That friendly, humble tradition never stopped. Anyone can read an RFC for free, anyone can suggest a new idea, and the best ideas are tested on real machines before they become standards. Engineer David Clark summed up the spirit in 1992 with a famous line: 'We reject: kings, presidents and voting. We believe in: rough consensus and running code.' In other words, instead of one boss deciding, people argue it out until most agree, and they prove ideas work by actually building them.","The pile of RFCs has kept growing for more than half a century. They cover everything from how email addresses are written to how bundles travel to spacecraft, and a few are even jokes published on April Fools' Day, including one about sending Internet packets by carrier pigeon. RFC numbers have now passed 10,000: in October 2026 the RFC Editor's website was listing new documents numbered as high as RFC 10,080.","The Internet Society's history of the Internet, written by pioneers including Cerf, Kahn and Kleinrock, says that free and open access to the basic documents was one of the keys to the Internet's success. The same authors warned that the biggest danger to the Internet's future may not be technical at all. It may be whether people can keep agreeing on how it should change. In other words, the Internet's most important protocol is cooperation."]},{"heading":"Your Turn: The Next Hop","paragraphs":["Every person in this story started somewhere ordinary. Charley Kline was a student staying late in a university lab. Ray Tomlinson was tinkering with a side project nobody asked him to do. Tim Berners-Lee was frustrated that he could not find information at work. Quentin Stafford-Fraser just wanted to know whether there was any coffee left. None of them knew they were making history. They were curious, they built something, and they shared it.","The next chapters of the Internet's story have not been written yet, and some of them may be written by you. Maybe you will design a network that links a Moon base to Earth, or a cheaper way to bring connections to villages that have never had them. Maybe you will invent a safer way to protect people's privacy, a fairer way to rank search results, a game that brings faraway friends together, or a website that teaches something wonderful. You do not need to wait until you are grown up to start: learning to code, building a small website, testing ideas and asking good questions are exactly how the pioneers began.","As you build, remember the lessons the Internet learned the hard way. The early network trusted everyone, and that turned out to be a mistake, so build things that are safe. Online spaces are full of real people with real feelings, so build things that are kind. Open rules let millions of strangers cooperate, so share what you learn. And be honest about what you know and what you do not, because accurate information is what makes a network of billions of people actually useful.","Late in the evening of 29 October 1969, in a room at UCLA, a student typed 'L', then 'O', and the computer at the other end crashed. Two letters were all that made it across. From those two letters grew a network that now connects billions of people, stretches under every ocean, circles the Earth in satellites and is reaching out toward Mars. The Internet began with 'LO'. Somewhere, right now, a packet is waiting at the edge of the network for its next hop. What you send next is up to you."]}],"deepDive":"Delay- and disruption-tolerant networking (DTN) challenges one of the Internet's quiet assumptions: that a continuous end-to-end path exists between sender and receiver while they talk. TCP's design relies on round-trip times measured in milliseconds; it sets timers, waits for acknowledgements and retransmits when they do not arrive. Between Earth and Mars, where one-way light time ranges from about 3 to 22 minutes and links are scheduled around planetary rotation, antenna availability and solar conjunction, those timers make no sense. DTN adds an overlay layer, the Bundle Protocol, that sits above whatever links are available. Each node stores bundles persistently and forwards them when a contact opens, and nodes can accept 'custody' responsibility for delivering them. Routing can use contact plans, because orbital mechanics makes many future links predictable. The architecture was described in RFC 4838 (2007), the first Bundle Protocol in RFC 5050 (2007), and Bundle Protocol Version 7 in RFC 9171 (2022), developed in the IETF and the Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems. Low-orbit satellite constellations raise different debates. Supporters point to fast, low-latency coverage for remote areas and disaster response. Critics raise light pollution for optical astronomy, radio interference, orbital congestion and the long-term risk of collisions creating debris, as well as questions about a single private company controlling critical communications. Governance debates are just as lively. The 'multistakeholder' model, in which bodies such as the IETF, W3C, ICANN and regional address registries coordinate the network with input from governments, companies and civil society, was strengthened when the US government transferred stewardship of the IANA functions in 2016. Some governments argue for a larger role for intergovernmental bodies; others fear that would invite censorship. Historians of technology often note that the Internet's success came less from any single breakthrough than from open, loosely coordinated standards that let many independent players build on each other, and many experts see keeping that openness as the central challenge for the next fifty years.","funFact":"There is a real, published Internet document, RFC 1149 from 1 April 1990, describing how to send Internet packets by carrier pigeon, and in 2001 a group in Norway actually tested it with real pigeons.","check":{"q":"What is the key idea that lets delay-tolerant networking work between planets?","a":"Store and forward: each node keeps data safe until the next link is available","wrong":["Sending signals faster than light","Using one very long cable from Earth to Mars","Repeating every message thousands of times without waiting"]},"sources":[{"title":"A Better Network for Outer Space","publisher":"MIT Technology Review","url":"https://www.technologyreview.com/s/411092/a-better-network-for-outer-space/"},{"title":"NASA Sends First Space Mails via New Interplanetary Internet","publisher":"Discover Magazine","url":"https://www.discovermagazine.com/nasa-sends-first-space-mails-via-new-interplanetary-internet-39223"},{"title":"Delay/Disruption Tolerant Networking","publisher":"NASA","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/communicating-with-missions/delay-disruption-tolerant-networking/"},{"title":"RFC 9171: Bundle Protocol Version 7","publisher":"RFC Editor / IETF","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9171.html"},{"title":"Brief History of the Internet","publisher":"Internet Society","url":"https://www.internetsociety.org/internet/history-internet/brief-history-internet/"},{"title":"RFC Editor home page (latest RFCs)","publisher":"RFC Editor","url":"https://www.rfc-editor.org/"},{"title":"Starlink","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starlink"},{"title":"Net neutrality in the United States","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Net_neutrality_in_the_United_States"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/internet/20-1.jpg","alt":"A line of Starlink satellites streaking across the starry night sky over Georgia, USA, in April 2020.","file":"Starlink 6 satellites.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Starlink_6_satellites.jpg","author":"Jud McCranie","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/20-2.jpg","alt":"Vint Cerf, co-designer of TCP/IP and one of the \"fathers of the Internet\", photographed at the Royal Society.","file":"Dr Vint Cerf ForMemRS.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dr_Vint_Cerf_ForMemRS.jpg","author":"Duncan.Hull","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/internet/20-3.jpg","alt":"NASA artwork of the Deep Impact spacecraft after releasing its impactor toward a comet.","file":"Deep Impact.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deep_Impact.jpg","author":"NASA/JPL","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3828}],"words":80992};
